globalThis.B1_BANK={"version":1,"level":"B1 Intermediate","modules":[{"title":"Present & future","first":1,"last":5},{"title":"Stories & conditionals","first":6,"last":13},{"title":"Ability, obligation & deduction","first":14,"last":17},{"title":"Passive, relatives & connections","first":18,"last":22},{"title":"Articles & description","first":23,"last":28},{"title":"Quantities & verb patterns","first":29,"last":33},{"title":"Reporting & prepositions","first":34,"last":37},{"title":"Phrasal verbs & preferences","first":38,"last":41}],"lessons":[{"n":1,"title":"Present Simple vs Present Continuous — B1 Guide","goal":"Describe routines, temporary situations and changing trends.","theory":"\nAll-in-one reference: forms, uses, time expressions, stative/dynamic traps, common mistakes, and quick practice.\n\n**B1 GrammarPresent TensesForm + UsePractice**\n\n### Quick decision guide\n\n**Use Present Simple**\n\n- Habits & routines: **I walk to work.**\n- Facts & permanent states: **Water boils at 100°C.**\n- Schedules/timetables: **The train leaves at 06:42.**\n- Stative meanings: **I believe you.**\n\n**Use Present Continuous**\n\n- Now/around now: **I’m working.**\n- Temporary patterns: **I’m driving this week.**\n- Changing situations: **Prices are rising.**\n- Arrangements (near future): **We’re meeting on Friday.**\n\n**Stative check**\n\n- If the verb is stative (*know, like, own*) → **Present Simple**.\n- Some verbs switch meaning (*have, think, see, be, look*).\n\n### Present Simple — Form\n\n**Affirmative**\n\nI/you/we/they **work**. • He/she/it **works**.\n\n**Negative**\n\nI/you/we/they **don’t work**. • He/she/it **doesn’t work**.\n\n**Question**\n\n**Do** I/you/we/they work? • **Does** he/she/it work?\n\n#### Spelling (3rd person)\n\n- + **-s**: play → **plays**\n- + **-es** after **-sh, -ch, -ss, -x, -o**: watch → **watches**\n- Consonant + y → **-ies**: study → **studies**\n\n**Short forms:** don’t / doesn’t\n\n### Present Continuous — Form\n\n**Affirmative**\n\n**am/is/are + V-ing**: I’m working; She’s working; They’re working.\n\n**Negative**\n\nI’m **not** working; She **isn’t** working; They **aren’t** working.\n\n**Question**\n\n**Am/Is/Are** + subject + V-ing?\n\n#### -ing spelling\n\n- Drop silent **e**: make → **making**\n- Double final consonant after short stressed vowel: run → **running**\n- **ie → y**: lie → **lying**\n\n**Short forms:** ’m not, isn’t/’s not, aren’t/’re not\n\n### Present Simple — Use\n\n- Habits & routines: **I wash my hair every day.**\n- Facts & permanent states: **She’s very tall.**\n- Adverbs of frequency before main verb / after *be*: **He usually gets up early**; **I am never late**.\n- Frequency expressions at the end: **We go to the cinema once a month.**\n- Timetables: **The train leaves at 4.**\n- Narrative/summary, commentaries, headlines, instructions.\n\n### Present Continuous — Use\n\n- Actions now / around now: **I’m reading a new book.**\n- Temporary actions: **I’m taking the bus this week.**\n- With “now/these days/this week/at the moment”.\n- Changing/developing: **Your English is improving.**\n- Future arrangements: **We’re leaving tomorrow at 7.**\n- Annoying repeated habits with *always/constantly/forever*: **He’s always losing his keys.**\n\n### Time expressions & adverb placement\n\n**Present Simple:** always, usually, often, sometimes, hardly ever, never; every day/week; once/twice a week; on Mondays; at the weekend.  \n**Placement:** adverbs go **before** the main verb / **after** *be*. Expressions go at the **end**.\n\n**Present Continuous:** now, right now, at the moment, currently, today, these days, this week/month, nowadays.  \n**Placement:** typically at the **end** or the **beginning**.\n\n### Stative vs dynamic verbs (key traps)\n\n| **Stative meaning (use Present Simple)** | **Dynamic meaning (use Present Continuous)** |\n| --- | --- |\n| **have** (=possess): **I have two cats.** | **have** (=experience): **We’re having lunch/a meeting.** |\n| **think** (=believe): **I think it’s a good idea.** | **think** (=consider): **I’m thinking about moving.** |\n| **see** (=perceive): **I see your point.** | **see** (=meet/visit): **I’m seeing the dentist tomorrow.** |\n| **be** (=state): **James is a doctor.** | **be** (=behave): **You’re being very kind today.** |\n| **look** (=appear): **Tom looks tired.** | **look** (=focus eyes): **She’s looking at you.** |\n\n**Use Present Simple** with stative verbs of sense/opinion/possession: *know, believe, like, love, want, need, own, belong, seem, appear, taste, smell, feel\\** (state).  \n\\*Continuous is OK only for voluntary actions/temporary meanings (e.g., **I’m tasting the soup.**)\n\n### Common B1 mistakes\n\n- **She work every day.** → **She works every day.**\n- **I’m know the answer.** → **I know the answer.**\n- **I’m not understand.** → **I don’t understand.**\n- **Do you are going?** → **Are you going?**\n- **I am agree.** → **I agree.**\n- **We’re go to Spain next week.** → **We’re going to Spain next week.** / **We’re flying next week.**\n- **He’s always to be late.** → **He’s always arriving late.** (annoyance)\n- **I’m needing help.** → **I need help.**\n\n","module":0,"audioNumber":2,"audioTitle":"Life in a Very Big Family","speaker":"Ryan","audio":"https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/67aa2baa0c65412632c4b3d1/69776353264693754b633e82_Life%20in%20a%20Very%20Big%20Family_.mp3","transcript":["Hi, I’m Ryan. Today I want to tell you about a family I know that is very big. The parents, Laura and Mike, already have nine children, and now they are expecting their tenth baby. For many people this sounds shocking, but for them it feels normal, because they have lived this way for years.","Their house is always busy. In the morning, they wake up early because the younger kids need help with breakfast, clothes, and school bags. The older children are more independent. They make simple food, pack lunches, and sometimes help their brothers and sisters get ready. In this family, everyone has small responsibilities. For example, one child feeds the dog, another sets the table, and someone else helps clean the kitchen after dinner.","Of course, raising so many kids is not easy. The parents say the hardest part is time. They don’t have much quiet time, and they need to plan everything. Shopping takes a long time, and the grocery bill is big. They also need a bigger car, and family trips are expensive. Sometimes the parents feel tired, and they admit that they can’t give the same attention to every child every day.","But there are also positive sides. The children learn to share, cooperate, and solve problems together. They are rarely bored, because they always have someone to play with. The house feels warm and full of energy. The parents say they laugh a lot, even on difficult days.","Personally, I think this lifestyle is not for everyone. But if a family is organized, supportive, and realistic about the challenges, it can work. For them, the new baby is not just “number ten.” It is another person they will love and care for—and they are already preparing the room, buying baby clothes, and planning a new routine."],"mappingNote":"Routine versus current change: have nine children / are expecting a baby.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"Laura usually ___ breakfast for the younger children.","answer":"makes","options":["makes","is making","make"],"why":"Usually marks a routine; Laura takes makes.","id":"g1-1"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Please wait. Mike ___ the baby clothes right now.","answer":"is sorting","options":["is sorting","sorts","sort"],"why":"Right now describes an action in progress.","id":"g1-2"},{"type":"gap","prompt":"More families ___ online these days. (shop; continuous)","answer":"are shopping","why":"Use are + shopping for a developing trend.","id":"g1-3"},{"type":"gap","prompt":"I ___ this family needs support. (think; opinion)","answer":"think","why":"Think meaning believe is normally stative.","id":"g1-4"},{"type":"order","prompt":"Build a natural sentence. Use every word.","answer":"The children are getting more independent.","why":"Getting describes a change in progress.","id":"g1-5"},{"type":"edit","prompt":"Correct the sentence: She doesn’t knows the new routine.","answer":"She doesn’t know the new routine.","why":"After doesn’t use the base verb.","id":"g1-6"},{"type":"rewrite","prompt":"Rewrite to show a temporary situation. Start “This month” and use stay: I stay with my sister.","answer":"This month I am staying with my sister.|This month I’m staying with my sister.","why":"A temporary current situation uses present continuous.","id":"g1-7"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"“He is always interrupting me.” What does the speaker most likely express?","answer":"Annoyance about a repeated habit","options":["Annoyance about a repeated habit","A public timetable","A single completed event"],"why":"Continuous with always often expresses irritation.","id":"g1-8"}],"listening":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"How many children do Laura and Mike already have?","answer":"Nine","options":["Nine","Ten","Eight"],"evidence":"already have nine children","id":"a2-1"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What do they say is the hardest part?","answer":"Time","options":["Time","Finding a dog","Learning languages"],"evidence":"the hardest part is time","id":"a2-2"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is a benefit for the children?","answer":"They learn to cooperate.","options":["They learn to cooperate.","They never have responsibilities.","They never disagree."],"evidence":"share, cooperate, and solve problems together","id":"a2-3"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"The parents can give equal attention to every child every day.","answer":"False","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"they can’t give the same attention","id":"a2-4"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"The older children sometimes help younger ones get ready.","answer":"True","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"sometimes help their brothers and sisters get ready","id":"a2-5"},{"type":"sequence","prompt":"Put these events or ideas in the order they appear in the recording.","events":[["The parents wake up early.","they wake up early"],["The older children pack lunches.","pack lunches"],["The kitchen is cleaned after dinner.","clean the kitchen after dinner"]],"answer":["The parents wake up early.","The older children pack lunches.","The kitchen is cleaned after dinner."],"evidence":"they wake up early / pack lunches / clean the kitchen after dinner","id":"a2-6"}],"vocab":[{"id":"v2-1","word":"expecting","definition":"waiting for the birth of a baby"},{"id":"v2-2","word":"responsibility","definition":"a duty you are expected to carry out"},{"id":"v2-3","word":"independent","definition":"able to do things without help"},{"id":"v2-4","word":"cooperate","definition":"work together towards a shared aim"},{"id":"v2-5","word":"supportive","definition":"giving help and encouragement"}],"writing":"Describe the advantages and challenges of living in a large household. Focus: Describe routines, temporary situations and changing trends.","speaking":"Compare a small and a large family, avoiding assumptions about every family. Focus: Describe routines, temporary situations and changing trends."},{"n":2,"title":"Future forms — Will · Be going to · Present continuous","goal":"Choose future forms by intention, arrangement, evidence and instant decision.","theory":"\n**Predictions, plans & arrangements, timetables, time clauses, adverb placement, and common pitfalls (B1).**\n\n**willbe going topresent continuouspresent simple (timetables)time clausesmay/might**\n\n**🧩Forms**\n\n- **will + base verb** — *I’ll call you later.*\n- **be going to + base verb** — *We’re going to visit my aunt.*\n- **present continuous (future)** — *I’m meeting Anna at 6.*\n- **present simple (timetable)** — *The train leaves at 07:12.*\n\n**Short forms: I’ll / won’t · I’m/you’re/he’s going to**\n\n**🔮Predictions**\n\n**MEANING**\n\n- **will** = opinion/guess: *I think he’ll win.*\n- **be going to** = present evidence: *Look at those clouds — it’s going to rain.*\n- **may/might** = possible (not sure): *I might join you later.*\n\nℹ️\n\nUse **probably** after the modal: `will probably`, `is probably going to`. Prefer **Maybe/Perhaps** at the start.\n\n**⚡Decisions**\n\n- **will** = decision now: *We’re out of milk? I’ll buy some.*\n- **be going to** = prior plan/intention: *I’m going to start yoga next month.*\n\n**📅Arrangements**\n\n- **present continuous** for fixed plans with people/time: *We’re having lunch at 1.*\n- **be going to** can also express arranged plans.\n- Tip: include a time word — *tonight / at 6 / next week*.\n\n**🕘Timetables**\n\n- **present simple** for fixed schedules: *The exam starts at 9.*\n\n**⏱️Future time clauses**\n\n**RULE**After **when, as soon as, until, before, after, if** → use **present** (not *will*).  \n**✅** *I’ll call you when I get home.*   **❌** *…when I will get…*\n\n**🤝Will: offers / requests / promises**\n\n- **Offers**: *I’ll carry that for you.* · *Shall I help?* (BrE)\n- **Requests**: *Will you open the window, please?*\n- **Promises/refusals**: *I’ll always love you.* · *He won’t do it.*\n- **Suggestions**: *Shall we go out tonight?* (BrE)\n- **Future facts**: *The president will visit Rome in May.*\n\n**🧭Adverbs of certainty**\n\n#### Start of sentence\n\n- **Maybe / Perhaps** he’ll call.\n\n#### After the modal\n\n- She **will probably** be late.\n- They’re **probably going to** move.\n\n**🎯Feel the difference**\n\n- **Spontaneous vs planned**: *I’ll make a sandwich.* / *I’m going to cook soup tonight.*\n- **Evidence vs belief**: *It’s going to break!* / *It’ll be fine.*\n- **Arrangement vs timetable**: *We’re meeting at 6.* / *The film starts at 6.*\n\n**🛠️Common mistakes**\n\n- **❌** *I’ll text you when I will arrive.* → **✅** *…when I arrive.*\n- **❌** *It might probably snow.* → **✅** *It will probably snow / Maybe it’ll snow.*\n- **❌** *I go to dentist tomorrow.* → **✅** *I’m seeing the dentist tomorrow.*\n- **❌** *He don’t will come.* → **✅** *He won’t come.*\n\n**➕Useful extras**\n\n- **be about to** = very near future: *I’m about to leave.*\n- **be due to / be set to**: *The flight is due to land at 9.*\n- **future continuous** (*will be V-ing*): *This time tomorrow I’ll be flying.* / Polite: *Will you be joining us?*\n\n**🤔May & might (future)**\n\n- Possible: *I might be late for dinner.*\n- Not certain plan: *I might go to Denmark next September.*\n- Negative possibility: *We may not have enough time.*\n\n","module":0,"audioNumber":10,"audioTitle":"Learning a New Skill for the Future","speaker":"Sofia","audio":"https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/67aa2baa0c65412632c4b3d1/6975f90657c0ac11f0f2fe11_SCRIPT%20(Female%20speaker%20%E2%80%94%20Sofia)_%20%E2%80%9CLearning%20a%20New%20Skill%20for%20the%20Future%E2%80%9D_.mp3","transcript":["Hi, I’m Sofia. Today I want to talk about learning a new skill, because I’m trying to change my life step by step. This year I decided to learn digital skills for the future, not just for fun. I want to feel more confident at work and have more options later.","A few months ago, I noticed something simple: many jobs are changing. People use online tools, automation, and AI more and more. At first, it sounded scary, because I thought, “This is not for me.” But then I realized that I don’t need to be a genius. I just need a clear plan and a little practice every day.","So I started small. I chose one topic: how to organize tasks and save time. I learned how to use calendars, reminders, and simple templates. Then I tried basic automation—like sending messages automatically or moving information from one place to another. It felt difficult in the beginning, but after a week I could already see results. I wasted less time, and I felt calmer.","Now I’m learning how to write better prompts for AI tools. I use them to check my English, create short texts, and explain words in a simple way. I don’t copy everything. I read the result, change it, and learn from it. It’s like having a patient tutor.","My next goal is to build a small project. I want to create a simple learning page with short listening texts and exercises. I will improve it every month. I know it won’t be perfect at first, but that’s okay. The important thing is progress.","For me, learning a new skill is not only about technology. It’s about preparing for the future, being flexible, and believing that I can grow."],"mappingNote":"Future intention to build a project / prediction that it will improve.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"“The printer has stopped.” “I ___ check it for you.”","answer":"will","options":["will","am going","am"],"why":"An offer made now uses will.","id":"g2-1"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"The interview is booked for 10 tomorrow. I ___ the manager then.","answer":"am meeting","options":["am meeting","meet usually","will meeting"],"why":"Present continuous expresses a fixed arrangement.","id":"g2-2"},{"type":"gap","prompt":"Sofia has already decided: she ___ build a learning page. (be going to)","answer":"is going to","why":"An earlier intention uses be going to.","id":"g2-3"},{"type":"gap","prompt":"I’ll send the link when I ___ home. (get)","answer":"get","why":"A future time clause uses present simple.","id":"g2-4"},{"type":"order","prompt":"Build a natural sentence. Use every word.","answer":"The next train leaves at seven.","why":"Public timetables use present simple.","id":"g2-5"},{"type":"edit","prompt":"Correct the sentence: He don’t will miss the deadline.","answer":"He won’t miss the deadline.","why":"Will forms its negative with not, without do.","id":"g2-6"},{"type":"rewrite","prompt":"Rewrite as an arrangement using meet: I have a booked meeting with Anna at six tomorrow. Start “I”.","answer":"I am meeting Anna at six tomorrow.|I’m meeting Anna at six tomorrow.","why":"Use present continuous for an arranged meeting.","id":"g2-7"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"“Look! The glass is going to fall.” Why use going to?","answer":"The speaker sees present evidence.","options":["The speaker sees present evidence.","The speaker is offering help.","The event happened yesterday."],"why":"Going to can predict from what is happening now.","id":"g2-8"}],"listening":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why does Sofia want digital skills?","answer":"More confidence and future options","options":["More confidence and future options","To avoid all work","To become famous immediately"],"evidence":"feel more confident at work","id":"a10-1"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What was her first topic?","answer":"Organising tasks and saving time","options":["Organising tasks and saving time","Building complex robots","Making cinema films"],"evidence":"how to organize tasks and save time","id":"a10-2"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How does she handle AI results?","answer":"She reads, changes and learns from them.","options":["She reads, changes and learns from them.","She copies everything.","She ignores all feedback."],"evidence":"read the result, change it","id":"a10-3"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"She expects her first project to be perfect.","answer":"False","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"it won’t be perfect at first","id":"a10-4"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"She wants to improve the project every month.","answer":"True","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"improve it every month","id":"a10-5"},{"type":"sequence","prompt":"Put these events or ideas in the order they appear in the recording.","events":[["She chose a small practical topic.","I chose one topic"],["She tried basic automation.","I tried basic automation"],["She started learning to write prompts.","Now I’m learning how to write better prompts"]],"answer":["She chose a small practical topic.","She tried basic automation.","She started learning to write prompts."],"evidence":"I chose one topic / I tried basic automation / Now I’m learning how to write better prompts","id":"a10-6"}],"vocab":[{"id":"v10-1","word":"automation","definition":"making a process run with less manual work"},{"id":"v10-2","word":"template","definition":"a reusable pattern for creating something"},{"id":"v10-3","word":"prompt","definition":"an instruction or question given to an AI tool"},{"id":"v10-4","word":"flexible","definition":"able to adapt to changing situations"},{"id":"v10-5","word":"option","definition":"a possible choice"}],"writing":"Outline a small learning project with a purpose, steps and a future plan. Focus: Choose future forms by intention, arrangement, evidence and instant decision.","speaking":"Explain a skill you want to learn and how you will practise it. Focus: Choose future forms by intention, arrangement, evidence and instant decision."},{"n":3,"title":"Present Perfect — Form & Use","goal":"Connect past events and continuing situations to the present.","theory":"\n**B1 Grammar • Light Theme**\n\nClear rules, common time expressions, *been vs gone*, *for vs since*, and how it contrasts with the Past Simple.\n\n**Form**\n\n### Build it\n\n**have/has** + **past participle** (V3) → *regular* **-ed** / *irregular* 3rd form\n\n#### Positive\n\n- I/You/We/They **have seen** it.\n- He/She/It **has finished**.\n\nShort forms: **’ve** / **’s**\n\n#### Negative\n\n- I/You/We/They **haven’t** seen it.\n- He/She/It **hasn’t** finished.\n\nUse **haven’t/hasn’t** + V3\n\n#### Questions & short answers\n\n| **Question** | **Short answer** |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Have** you finished *yet*? | **Yes, I have.** / **\\*Yes, I’ve.** |\n| **Has** she called? | **No, she hasn’t.** |\n\n**Use**\n\n### When do we use the Present Perfect?\n\n1. **Past events with no time given** (relevant now)\n\n   I **have broken** my arm. • She **has found** a job.\n2. **Recent events** with **just**, **already**, **yet**, **recently**\n\n   He has **just** called. • I’ve **already** finished. • Have you finished **yet**?\n3. **Life experiences** (time not stated) — with **ever**, **never**, **before**, superlatives + **ever**\n\n   Have you **ever** eaten insects? • I’ve **never** read that book. • It’s the best film I’ve **ever** seen.\n4. **How many times up to now**\n\n   I’ve watched it **three times**. • We’ve been to Paris **twice**.\n5. **Unfinished time** or situations from past → now — with **for**, **since**, **how long**, **all + time**, **lately**still true\n\n   We’ve been married **for** 25 years. • I’ve lived here **since** 2012. • How long have you been friends?\n\nDo not attach a finished-time expression directly to a present-perfect event: not “I have bought it two years ago.” Use “I bought it two years ago” or “I have had it for two years.” “Since two years ago” can be grammatical as a starting point, although “for two years” is usually simpler.\n\n**Time words**\n\n### Typical expressions\n\n- **just**, **already**, **yet**, **recently**, **lately**\n- **today**, **this week/month/year** (period not finished)\n- **ever**, **never**, **before**\n- **for** + period • **since** + point in time • **all** + time\n\n*Have you seen Ted **today**?* (today isn’t finished)\n\n**Been vs. Gone**\n\n### Where is the person now?\n\n- **has gone to** — the person is **there now**.  \n  Tom **has gone to** Ireland. (He’s in Ireland.)\n- **has been to** — the person **visited and returned**.  \n  We **have been to** Ireland three times.\n\n**For vs Since**\n\n### Choose the right linker\n\n#### **for** + period\n\n- for two hours\n- for three years\n- for a long time\n\n#### **since** + point in time\n\n- since Monday\n- since 2012\n- since I was a kid\n\n**Present Perfect vs Past Simple**\n\n### Use the Present Perfect when…\n\n- **When** it happened is not stated or not important.\n\n  I **have lost** my keys.\n- The time period is **unfinished** (today / this week / this year).\n\n  I **have seen** her **today**.\n- You introduce news/experience; details may follow in Past Simple.\n\n  A: I **have been** to the cinema. — B: What **did** you see?\n\n### Use the Past Simple when…\n\n- You **say or ask when** it happened, or you use a **finished time**.\n\n  **\\*We’ve arrived yesterday.**  \n  **We arrived yesterday.**\n- You give **details** about a finished past event.\n\n  I’ve broken my arm. It **happened** when I **fell** off my bike.\n\n","module":0,"audioNumber":15,"audioTitle":"the most ancient cities in the world.","speaker":"Jordan","audio":"https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/67aa2baa0c65412632c4b3d1/697731615226f422e628292d_The%20Most%20Ancient%20Cities%20in%20the%20World_.mp3","transcript":["Hi, I’m Jordan. Today I want to talk about some of the most ancient cities in the world. When we say “ancient,” we usually mean places where people have lived for thousands of years, and the city has continued, in some form, up to modern times. It’s hard to be 100% sure, because history is complicated, but these cities are often mentioned as the oldest.","One famous example is Damascus, in Syria. Many historians believe it is one of the oldest continuously inhabited cities. It has been important for trade, religion, and politics for a very long time. When you walk through the old parts of Damascus, you can still see narrow streets and traditional markets.","Another ancient city is Jericho, in the West Bank. Some archaeological evidence suggests people lived there many thousands of years ago. Jericho is known for its ancient walls and its location near a water source, which helped people survive in a dry region.","In Europe, Athens in Greece is also very old. It became a powerful city in ancient times and is famous for democracy, philosophy, and art. Today you can visit places like the Acropolis and imagine what life was like in the past.","In the Middle East, there is Byblos in Lebanon, another city linked to very early history. It was an important port and trading center. Some people even connect the name “Bible” to Byblos because of trade in writing materials long ago.","What I find interesting is that ancient cities are not just museums. People live there now. They go to work, buy food, and meet friends, but they are surrounded by history every day. For travelers, visiting an ancient city is like stepping into a living timeline. You can see modern life and ancient stones in the same place, and it makes you respect how long human life and culture have been developing."],"mappingNote":"Continuing history: people have lived / the city has continued / it has been important.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"Athens ___ an important cultural centre for centuries.","answer":"has been","options":["has been","is since","was yesterday"],"why":"For centuries connects an ongoing state to now.","id":"g3-1"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Maya has gone to the museum. Where is she?","answer":"She has not returned yet.","options":["She has not returned yet.","She returned yesterday.","She has never visited it."],"why":"Gone to means the visit is still in progress.","id":"g3-2"},{"type":"gap","prompt":"We ___ this exhibition twice. (see; present perfect)","answer":"have seen","why":"Use have + irregular participle seen.","id":"g3-3"},{"type":"gap","prompt":"Have you finished the report ___? (already/yet)","answer":"yet","why":"Yet normally ends a question about completion.","id":"g3-4"},{"type":"order","prompt":"Build a natural sentence. Use every word.","answer":"I have never visited an ancient city.","why":"Never comes between have and the participle.","id":"g3-5"},{"type":"edit","prompt":"Correct the sentence: They have arrived yesterday.","answer":"They arrived yesterday.","why":"Yesterday is finished past time: past simple.","id":"g3-6"},{"type":"rewrite","prompt":"Combine using since: We met in 2020. We are still friends. Start “We have”.","answer":"We have been friends since 2020.","why":"A continuing state from a past point uses present perfect.","id":"g3-7"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"“I have lost my keys.” What is emphasised?","answer":"The loss matters now.","options":["The loss matters now.","The exact date of the loss","A regular daily habit"],"why":"The present perfect connects a past event with now.","id":"g3-8"}],"listening":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why is naming the oldest city difficult?","answer":"History is complicated.","options":["History is complicated.","No ancient cities survive.","All cities were founded together."],"evidence":"history is complicated","id":"a15-1"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which city is linked to ancient walls and a water source?","answer":"Jericho","options":["Jericho","Athens","Byblos"],"evidence":"Jericho is known for its ancient walls","id":"a15-2"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which site does Jordan mention in Athens?","answer":"The Acropolis","options":["The Acropolis","The Eiffel Tower","The Colosseum"],"evidence":"the Acropolis","id":"a15-3"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"Byblos was an important port.","answer":"True","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"an important port","id":"a15-4"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"Ancient cities are only museums without residents.","answer":"False","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"People live there now","id":"a15-5"},{"type":"sequence","prompt":"Put these events or ideas in the order they appear in the recording.","events":[["Jordan describes Damascus.","Damascus"],["He describes Jericho.","Jericho"],["He describes Athens.","Athens in Greece"]],"answer":["Jordan describes Damascus.","He describes Jericho.","He describes Athens."],"evidence":"Damascus / Jericho / Athens in Greece","id":"a15-6"}],"vocab":[{"id":"v15-1","word":"inhabited","definition":"having people living in a place"},{"id":"v15-2","word":"archaeological","definition":"related to studying remains of past societies"},{"id":"v15-3","word":"trade","definition":"buying and selling goods"},{"id":"v15-4","word":"port","definition":"a place where ships load and unload"},{"id":"v15-5","word":"timeline","definition":"an ordered sequence of events in time"}],"writing":"Describe a historic place, separating known information from uncertain claims. Focus: Connect past events and continuing situations to the present.","speaking":"Explain how old and modern life can exist in the same city. Focus: Connect past events and continuing situations to the present."},{"n":4,"title":"For, Since, From — What’s the difference?","goal":"Distinguish durations, starting points and scheduled time ranges.","theory":"\n### Quick rules\n\n- **for** + *period of time* (how long): *for two weeks, for a long time, for ages*.\n- **since** + *starting point* (when it began): *since 2019, since 10 o’clock, since I moved here*.\n- **from** marks a *starting point* for ranges/schedules: *from 9 to 5, from Monday to Friday, from now on* (not with present perfect meaning “until now”).\n\n**for**/**since** are common with the Present/Past Perfect (simple or continuous). **from** is used in other tenses for timetables, plans, or finished time periods.\n\n### *for* — duration\n\n| **Use** | **Examples** |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Present perfect** (ongoing to now) | We’ve been here **for** a few hours. • He’s been studying **for** a long time. |\n| **Past perfect** (ongoing before a past point) | When I met them, they had been married **for** ten years. |\n| **Past simple** (finished duration) | I lived in London **for** 20 years. (not now) |\n| **Future/Plans** | I’ll be away **for** three days. • We’re renting the place **for** the summer. |\n\n- Common phrases: *for ages*, *for a while*, *for the last two weeks*, *for the whole day*.\n\n### *since* — starting point to now\n\n| **Use** | **Examples** |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Present perfect (simple/continuous)** | I have known John **since** I was a child. • He’s been working here **since** 2018. |\n| **Past perfect** (viewed from a past time) | I was exhausted; I had been at work **since** six a.m. |\n| **Conjunction “since + clause”** | *since I got up*, *since we moved*. • *Ever since* we met, we’ve kept in touch. |\n\n- Adverbials: *since then*, *ever since*.\n\n### *from* — ranges, schedules, and starting points (not with “until now” meaning)\n\n- **Range**: *from* A **to/until/till** B — *I work from 8 a.m. to 6 p.m.*\n- **Start-only (non-perfect)**: *Masks will be compulsory from tomorrow.* • *I’m usually here from six o’clock.*\n- **New rule moving forward**: *From now on*, meetings start at 9.\n- **Finished time span**: *The city reported 400 new cases from Friday to Sunday.*\n\nDo **not** use *from* with the present perfect to mean “since then”: ✗ *I’ve lived here from 2019* → ✓ *I’ve lived here **since** 2019* / ✓ *I lived here **from** 2019 **to** 2021*.\n\n### Summary table\n\n| **Word** | **Main idea** | **Typical tenses** | **Examples** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **for** | duration / how long | present perfect, past perfect, past simple, future | for two weeks; for years; for a while |\n| **since** | starting point → up to now | present perfect (often), past perfect | since 2010; since last night; since I arrived |\n| **from** | start of a range/schedule | present simple (habits), future, past simple | from 9 to 5; from Monday to Friday; from now on |\n\n### Common mistakes\n\n- ✗ *I’ve known her for 2019* → ✓ **since** 2019.\n- ✗ *I’ve lived here from 2015* → ✓ **since** 2015 / ✓ **from** 2015 **to** 2018.\n- ✗ *I know him since last year* → ✓ *I’ve known him since last year.*\n- Using **since** with a *period*: ✗ *since two weeks* → ✓ **for** two weeks.\n- Confusing **during** (when) with **for** (how long): *during the meeting* vs *for an hour*.\n\n### Extra notes\n\n- **Present perfect simple vs continuous**: both work with *for/since*. Use continuous for activities (*I’ve been reading for hours*), simple for states/results (*I’ve lived here since 2016*).\n- **Questions**: *How long … for?* / *Since when …?* — *How long have you worked here?* • *Since when have you known her?*\n\n","module":0,"audioNumber":2,"audioTitle":"Life in a Very Big Family","speaker":"Ryan","audio":"https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/67aa2baa0c65412632c4b3d1/69776353264693754b633e82_Life%20in%20a%20Very%20Big%20Family_.mp3","transcript":["Hi, I’m Ryan. Today I want to tell you about a family I know that is very big. The parents, Laura and Mike, already have nine children, and now they are expecting their tenth baby. For many people this sounds shocking, but for them it feels normal, because they have lived this way for years.","Their house is always busy. In the morning, they wake up early because the younger kids need help with breakfast, clothes, and school bags. The older children are more independent. They make simple food, pack lunches, and sometimes help their brothers and sisters get ready. In this family, everyone has small responsibilities. For example, one child feeds the dog, another sets the table, and someone else helps clean the kitchen after dinner.","Of course, raising so many kids is not easy. The parents say the hardest part is time. They don’t have much quiet time, and they need to plan everything. Shopping takes a long time, and the grocery bill is big. They also need a bigger car, and family trips are expensive. Sometimes the parents feel tired, and they admit that they can’t give the same attention to every child every day.","But there are also positive sides. The children learn to share, cooperate, and solve problems together. They are rarely bored, because they always have someone to play with. The house feels warm and full of energy. The parents say they laugh a lot, even on difficult days.","Personally, I think this lifestyle is not for everyone. But if a family is organized, supportive, and realistic about the challenges, it can work. For them, the new baby is not just “number ten.” It is another person they will love and care for—and they are already preparing the room, buying baby clothes, and planning a new routine."],"mappingNote":"The family have lived this way for years; contrast duration with a starting point.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"We have known our neighbours ___ 2018.","answer":"since","options":["since","for","from"],"why":"2018 is a starting point.","id":"g4-1"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"The library is open ___ nine to six.","answer":"from","options":["from","since","for"],"why":"A scheduled range uses from … to.","id":"g4-2"},{"type":"gap","prompt":"Ryan has waited ___ half an hour.","answer":"for","why":"Half an hour is a duration.","id":"g4-3"},{"type":"gap","prompt":"They have lived here ___ they got married.","answer":"since","why":"Since can introduce a clause naming the start.","id":"g4-4"},{"type":"order","prompt":"Build a natural sentence. Use every word.","answer":"From now on we will plan ahead.","why":"From now on introduces a new rule going forward.","id":"g4-5"},{"type":"edit","prompt":"Correct the sentence: I know Laura since last year.","answer":"I have known Laura since last year.","why":"Use present perfect for a state continuing from a past start.","id":"g4-6"},{"type":"rewrite","prompt":"Rewrite with for: She started working here three years ago and still works here. Start “She has”.","answer":"She has worked here for three years.|She has been working here for three years.","why":"Both perfect forms can describe the ongoing period.","id":"g4-7"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which sentence clearly describes a finished period?","answer":"I lived there from 2016 to 2019.","options":["I lived there from 2016 to 2019.","I have lived there since 2016.","I have been living there for years."],"why":"A completed range with past simple is finished.","id":"g4-8"}],"listening":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"How many children do Laura and Mike already have?","answer":"Nine","options":["Nine","Ten","Eight"],"evidence":"already have nine children","id":"a2-1"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What do they say is the hardest part?","answer":"Time","options":["Time","Finding a dog","Learning languages"],"evidence":"the hardest part is time","id":"a2-2"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is a benefit for the children?","answer":"They learn to cooperate.","options":["They learn to cooperate.","They never have responsibilities.","They never disagree."],"evidence":"share, cooperate, and solve problems together","id":"a2-3"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"The parents can give equal attention to every child every day.","answer":"False","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"they can’t give the same attention","id":"a2-4"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"The older children sometimes help younger ones get ready.","answer":"True","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"sometimes help their brothers and sisters get ready","id":"a2-5"},{"type":"sequence","prompt":"Put these events or ideas in the order they appear in the recording.","events":[["The parents wake up early.","they wake up early"],["The older children pack lunches.","pack lunches"],["The kitchen is cleaned after dinner.","clean the kitchen after dinner"]],"answer":["The parents wake up early.","The older children pack lunches.","The kitchen is cleaned after dinner."],"evidence":"they wake up early / pack lunches / clean the kitchen after dinner","id":"a2-6"}],"vocab":[{"id":"v2-1","word":"expecting","definition":"waiting for the birth of a baby"},{"id":"v2-2","word":"responsibility","definition":"a duty you are expected to carry out"},{"id":"v2-3","word":"independent","definition":"able to do things without help"},{"id":"v2-4","word":"cooperate","definition":"work together towards a shared aim"},{"id":"v2-5","word":"supportive","definition":"giving help and encouragement"}],"writing":"Describe the advantages and challenges of living in a large household. Focus: Distinguish durations, starting points and scheduled time ranges.","speaking":"Compare a small and a large family, avoiding assumptions about every family. Focus: Distinguish durations, starting points and scheduled time ranges."},{"n":5,"title":"Present Perfect: Simple vs Continuous (B1)","goal":"Distinguish completed results, quantities and ongoing activity.","theory":"\nForm · Meaning · Signal words · Typical mistakes\n\n## Quick overview\n\n- **Present perfect simple** = *have/has + past participle (V3)* → finished actions with a link to now, life experience, *how many times*, or *states since the past*.\n- **Present perfect continuous** = *have/has been + -ing* → *action* verbs for *duration* from past until now, repeated activity, or recent activity with present results.\n- Use **for** + period (*for two years*), **since** + starting point (*since 2022*), **how long** to ask about duration.\n\n### Form: Present perfect simple\n\n**Affirmative:** *I/you/we/they have* + V3 · *He/she/it has* + V3\n\n✅ I've finished. · ✅ She has found a job.\n\n**Negative:** *haven’t / hasn’t* + V3\n\n❎ I haven’t seen him yet.\n\n**Questions:** *Have/Has* + subject + V3?\n\n⟲ Have you met her? · Has he called?\n\n**Short answers:** Yes, I *have*. / No, she *hasn’t*. (*Not*: “Yes, I’ve.”)\n\n**Short forms:** *’ve, ’s, haven’t, hasn’t*\n\n### Form: Present perfect continuous\n\n**Affirmative:** *have/has been* + V-ing\n\n✅ We have been studying all morning.\n\n**Negative:** *haven’t / hasn’t been* + V-ing\n\n❎ She hasn’t been sleeping well lately.\n\n**Questions:** *Have/Has* + subject + *been* + V-ing?\n\n⟲ Has he been working here long?\n\n### Present perfect simple — main uses\n\n1. **Past recent events** (time not mentioned). She’s had an accident. · I’ve passed the exam.\n2. **Life experience** (we don’t say when). Have you ever tried sushi? · I’ve never been to India.\n3. **Unfinished states** from past to now (often stative verbs). We’ve known each other since school.\n4. **Results** of a completed action now. I’ve washed the car. (It’s clean.)\n5. **How many times** until now. I’ve seen that film three times.\n6. **Been to** vs **gone to**: Tom has gone to Ireland (he’s there now). · We have been to Ireland (we’re back).\n\n### Present perfect continuous — main uses\n\n1. **Duration** from past until now with action verbs. I’ve been working here for five years.\n2. **Recent activity** with present evidence. Have you been crying? (Your eyes are red.)\n3. **Repeated activity** up to now. She’s been calling you all week.\n4. **Emphasis on how long**. I’ve been waiting for hours!\n\n### Signal words & word order\n\n**Simple:** *just, already, ever, never, before, recently, today/this week, so far, yet*\n\n- *just / already / ever / never* normally go **between** *have/has* and the verb: I’ve just finished. · Have you ever been…?\n- *yet* → end position in negatives & questions: I haven’t finished yet. · Have you called yet?\n- *still* with negatives goes before *have/has*: I still haven’t heard from her.\n\n**Continuous:** *for, since, how long, lately, recently, all day/week/month*\n\n- **for** + period (*for two hours*) · **since** + point in time (*since 7 a.m.*)\n- *How long* + present perfect (simple/continuous): How long have you been learning English?\n\n### Simple vs Continuous — which one?\n\n**Use case**\n\n**Choose**\n\n**Example**\n\nFinished action with a present result\n\nSimple\n\nI’ve cleaned the kitchen. (It’s clean now.)\n\nOngoing activity / duration\n\nContinuous\n\nI’ve been cleaning the kitchen *for an hour*.\n\nQuantity / number done\n\nSimple\n\nI’ve written *twenty* emails.\n\nProcess evidence (tired/dirty/sweaty)\n\nContinuous\n\nI’m sweaty — I’ve been running.\n\nStative verbs (be, know, like, own, seem…)\n\nSimple only\n\nWe’ve known each other for years. *Not:* ✗ We’ve been knowing…\n\n### Present perfect or past simple?\n\n- **Present perfect** when *time is not mentioned* or is *unfinished*: I’ve seen John today. (Today isn’t over.)\n- **Past simple** when the *time is finished* or mentioned: I saw John yesterday / at 3 p.m.\n- Introduce with present perfect, then give details in past simple: I’ve broken my arm. — How did it happen?\n- **Never use “ago”** with the present perfect. ✔ I’ve lived here for two years. ✗ I’ve lived here two years ago.\n\n### Common mistakes to avoid\n\n- *\\*Have you finished yet? — Yes, I have.* (**Not**: “Yes, I’ve.”)\n- **May/might + bare infinitive**: *might arrive* (no *to*).\n- *Ever/never/just/already* position: She’s already left. · I’ve just arrived.\n- **Been vs gone:** *has gone to* (still there) vs *has been to* (visited & back).\n\n","module":0,"audioNumber":20,"audioTitle":"Talking About Money","speaker":"Ethan","audio":"https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/67aa2baa0c65412632c4b3d1/696f5d6cefd9e406c6222b80_SCRIPT%203%20(Male%20speaker%20%E2%80%94%20Ethan)_%20A%20mistake%20I%20made%20with%20money.mp3","transcript":["Hi, I’m Ethan, and my relationship with money has changed a lot over the years. When I was a teenager, I didn’t really think about it. If I had some cash, I spent it. I bought snacks, games, and things I didn’t need. I didn’t save because I felt like saving was only for “older people.”","My first real job changed that. I worked part-time and got paid every two weeks. At first, it felt amazing. I finally had my own income, so I treated myself all the time. But then I had a month where everything happened at once: my phone broke, my friend had a birthday, and I had to pay for a course. I realised I had no safety net. I wasn’t in debt, but I felt stressed because I couldn’t cover everything comfortably.","After that, I started doing something simple. I divided my money into three parts: essentials, savings, and “fun.” Essentials were transport, food, and phone bills. Savings were small at first—maybe 10%. Fun was whatever was left. The system was not perfect, but it gave me control. I stopped feeling guilty about spending because I knew I had already saved something.","One of the biggest lessons I learned was about subscriptions and small daily spending. I used to think, “It’s only a coffee,” or “It’s only a small fee,” but those small costs add up. When I checked my bank app, I was shocked. I had multiple subscriptions I didn’t even use, and they were quietly taking money every month. I cancelled most of them, and the difference was real.","Now, I still enjoy life, but I try to be intentional. I don’t buy things just because they are on sale. I ask myself, “Would I buy this at full price?” If the answer is no, I usually skip it. I also try to save for experiences, not only for objects. For me, money is not only about having more. It’s about having less stress and more freedom."],"mappingNote":"Present result: relationship with money has changed; source has no perfect continuous, practised through a follow-up context.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"The budget is ready. I ___ all the figures.","answer":"have checked","options":["have checked","have been check","am checked"],"why":"A completed result uses present perfect simple.","id":"g5-1"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"I’m tired because I ___ accounts all morning.","answer":"have been checking","options":["have been checking","have checked yesterday","am check"],"why":"Continuous emphasises the recent process and duration.","id":"g5-2"},{"type":"gap","prompt":"She ___ three subscriptions today. (cancel; present perfect simple)","answer":"has cancelled|has canceled","why":"A number of completed actions takes the simple form.","id":"g5-3"},{"type":"gap","prompt":"How long ___ you ___ for the refund? (wait; present perfect continuous)","answer":"have been waiting","why":"Write the missing words in order: have + been waiting.","id":"g5-4"},{"type":"order","prompt":"Build a natural sentence. Use every word.","answer":"We have owned this flat for years.","why":"Own is stative; use perfect simple.","id":"g5-5"},{"type":"edit","prompt":"Correct the sentence: I have been knowing him for ten years.","answer":"I have known him for ten years.","why":"Knowing a person is a state, not an ongoing action.","id":"g5-6"},{"type":"rewrite","prompt":"Rewrite to emphasise an activity continuing for two hours: I started studying at nine. It is eleven and I am still studying. Start “I have”.","answer":"I have been studying for two hours.","why":"Continuous highlights duration of the activity.","id":"g5-7"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which question asks about completed quantity?","answer":"How many emails have you written?","options":["How many emails have you written?","How long have you been writing?","Are you writing an email?"],"why":"How many + perfect simple asks for completed items.","id":"g5-8"}],"listening":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"What problem exposed Ethan’s lack of savings?","answer":"Several expenses arrived in one month.","options":["Several expenses arrived in one month.","He lost all his income forever.","He bought a house."],"evidence":"everything happened at once","id":"a20-1"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How did he divide his budget?","answer":"Essentials, savings and fun","options":["Essentials, savings and fun","Travel, rent and gifts only","Cash and debt only"],"evidence":"I divided my money into three parts","id":"a20-2"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What did he cancel after checking his bank app?","answer":"Unused subscriptions","options":["Unused subscriptions","His job","All transport"],"evidence":"I cancelled most of them","id":"a20-3"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"He was in debt during the difficult month.","answer":"False","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"I wasn’t in debt","id":"a20-4"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"He now asks whether he would pay full price.","answer":"True","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"Would I buy this at full price?","id":"a20-5"},{"type":"sequence","prompt":"Put these events or ideas in the order they appear in the recording.","events":[["He spent freely as a teenager.","When I was a teenager"],["He created a three-part budget.","three parts"],["He cancelled unused subscriptions.","I cancelled most of them"]],"answer":["He spent freely as a teenager.","He created a three-part budget.","He cancelled unused subscriptions."],"evidence":"When I was a teenager / three parts / I cancelled most of them","id":"a20-6"}],"vocab":[{"id":"v20-1","word":"income","definition":"money earned or received regularly"},{"id":"v20-2","word":"safety net","definition":"something that protects you during difficulty"},{"id":"v20-3","word":"essentials","definition":"things that are necessary"},{"id":"v20-4","word":"subscription","definition":"a regular payment for continuing access"},{"id":"v20-5","word":"intentional","definition":"done with a clear purpose"}],"writing":"Explain a simple budgeting approach and one lesson from a spending mistake. Focus: Distinguish completed results, quantities and ongoing activity.","speaking":"Compare saving for objects and experiences without giving personal financial advice. Focus: Distinguish completed results, quantities and ongoing activity."},{"n":6,"title":"Past Simple • Past Continuous • Past Perfect — form & use","goal":"Tell a story with main events, background and earlier causes.","theory":"\n### Timeline (quick map)\n\nPast Perfect = earlier past (before the main event)Past Simple = main events (finished, sequence)Past Continuous = background / in progress (often interrupted)\n\nTypical narrative recipe: **set the scene** with *past continuous* → tell the **events in order** with *past simple* → mention what had happened **before** with *past perfect*.\n\n### Forms you’ll need\n\n| **Tense** | **Affirmative** | **Negative** | **Question** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Past simple | **V₂** / **-ed** (affirmative; **did + base** can be emphatic) (irregular: *went, saw*) | **didn’t** + base | **Did** + subject + base? |\n| Past continuous | **was/were** + **V-ing** | **wasn’t/weren’t** + V-ing | **Was/Were** + subject + V-ing? |\n| Past perfect | **had** + **V₃/-ed** | **hadn’t** + V₃ | **Had** + subject + V₃? |\n\nSignal words: *yesterday, last…, in 2019* (PS); *while, when, as* (PC); *before, after, by the time, already, just* (PP). With duration & stative verbs: *for, since, how long, always* (PP).\n\n### Past simple — main uses\n\n- **Finished actions** in a finished time: *We ate out yesterday.*\n- **Events in sequence** (chronological order) in stories: *She opened the door, looked at us and went away.*\n- **Past habits & states**: *We often went to the bar.* • *He was very fit.*\n\n#### When vs while\n\n- **when + PS** often introduces the *short event* that interrupts.\n- **while + PC** introduces the *longer action* in progress.\n- e.g. *I was cooking* when *he arrived*. • *While* *I was cooking*, *he watched TV*.\n\n### Past continuous — main uses\n\n- **Background / scene-setting**: *The sun was shining and people were lying on the beach.*\n- **Action in progress** at a past time, often **interrupted** by PS: *We were talking when she opened the door.*\n- **Two parallel actions**: *I was walking home and listening to a podcast.*\n\n### Past perfect — main uses\n\n- **Earlier past**: event finished before the main past event: *When she arrived, he had already left.*\n- **Past experience before a point**: *When I met her, I had never been in a serious relationship.*\n- **Result visible later**: *He noticed I had cleaned the car — it was shiny.*\n- **Duration with stative verbs** (*for/since/how long*): *When Anne died, they had been married for 48 years.*\n\nUse **PP** only when the order isn’t clear or you want to emphasise the *earlier* action. If the order is obvious, *past simple* is fine.\n\n### How to tell a story (narrative tenses)\n\n1. **Set the scene** with *past continuous*: *It was getting dark and I was walking home…*\n2. **Move the action** with *past simple*: *A car crashed into another one. The police arrived…*\n3. **Give background cause** with *past perfect*: *The driver hadn’t seen the light because he was using his phone.*\n\n### Mini examples\n\n- **PS:** We went to the zoo last week.\n- **PC:** I was walking home when I saw an accident.\n- **PP:** She had finished dinner before we called.\n- **Interrupted:** They were talking when the teacher came in.\n- **Experience before:** I had never been to Rome before 2018.\n- **Duration earlier:** By 2010 they had lived abroad for six years.\n\n","module":1,"audioNumber":26,"audioTitle":"Biography of Mozart","speaker":"Jake","audio":"https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/67aa2baa0c65412632c4b3d1/6977220963e248121d7c7f12_Biography%20of%20Mozart.mp3","transcript":["Hi, I’m Jake. Today I want to tell you about the life of Wolfgang Amadeus Mozart, one of the most famous composers in history.","Mozart was born in 1756 in Salzburg, which is now in Austria. His family was very musical. His father, Leopold Mozart, was a musician and a music teacher. He noticed very early that Wolfgang had a special talent. When Mozart was only a small child, he could play the keyboard and understand music in a surprising way.","Mozart did not have a normal childhood. Instead of spending his time only with toys and games, he practiced music many hours a day. His father took him and his sister, Nannerl, on long trips around Europe. They played music for kings, queens, and rich families. People were amazed because Mozart was so young, but he played like an adult professional.","As he got older, Mozart wanted more freedom. He left Salzburg and moved to Vienna, a city full of music and culture. In Vienna, he worked as a composer and pianist. He wrote music for concerts, for the church, and for the theatre. He became especially famous for his operas, such as The Marriage of Figaro, Don Giovanni, and The Magic Flute. His music was full of emotion, energy, and beautiful melodies.","However, Mozart’s life was not always easy. He often had money problems, because being a freelance musician was risky. He worked very hard, but he did not always earn enough.","Sadly, Mozart died very young, in 1791, when he was only 35 years old. Even though his life was short, Mozart wrote more than 600 pieces of music. Today, people still listen to his work all over the world, and many musicians say his music is some of the best ever created."],"mappingNote":"Mozart’s biography gives a chronological past narrative: was born, practised, moved and died. Follow-up tasks contrast main events, background and earlier causes.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"I ___ other venues when my manager called.","answer":"was checking","options":["was checking","checked tomorrow","had check"],"why":"Past continuous gives the background action.","id":"g6-1"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"When we arrived, the guests ___ already ___.","answer":"had left","options":["had left","have leave","were leave"],"why":"Past perfect marks an earlier event.","id":"g6-2"},{"type":"gap","prompt":"The team ___ a new venue and sent invitations. (find; past simple)","answer":"found","why":"Finished narrative event: irregular found.","id":"g6-3"},{"type":"gap","prompt":"People ___ while we tested the microphone. (chat; past continuous)","answer":"were chatting","why":"Plural subject + were + chatting.","id":"g6-4"},{"type":"order","prompt":"Build a natural sentence. Use every word.","answer":"The event started after we had fixed the lights.","why":"Past perfect highlights the earlier repair.","id":"g6-5"},{"type":"edit","prompt":"Correct the sentence: Did you booked the new venue?","answer":"Did you book the new venue?","why":"After did use the base verb.","id":"g6-6"},{"type":"rewrite","prompt":"Combine with when and past perfect: I arrived. The lecture started before that. Start “When I arrived”.","answer":"When I arrived, the lecture had started.","why":"The past perfect places the start before the arrival.","id":"g6-7"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"“When she arrived, I was packing.” Which is true?","answer":"Packing had already begun.","options":["Packing had already begun.","Packing only began after she arrived.","Packing happens every day."],"why":"Past continuous shows an action in progress at arrival.","id":"g6-8"}],"listening":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"Where was Mozart born?","answer":"Salzburg","options":["Salzburg","Vienna","Paris"],"evidence":"in Salzburg","id":"a26-1"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Who recognised his talent early?","answer":"His father Leopold","options":["His father Leopold","His employer in Vienna","A television producer"],"evidence":"His father, Leopold Mozart","id":"a26-2"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which city did he move to for more freedom?","answer":"Vienna","options":["Vienna","Rome","Berlin"],"evidence":"moved to Vienna","id":"a26-3"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"Mozart’s life was always financially easy.","answer":"False","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"money problems","id":"a26-4"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"He wrote more than six hundred pieces according to Jake.","answer":"True","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"more than 600 pieces","id":"a26-5"},{"type":"sequence","prompt":"Put these events or ideas in the order they appear in the recording.","events":[["He travelled with his father and sister.","long trips around Europe"],["He moved to Vienna.","moved to Vienna"],["He died in 1791.","in 1791"]],"answer":["He travelled with his father and sister.","He moved to Vienna.","He died in 1791."],"evidence":"long trips around Europe / moved to Vienna / in 1791","id":"a26-6"}],"vocab":[{"id":"v26-1","word":"composer","definition":"a person who writes music"},{"id":"v26-2","word":"talent","definition":"a natural ability to do something well"},{"id":"v26-3","word":"freelance","definition":"working independently for different clients"},{"id":"v26-4","word":"melody","definition":"a sequence of musical notes forming a tune"},{"id":"v26-5","word":"opera","definition":"a dramatic performance told through singing and music"}],"writing":"Write a short biography with a clear timeline and a balanced description. Focus: Tell a story with main events, background and earlier causes.","speaking":"Retell key moments in Mozart’s life and separate facts from your opinion. Focus: Tell a story with main events, background and earlier causes."},{"n":7,"title":"Usually, used to, be used to, get used to","goal":"Contrast past habits, present routines and becoming accustomed.","theory":"\n### Quick map\n\n- **used to + base verb** → past habits/states that are **no longer true**.\n- **be used to + noun/-ing** → **be accustomed to** something (a state).\n- **get used to + noun/-ing** → **become accustomed** (the process/change).\n- **usually** (+ present simple) → **present** habits/routines.\n\n### used to + infinitive (past habit/state)\n\n- Repeated past actions: *When I was a child, I used to go skiing every winter.*\n- Past states now false: *I used to live with my mum.* / *She used to be blond.*\n\n#### Forms\n\n- Affirmative: **S + used to + V** → *We used to play*.\n- Negative: **didn’t use to + V** → *I didn’t use to do* sport.\n- Questions: **Did + S + use to + V … ?** → *Did you use to study* in the library?\n\n✔ Standard negative is **didn’t use to**. (You may see *didn’t used to* in some varieties.)\n\n### be used to vs get used to\n\n- **be used to** = be accustomed (state, not usually continuous):  \n  *It was hard at first, but now I’m used to it.* / *I’m not used to driving on the left.*\n- **get used to** = become accustomed (process, often in continuous):  \n  *I’ve just started nights; I’m getting used to working late.* / *You’ll get used to the accent.*\n\n#### Form rule\n\n**be/get used to** take a noun or -ing form because *to* is a preposition: *She’s used to the cold*; *He’s getting used to working with John*.\n\n### usually (present habits)\n\n- Use with the **present simple** for current routines: *We usually go to the cinema on Fridays.*\n- Word order: mid position → *He usually gets up early*; after *be* → *She is usually late*.\n\nDon’t use *used to* for the present. ❌ *We use to go* → ✔ *We usually go*.\n\n### Common mistakes\n\n- ❌ *I’m used to work with John.* → ✔ *I’m used to working with John.*\n- ❌ *Did you used to…?* (non-standard) → ✔ *Did you use to…?*\n- ❌ Continuous with *be used to*: *I’m being used to*… → ✔ *I’m used to*… (state). Use *getting* for the process.\n\n### Mini examples\n\n- **Past habit:** We used to camp by the lake every summer.\n- **Past state:** He used to be shy at school.\n- **Present habit:** I usually read before bed.\n- **Accustomed (state):** She’s used to spicy food now.\n- **Becoming accustomed:** They’re getting used to the new software.\n\n","module":1,"audioNumber":21,"audioTitle":"Moments that changed my opinion","speaker":"Ethan","audio":"https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/67aa2baa0c65412632c4b3d1/696f6fb24f6ac5577ca7cb94_SCRIPT%201%20(Male%20speaker%20%E2%80%94%20Ethan)_%20I%20changed%20my%20opinion%20about%20public%20transport.mp3","transcript":["Hi, I’m Ethan. For a long time, I thought public transport was always uncomfortable. In my mind, buses were slow, trains were crowded, and the subway was stressful. I preferred driving because I liked feeling independent. If I needed to go somewhere, I would take my car even for short trips.","But last year, something happened that changed my opinion. My car broke down on a Monday morning, right before a busy week. I had meetings, errands, and a dentist appointment. At first, I panicked because I thought I wouldn’t manage without a car. Still, I didn’t have a choice, so I bought a weekly pass and started using the subway and buses.","The first surprise was time. I expected my commute to be longer, but it wasn’t. In fact, it was sometimes faster because I didn’t sit in traffic. The second surprise was how much I could do while travelling. I listened to podcasts, answered messages, and even read a few chapters of a book. When I drove, I couldn’t do any of that.","Of course, it wasn’t perfect. Some days the train was full, and once a bus was late. But I realised something important: I didn’t need public transport to be perfect. I just needed it to be reliable most of the time. And it was.","Another thing that changed my mind was money. When I calculated the cost of petrol, parking, and small repairs, I saw how expensive driving was. I hadn’t noticed it before because I paid little by little. But when I looked at the numbers, the difference was clear.","Now I still drive sometimes, especially on weekends or when I go out of town. But during the week, I often choose public transport. I used to think it was only for people who don’t have a car. Now I think it’s simply a smart option, and it makes city life easier."],"mappingNote":"Explicit used to think / past routine versus current choices; becoming accustomed is a follow-up.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"I ___ drive every day, but now I take the train.","answer":"used to","options":["used to","am used to","usually to"],"why":"Used to + base verb describes a former habit.","id":"g7-1"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Taking the subway feels normal now. I am used to ___ it.","answer":"taking","options":["taking","take","took"],"why":"To is a preposition here, so use -ing.","id":"g7-2"},{"type":"gap","prompt":"Did Ethan ___ to travel by car every day?","answer":"use","why":"Did already marks the past: use to.","id":"g7-3"},{"type":"gap","prompt":"The new route still feels strange. I’m ___ used to it.","answer":"getting","why":"Getting used to describes the process.","id":"g7-4"},{"type":"order","prompt":"Build a natural sentence. Use every word.","answer":"I usually listen to podcasts on the train.","why":"Usually goes before the main verb.","id":"g7-5"},{"type":"edit","prompt":"Correct the sentence: She is used to commute by train.","answer":"She is used to commuting by train.","why":"Be used to takes a noun or gerund.","id":"g7-6"},{"type":"rewrite","prompt":"Rewrite with used to: I drove to work every day in the past, but I do not now. Start “I”.","answer":"I used to drive to work every day.","why":"Used to describes a former repeated action.","id":"g7-7"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"“I am getting used to the software.” What does this mean?","answer":"It is becoming more familiar.","options":["It is becoming more familiar.","It was only used in the past.","It has always felt completely familiar."],"why":"Getting used to names the process of adaptation.","id":"g7-8"}],"listening":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"What forced Ethan to use public transport?","answer":"His car broke down.","options":["His car broke down.","He moved to another country.","He lost his driving licence."],"evidence":"My car broke down","id":"a21-1"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What surprised him about commuting time?","answer":"It was sometimes faster.","options":["It was sometimes faster.","It always doubled.","It took all day."],"evidence":"sometimes faster","id":"a21-2"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What costs did he compare?","answer":"Petrol, parking and repairs","options":["Petrol, parking and repairs","Only train tickets","Only insurance"],"evidence":"petrol, parking, and small repairs","id":"a21-3"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"He never drives now.","answer":"False","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"I still drive sometimes","id":"a21-4"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"He could read while travelling by public transport.","answer":"True","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"read a few chapters of a book","id":"a21-5"},{"type":"sequence","prompt":"Put these events or ideas in the order they appear in the recording.","events":[["His car stopped working.","My car broke down"],["He bought a weekly pass.","bought a weekly pass"],["He compared driving costs.","calculated the cost"]],"answer":["His car stopped working.","He bought a weekly pass.","He compared driving costs."],"evidence":"My car broke down / bought a weekly pass / calculated the cost","id":"a21-6"}],"vocab":[{"id":"v21-1","word":"errand","definition":"a short journey to do a practical task"},{"id":"v21-2","word":"weekly pass","definition":"a ticket valid for a week"},{"id":"v21-3","word":"reliable","definition":"able to be trusted to work well"},{"id":"v21-4","word":"repair","definition":"an action that fixes something damaged"},{"id":"v21-5","word":"little by little","definition":"gradually in small amounts"}],"writing":"Describe a time evidence changed an opinion and explain the new view. Focus: Contrast past habits, present routines and becoming accustomed.","speaking":"Present your old and new opinions, then explain the turning point. Focus: Contrast past habits, present routines and becoming accustomed."},{"n":8,"title":"During · For · While (B1)","goal":"Explain when an event happens and how long it lasts.","theory":"\nMeaning · Patterns · Word order · Common contrasts\n\n## Quick overview\n\n- **during** = *when* something happens, used with a **noun**.\n- **for** = *how long* something happens, used with a **time period**.\n- **while** = two actions at the *same time*, used before a **clause** (subject + verb).\n\n### during + noun ➜ “when?”\n\n- Events, time blocks, or activities: during the meeting / the night / the flight / the holidays.\n- Means “at some point in” (not necessarily the whole period). I fell asleep during the film.\n- **Not** followed by a verb. Use a noun or -ing noun: during the lesson · during reading time.\n- **during vs in**: *in* is general time (in summer), *during* links to a specific period/event (during our summer trip).\n- **throughout** = from beginning to end of a period. It rained throughout the night.\n\n### for + time period ➜ “how long?”\n\n- Used with any tense: I waited for two hours. · We’ll be away for a week. · I’ve known her for years.\n- Typical periods: for five minutes / for three days / for ages / for the whole day.\n- In informal speech, *for* may be dropped, esp. in BrE: I’ve known her ten years. (informal)\n- **for vs since**: *for* + period; *since* + starting point. for two years · since 2023\n\n### while + subject + verb ➜ “at the same time”\n\n- Two simultaneous actions: I cooked while she set the table.\n- Often with continuous forms for background action: I hurt my back while I was lifting boxes.\n- **Punctuation:** If the *while*-clause comes first, add a comma. While I was waiting, I read the news.\n- **while vs when**: use *while* for two longer actions in progress; use *when* for a single event interrupting another. I was having a shower when the phone rang.\n- **while vs as**: *as* also means “at the same time” and can suggest gradual change. As it was getting dark, we went home.\n\n### during vs for\n\n**Meaning**\n\n**Pattern**\n\n**Example**\n\nWhen (inside a period)\n\n**during** + noun\n\nI usually run during the weekend.\n\nHow long (length)\n\n**for** + time period\n\nI run for one hour every day.\n\n### during vs while\n\n**Meaning**\n\n**Pattern**\n\n**Example**\n\nWhen within an event\n\n**during** + noun\n\nI fell asleep during the film.\n\nTwo actions at the same time\n\n**while** + clause\n\nI fell asleep while I was watching the film.\n\n### Common mistakes\n\n- ✗ *during two hours* → ✔ **for** two hours.\n- ✗ *for the night* (meaning “at some time in the night”) → ✔ **during** the night. (*for the night* means “for one night as accommodation”.)\n- ✗ *while the meeting* → ✔ **during** the meeting *or* ✔ **while** the meeting **was taking place**.\n- Comma rule: ✔ *While*… , main clause. ✔ Main clause *while*… (no comma).\n\n","module":1,"audioNumber":16,"audioTitle":"My Weekend Hike","speaker":"Alex","audio":"https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/67aa2baa0c65412632c4b3d1/6972475e6fffe2f71b511e75_B1%20Intermediate)%20%E2%80%94%20%E2%80%9CMy%20Weekend%20Hike%E2%80%9D%20Alex.mp3","transcript":["Hi, I’m Alex. Last weekend I went on a hike not far from my city, and it was exactly what I needed. I spend most of my week indoors, so on Saturday morning I decided to get some fresh air and move my body a little.","I didn’t plan anything complicated. The night before, I checked the weather, charged my phone, and packed a small backpack. I took a bottle of water, a sandwich, some nuts, and a light jacket. I also wore comfortable shoes because I knew the path could be muddy after rain.","I left home early, around eight, and took a bus to the edge of the city. From there, I walked for about twenty minutes until I reached the start of the trail. At first the hike was easy. The road was wide, and I could walk fast. But after a while it became narrower and steeper. I had to slow down and watch my steps. I met a few people on the way—some were jogging, some were walking dogs, and others were taking photos.","My favorite part was the view near the top. There was a quiet spot with rocks and tall trees, and I could see the city in the distance. I sat there for a while, ate my sandwich, and just listened to the wind. It felt peaceful, like my mind finally had space.","On the way down, I took a different route. It was a bit longer, but it was more interesting because it went next to a small river. I almost slipped once, but luckily I caught myself. When I got back home in the afternoon, I was tired, but in a good way. My legs hurt a little, but my mood was much better.","Now I want to do this more often. It doesn’t cost much, it reduces stress, and it helps me feel more active. Next time, I’m going to invite a friend, but I’m also fine going alone."],"mappingNote":"Durations and simultaneous hiking actions support for / while; during is applied to the trip context.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"Alex packed his bag ___ the evening.","answer":"during","options":["during","while","for two"],"why":"During is followed by a noun phrase.","id":"g8-1"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"He walked ___ about twenty minutes.","answer":"for","options":["for","during","while"],"why":"For introduces a duration.","id":"g8-2"},{"type":"gap","prompt":"I checked the map ___ my friend was resting.","answer":"while","why":"While introduces a clause with subject and verb.","id":"g8-3"},{"type":"gap","prompt":"We stayed in the shelter ___ the storm.","answer":"during","why":"The storm is an event noun.","id":"g8-4"},{"type":"order","prompt":"Build a natural sentence. Use every word.","answer":"While I was waiting, I read the news.","why":"A while-clause first normally takes a comma in writing.","id":"g8-5"},{"type":"edit","prompt":"Correct the sentence: We walked during two hours.","answer":"We walked for two hours.","why":"Two hours answers how long, so use for.","id":"g8-6"},{"type":"rewrite","prompt":"Rewrite using while: During my wait at the station, I read a magazine. Start “While I was”.","answer":"While I was waiting at the station, I read a magazine.","why":"While takes a clause; during takes a noun.","id":"g8-7"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"“I fell asleep during the film.” Does during mean the whole film?","answer":"No; it can mean at some point within it.","options":["No; it can mean at some point within it.","Yes; always the entire duration","It states how many hours the film lasted."],"why":"During locates an event inside a period.","id":"g8-8"}],"listening":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"What did Alex check the night before?","answer":"The weather","options":["The weather","A flight booking","His work emails"],"evidence":"I checked the weather","id":"a16-1"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What happened to the path as he climbed?","answer":"It became narrower and steeper.","options":["It became narrower and steeper.","It became a motorway.","It disappeared under snow."],"evidence":"narrower and steeper","id":"a16-2"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What was special about the return route?","answer":"It followed a small river.","options":["It followed a small river.","It was much shorter.","It passed through an airport."],"evidence":"next to a small river","id":"a16-3"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"Alex took a light jacket.","answer":"True","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"a light jacket","id":"a16-4"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"He definitely wants to avoid hiking alone next time.","answer":"False","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"I’m also fine going alone","id":"a16-5"},{"type":"sequence","prompt":"Put these events or ideas in the order they appear in the recording.","events":[["He packed a backpack.","packed a small backpack"],["He sat near the top.","view near the top"],["He took a different way down.","a different route"]],"answer":["He packed a backpack.","He sat near the top.","He took a different way down."],"evidence":"packed a small backpack / view near the top / a different route","id":"a16-6"}],"vocab":[{"id":"v16-1","word":"muddy","definition":"covered with wet soil"},{"id":"v16-2","word":"steep","definition":"rising or falling at a sharp angle"},{"id":"v16-3","word":"route","definition":"a way taken from one place to another"},{"id":"v16-4","word":"slip","definition":"lose your footing unintentionally"},{"id":"v16-5","word":"in the distance","definition":"far away but still visible"}],"writing":"Tell the story of an outdoor trip with preparation, an event and reflection. Focus: Explain when an event happens and how long it lasts.","speaking":"Retell Alex’s hike without inventing details, then suggest a next trip. Focus: Explain when an event happens and how long it lasts."},{"n":9,"title":"B1 Review: Present, Past & Future Verb Forms","goal":"Review tense choice by time, focus and the speaker’s purpose.","theory":"\n### Present simple — meaning & signals\n\n- Habits/routines: *I wash* my hair **every day**.\n- Permanent facts & states: *Water boils* at 100°C. *I like* soup.\n- With adverbs of frequency (mid-position): *She usually gets up* early; *He is never* late.\n- Timetabled future: *The train leaves at 7*.\n\nForm: base verb; 3rd person singular **-s/-es**. Negatives/questions with **do/does**.\n\n### Present continuous — meaning & signals\n\n- Actions **now/around now**: *I’m brushing* my teeth.\n- Temporary situations & trends: *She’s living* with friends; *Prices are rising*.\n- Future arrangements (with time): *I’m meeting* John tonight.\n\nForm: **am/is/are + V-ing**. **Stative verbs** (e.g., *know, like, own, seem*) are normally not used in continuous forms.\n\n### Present perfect simple — use\n\n- Past events with **no time** given: *I’ve passed* the exam.\n- Recent results: **just/already/yet** → *She’s just called*; *Have you finished yet?*\n- Experiences: **ever/never/before** → *I’ve never been* to India.\n- Unfinished time & duration: **for/since/how long** → *We’ve lived* here for 10 years.\n- **Been** vs **gone**: *Tom has gone* to Ireland (there now); *We’ve been* to Ireland twice (not there now).\n\nForm: **have/has + past participle**. ❌ Don’t use *ago* with this tense.\n\n### Present perfect continuous — use\n\n- Actions from past till now (often with result): *I’ve been working* all day → I’m tired.\n- Duration & repetition: **for/since/how long/lately/all day**.\n- Contrast: *How long* → use **PPC**; *How many/how much* done → use **PPS** (*I’ve written* 20 emails).\n\nForm: **have/has been + V-ing**. Prefer with **action** verbs; use PPS with stative verbs (*We’ve had* this car for years).\n\n### Past simple\n\n- Finished past actions: *We moved* last year.\n- Main events in order (narratives): *She opened* the door and *went* in.\n- Past habits & states: *We often walked* to school; *He was* very fit.\n\nForm: **V-ed** / irregular forms; negatives/questions with **did**.\n\n### Past continuous\n\n- Background/scene: *It was getting* dark.\n- Action in progress interrupted by PS: *We were talking* when she *came*.\n- **while** + PC (parallel) • **when** + PS (shorter event).\n\nForm: **was/were + V-ing**.\n\n### Past perfect\n\n- Earlier past: *By the time* the police arrived, he *had left*.\n- Experiences/states before another past time: *I had never been* in a serious relationship.\n- Duration with stative verbs: *They had been* married for 48 years.\n\nForm: **had + past participle**.\n\n### Future forms — quick guide\n\n| **Form** | **Use** | **Example** |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **will** | Predictions (opinion), instant decisions, offers, promises, requests. | *I think* it will rain; “We’re out of sugar.” — “I’ll buy some.” |\n| **be going to** | Plans/intentions; predictions with present evidence. | Look at those clouds — it’s going to rain. |\n| **be + V-ing** | Arrangements (time/place decided). Use a time expression. | I’m meeting the dentist at 6. |\n| **present simple** | Public timetables/schedules. | The exam starts on Monday. |\n| **may / might** | Possible future (not sure). | I might be late tonight. |\n\nTime clauses: after *when/as soon as/until/before/after*, use **present** for future meaning → *I’ll call you when I arrive*.\n\n### Signal words & mini-contrasts\n\n| **Tense** | **Common adverbials** |\n| --- | --- |\n| Present simple | always, usually, often, sometimes, never; every day/week; on Mondays |\n| Present continuous | now, right now, at the moment; these days, this week |\n| Present perfect simple | ever, never, before; just, already, yet; recently; today/this week (unfinished) |\n| Present perfect continuous | for, since, how long; all day/week; lately |\n| Past simple | yesterday, last …, in 2019, … ago, when I was … |\n| Past continuous | while, when (background action) |\n| Past perfect | by the time, already, just, never, before |\n\n- **for** + period vs **since** + starting point.\n- **been** (visited and returned) vs **gone** (there now).\n- **while** + past continuous; **when** + past simple (shorter event).\n\n### Forms snapshot\n\n| **Tense** | **Form** | **Example** |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Present simple | base / **-s** (3rd sg) | She *works*. |\n| Present continuous | **am/is/are + V-ing** | They *are studying*. |\n| Present perfect simple | **have/has + V3** | I *have finished*. |\n| Present perfect continuous | **have/has been + V-ing** | She *has been reading*. |\n| Past simple | **V-ed** / irregular | We *went*. |\n| Past continuous | **was/were + V-ing** | I *was driving*. |\n| Past perfect | **had + V3** | They *had left*. |\n| Future (will) | **will + base** | I *will call*. |\n| Future (going to) | **am/is/are going to + base** | It’s *going to rain*. |\n| Future (arrangement) | **am/is/are + V-ing** (+ time) | *I’m flying* tomorrow. |\n| Future (timetable) | present simple | The film *starts* at 8. |\n\nKeep it simple: choose the tense by **time** (finished / unfinished / earlier past / future type) and by **focus** (result vs. duration / background vs. main event).\n\n","module":1,"audioNumber":3,"audioTitle":"Moving to a new apartment and starting over","speaker":"Elena","audio":"https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/67aa2baa0c65412632c4b3d1/6975d3adafd5a9c2c43b0346_SCRIPT%20(Female%20speaker%20%E2%80%94%20Elena)_%20Moving%20to%20a%20new%20apartment%20and%20starting%20over%20(B1).mp3","transcript":["Hi, I’m Elena. Recently, I moved to a new apartment, and it felt like a fresh start for me. I didn’t move to a different city, but even changing neighborhoods made a big difference. My old place was small and noisy. The street was busy, and at night I could hear cars and people outside. I got used to it, but I was always tired in the morning.","When I found the new apartment, it wasn’t perfect, but it had two things I really wanted: more light and more quiet. The first week was stressful. I had boxes everywhere, and I couldn’t find simple things like my phone charger or a clean towel. I also realized I had too many unnecessary items. So I started sorting my stuff. I donated some clothes, threw away broken things, and kept only what I actually use.","Now I’m slowly making the space feel like home. I bought a small desk because I sometimes work online, and I put a plant near the window. I also made a simple routine: I wake up, open the curtains, and drink coffee while I plan my day. It sounds small, but it helps me feel calm and organized.","The best part is that this move pushed me to change other habits too. I started walking more, cooking at home more often, and spending less time on my phone in the evening. I still have a lot to do—like hanging pictures and choosing a lamp—but I enjoy the process. For me, moving wasn’t only about a new apartment. It was about creating a better daily life, step by step."],"mappingNote":"Integrated present routine, current process, past move and future plans.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"Elena ___ to her new flat three months ago.","answer":"moved","options":["moved","has moved","moves"],"why":"Ago marks finished past time.","id":"g9-1"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"She cannot talk now; she ___ a lamp.","answer":"is choosing","options":["is choosing","chooses usually","has choose"],"why":"Now calls for an action in progress.","id":"g9-2"},{"type":"gap","prompt":"I ___ five boxes so far. (unpack; perfect simple)","answer":"have unpacked","why":"So far counts completed results up to now.","id":"g9-3"},{"type":"gap","prompt":"The furniture arrived while I ___ lunch. (cook; past continuous)","answer":"was cooking","why":"Use past continuous for the background.","id":"g9-4"},{"type":"order","prompt":"Build a natural sentence. Use every word.","answer":"I am going to invite my neighbours tomorrow.","why":"Going to states a future intention.","id":"g9-5"},{"type":"edit","prompt":"Correct the sentence: I will call you when I will finish.","answer":"I will call you when I finish.","why":"The future time clause uses present simple.","id":"g9-6"},{"type":"rewrite","prompt":"Rewrite with present perfect: I started owning this car five years ago and still own it. Start “I have”.","answer":"I have owned this car for five years.","why":"Own is stative and takes perfect simple.","id":"g9-7"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which form best supplies the background in a past story?","answer":"It was raining as I left the office.","options":["It was raining as I left the office.","It rains every Monday.","It will rain tomorrow."],"why":"Past continuous sets a scene around a past event.","id":"g9-8"}],"listening":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"Did Elena move to another city?","answer":"No, she changed neighbourhoods.","options":["No, she changed neighbourhoods.","Yes, she moved overseas.","The transcript does not say."],"evidence":"I didn’t move to a different city","id":"a3-1"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which two things did she want in her new home?","answer":"More light and quiet","options":["More light and quiet","A bigger television and pool","A cheaper car and garden"],"evidence":"more light and more quiet","id":"a3-2"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What did she do with some clothes?","answer":"Donated them","options":["Donated them","Sold all of them","Stored them at work"],"evidence":"I donated some clothes","id":"a3-3"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"Elena has finished every home-improvement task.","answer":"False","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"I still have a lot to do","id":"a3-4"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"She started spending less time on her phone in the evening.","answer":"True","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"spending less time on my phone in the evening","id":"a3-5"},{"type":"sequence","prompt":"Put these events or ideas in the order they appear in the recording.","events":[["She found a new apartment.","When I found the new apartment"],["She sorted unnecessary items.","I started sorting my stuff"],["She created a morning routine.","I also made a simple routine"]],"answer":["She found a new apartment.","She sorted unnecessary items.","She created a morning routine."],"evidence":"When I found the new apartment / I started sorting my stuff / I also made a simple routine","id":"a3-6"}],"vocab":[{"id":"v3-1","word":"fresh start","definition":"a chance to begin again in a better way"},{"id":"v3-2","word":"neighbourhood","definition":"an area within a town or city"},{"id":"v3-3","word":"sort","definition":"organise things into groups"},{"id":"v3-4","word":"donate","definition":"give something to help others"},{"id":"v3-5","word":"routine","definition":"a regular way of doing things"}],"writing":"Describe a change to your home and how it affected your daily life. Focus: Review tense choice by time, focus and the speaker’s purpose.","speaking":"Recommend three small changes that could make a home calmer. Focus: Review tense choice by time, focus and the speaker’s purpose."},{"n":10,"title":"First conditional & future time clauses (B1)","goal":"State real conditions, requirements and future time relationships.","theory":"\n### Quick idea\n\n- Use the **first conditional** for real/likely results in the future.\n- Use **future time clauses** with **when / as soon as / before / after / until / once / while** to talk about future time. *No* future form inside those clauses.\n\nComma rule: put a comma after the time/if clause when it comes first. No comma when it comes second.\n\n### Core form\n\n| **Pattern** | **Example** |\n| --- | --- |\n| **if + present** → **will + base** | *If it rains*, **we’ll stay** home. |\n| **if + present** → **modal / imperative** | *If you finish early*, **you can** leave. • **Call** me *if you need anything*. |\n| **when/after/etc. + present** → **will + base** | **We’ll start** *when everyone arrives*. |\n\nWe sometimes use **present perfect** in the time clause to emphasise completion: *After you’ve finished*, **we’ll go**.\n\n### Meaning & variations\n\n- **Real condition** The if-clause speaks about a possible situation in the future; the main clause is the result.\n- **Modals in the main clause** You may/might/can/should/must use a modal instead of *will*: *If he doesn’t train harder, he **might** lose.*\n- **Imperatives** are fine in results: *If you’re cold, **close** the window.*\n\n### Common partners\n\n- **unless** = *if not*: *I won’t go on holiday **unless** I save money.*\n- **as long as / provided (that)** = condition/requirement: *You can borrow it **as long as** you bring it back tomorrow.*\n- **in case** = for prevention (not conditional result): *Take an umbrella **in case** it rains.* (reason), not \\**We’ll stay in case it rains* → say *if*.\n\n### Future time clauses — keep the verb in the present\n\n#### Use these words\n\n**when, as soon as, before, after, until, once, while**\n\n- *When I’m 70*, **I’ll retire**.\n- **We’ll call** *as soon as we arrive*.\n- **I’ll wait** *until you’re ready*.\n- **She’ll text** *after she gets home* / *after she’s got home* (completion).\n\n#### What NOT to do\n\n- ✘ *If you will be* free, **call** me. → ✔ *If you are* free, **call** me.\n- ✘ **I’ll call** *when I will arrive*. → ✔ **I’ll call** *when I arrive*.\n\nYou can use the **present continuous** in the time clause for arranged future: *While you’re driving*, **don’t text**.\n\n### Mini reference\n\n| **Clause type** | **Verb form inside the clause** | **Typical result clause** |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **If** (real condition) | Present Simple / Present Perfect | *will* / modal / imperative |\n| **When / As soon as / Before / After / Until / Once / While** | Present Simple (or Present Perfect) | *will* / modal / imperative |\n| **Unless** (= if not) | Present Simple | *will* / modal / imperative |\n| **In case** (reason/prevention) | Present Simple | Often imperative or *take/bring* etc. |\n\n","module":1,"audioNumber":5,"audioTitle":"Are exams necessary?","speaker":"Ryan","audio":"https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/67aa2baa0c65412632c4b3d1/6975e60d5bf60318198f54c3_SCRIPT%201%20(Male%20speaker%20%E2%80%94%20Ryan).mp3","transcript":["Hi, I’m Ryan. I think exams are necessary, but only in a smart way. In school and university, we need some method to check progress. Without exams, it can be hard to know if students really understand the material or if they are just present in class. Exams create a clear deadline, and deadlines can help people focus. When I was a student, I didn’t always feel motivated, but before an exam I studied seriously. That pressure helped me build discipline.","At the same time, I don’t believe exams should be the only measure. Some people are good at tests, but they don’t use the knowledge well in real life. Others understand a subject deeply, but they feel anxious, forget everything, and perform badly for two hours. That doesn’t mean they are “bad students.” It means the method is limited.","So for me, exams are useful if they are balanced with other things: projects, presentations, and regular homework. Also, exams should test understanding, not memorization. If an exam only asks you to repeat facts, you will forget them quickly. But if it asks you to explain ideas, solve problems, or apply knowledge, then it becomes meaningful. In short, yes—exams are necessary, but the system needs variety and fairness."],"mappingNote":"Explicit first conditional: If an exam only asks … you will forget; future time clauses are follow-up practice.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"If you revise regularly, you ___ more confident.","answer":"will feel","options":["will feel","would felt","will feeling"],"why":"Real future result: will + base verb.","id":"g10-1"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"You can join the trip ___ you return the form tomorrow.","answer":"as long as","options":["as long as","in case of","despite"],"why":"As long as gives a requirement.","id":"g10-2"},{"type":"gap","prompt":"I won’t book a hotel unless I ___ enough money. (save)","answer":"save","why":"Unless means if not; use present simple.","id":"g10-3"},{"type":"gap","prompt":"Call me as soon as the lecture ___. (end)","answer":"ends","why":"Future time clause: present simple with -s.","id":"g10-4"},{"type":"order","prompt":"Build a natural sentence. Use every word.","answer":"Take a notebook in case your phone dies.","why":"In case gives a reason for preparation.","id":"g10-5"},{"type":"edit","prompt":"Correct the sentence: If the exam will be difficult, ask for help.","answer":"If the exam is difficult, ask for help.","why":"Use present simple in this real if-clause.","id":"g10-6"},{"type":"rewrite","prompt":"Rewrite using unless: I will join the trip only if I save enough money. Start “I won’t”.","answer":"I won’t join the trip unless I save enough money.|I will not join the trip unless I save enough money.","why":"Unless replaces if not without adding will in its clause.","id":"g10-7"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"“Take a charger in case your battery runs out.” Why take it?","answer":"To prepare before a possible problem","options":["To prepare before a possible problem","Only after the battery has already run out","Because the speaker knows it ran out yesterday"],"why":"In case gives a reason for advance preparation.","id":"g10-8"}],"listening":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is Ryan’s main view?","answer":"Exams are useful as part of a varied system.","options":["Exams are useful as part of a varied system.","All exams should be removed.","Exams should replace all projects."],"evidence":"balanced with other things","id":"a5-1"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why can exams help motivation?","answer":"They create a clear deadline.","options":["They create a clear deadline.","They guarantee a job.","They remove all pressure."],"evidence":"Exams create a clear deadline","id":"a5-2"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What should a meaningful exam test?","answer":"Understanding and application","options":["Understanding and application","Only memorised facts","Only speed"],"evidence":"test understanding, not memorization","id":"a5-3"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"A poor test performance always means a student lacks understanding.","answer":"False","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"Others understand a subject deeply","id":"a5-4"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"Ryan supports projects and presentations alongside exams.","answer":"True","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"projects, presentations, and regular homework","id":"a5-5"},{"type":"sequence","prompt":"Put these events or ideas in the order they appear in the recording.","events":[["Exams can focus students.","deadlines can help people focus"],["Test anxiety limits the method.","they feel anxious"],["A balanced system is recommended.","balanced with other things"]],"answer":["Exams can focus students.","Test anxiety limits the method.","A balanced system is recommended."],"evidence":"deadlines can help people focus / they feel anxious / balanced with other things","id":"a5-6"}],"vocab":[{"id":"v5-1","word":"progress","definition":"improvement towards a goal"},{"id":"v5-2","word":"discipline","definition":"the ability to keep working consistently"},{"id":"v5-3","word":"anxious","definition":"worried and uneasy"},{"id":"v5-4","word":"balanced","definition":"giving suitable weight to different parts"},{"id":"v5-5","word":"apply knowledge","definition":"use what you know in a situation"}],"writing":"Give your opinion on exams with one argument, one limitation and a proposal. Focus: State real conditions, requirements and future time relationships.","speaking":"Explain how you would assess learning fairly. Focus: State real conditions, requirements and future time relationships."},{"n":11,"title":"Second conditional — unreal present/future","goal":"Explore imaginary present or future choices without treating them as facts.","theory":"\n### Meaning\n\nWe use the second conditional to talk about **imaginary** or **unlikely** situations in the present or future and their results.\n\n- *If we had a country house, we would go there every weekend.*\n- *Would you travel more if you won the lottery?*\n\n### Form\n\n| **Part** | **Structure** | **Example** |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| If-clause | **if + past simple** (or **were**) | If I **had** more time… / If he **were** richer… |\n| Main clause | **would / could / might + base verb** | …I **would learn** Italian. / …we **could buy** a car. |\n| Negative | **wouldn’t + base verb** | If it **rained**, we **wouldn’t go**. |\n| Questions | **Would + subject + base verb … if …?** | *Would you move* if you **found** a better job? |\n\n⚠️ Don’t use **would** in the *if*-clause: *\\*If I would have time…* → **If I had time…**\n\n### Word order & commas\n\n- *If I won* the lottery, *I’d buy* a yacht. **comma ✓**\n- *I’d buy* a yacht *if I won* the lottery. **no comma ✓**\n\n### unless = if (not)\n\n- *I wouldn’t drive there unless I had a 4×4.*\n- = *I wouldn’t drive there if I didn’t have a 4×4.*\n\n### Would / could / might\n\n- **would** → result in general: *It would be expensive.*\n- **could** → ability/possibility: *We could save money.*\n- **might** → weaker possibility: *He might change his mind.*\n\n### Was or were?\n\n- Both are possible in spoken English: *If he was/were here, he’d help.*\n- Use **were** in the set phrase **If I were you** (giving advice):  \n  *If I were you, I’d stay at home and rest.*\n\n### Quick examples\n\n- **Plan:** If we lived nearer, we would visit more often.\n- **Ability:** If I spoke Japanese, I could work in Tokyo.\n- **Possibility:** We might buy it if it was/were cheaper.\n- **Negative:** If it snowed, the flight wouldn’t leave.\n\n","module":1,"audioNumber":9,"audioTitle":"How People Can Build a Career Change","speaker":"Ethan","audio":"https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/67aa2baa0c65412632c4b3d1/69765c2549b820f3ac47dc51_How%20People%20Can%20Build%20a%20Career%20Change.mp3","transcript":["Hi, I’m Ethan. Today I want to talk about something many adults think about: changing your career and starting a new direction.","When people hear “career change,” they imagine quitting their job and beginning again from zero. That sounds risky, especially if you have bills to pay. But in real life, most career changes happen slowly. It’s more like building a bridge from your current life to a new one.","A friend of mine worked in a hotel for years. He liked the people, but he felt tired of the same routine. He didn’t quit immediately. First, he asked himself simple questions: What do I enjoy? What am I good at? What kind of day do I want to have? He noticed he liked solving problems and helping customers, so he started learning basic IT support online. Just one hour in the evenings, a few times a week.","At the beginning, he felt confused. New words, new tools, new rules. But after a few months, he could explain simple technical problems. He also talked to people who already worked in IT. This helped him understand what the job is really like, not just what it looks like on social media.","Later, he updated his CV and applied for junior roles. He got rejected several times, but he learned from each interview. Finally, he found a company that wanted someone with strong communication skills. His hotel experience became an advantage, not a weakness.","For me, the main lesson is this: a career change is not only about learning new skills. It’s also about being patient, staying consistent, and using what you already have. If you take small steps, the future can look very different in one year."],"mappingNote":"Career-change context supports imagined alternatives; second conditional is not stated in the recording.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"If I ___ a free year, I would retrain.","answer":"had","options":["had","have","would have"],"why":"Unreal condition uses past simple.","id":"g11-1"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"If I were you, I ___ keep my current job while studying.","answer":"would","options":["would","will to","had"],"why":"Advice pattern: If I were you, I would …","id":"g11-2"},{"type":"gap","prompt":"If she spoke French, she ___ work in Paris. (ability modal)","answer":"could","why":"Could expresses hypothetical ability.","id":"g11-3"},{"type":"gap","prompt":"I wouldn’t apply unless the role ___ flexible. (be)","answer":"was|were","why":"Past form gives the unreal condition; was and were are possible.","id":"g11-4"},{"type":"order","prompt":"Build a natural sentence. Use every word.","answer":"Would you change careers if you had more savings?","why":"Question result uses would + subject + base verb.","id":"g11-5"},{"type":"edit","prompt":"Correct the sentence: If I would know the answer, I would tell you.","answer":"If I knew the answer, I would tell you.","why":"Do not put would in this if-clause.","id":"g11-6"},{"type":"rewrite","prompt":"Give advice using If I were you: You should ask for feedback.","answer":"If I were you, I would ask for feedback.|If I were you, I’d ask for feedback.","why":"Fixed advice phrase uses were and would.","id":"g11-7"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"“If I had a free month, I would travel.” Does had refer to a past month here?","answer":"No; it imagines a present or future situation.","options":["No; it imagines a present or future situation.","Yes; always a finished past month","It describes a definite booking."],"why":"The past form marks unreality, not necessarily past time.","id":"g11-8"}],"listening":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"How does Ethan describe most career changes?","answer":"Gradual","options":["Gradual","Instant","Impossible"],"evidence":"most career changes happen slowly","id":"a9-1"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What did his friend learn?","answer":"Basic IT support","options":["Basic IT support","Advanced medicine","Restaurant cooking"],"evidence":"basic IT support online","id":"a9-2"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which previous skill became an advantage?","answer":"Communication","options":["Communication","Aircraft repair","Professional painting"],"evidence":"strong communication skills","id":"a9-3"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"The friend quit his hotel job immediately.","answer":"False","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"He didn’t quit immediately","id":"a9-4"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"He was rejected several times before finding a role.","answer":"True","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"He got rejected several times","id":"a9-5"},{"type":"sequence","prompt":"Put these events or ideas in the order they appear in the recording.","events":[["He asked what he enjoyed.","What do I enjoy?"],["He studied IT support.","learning basic IT support online"],["He applied for junior roles.","applied for junior roles"]],"answer":["He asked what he enjoyed.","He studied IT support.","He applied for junior roles."],"evidence":"What do I enjoy? / learning basic IT support online / applied for junior roles","id":"a9-6"}],"vocab":[{"id":"v9-1","word":"career change","definition":"moving into a different type of work"},{"id":"v9-2","word":"consistent","definition":"continuing regularly without frequent changes"},{"id":"v9-3","word":"junior role","definition":"a job intended for someone with limited experience"},{"id":"v9-4","word":"reject","definition":"decide not to accept a person or proposal"},{"id":"v9-5","word":"advantage","definition":"something that makes success more likely"}],"writing":"Design a realistic three-step plan for a possible career change. Focus: Explore imaginary present or future choices without treating them as facts.","speaking":"Discuss how previous experience could help in a new career. Focus: Explore imaginary present or future choices without treating them as facts."},{"n":12,"title":"First vs Second Conditionals — form & use","goal":"Choose first or second conditional according to likelihood and meaning.","theory":"\n### Quick difference\n\n- **First conditional** → **real/possible** present or future situations and their results.\n- **Second conditional** → **unreal / imaginary** present or future situations and their results.\n- Choice depends on how *probable* you think the situation is.\n\n### Forms\n\n| **First conditional** | **Second conditional** |\n| --- | --- |\n| **if + present simple** → **will + base**<br>*If it **rains**, we **will stay** in.*<br>Main clause can also be **imperative** / **can/may/might/should + base**:<br>*If he **calls**, **tell** him.* / *If you **study**, you **can pass**.* | **if + past simple** (often **were**) → **would/could/might + base**<br>*If I **had** more time, I **would learn** Italian.*<br>*If she **were** taller, she **could play** basketball.* |\n\n⚠️ Don’t use **will/would** in the *if*-clause.\n\n### Word order & commas\n\n- *If I see Sara*, *I’ll tell* her. **comma ✓**\n- *I’ll tell* her *if I see* Sara. **no comma ✓**\n\n### Useful connectors (First)\n\n- **unless** = if not: *We won’t go unless it’s sunny.*\n- **as long as / provided (that)**: *You can come as long as you’re on time.*\n- **in case** (+ present): *Take an umbrella in case it rains.*\n\n### Was or were? (Second)\n\n- Both are possible in speech: *If he was/were here, he’d help.*\n- Fixed advice phrase: **If I were you** … *I’d talk to her.*\n\n### Choosing between them\n\n- **Possible:** *If I find her, I ’ll tell her.* (I think I might find her.)\n- **Unlikely:** *If I found her, I would tell her.* (I think it’s improbable.)\n- **Request/imperative:** *If you see Mark, ask him to call me.*\n- **Imaginary plan:** *If we won the lottery, we might move abroad.*\n\n","module":1,"audioNumber":10,"audioTitle":"Learning a New Skill for the Future","speaker":"Sofia","audio":"https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/67aa2baa0c65412632c4b3d1/6975f90657c0ac11f0f2fe11_SCRIPT%20(Female%20speaker%20%E2%80%94%20Sofia)_%20%E2%80%9CLearning%20a%20New%20Skill%20for%20the%20Future%E2%80%9D_.mp3","transcript":["Hi, I’m Sofia. Today I want to talk about learning a new skill, because I’m trying to change my life step by step. This year I decided to learn digital skills for the future, not just for fun. I want to feel more confident at work and have more options later.","A few months ago, I noticed something simple: many jobs are changing. People use online tools, automation, and AI more and more. At first, it sounded scary, because I thought, “This is not for me.” But then I realized that I don’t need to be a genius. I just need a clear plan and a little practice every day.","So I started small. I chose one topic: how to organize tasks and save time. I learned how to use calendars, reminders, and simple templates. Then I tried basic automation—like sending messages automatically or moving information from one place to another. It felt difficult in the beginning, but after a week I could already see results. I wasted less time, and I felt calmer.","Now I’m learning how to write better prompts for AI tools. I use them to check my English, create short texts, and explain words in a simple way. I don’t copy everything. I read the result, change it, and learn from it. It’s like having a patient tutor.","My next goal is to build a small project. I want to create a simple learning page with short listening texts and exercises. I will improve it every month. I know it won’t be perfect at first, but that’s okay. The important thing is progress.","For me, learning a new skill is not only about technology. It’s about preparing for the future, being flexible, and believing that I can grow."],"mappingNote":"Learning-future context contrasts realistic steps with imagined possibilities.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"A realistic plan: If I finish the course, I ___ for a new role.","answer":"will apply","options":["will apply","would applied","had applied"],"why":"Real future result uses will.","id":"g12-1"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"An imaginary situation: If I ___ a millionaire, I would fund free classes.","answer":"were","options":["were","am","will be"],"why":"Past were expresses an unreal present situation.","id":"g12-2"},{"type":"gap","prompt":"If you need help tomorrow, ___ me. (call; imperative)","answer":"call","why":"A real conditional can have an imperative result.","id":"g12-3"},{"type":"gap","prompt":"If I had more free time, I ___ learn coding. (would)","answer":"would","why":"Would gives an imagined result.","id":"g12-4"},{"type":"order","prompt":"Build a natural sentence. Use every word.","answer":"We might move abroad if we won a scholarship.","why":"Past won + might marks a hypothetical possibility.","id":"g12-5"},{"type":"edit","prompt":"Correct the sentence: I would study abroad if I will have money.","answer":"I would study abroad if I had money.","why":"Match the unreal condition with would in the result.","id":"g12-6"},{"type":"rewrite","prompt":"Make this imaginary using second conditional: If I have more time, I will study abroad.","answer":"If I had more time, I would study abroad.|If I had more time, I’d study abroad.","why":"Change both the condition and result, not just one.","id":"g12-7"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Compare “If I see him, I’ll ask” and “If I saw him, I’d ask”. Which sounds more possible?","answer":"The first sentence","options":["The first sentence","The second sentence","Both explicitly describe the past"],"why":"First conditional presents the situation as more realistic.","id":"g12-8"}],"listening":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why does Sofia want digital skills?","answer":"More confidence and future options","options":["More confidence and future options","To avoid all work","To become famous immediately"],"evidence":"feel more confident at work","id":"a10-1"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What was her first topic?","answer":"Organising tasks and saving time","options":["Organising tasks and saving time","Building complex robots","Making cinema films"],"evidence":"how to organize tasks and save time","id":"a10-2"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How does she handle AI results?","answer":"She reads, changes and learns from them.","options":["She reads, changes and learns from them.","She copies everything.","She ignores all feedback."],"evidence":"read the result, change it","id":"a10-3"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"She expects her first project to be perfect.","answer":"False","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"it won’t be perfect at first","id":"a10-4"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"She wants to improve the project every month.","answer":"True","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"improve it every month","id":"a10-5"},{"type":"sequence","prompt":"Put these events or ideas in the order they appear in the recording.","events":[["She chose a small practical topic.","I chose one topic"],["She tried basic automation.","I tried basic automation"],["She started learning to write prompts.","Now I’m learning how to write better prompts"]],"answer":["She chose a small practical topic.","She tried basic automation.","She started learning to write prompts."],"evidence":"I chose one topic / I tried basic automation / Now I’m learning how to write better prompts","id":"a10-6"}],"vocab":[{"id":"v10-1","word":"automation","definition":"making a process run with less manual work"},{"id":"v10-2","word":"template","definition":"a reusable pattern for creating something"},{"id":"v10-3","word":"prompt","definition":"an instruction or question given to an AI tool"},{"id":"v10-4","word":"flexible","definition":"able to adapt to changing situations"},{"id":"v10-5","word":"option","definition":"a possible choice"}],"writing":"Outline a small learning project with a purpose, steps and a future plan. Focus: Choose first or second conditional according to likelihood and meaning.","speaking":"Explain a skill you want to learn and how you will practise it. Focus: Choose first or second conditional according to likelihood and meaning."},{"n":13,"title":"Third conditional — past unreal situations","goal":"Reflect on past mistakes and distinguish past from present imagined results.","theory":"\n### Meaning\n\n- Use the third conditional to imagine a **different past** and its **imagined past result**.\n- It expresses *regret*, *criticism*, or simple *speculation* about something that didn’t happen.\n\n### Form\n\n| **If-clause (condition)** | **Main clause (result)** |\n| --- | --- |\n| **if + past perfect**<br>*If you **had studied** …* | **would / could / might + have + past participle**<br>*… you **would have passed**.* |\n| Past perfect continuous is also possible to show duration: *If she had been working harder, she would have finished earlier.* | |\n\nComma after the if-clause when it comes first: *If I had known, I would have called.* No comma when the main clause comes first.\n\n### Clear examples\n\n- **Fact:** I didn’t go to class.  \n  **Imagined result:** *If I had gone to class, I would have passed.*\n- *If he hadn’t taken his helmet, he might have died.*\n- *If the jacket had been cheaper, I could have bought it.*\n- *I wouldn’t have been late if I hadn’t overslept.*\n\n### Useful variations\n\n- **Unless** = if not: *I wouldn’t have arrived on time unless I had taken a taxi.*\n- **Inversion (formal)** — drop *if* and invert: *Had I known, I would have helped.*\n- **Modal choice** — third-conditional results commonly use *would/could/might have*. *You shouldn’t have left so late* is separate past criticism with *should have*, not by itself a third conditional.\n\n### Mixed conditional (Type 3 → 2)\n\nPast condition with a **present result** now.\n\n- *If I had studied harder, I **would have** a better job now.*\n- *If she hadn’t moved away, we **would still be** neighbours.*\n\n","module":1,"audioNumber":4,"audioTitle":"My college experience","speaker":"Ryan","audio":"https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/67aa2baa0c65412632c4b3d1/696f3792729243672263f240_SCRIPT%201%20(Male%20speaker%20%E2%80%94%20Ryan)_%20My%20college%20experience.mp3","transcript":["Hi, I’m Ryan. I started college when I was nineteen, and honestly, the first month was a shock. In high school, teachers reminded us about everything. In college, no one chased me. I had to plan my time, remember deadlines, and figure out my own routine. At the beginning I missed two small assignments because I didn’t check the online system properly. It wasn’t a disaster, but it was a good lesson.","I studied Business Administration at a big public university. The campus was like a small city: libraries, cafés, sports halls, and buildings for every department. My first-year classes were huge. In one lecture there were more than two hundred students. The professor spoke fast, the slides changed quickly, and if you didn’t take notes, you were lost. After a few weeks I learned a simple strategy: I always read a little before class, and I wrote down questions. That way, the lecture made more sense.","The best part of college for me was meeting people from different places. My roommate was from a small town, and my best friend was an international student. We had very different backgrounds, but we helped each other. We studied together before exams, shared food, and talked late at night about our plans. I also joined a student club connected to marketing. We organised small events, invited guest speakers, and worked on real projects for local businesses. That experience helped me a lot later when I applied for internships.","Of course, it wasn’t always fun. There were stressful weeks with exams and presentations. I remember one semester when I worked part-time and studied full-time. I slept too little and drank too much coffee. Now, when I look back, I understand that college taught me more than business theory. It taught me independence, communication, and how to keep going when things are hard."],"mappingNote":"Missed assignments supply a counterfactual past context; no third conditional appears in the original audio.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"Ryan missed a deadline. If he had checked the system, he ___ it.","answer":"would have seen","options":["would have seen","would see yesterday","will have see"],"why":"Imagined past result: would have + participle.","id":"g13-1"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"If I had taken the earlier train, I ___ late yesterday.","answer":"would not have been","options":["would not have been","will not be","would not be now"],"why":"Both condition and result refer to an unreal past.","id":"g13-2"},{"type":"gap","prompt":"If she ___ harder, she might have passed. (study; past perfect)","answer":"had studied","why":"The condition uses had + participle.","id":"g13-3"},{"type":"gap","prompt":"If I had trained as a designer, I ___ a different job now. (would have)","answer":"would have","why":"Here have is the base verb; now makes the result present.","id":"g13-4"},{"type":"order","prompt":"Build a natural sentence. Use every word.","answer":"I could have helped if you had called.","why":"Could have expresses a possible past result.","id":"g13-5"},{"type":"edit","prompt":"Correct the sentence: If I would have known, I would have called.","answer":"If I had known, I would have called.","why":"The third-conditional if-clause uses past perfect.","id":"g13-6"},{"type":"rewrite","prompt":"Rewrite as third conditional: I did not check the map, so I got lost. Start “If I had”.","answer":"If I had checked the map, I would not have got lost.|If I had checked the map, I would not have gotten lost.|If I had checked the map, I wouldn’t have got lost.|If I had checked the map, I wouldn’t have gotten lost.","why":"Past perfect condition + imagined past result.","id":"g13-7"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"“If I had accepted the job, I would live in Paris now.” What is the result time?","answer":"Present","options":["Present","Past","A definite future arrangement"],"why":"A past unreal condition can have a present mixed result.","id":"g13-8"}],"listening":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"How old was Ryan when he started college?","answer":"Nineteen","options":["Nineteen","Twenty-one","Seventeen"],"evidence":"when I was nineteen","id":"a4-1"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What did he study?","answer":"Business Administration","options":["Business Administration","Medicine","Engineering"],"evidence":"I studied Business Administration","id":"a4-2"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What helped lectures make more sense?","answer":"Reading beforehand and writing questions","options":["Reading beforehand and writing questions","Avoiding note-taking","Studying only on exam day"],"evidence":"read a little before class","id":"a4-3"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"Ryan missed two small assignments at first.","answer":"True","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"I missed two small assignments","id":"a4-4"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"He never combined part-time work with full-time study.","answer":"False","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"I worked part-time and studied full-time","id":"a4-5"},{"type":"sequence","prompt":"Put these events or ideas in the order they appear in the recording.","events":[["He missed assignments.","I missed two small assignments"],["He learned a lecture strategy.","I learned a simple strategy"],["He joined a marketing club.","I also joined a student club connected to marketing"]],"answer":["He missed assignments.","He learned a lecture strategy.","He joined a marketing club."],"evidence":"I missed two small assignments / I learned a simple strategy / I also joined a student club connected to marketing","id":"a4-6"}],"vocab":[{"id":"v4-1","word":"deadline","definition":"the latest time for completing a task"},{"id":"v4-2","word":"assignment","definition":"a piece of work set for a student"},{"id":"v4-3","word":"lecture","definition":"a talk given to a class of students"},{"id":"v4-4","word":"internship","definition":"a temporary job providing work experience"},{"id":"v4-5","word":"background","definition":"a person’s earlier life and experience"}],"writing":"Write advice for a new college student using a realistic example. Focus: Reflect on past mistakes and distinguish past from present imagined results.","speaking":"Describe a learning mistake and what you would do differently. Focus: Reflect on past mistakes and distinguish past from present imagined results."},{"n":14,"title":"Can, could, be able to","goal":"Express ability across tenses and distinguish general from specific past success.","theory":"\nHow we talk about ability, possibility, permission and requests — with the right tense.\n\n## Quick map\n\n**Meaning**\n\n**Common form(s)**\n\n**Example**\n\nGeneral ability (now)\n\n**can** / can’t + base verb\n\nI can swim. · I can’t drive.\n\nGeneral ability (past)\n\n**could** / couldn’t + base verb\n\nShe could read at four. · We couldn’t ski.\n\nSpecific success on one occasion (past)\n\n**was/were able to** / **managed to**\n\nThey were able to / managed to escape.\n\nOther tenses (future, perfect, infinitive, -ing)\n\n**be able to**\n\nI will be able to join. · I haven’t been able to study. · to be able to help · being able to travel\n\nPossibility (general)\n\n**can** / can’t\n\nAnyone can win. · I can’t come next week.\n\nPermission\n\n**can** / can’t · polite: **Could** I/you…?\n\nCan I sit here? · You can’t park here. · Could you sign here?\n\nSenses\n\n**can** + see/hear/feel/smell/taste\n\nI can hear music. · I can’t see him.\n\n## Forms & tense choices\n\n- **can/could** are modals → use the *base verb* (no *to*): can speak, could find (not to can / could to find).\n- **can** commonly expresses present ability and can also refer to future availability (I can come tomorrow); **could** expresses past ability, politeness or hypothetical possibility. For all *other* verb forms use **be able to**: will be able to, have been able to, to be able to, being able to.\n- **Questions/negatives**: modal + subject + base verb / modal + not + base verb: Can you help? · I can’t help. · Could she swim?\n\n## Core uses\n\n### Ability\n\n- **General ability**: He can play chess / When she was six, she could read.\n- **One specific occasion (past)**: use was/were able to or managed to. It was foggy, but we were able to land. ✅ In the negative, couldn’t also works: We couldn’t land.\n\n### Possibility\n\n- **General/real-world possibility**: It can get very hot here.\n- **Plans/uncertain future**: prefer may/might at B1 (not required here, but common): I might come later.\n\n### Permission\n\n- **can/can’t** for rules: You can’t smoke inside.\n- **Requests / asking permission**: Can I…? (neutral/common), Could I…? (more polite).\n- Other tenses use **be allowed to**: When I was a child, I wasn’t allowed to stay out late. · Will we be allowed to leave early?\n\n### Requests\n\n- Can you open the window? (friendly) · Could you possibly open the window? (politer).\n\n### Senses & stative verbs\n\n- For ordinary immediate perception, prefer **can**: Can you hear that? Continuous forms can have special meanings or contexts, so they are not universally impossible.\n\n## Contrasts & common pitfalls\n\n- **Specific past success**: We were able to fix it / We managed to fix it (not usually we could fix it for one event).\n- **Infinitive/gerund**: use be able to: I’d like to be able to play · Being able to work remotely is great.\n- **Politeness scale** (requests): Can you…? → neutral/common; Could you…? → more polite.\n- **Logical impossibility (present)** at B1 is often heard with can’t: It can’t be Tom — he’s abroad.\n\n## Mini summary\n\nUse **can/could** for general ability, possibility, permission and requests. For *other tenses* (future, perfect, infinitive, -ing), switch to **be able to**. For *one successful event in the past*, say **was/were able to** or **managed to** (negative: **couldn’t** is fine). With *senses*, prefer **can + verb**. In requests, **could** is politer than **can**.\n\n","module":2,"audioNumber":11,"audioTitle":"Where did you stay?","speaker":"Liam","audio":"https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/67aa2baa0c65412632c4b3d1/696f27572805207d9d7a5c5e_B1%20Where%20did%20you%20stay_%20(Liam).mp3","transcript":["Hi, I’m Liam. Last autumn I went to Lisbon for nine days, and I stayed in a small guesthouse in the old part of the city. It wasn’t a big hotel with a gym and a pool, but it had character. The building was old, with narrow stairs and high ceilings, and my room had wooden floors and a small balcony. I could see a bit of the river if I leaned over the railing.","I chose that place because of the location. I wanted to be able to walk everywhere, and I really could. In the mornings I walked to cafés for coffee and pastries, then I took the tram to different neighbourhoods. In the evening I didn’t need to worry about transport because I could always walk back, even if it was late.","The guesthouse was comfortable, but not perfect. The room was clean and the bed was fine, yet the sound insulation was weak. On my first night I could hear people talking in the corridor, and I woke up early because someone slammed a door. After that, I used earplugs and it was much better. Another small issue was the shower: the water pressure changed sometimes, especially when other guests were using water.","The good part was the staff. They were friendly and gave me great tips, like which viewpoint was less crowded and where to eat seafood without paying tourist prices. They also arranged a taxi to the airport on my last day, so it was stress-free. Overall, it was a great stay: simple, central, and full of atmosphere. Next time I would still choose a guesthouse, but I might pick a room that faces the inner courtyard for more quiet."],"mappingNote":"Explicit could see / wanted to be able to walk; specific-success tasks apply the travel context.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"When Liam was a child, he ___ swim well.","answer":"could","options":["could","managed","was can"],"why":"Could describes general past ability.","id":"g14-1"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Despite the delay, we ___ catch the last tram.","answer":"managed to","options":["managed to","could to","can yesterday"],"why":"Specific successful past event: managed to.","id":"g14-2"},{"type":"gap","prompt":"I haven’t ___ able to book a quiet room.","answer":"been","why":"Perfect ability form: have been able to.","id":"g14-3"},{"type":"gap","prompt":"Next month I will be able ___ travel alone.","answer":"to","why":"Be able to takes to + base verb.","id":"g14-4"},{"type":"order","prompt":"Build a natural sentence. Use every word.","answer":"Could you help me with this bag?","why":"Could makes a polite request.","id":"g14-5"},{"type":"edit","prompt":"Correct the sentence: I would like to can speak Portuguese.","answer":"I would like to be able to speak Portuguese.","why":"Can has no infinitive; use to be able to.","id":"g14-6"},{"type":"rewrite","prompt":"Rewrite using managed to: Despite the difficulty, we successfully found a room yesterday. Start “We”.","answer":"We managed to find a room yesterday.","why":"Managed to expresses success on one occasion.","id":"g14-7"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why say “I haven’t been able to call”?","answer":"Can has no perfect form.","options":["Can has no perfect form.","Could always replaces have.","Able must always refer to physical strength."],"why":"Other tenses use be able to.","id":"g14-8"}],"listening":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"Where did Liam stay?","answer":"A guesthouse in Lisbon","options":["A guesthouse in Lisbon","A large hotel in Rome","A campsite in Madrid"],"evidence":"a small guesthouse","id":"a11-1"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why did he choose it?","answer":"Its location","options":["Its location","Its swimming pool","Its gym"],"evidence":"because of the location","id":"a11-2"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What helped with noise?","answer":"Earplugs","options":["Earplugs","A new balcony","A taxi"],"evidence":"I used earplugs","id":"a11-3"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"He stayed for nine days.","answer":"True","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"for nine days","id":"a11-4"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"The water pressure was always stable.","answer":"False","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"the water pressure changed sometimes","id":"a11-5"},{"type":"sequence","prompt":"Put these events or ideas in the order they appear in the recording.","events":[["He chose a central location.","because of the location"],["He experienced noise on the first night.","On my first night"],["Staff arranged an airport taxi.","arranged a taxi to the airport"]],"answer":["He chose a central location.","He experienced noise on the first night.","Staff arranged an airport taxi."],"evidence":"because of the location / On my first night / arranged a taxi to the airport","id":"a11-6"}],"vocab":[{"id":"v11-1","word":"guesthouse","definition":"a small place providing accommodation"},{"id":"v11-2","word":"railing","definition":"a barrier along an edge for safety"},{"id":"v11-3","word":"sound insulation","definition":"material or design reducing sound transmission"},{"id":"v11-4","word":"earplugs","definition":"small objects placed in ears to reduce noise"},{"id":"v11-5","word":"courtyard","definition":"an open area surrounded by buildings"}],"writing":"Write a balanced accommodation review with benefits, problems and advice. Focus: Express ability across tenses and distinguish general from specific past success.","speaking":"Choose between two rooms and explain your preference. Focus: Express ability across tenses and distinguish general from specific past success."},{"n":15,"title":"Must / Mustn’t vs Have to / Don’t have to — form & use","goal":"Separate obligations, prohibitions and optional actions.","theory":"\n### Quick meaning\n\n- **must** speaker’s own view → **internal obligation / strong advice**.\n- **have to** rule, timetable, situation → **external obligation / necessity**.\n- **mustn’t** = **prohibition** (it’s not allowed).\n- **don’t/doesn’t have to** = **no necessity** (optional). *Not* the same as *mustn’t*.\n\n### Forms you’ll need\n\n| **Meaning** | **Present** | **Past** | **Future** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Obligation | **must** / **have/has to** | **had to** | **will have to** |\n| Negative | **mustn’t** / **don’t/doesn’t have to** | **didn’t have to** | **won’t have to** |\n| Questions | **Do/Does … have to…?** (more common than **Must…?**) | **Did … have to…?** | **Will … have to…?** |\n\n⚠️ **must** has no past or infinitive forms; use *have to* instead (e.g., **to have to**, **have had to**, **having to**).\n\n### Use must\n\n- Internal obligation / the speaker’s opinion: *You must see* the new exhibition.\n- Strong advice: *You must* drink more water.\n- Negative prohibition: **mustn’t** *You mustn’t park* here.\n\n### Use have to\n\n- External obligation (rules, schedules, situations): *We have to wear* helmets at work.\n- All tenses are possible: *I’ve had to work* weekends since May.\n- Spoken BrE alternative: **have got to** → *I’ve got to go* now.\n\n### Don’t mix these up\n\n#### mustn’t = prohibited\n\n- *You mustn’t use* your phone in exams. **✖ not allowed**\n\n#### don’t have to = not necessary\n\n- *You don’t have to wait*—I can email you later. **✔ optional**\n\nSynonyms: **can’t** / *be not allowed to* ≈ **mustn’t**.\n\n### Making negatives & questions\n\n- **Have to** uses **do/does/did**: *Do you have to* leave now? • *She doesn’t have to* work tonight. • *Did you have to* pay?\n- **Must** negative is **mustn’t**: *You mustn’t* be late.\n- Questions with **must** are possible but less common: *Must we* fill in this form?\n\n### Related verbs\n\n- **should / shouldn’t** = advice/opinion, weaker than *must/have to*.\n- **need to** = necessity. Negative: *don’t/doesn’t need to* ≈ *don’t have to*. BrE modal form: *needn’t* + base verb (e.g., *You needn’t come*).\n\n### Mini examples\n\n- **Internal obligation:** I must call my parents.\n- **External rule:** Visitors have to wear badges.\n- **Future necessity:** You will have to renew your visa.\n- **Past necessity:** We had to cancel the trip.\n- **No necessity:** You don’t have to print the form.\n- **Prohibition:** Employees mustn’t share passwords.\n\n","module":2,"audioNumber":7,"audioTitle":"Workplace etiquette and small mistakes","speaker":"Ethan","audio":"https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/67aa2baa0c65412632c4b3d1/696f77d424888b9201e07369_SCRIPT%201%20(Male%20speaker%20%E2%80%94%20Ethan)_%20First%20week%20in%20the%20office.mp3","transcript":["Hi, I’m Ethan. When I started my current job, I learned something quickly: in an office, you don’t only work—you also follow a lot of small rules. Some rules are written, and some are not, but they still matter.","On my first day, my manager said, “You must wear your badge at all times.” That was clear. But there were other things I had to discover myself. For example, we have shared meeting rooms. You must book them before you use them, and you must leave the room clean. You can’t just walk in and take it if another team has reserved it. I didn’t know this, and once I almost interrupted a meeting. It was embarrassing, but it taught me a lesson.","Another thing is communication. In my team, you should reply to messages within a reasonable time, even if it’s just “I’ll check and get back to you.” People don’t expect you to answer instantly, so you don’t have to be online every second. But you shouldn’t disappear for hours without warning, especially during work time.","There are also unwritten rules about noise. You can talk, of course, but you shouldn’t speak loudly near people who are concentrating. If you need a long call, you should use a meeting room or a quiet area. And you definitely can’t play videos without headphones. That one is a big “no.”","I also learned about timing. You must join meetings on time. If you are late, you should send a quick message. It sounds simple, but it shows respect. And if you don’t understand something, you should ask. You don’t have to pretend you know everything. In fact, it’s better to ask early than to make a bigger mistake later.","Now I feel much more comfortable at work. I still make small mistakes sometimes, but I know the basics. Office etiquette is not about being perfect. It’s about helping everyone work together in a smooth way."],"mappingNote":"Direct matches: must wear / had to discover / don’t have to be online / can’t play videos.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"Printing is optional. You ___ print the report.","answer":"don’t have to","options":["don’t have to","mustn’t","must"],"why":"Don’t have to means not necessary.","id":"g15-1"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"The company bans sharing passwords. You ___ share them.","answer":"mustn’t","options":["mustn’t","don’t have to","must to"],"why":"Mustn’t gives a prohibition.","id":"g15-2"},{"type":"gap","prompt":"Yesterday we ___ to book the meeting room.","answer":"had","why":"Past obligation uses had to.","id":"g15-3"},{"type":"gap","prompt":"Does she ___ to wear a badge?","answer":"have","why":"Does + subject + have to.","id":"g15-4"},{"type":"order","prompt":"Build a natural sentence. Use every word.","answer":"Visitors have to sign in at reception.","why":"External requirement uses have to.","id":"g15-5"},{"type":"edit","prompt":"Correct the sentence: He musts wear his badge.","answer":"He must wear his badge.","why":"Modal must has the same form for all subjects.","id":"g15-6"},{"type":"rewrite","prompt":"Rewrite using don’t have to: It is not necessary for you to stay.","answer":"You don’t have to stay.|You do not have to stay.","why":"No necessity is different from a prohibition.","id":"g15-7"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"“You mustn’t enter” versus “You don’t have to enter”: which bans entry?","answer":"You mustn’t enter.","options":["You mustn’t enter.","You don’t have to enter.","Both ban entry."],"why":"Mustn’t means forbidden; don’t have to means optional.","id":"g15-8"}],"listening":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"What must Ethan wear at all times?","answer":"His badge","options":["His badge","Headphones","A uniform jacket"],"evidence":"wear your badge at all times","id":"a7-1"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How should a shared meeting room be left?","answer":"Clean","options":["Clean","Locked by the last visitor","Full of personal belongings"],"evidence":"leave the room clean","id":"a7-2"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What should someone do if late for a meeting?","answer":"Send a quick message","options":["Send a quick message","Disappear for the day","Pretend the meeting was cancelled"],"evidence":"send a quick message","id":"a7-3"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"Employees must stay online every second.","answer":"False","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"you don’t have to be online every second","id":"a7-4"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"Videos without headphones are not acceptable.","answer":"True","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"can’t play videos without headphones","id":"a7-5"},{"type":"sequence","prompt":"Put these events or ideas in the order they appear in the recording.","events":[["Ethan learns the badge rule.","wear your badge"],["He learns room-booking rules.","must book them"],["He discusses timely communication.","Another thing is communication"]],"answer":["Ethan learns the badge rule.","He learns room-booking rules.","He discusses timely communication."],"evidence":"wear your badge / must book them / Another thing is communication","id":"a7-6"}],"vocab":[{"id":"v7-1","word":"etiquette","definition":"accepted rules of polite behaviour"},{"id":"v7-2","word":"badge","definition":"a small identification sign worn on clothing"},{"id":"v7-3","word":"reasonable","definition":"fair and sensible"},{"id":"v7-4","word":"concentrate","definition":"give close attention to a task"},{"id":"v7-5","word":"pretend","definition":"act as if something is true when it is not"}],"writing":"Create a helpful welcome message about office rules for a new colleague. Focus: Separate obligations, prohibitions and optional actions.","speaking":"Explain a workplace rule, an optional action and a piece of advice. Focus: Separate obligations, prohibitions and optional actions."},{"n":16,"title":"Had better / It’s time","goal":"Give urgent advice and describe overdue actions with appropriate tone.","theory":"\n### Quick rules\n\n- **Had better** gives *strong, urgent advice* or a *warning* about the **near future**: *You’d better call her now.*\n- **It’s (about/high) time** says that something *should happen now* (often it is *already late/overdue*): *It’s time we left.*\n- Both talk about present/near-future situations (not past events).\n\nTone: **had better** can sound strict or threatening; **it’s time** often sounds like a complaint or criticism when used with a subject + past form.\n\n### Form — *had better*\n\n| **Pattern** | **Example** |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Subject + had better + bare infinitive**<br>(short form **’d better**) | *I’d better* leave now. • *You’d better* tell Mum. |\n| **Negative**: *had better not + V* | You’d *better not* be late. • I’d *better not* forget. |\n| **Questions** (formal/rare): *Hadn’t* you better…? / *Had* we better…? | *Hadn’t you better* call them first? |\n| **Informal** (esp. AmE): drop *had* | *You better* go home. (informal speech) |\n\n- **Meaning** urgent advice + possible *bad result* if ignored: *He’d better return the money, or he’ll be in trouble.*\n- **Time** immediate present/near future: *You’d better sit down; you look pale.*\n\n**No “to”** after *better*: ✗ *I’d better to take a look* → ✓ *I’d better take a look*. Do not use **would better** or **have better**.\n\n### Form — *it’s time*\n\n| **Pattern** | **Example** |\n| --- | --- |\n| **It’s time + to-infinitive** | *It’s time to* make a decision. |\n| **It’s time for + person + to-infinitive** | *It’s time for you to* see a doctor. |\n| **It’s (about/high) time + subject + past simple**<br>past form, present meaning | *It’s (about) time you* went to the doctor. • *It’s high time we* left. |\n| **Softer/ongoing** (less common): past continuous | *It’s time we were going.* |\n\n- **Meaning** says what *should happen now or soon*; with *subject + past* it often shows **criticism/complaint**: *It’s time you paid me back.*\n- **Intensifiers** *about/high* make it stronger or more overdue.\n\n### Key contrasts & register\n\n- **had better** vs **should/ought to**: *should/ought to* = general advice; *had better* = urgent advice + implied danger/consequence. *You should try the cake.* vs *He’d better hurry or he’ll miss the train.*\n- **had better** is modal-like: no –s in 3rd person, no *to*, no continuous/perfect forms (*✗ He is having better to…*).\n- **Tone**: *had better* may sound strict or threatening; avoid with superiors/strangers unless the situation is serious.\n- **It’s time** with subject + past = stronger/critical; with *to-infinitive* = neutral instruction or plan.\n\n### Common mistakes\n\n- ✗ *I’d better to go.* → ✓ *I’d better go.*\n- ✗ *Would better* / *have better*. → ✓ *had better* only (short: *’d better*).\n- ✗ Using past meaning: *had* is a fixed form; it talks about *now/near future*, not the past.\n- Overusing questions with *had better*. Prefer *Should I…?* / *Do you think I should…?*\n\n### Mini example bank\n\n- **had better** It’s freezing — **you’d better** wear a coat. • **You’d better not** say a word about this.\n- **warning** **He’d better** give me my money back, or he’ll be in trouble.\n- **it’s time + to** **It’s time to** choose a date. • **It’s time for you to** apply.\n- **it’s (about/high) time + past** **It’s about time you** started looking for a job. • **It’s high time we** left.\n\nPronunciation/short forms: in speech we usually say **’d better** (*You’d better*). In informal speech, especially AmE, people sometimes say *You better*.\n\n","module":2,"audioNumber":7,"audioTitle":"Workplace etiquette and small mistakes","speaker":"Ethan","audio":"https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/67aa2baa0c65412632c4b3d1/696f77d424888b9201e07369_SCRIPT%201%20(Male%20speaker%20%E2%80%94%20Ethan)_%20First%20week%20in%20the%20office.mp3","transcript":["Hi, I’m Ethan. When I started my current job, I learned something quickly: in an office, you don’t only work—you also follow a lot of small rules. Some rules are written, and some are not, but they still matter.","On my first day, my manager said, “You must wear your badge at all times.” That was clear. But there were other things I had to discover myself. For example, we have shared meeting rooms. You must book them before you use them, and you must leave the room clean. You can’t just walk in and take it if another team has reserved it. I didn’t know this, and once I almost interrupted a meeting. It was embarrassing, but it taught me a lesson.","Another thing is communication. In my team, you should reply to messages within a reasonable time, even if it’s just “I’ll check and get back to you.” People don’t expect you to answer instantly, so you don’t have to be online every second. But you shouldn’t disappear for hours without warning, especially during work time.","There are also unwritten rules about noise. You can talk, of course, but you shouldn’t speak loudly near people who are concentrating. If you need a long call, you should use a meeting room or a quiet area. And you definitely can’t play videos without headphones. That one is a big “no.”","I also learned about timing. You must join meetings on time. If you are late, you should send a quick message. It sounds simple, but it shows respect. And if you don’t understand something, you should ask. You don’t have to pretend you know everything. In fact, it’s better to ask early than to make a bigger mistake later.","Now I feel much more comfortable at work. I still make small mistakes sometimes, but I know the basics. Office etiquette is not about being perfect. It’s about helping everyone work together in a smooth way."],"mappingNote":"Office-etiquette scenarios provide urgent-advice applications; had better / it’s time are not in the source.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"The client is waiting. You ___ call now or lose the booking.","answer":"had better","options":["had better","would better","have better"],"why":"Had better expresses urgent advice with a consequence.","id":"g16-1"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"It’s high time we ___ the broken printer.","answer":"replaced","options":["replaced","will replace","to replace"],"why":"Subject + past form has present overdue meaning.","id":"g16-2"},{"type":"gap","prompt":"You’d better ___ share that confidential file.","answer":"not","why":"Negative: had better not + base verb.","id":"g16-3"},{"type":"gap","prompt":"It’s time for us ___ leave.","answer":"to","why":"For + person + to-infinitive.","id":"g16-4"},{"type":"order","prompt":"Build a natural sentence. Use every word.","answer":"It is time we changed the schedule.","why":"Past changed here means an overdue present action.","id":"g16-5"},{"type":"edit","prompt":"Correct the sentence: I’d better to ask the manager.","answer":"I’d better ask the manager.","why":"Use the bare infinitive after had better.","id":"g16-6"},{"type":"rewrite","prompt":"Rewrite using It’s high time + subject + past: We should replace the old computer now; it is overdue.","answer":"It’s high time we replaced the old computer.|It is high time we replaced the old computer.","why":"Past form has a present overdue meaning.","id":"g16-7"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which advice sounds most urgent and implies a consequence?","answer":"You’d better leave or you’ll miss the flight.","options":["You’d better leave or you’ll miss the flight.","You should try this new café sometime.","I like leaving early."],"why":"Had better often implies a bad outcome if ignored.","id":"g16-8"}],"listening":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"What must Ethan wear at all times?","answer":"His badge","options":["His badge","Headphones","A uniform jacket"],"evidence":"wear your badge at all times","id":"a7-1"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How should a shared meeting room be left?","answer":"Clean","options":["Clean","Locked by the last visitor","Full of personal belongings"],"evidence":"leave the room clean","id":"a7-2"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What should someone do if late for a meeting?","answer":"Send a quick message","options":["Send a quick message","Disappear for the day","Pretend the meeting was cancelled"],"evidence":"send a quick message","id":"a7-3"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"Employees must stay online every second.","answer":"False","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"you don’t have to be online every second","id":"a7-4"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"Videos without headphones are not acceptable.","answer":"True","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"can’t play videos without headphones","id":"a7-5"},{"type":"sequence","prompt":"Put these events or ideas in the order they appear in the recording.","events":[["Ethan learns the badge rule.","wear your badge"],["He learns room-booking rules.","must book them"],["He discusses timely communication.","Another thing is communication"]],"answer":["Ethan learns the badge rule.","He learns room-booking rules.","He discusses timely communication."],"evidence":"wear your badge / must book them / Another thing is communication","id":"a7-6"}],"vocab":[{"id":"v7-1","word":"etiquette","definition":"accepted rules of polite behaviour"},{"id":"v7-2","word":"badge","definition":"a small identification sign worn on clothing"},{"id":"v7-3","word":"reasonable","definition":"fair and sensible"},{"id":"v7-4","word":"concentrate","definition":"give close attention to a task"},{"id":"v7-5","word":"pretend","definition":"act as if something is true when it is not"}],"writing":"Create a helpful welcome message about office rules for a new colleague. Focus: Give urgent advice and describe overdue actions with appropriate tone.","speaking":"Explain a workplace rule, an optional action and a piece of advice. Focus: Give urgent advice and describe overdue actions with appropriate tone."},{"n":17,"title":"Modal verbs of deduction — must / can’t / may / might / could","goal":"Make evidence-based guesses with different levels of certainty.","theory":"\n### What they mean (probability)\n\n0%100%\n\n- **can’t** = (almost) impossible / I’m sure it’s NOT true.\n- **may / might / could** = maybe true (uncertain possibility).\n- **must** = (almost) certain / I’m sure it’s true.\n\nAll these modals express a *guess* based on evidence.\n\n### Forms you’ll use\n\n| **Time** | **Structure** | **Example** |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Now / future | **modal + base verb** | He **must be** tired. • She **might know** the answer. |\n| Action in progress | **modal + be + -ing** | They **must be having** lunch now. |\n| Past event | **modal + have + V3** | He **must have left**. • They **might have missed** the bus. • It **can’t have rained**. |\n| Past in progress | **modal + have been + -ing** | She **might have been waiting** for hours. |\n\n### must (sure/almost sure)\n\n- Strong positive deduction: *The lights are off — they must be asleep.*\n- Past: *The door was locked — he must have left earlier.*\n\n### can’t (sure/almost sure NOT)\n\n- Present: *She can’t be at home; I saw her at work.*\n- Past: *He can’t/couldn’t have taken it — he was abroad.*\n\n⚠️ Don’t use **mustn’t** for deduction. *Mustn’t* = prohibition.\n\n### may / might / could (possible)\n\n- Present: *That noise might be the neighbours.*\n- Past: *She may/might/could have forgotten the time.*\n\nIn deduction, *could* ≈ *may/might* (often slightly weaker). Negative: *may/might not* (not \\*can not).\n\n### Don’t use can for a specific deduction\n\n- ❌ *He can be at home now.*\n- ✅ *He may/might/could be at home now.*\n\n**Can** is fine for general possibility: *Winters can be very cold here.*\n\n### Quick examples\n\n- **Evidence:** Keys aren’t here. → *They must be in the car.*\n- **Evidence:** It’s silent. → *They might be studying.*\n- **Past:** Phone was off. → *She could have been sleeping.*\n- **Past (not true):** Ticket unused. → *He can’t have travelled.*\n\n","module":2,"audioNumber":27,"audioTitle":"Hidden Underground Tunnel Network","speaker":"Jake","audio":"https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/67aa2baa0c65412632c4b3d1/69776d90f0ad342d528014f8_Hidden%20Underground%20Tunnel%20Network.mp3","transcript":["Hi, I’m Jake. Today I want to tell you about something unusual under many big cities: hidden underground tunnels. I don’t mean modern metro lines. I’m talking about old service tunnels that people don’t notice, and sometimes they don’t even know they exist.","In one city I visited, there was a small museum tour about these tunnels. Many of them were built a long time ago for practical reasons. Some tunnels carried water, some were used for heating pipes, and others were made for delivering goods between buildings. During cold winters, workers could move underground and avoid bad weather. In the past, it was also safer and faster than using crowded streets.","Now most of these tunnels are closed to the public. They can be dangerous because the floors are wet, the air is heavy, and parts of the tunnels may be unstable. Some places also have old cables, broken stairs, or deep holes. That’s why legal tours usually show only a small, safe section. On the tour, we walked through a short corridor with low ceilings, and we saw signs on the walls, old metal doors, and pipes that were no longer used. The guide told us that some tunnels connect important buildings, like old stations, warehouses, and basements of historic hotels.","What I found most interesting was the feeling down there. Above ground, the city was loud and full of traffic. Underground, it was quiet, cool, and a bit creepy. You could hear water drops and your own footsteps. It made me think about how cities have “two lives”: the one we see every day, and the one hidden below.","I’m not planning to explore tunnels by myself, because it’s risky and often illegal. But as a story and a piece of history, underground tunnels are fascinating. They show how people solved problems in the past—and how much is still hidden under our feet."],"mappingNote":"Tunnel context provides evidence-based guesses; modals in audio chiefly express possibility, not full deduction patterns.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"The tunnel floor is wet. There ___ be a leak nearby. (strong deduction)","answer":"must","options":["must","mustn’t","can"],"why":"Must expresses a strong positive deduction.","id":"g17-1"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"The gate is locked and the guard has the only key. A visitor ___ have opened it.","answer":"can’t","options":["can’t","mustn’t","can"],"why":"Can’t have expresses a negative past deduction.","id":"g17-2"},{"type":"gap","prompt":"The guide isn’t answering. She might ___ leading a tour.","answer":"be","why":"Action in progress: might be + -ing.","id":"g17-3"},{"type":"gap","prompt":"The lights are off. They must ___ closed the museum.","answer":"have","why":"Past deduction: must have + participle.","id":"g17-4"},{"type":"order","prompt":"Build a natural sentence. Use every word.","answer":"The noise might have come from the pipes.","why":"Might have + participle gives a possible past explanation.","id":"g17-5"},{"type":"edit","prompt":"Correct the sentence: He mustn’t be there; I saw him elsewhere.","answer":"He can’t be there; I saw him elsewhere.","why":"Mustn’t is prohibition; can’t is negative deduction.","id":"g17-6"},{"type":"rewrite","prompt":"Rewrite as a strong positive past deduction: I am almost sure she left early. Start “She must”.","answer":"She must have left early.","why":"Must have + participle gives a strong past deduction.","id":"g17-7"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"“She might not have seen the email.” What does it mean?","answer":"It is possible she did not see it.","options":["It is possible she did not see it.","She was forbidden to read it.","The speaker is certain she saw it."],"why":"Might not gives uncertain negative possibility.","id":"g17-8"}],"listening":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"What kind of tunnels does Jake describe?","answer":"Old service tunnels","options":["Old service tunnels","Modern metro lines only","New tourist shopping tunnels"],"evidence":"old service tunnels","id":"a27-1"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why do legal tours show limited sections?","answer":"Other sections can be dangerous.","options":["Other sections can be dangerous.","The tunnels are all brightly lit.","There is no history elsewhere."],"evidence":"They can be dangerous","id":"a27-2"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How did underground life feel compared with above ground?","answer":"Quiet, cool and a bit creepy","options":["Quiet, cool and a bit creepy","Hot and full of car traffic","Exactly the same"],"evidence":"quiet, cool, and a bit creepy","id":"a27-3"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"Jake plans to explore tunnels on his own.","answer":"False","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"I’m not planning to explore tunnels by myself","id":"a27-4"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"Some tunnels were used to deliver goods.","answer":"True","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"delivering goods between buildings","id":"a27-5"},{"type":"sequence","prompt":"Put these events or ideas in the order they appear in the recording.","events":[["He joins a museum tour.","a small museum tour"],["He walks through a short corridor.","walked through a short corridor"],["He reflects on two city lives.","cities have “two lives”"]],"answer":["He joins a museum tour.","He walks through a short corridor.","He reflects on two city lives."],"evidence":"a small museum tour / walked through a short corridor / cities have “two lives”","id":"a27-6"}],"vocab":[{"id":"v27-1","word":"service tunnel","definition":"an underground passage for utilities or practical work"},{"id":"v27-2","word":"unstable","definition":"not secure and liable to change or collapse"},{"id":"v27-3","word":"warehouse","definition":"a building used to store goods"},{"id":"v27-4","word":"creepy","definition":"causing an uneasy or frightened feeling"},{"id":"v27-5","word":"beneath","definition":"under or below something"}],"writing":"Describe a legal historic tour and explain its atmosphere and limitations. Focus: Make evidence-based guesses with different levels of certainty.","speaking":"Compare what visitors see above ground with what may be hidden below. Focus: Make evidence-based guesses with different levels of certainty."},{"n":18,"title":"Passive voice — forms & use","goal":"Describe processes in the passive across common tenses.","theory":"\n### What the passive is\n\n- The subject is the **receiver** of the action: *The photos are taken in Africa.*\n- Form = **be** (in the tense you need) + **past participle** (V3).\n- Agent (the doer) is optional, with **by**: *The bridge was opened by the mayor.*\n\nUse the passive to focus on the result, when the agent is unknown/obvious/unimportant, or in formal/process writing.\n\n### Passive across common tenses\n\n| **Tense** | **Active** | **Passive** |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Present simple | They make cars. | Cars are made. |\n| Present continuous | They are building a road. | A road is being built. |\n| Present perfect | They have finished the work. | The work has been finished. |\n| Past simple | They closed the shop. | The shop was closed. |\n| Past continuous | They were painting the room. | The room was being painted. |\n| Past perfect | They had repaired it. | It had been repaired. |\n| Future (will) | They will announce the results. | The results will be announced. |\n| Be going to | They are going to open a café. | A café is going to be opened. |\n| Modals | They must/can fix it. | It must/can be fixed. |\n\n### Agent: by + person/thing\n\n- Use when the agent is important/new: *The novel was written by Atwood.*\n- Omit for general/obvious agents: *Taxes are collected every year.*\n- Keep other prepositions: *It is made of wood*; *He is known for his art.*\n\n#### From active to passive\n\n- Active: *People speak English worldwide.*\n- Passive: *English is spoken worldwide.*\n\n### Verbs with two objects\n\n- Active: *They gave me a ticket.*\n- Two passives are possible:  \n  *I was given a ticket.* / *A ticket was given to me.*\n\nThe version with the person as subject (*I was given…*) is more natural.\n\n### get-passive & causative\n\n- **get + V3** (more informal; change/accident): *He got promoted.* / *My bike got stolen.*\n- **have/get + object + V3** (causative = someone does it for us):  \n  *I had my hair cut.* / *We got the boiler fixed.*\n\n### Questions, negatives & non-finite forms\n\n- Questions: invert the first auxiliary: *Is it made here?* / *Has it been made?* / *Will it be made?*\n- Negatives: *isn’t / wasn’t / hasn’t been* + V3 → *The file wasn’t saved.*\n- Infinitive passive: **to be + V3** → *needs to be cleaned*.\n- -ing passive: **being + V3** → *after being told the news…*\n\n### Mini examples\n\n- **Process:** Chocolate is made from cocoa beans.\n- **Unknown agent:** My wallet was stolen on the bus.\n- **News style:** Three people have been arrested.\n- **Modal:** The report must be submitted by Friday.\n- **Be going to:** A new park is going to be built here.\n\n","module":3,"audioNumber":29,"audioTitle":"Burgers Made From Insects","speaker":"Jordan","audio":"https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/67aa2baa0c65412632c4b3d1/697759a686e83767e9645767_Burgers%20Made%20From%20Insects_.mp3","transcript":["Hi, I’m Jordan. Today I want to talk about a food idea that sounds strange to many people: burgers made from insects. In some countries, people have eaten insects for a long time, but in Europe and the US it is still unusual. Now, some companies are making insect burgers and trying to sell them as a modern, eco-friendly choice.","So, what are these burgers made of? Usually, the insects are dried and ground into a fine powder. Then the powder is mixed with other ingredients, like spices, vegetables, or grains, to create a patty. The goal is to make something that looks and tastes similar to a normal burger, but with a different source of protein.","One reason people talk about insect burgers is the environment. Some experts say insects need less land, less water, and less food than cows. They also produce fewer greenhouse gases. Because of this, insect-based food is sometimes presented as a possible solution for the future, especially as the world population grows.","Another reason is nutrition. Insects can be high in protein, and they may contain useful vitamins and minerals. Supporters say it’s a smart way to get protein without using so many natural resources.","But there are also problems. The biggest one is psychology. Many people feel disgusted by the idea, even if the burger doesn’t look like insects. There are also questions about allergies. People who are allergic to shellfish might react to insects too, so clear labeling is important. And, of course, people want to know if it is safe, how it is produced, and what rules companies follow.","Personally, I think insect burgers are an interesting experiment. Some people will never try them, and that’s normal. But others may be curious, especially if the taste is good and the price is reasonable. Maybe in the future it will feel normal—just like sushi or tofu became normal in many places over time."],"mappingNote":"Explicit process passive: are dried / ground / mixed / presented / how it is produced.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"The powder ___ with vegetables during production.","answer":"is mixed","options":["is mixed","mixes itself always","is mix"],"why":"Present passive: is + participle.","id":"g18-1"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"The kitchen is unavailable: it ___.","answer":"is being cleaned","options":["is being cleaned","is cleaning it","has cleaning"],"why":"An ongoing passive uses is being + participle.","id":"g18-2"},{"type":"gap","prompt":"The ingredients have ___ checked.","answer":"been","why":"Present perfect passive: have been checked.","id":"g18-3"},{"type":"gap","prompt":"The packaging must ___ labelled clearly.","answer":"be","why":"Modal passive: must be + participle.","id":"g18-4"},{"type":"order","prompt":"Build a natural sentence. Use every word.","answer":"A new factory is going to be opened.","why":"Going-to passive includes be + participle.","id":"g18-5"},{"type":"edit","prompt":"Correct the sentence: The ingredients have been check by workers.","answer":"The ingredients have been checked by workers.","why":"A perfect passive needs been + past participle checked.","id":"g18-6"},{"type":"rewrite","prompt":"Rewrite in the passive: They have checked the labels.","answer":"The labels have been checked.","why":"Perfect passive needs have been + participle.","id":"g18-7"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"“The room is being painted.” What does being add?","answer":"The painting is in progress.","options":["The painting is in progress.","The painting always happens daily.","The room has painted something."],"why":"Being forms the continuous passive.","id":"g18-8"}],"listening":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"How are the insects prepared in the described process?","answer":"Dried and ground into powder","options":["Dried and ground into powder","Served alive in a bun","Only frozen whole"],"evidence":"dried and ground into a fine powder","id":"a29-1"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is one environmental argument mentioned?","answer":"Insects may need fewer resources than cows.","options":["Insects may need fewer resources than cows.","Insects need no resources at all.","All transport pollution disappears."],"evidence":"less land, less water, and less food","id":"a29-2"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why is clear labelling important?","answer":"Some people may have allergic reactions.","options":["Some people may have allergic reactions.","It makes all food cheaper.","It hides the ingredients."],"evidence":"People who are allergic to shellfish","id":"a29-3"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"Everyone is expected to try insect burgers.","answer":"False","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"Some people will never try them","id":"a29-4"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"Supporters mention protein as a benefit.","answer":"True","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"high in protein","id":"a29-5"},{"type":"sequence","prompt":"Put these events or ideas in the order they appear in the recording.","events":[["Jordan explains making the patties.","powder is mixed"],["He explains environmental arguments.","the environment"],["He describes concerns and allergies.","there are also problems"]],"answer":["Jordan explains making the patties.","He explains environmental arguments.","He describes concerns and allergies."],"evidence":"powder is mixed / the environment / there are also problems","id":"a29-6"}],"vocab":[{"id":"v29-1","word":"patty","definition":"a flat round portion used in a burger"},{"id":"v29-2","word":"protein","definition":"a nutrient important for body tissues"},{"id":"v29-3","word":"resource","definition":"something used to produce or support things"},{"id":"v29-4","word":"label","definition":"information attached to a product"},{"id":"v29-5","word":"reasonable","definition":"fair, sensible or not excessive"}],"writing":"Present a balanced view of a new food idea with benefits, concerns and uncertainty. Focus: Describe processes in the passive across common tenses.","speaking":"Explain the production process and discuss a personal preference without making health claims. Focus: Describe processes in the passive across common tenses."},{"n":19,"title":"Active vs Passive voice — what, when & why","goal":"Choose active or passive to control focus and keep agent information useful.","theory":"\n### Active sentence\n\n- **Subject = doer** of the action.\n- Pattern: **Subject + verb + object**.\n- Example: *Tom cleaned the room yesterday.*\n\n### Passive sentence\n\n- **Subject = receiver** of the action.\n- Pattern: **Object → Subject + be (tense) + past participle (+ by + agent)**.\n- Example: *The room was cleaned (by Tom) yesterday.*\n\nOnly **transitive verbs** (with an object) can form a passive. Intransitives like *arrive, happen, sleep* cannot.\n\n### When we use the passive\n\n- **Unknown agent** *A bank was robbed yesterday.*\n- **Obvious/irrelevant agent** *The robber was arrested last night.*\n- **Avoiding blame / not naming the agent** *I was told you called.*\n- **Topic focus** *Jurassic Park was filmed in 1993.*\n- **Formal / process writing** news, reports, instructions: *The meeting will be held at 10.*\n\n### Agent with by\n\n- Use **by + person/thing** if the doer matters: *The drug was discovered by Fleming.*\n- Omit the agent when it is unknown, obvious, or unimportant.\n- Keep other prepositions: *made of glass*, *known for innovation*.\n\n### How to transform active → passive\n\n| **Step** | **Tip** |\n| --- | --- |\n| 1. Move the object to subject position | *The letter* becomes the new subject. |\n| 2. Keep the same tense with **be** | present → *is/are*, past → *was/were*, perfect → *has/have been*, etc. |\n| 3. Use V3 (past participle) | *write → written*, *take → taken*, *close → closed*. |\n| 4. Add agent with **by** (optional) | only if it adds useful info. |\n| 5. Keep time/place adverbials | *yesterday, in 1993, at school* stay the same. |\n\n### Mini examples\n\n- **Active:** People speak English worldwide.  \n  **Passive:** English is spoken worldwide.\n- **Active:** The police will release a statement.  \n  **Passive:** A statement will be released.\n- **Active:** They are repairing the bridge.  \n  **Passive:** The bridge is being repaired.\n- **Active:** Someone had stolen my bike.  \n  **Passive:** My bike had been stolen.\n\nFor full passive forms in all tenses: **be** (tense) + **V3** — e.g., *is made, was built, has been discovered, is being renovated*.\n\n","module":3,"audioNumber":19,"audioTitle":"The Problem with Fast Fashion","speaker":"Jake","audio":"https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/67aa2baa0c65412632c4b3d1/69772745c25f5c43d958f016_The%20Problem%20with%20Fast%20Fashion_.mp3","transcript":["Hi, I’m Jake. Today I want to talk about fast fashion and why it has become a big problem.","Fast fashion means cheap clothes that are produced very quickly. New styles appear in shops every week, so people feel they need to buy something new all the time. At first, it sounds great: the prices are low, and you can change your look often. But there is a serious cost behind it.","One problem is waste. Many clothes are not made to last long. After a few washes, they can lose shape, get holes, or look old. People throw them away, and huge amounts of clothing end up in landfills. Some clothes are even burned, which also harms the environment.","Another problem is pollution. Making fabric uses a lot of water and energy. Some factories also use strong chemicals to dye clothes, and these chemicals can go into rivers. There is also a lot of pollution from transport, because clothes are often made in one country and sold in another.","Fast fashion can also affect workers. In some places, people work long hours for low pay. They may not have safe conditions, but the companies still want to produce faster and cheaper.","So what can we do? I think we can start with small changes. We can buy fewer items and choose better quality. We can repair clothes instead of throwing them away. We can also buy second-hand clothes, swap with friends, or donate what we don’t need. Fashion can be fun, but it is even better when it is more responsible."],"mappingNote":"Cheap clothes are produced / made / burned / sold; clear topic focus on clothes.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"Choose a passive focused on the clothes.","answer":"The clothes are produced quickly.","options":["The clothes are produced quickly.","Factories produce the clothes quickly.","The clothes produce factories quickly."],"why":"Passive makes the receiver the subject.","id":"g19-1"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which sentence is impossible as a normal passive?","answer":"The guests were arrived.","options":["The guests were arrived.","The fabric was dyed.","The coat was repaired."],"why":"Arrive has no direct object and cannot form this passive.","id":"g19-2"},{"type":"gap","prompt":"A designer created the logo. The logo was created ___ a designer.","answer":"by","why":"By introduces the agent.","id":"g19-3"},{"type":"gap","prompt":"They sent me a receipt. I ___ sent a receipt.","answer":"was","why":"The indirect object can become the passive subject.","id":"g19-4"},{"type":"order","prompt":"Build a natural sentence. Use every word.","answer":"The jacket was repaired with a special needle.","why":"With introduces an instrument, not the agent.","id":"g19-5"},{"type":"edit","prompt":"Correct the sentence: The company was sold the shirts yesterday.","answer":"The shirts were sold by the company yesterday.","why":"Make the active object the passive subject; retain the agent with by.","id":"g19-6"},{"type":"rewrite","prompt":"Rewrite in the passive and keep the agent: Maya repaired the coat.","answer":"The coat was repaired by Maya.","why":"Object becomes subject; past simple tense is retained.","id":"g19-7"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why might a report say “The documents were lost”?","answer":"The agent may be unknown or not being named.","options":["The agent may be unknown or not being named.","It must mean nobody caused the loss.","It means documents perform the action."],"why":"Passive does not necessarily identify the doer.","id":"g19-8"}],"listening":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does fast fashion involve?","answer":"Cheap clothes produced quickly","options":["Cheap clothes produced quickly","Only handmade luxury items","Clothes designed to last forever"],"evidence":"cheap clothes that are produced very quickly","id":"a19-1"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Where does much discarded clothing end up?","answer":"Landfills","options":["Landfills","Museums","Schools"],"evidence":"end up in landfills","id":"a19-2"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which action does Jake recommend?","answer":"Repairing clothes","options":["Repairing clothes","Buying something new every week","Throwing away all old clothes"],"evidence":"repair clothes","id":"a19-3"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"Clothing production uses water and energy.","answer":"True","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"a lot of water and energy","id":"a19-4"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"Workers always have safe conditions.","answer":"False","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"They may not have safe conditions","id":"a19-5"},{"type":"sequence","prompt":"Put these events or ideas in the order they appear in the recording.","events":[["Jake explains waste.","One problem is waste"],["He explains pollution.","Another problem is pollution"],["He suggests changes.","start with small changes"]],"answer":["Jake explains waste.","He explains pollution.","He suggests changes."],"evidence":"One problem is waste / Another problem is pollution / start with small changes","id":"a19-6"}],"vocab":[{"id":"v19-1","word":"landfill","definition":"a place where waste is buried"},{"id":"v19-2","word":"fabric","definition":"material used to make clothing"},{"id":"v19-3","word":"dye","definition":"colour a material using a substance"},{"id":"v19-4","word":"second-hand","definition":"previously owned by another person"},{"id":"v19-5","word":"swap","definition":"exchange one thing for another"}],"writing":"Recommend three practical changes to clothing habits and explain why. Focus: Choose active or passive to control focus and keep agent information useful.","speaking":"Discuss the trade-offs between cheap prices, quality and waste. Focus: Choose active or passive to control focus and keep agent information useful."},{"n":20,"title":"Relative Clauses — Defining vs Non-defining","goal":"Identify people and add extra information with correct relative clauses.","theory":"\n### Defining (restrictive) clause\n\n- **No commas** The clause is **essential** to identify the person/thing.\n- *The students who arrived late missed the quiz.* (Which students?)\n- Pronouns allowed: **who/that** (people), **which/that** (things), **whose** (possession).\n- **Omission** (zero pronoun) is possible when it is the **object**: *The book (which/that) I bought*.\n\n### Non-defining (non-restrictive) clause\n\n- **Between commas** Adds **extra** information; the reference is already clear.\n- *My sister, who lives in Cardiff, is a vet.*\n- Use **who/which/whose**. **Not** **that**.\n- **No omission** of the relative pronoun.\n\n### Choosing the relative word\n\n| **Refers to** | **Subject position** | **Object position** | **Possession / place / time / reason** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| People | **who** / **that** (defining) • **who** (non-defining) | **who** (informal) / **whom** (formal) / **that** (defining) | **whose** + noun: *the man whose car…* |\n| Things/animals | **which** / **that** (defining) • **which** (non-defining) | **which** / **that** (defining) | **whose** or *of which* (formal): *a film whose title…* |\n| Place / time / reason | **where** (= in/at which), **when** (= on/at which), **why** (= for which, usually after *reason*). | | |\n\n⚠️ **That** is common in defining clauses, especially after *all, everything, the only, the first, superlatives* (*the only book that…*). It is **not used** in non-defining clauses or after a preposition placed before the pronoun.\n\n### Omission & prepositions\n\n- **Omit** the pronoun only when it is the object: *The people (who/that) we met…*\n- **Keep** it for subjects and after **prepositions**.\n- Preposition at the end (neutral): *the person who I spoke to*.\n- Preposition before the pronoun (formal): *the person to whom I spoke*. (Use **whom/which**, not **that**.)\n\n### *which* referring to a whole clause\n\n- Use non-defining **which** to comment on the *previous idea*: *He missed the last train, which was unlucky.*\n- Do **not** use *that* here.\n\n### Reduced relative clauses (B1+ handy)\n\n- **Present participle** (active meaning): *Students taking the exam must switch off phones.* (= who are taking)\n- **Past participle** (passive meaning): *Items left on the bus will be donated.* (= that are left)\n- These examples are defining (no commas). Participial clauses can also add non-defining information: “The bridge, built in 1900, is still used.”\n\n### Common mistakes to avoid\n\n- **Two subjects:** ✖ *the man who he lives next door* → ✔ *the man who lives…*\n- **That with commas:** ✖ *My car, that I bought last year, …* → ✔ *My car, which I bought last year, …*\n- **Using *what* as a relative pronoun:** ✖ *the book what I liked* → ✔ *the book (that/which) I liked*. (*what* = “the thing that” with no antecedent: *I like what you did*.)\n- _**Whose**_** for people *and things*:** ✔ *a company whose products…*\n\nPunctuation: put commas **only** around non-defining clauses. Defining clauses take **no commas**.\n\n","module":3,"audioNumber":28,"audioTitle":"The Voice Behind the Metro Announcements","speaker":"Ryan","audio":"https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/67aa2baa0c65412632c4b3d1/69777117d897784581732ee9_The%20Voice%20Behind%20the%20Metro%20Announcements.mp3","transcript":["Hi, I’m Ryan. Today I want to tell you about a person most people never meet, but they hear every day: the woman behind the metro announcements.","In many cities, the voice that says things like “Mind the gap” or “Next station” belongs to a real voice actor. Let’s call her Claire. She is not famous like a movie star, but her voice is everywhere. Millions of commuters hear her on their way to work, school, or home, and most of them never ask, “Who is she?”","Claire started as a radio student. She practiced clear pronunciation and calm intonation. One day, she saw an advertisement for a recording job. The city transport company wanted a voice that sounded friendly, confident, and easy to understand. They tested many people, and Claire got the job.","Recording announcements is more difficult than it seems. Claire had to read hundreds of station names, warnings, and short messages. She repeated the same words again and again, but she needed to keep the tone consistent. Sometimes the producer stopped her and said, “A bit slower,” or “More neutral,” or “Smile with your voice.” She also learned how to pronounce names from different languages correctly, which took time and patience.","What I find interesting is how anonymous the job is. Claire can sit in a café and hear her own voice from a speaker, but nobody recognizes her. At first, that felt strange. Later, she said it felt powerful in a quiet way. Her voice helps people: tourists, older passengers, parents with children, and anyone who is tired after a long day.","So next time you hear a calm voice in public transport, remember: it might belong to someone who works hard behind the scenes—just to make your trip a little easier."],"mappingNote":"who works / voice that says / voice that sounded; non-defining relative in learning names correctly, which took time.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"Claire is the actor ___ recorded these announcements.","answer":"who","options":["who","what","where"],"why":"Who refers to a person and acts as subject.","id":"g20-1"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"My sister, ___ lives in Leeds, is a voice actor.","answer":"who","options":["who","that","zero pronoun"],"why":"Non-defining clauses require a pronoun and cannot use that.","id":"g20-2"},{"type":"gap","prompt":"That is the station ___ we changed trains.","answer":"where","why":"Where means at which for a place.","id":"g20-3"},{"type":"gap","prompt":"The passenger ___ bag was lost contacted staff.","answer":"whose","why":"Whose + noun expresses possession.","id":"g20-4"},{"type":"order","prompt":"Build a natural sentence. Use every word.","answer":"The voice that we hear belongs to Claire.","why":"That is the object of hear and could be omitted.","id":"g20-5"},{"type":"edit","prompt":"Correct the sentence: The woman who she recorded it is here.","answer":"The woman who recorded it is here.","why":"Do not repeat the relative subject with she.","id":"g20-6"},{"type":"rewrite","prompt":"Join with a non-defining relative: Claire lives in Leeds. She records announcements. Start “Claire, who”.","answer":"Claire, who lives in Leeds, records announcements.","why":"Extra information goes between commas; use who.","id":"g20-7"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which pronoun can be omitted?","answer":"that in “the book that I bought”","options":["that in “the book that I bought”","who in “the actor who recorded it”","who in “Claire, who lives here, …”"],"why":"Only an object relative in a defining clause can be omitted.","id":"g20-8"}],"listening":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"What kind of work did Claire train for initially?","answer":"Radio","options":["Radio","Medicine","Architecture"],"evidence":"a radio student","id":"a28-1"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What did the transport company want from a voice?","answer":"Friendly, confident and easy to understand","options":["Friendly, confident and easy to understand","Famous and very loud","Fast and dramatic"],"evidence":"friendly, confident, and easy to understand","id":"a28-2"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What was a recording challenge?","answer":"Keeping the tone consistent","options":["Keeping the tone consistent","Writing every train timetable","Building speakers"],"evidence":"keep the tone consistent","id":"a28-3"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"Claire is recognised by everyone who hears her voice.","answer":"False","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"nobody recognizes her","id":"a28-4"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"She had to learn names from different languages.","answer":"True","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"names from different languages correctly","id":"a28-5"},{"type":"sequence","prompt":"Put these events or ideas in the order they appear in the recording.","events":[["She sees a recording-job advertisement.","advertisement for a recording job"],["She gets the job.","Claire got the job"],["She records many station names.","hundreds of station names"]],"answer":["She sees a recording-job advertisement.","She gets the job.","She records many station names."],"evidence":"advertisement for a recording job / Claire got the job / hundreds of station names","id":"a28-6"}],"vocab":[{"id":"v28-1","word":"commuter","definition":"someone who travels regularly to work"},{"id":"v28-2","word":"intonation","definition":"the rise and fall of the voice in speech"},{"id":"v28-3","word":"neutral","definition":"not showing strong emotion or taking sides"},{"id":"v28-4","word":"anonymous","definition":"not publicly identified or recognised"},{"id":"v28-5","word":"behind the scenes","definition":"working without being seen by the public"}],"writing":"Describe an overlooked job and explain its value to other people. Focus: Identify people and add extra information with correct relative clauses.","speaking":"Role-play a producer giving polite recording instructions. Focus: Identify people and add extra information with correct relative clauses."},{"n":21,"title":"Clauses of contrast, purpose & reason","goal":"Connect contrast, reasons and intended purposes using suitable patterns.","theory":"\n### Quick map: form → what follows\n\n| **Connector** | **Followed by** | **Example** |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **although / even though / though** | clause (S + V) | *Although* it was late, we stayed. |\n| **however / nevertheless / even so** | new sentence or *;* + adverb + *,* | We were tired; *however*, we continued. |\n| **despite / in spite of** | noun / -ing • *or* *the fact that* + clause | *Despite* the rain, we played. / *In spite of the fact that* it rained… |\n| **whereas / while** (=contrast) | clause (S + V) | She likes tea, *whereas* I prefer coffee. |\n| **to** / **in order (not) to** / **so as (not) to** | *to*-infinitive | He whispered *so as not to* wake the baby. |\n| **so that** / **in order that** | clause (often with modal: can/could/will/would) | We left early *so that* we could park. |\n| **for** (+ noun / -ing) | noun phrase (purpose/function) | A brush *for painting*. |\n| **because** / **as** / **since** | clause (S + V) | We stayed inside *because* it rained. |\n| **because of** / **due to** / **owing to** / **thanks to** | noun phrase | The flight was delayed *due to* fog. |\n| **in case** (prevention) | clause | Take an umbrella *in case* it rains. (purpose of being prepared) |\n\n### Clauses of contrast\n\n- **Although / even though / though** + clause. No comma right after the connector.  \n  *Even though* we played well, we lost. / We lost, *though*.\n- **However** links sentences: “We were tired. However, we continued.” When inserted mid-clause it can take commas on both sides: “We were, however, ready.”  \n  We didn’t like the hotel; *however*, the view was amazing.\n- **Despite / in spite of** + noun / -ing. For a clause, use *the fact that*.  \n  *Despite* feeling tired, we went out. • *In spite of the fact that* it was snowing…\n- **Whereas / while** contrast two facts in one sentence.  \n  *Whereas* Tom is cautious, Anna takes risks.\n- **But / yet** are coordinators (place a comma before them in long sentences).  \n  It was late, *yet* nobody wanted to leave.\n\n❌ Don’t combine *although/even though* with *but/however* in the same contrast pair.\n\n❌ *despite of* is incorrect (use *despite* / *in spite of*).\n\n### Clauses of purpose\n\n- **to + infinitive** is the most common way: *She studied to pass.*\n- **in order (not) to / so as (not) to** are more formal; useful for negatives: *He spoke quietly so as not to disturb*.\n- **so that** + clause (often with modals): *We left early so that we would avoid traffic.*\n- **for** + noun / -ing = purpose or function of a thing/activity: *a bag for carrying tools*. (Not used before a full clause.)\n- **in case** + clause = to be prepared for a possible situation: *Take cash in case the card machine doesn’t work.*\n\nDifference: *for* + noun names the purpose/function; *to* + verb explains why someone acts now.\n\n### Clauses of reason\n\n- **because** + clause (neutral and most common). Fronted clause takes a comma: *Because it rained,* we stayed in.\n- **as / since** + clause (more formal / giving background): *Since* you’re here, let’s start.\n- **because of / due to / owing to** + noun phrase. *Due to* / *owing to* are formal; *thanks to* is usually positive: *Thanks to* your help, we finished.\n- Literary **for** (= because) links two clauses and is preceded by a comma in writing: It was late, *for* we were tired. (formal)\n\nWe normally avoid a comma before *because* unless needed to avoid ambiguity or when the clause comes first.\n\n### Purpose vs reason\n\n- **Purpose**: why someone *intends* to do something.  \n  I took a taxi *to get* there on time.\n- **Reason**: why something *happened*.  \n  I took a taxi *because* the bus was late.\n\n### Punctuation & register tips\n\n- Start-position adverbial clauses (Because/Although/Since…) are followed by a comma. Mid-position ones usually aren’t.\n- *However / therefore / nevertheless / even so* act like sentence adverbs → use **.** or **;** before, and a comma after.\n- *Though* can appear at the end of a sentence in conversation: *Nice place, though.*\n\n","module":3,"audioNumber":25,"audioTitle":"why languages die.","speaker":"Ethan","audio":"https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/67aa2baa0c65412632c4b3d1/697661dfff50ab3842cebebe_why%20languages%20die..mp3","transcript":["Hi, I’m Ethan. Today I want to talk about a sad but important topic: why languages die.","A language usually dies when people stop using it in daily life. This often happens slowly, over many years. One common reason is that a bigger, more powerful language becomes more useful. For example, if a country has one official language for school, work, and government, parents may decide to teach their children that language first. They want their kids to have better jobs and more opportunities. As a result, the small local language stays only with older people.","Another reason is migration. When families move to a new city or another country, they often change the language they speak at home. Children quickly learn the new language from school and friends, and they may feel shy about using the old language. Over time, they understand it, but they stop speaking it.","History also plays a big role. Some languages disappear because of war, colonization, or political pressure. In some places, people were punished for speaking their own language, so they stopped using it to protect themselves.","The problem is that when a language dies, we lose more than words. We lose stories, songs, jokes, and a special way of thinking. The good news is that some communities are trying to save their languages by teaching them in schools, making apps, and creating videos online. It’s not easy, but it can work if young people feel proud to speak their language."],"mappingNote":"Explicit reasons and purpose: because of war / stopped using it to protect themselves / by teaching.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"The class was cancelled ___ the storm.","answer":"because of","options":["because of","because","although of"],"why":"Because of takes a noun phrase.","id":"g21-1"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"___ few people spoke it, the language survived.","answer":"Although","options":["Although","Despite","Because of"],"why":"Although takes a complete clause.","id":"g21-2"},{"type":"gap","prompt":"They made videos ___ that young people could learn.","answer":"so","why":"So that + clause expresses purpose.","id":"g21-3"},{"type":"gap","prompt":"Despite ___ tired, she attended the class. (feel)","answer":"feeling","why":"Despite takes a noun or gerund.","id":"g21-4"},{"type":"order","prompt":"Build a natural sentence. Use every word.","answer":"We recorded the stories to preserve the language.","why":"To + verb states the purpose of an action.","id":"g21-5"},{"type":"edit","prompt":"Correct the sentence: Although it was difficult, but we continued.","answer":"Although it was difficult, we continued.","why":"Do not combine although with but in one contrast pair.","id":"g21-6"},{"type":"rewrite","prompt":"Rewrite using despite: Although it was raining, we continued.","answer":"Despite the rain, we continued.","why":"Despite takes a noun phrase, not a complete clause.","id":"g21-7"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"“She left early to find a seat.” What does to find express?","answer":"Purpose","options":["Purpose","The reason a past problem happened","A comparison"],"why":"To + infinitive explains an intended goal.","id":"g21-8"}],"listening":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"What often happens when a local language declines?","answer":"It remains mainly with older people.","options":["It remains mainly with older people.","Everyone starts writing novels in it.","It becomes every country’s official language."],"evidence":"stays only with older people","id":"a25-1"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What can migration change?","answer":"The language spoken at home","options":["The language spoken at home","The age of parents","All school subjects overnight"],"evidence":"change the language they speak at home","id":"a25-2"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How do some communities try to preserve languages?","answer":"Teaching, apps and online videos","options":["Teaching, apps and online videos","Banning young speakers","Stopping all school lessons"],"evidence":"teaching them in schools, making apps","id":"a25-3"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"Language loss affects stories and ways of thinking.","answer":"True","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"We lose stories, songs, jokes","id":"a25-4"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"Political pressure has never caused language decline.","answer":"False","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"political pressure","id":"a25-5"},{"type":"sequence","prompt":"Put these events or ideas in the order they appear in the recording.","events":[["Ethan explains powerful languages.","a bigger, more powerful language"],["He explains migration.","Another reason is migration"],["He explains preservation efforts.","trying to save their languages"]],"answer":["Ethan explains powerful languages.","He explains migration.","He explains preservation efforts."],"evidence":"a bigger, more powerful language / Another reason is migration / trying to save their languages","id":"a25-6"}],"vocab":[{"id":"v25-1","word":"migration","definition":"moving to live in another place"},{"id":"v25-2","word":"official language","definition":"a language used formally by a state"},{"id":"v25-3","word":"opportunity","definition":"a chance to do something useful"},{"id":"v25-4","word":"preserve","definition":"keep something from being lost or damaged"},{"id":"v25-5","word":"heritage","definition":"traditions and culture received from the past"}],"writing":"Propose a practical way to support a local language and explain its purpose. Focus: Connect contrast, reasons and intended purposes using suitable patterns.","speaking":"Explain what people lose when a language is no longer used. Focus: Connect contrast, reasons and intended purposes using suitable patterns."},{"n":22,"title":"Question tags — aren’t you? don’t you?","goal":"Confirm information with matching auxiliaries, pronouns and polarity.","theory":"\n### What they do\n\n- Add a short question to a statement to **confirm** information or invite agreement.\n- **Rising tone ⤴** = real question / not sure: *You’re coming, aren’t you?*\n- **Falling tone ⤵** = expecting agreement: *Nice day, isn’t it?*\n\n### Core pattern\n\n- **Same auxiliary** as in the statement + **subject pronoun**.\n- **Positive statement** → **negative tag**: *She is late, isn’t she?*\n- **Negative statement** → **positive tag**: *They don’t smoke, do they?*\n- If there’s **no auxiliary**, use **do/does/did**.\n\nComma before the tag; use pronouns (*I/you/he/she/it/we/they*).\n\n### Which auxiliary goes in the tag?\n\n| **Main verb** | **Example statement** | **Tag** |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **be** (present/past) | Her train *is* late. | *isn’t it?* • He *wasn’t* home, *was he?* |\n| **Present/Past Simple** | You *live* here. / She *left* early. | *don’t you?* / *didn’t she?* |\n| **Perfect** | They *have finished*. | *haven’t they?* |\n| **Continuous** | He *is working*. | *isn’t he?* |\n| **Future** | We *will* see. | *won’t we?* |\n| **Modals** | She *can* drive. | *can’t she?* (also *shouldn’t/should*, *mustn’t/must* etc.) |\n\n### Special cases\n\n- **I am** → tag **aren’t I?** *I’m late, aren’t I?*\n- **Let’s…** → *shall we?* *Let’s start, shall we?*\n- **Imperatives** → requests: *Open the door, will you?* (polite offers: *Have a seat, won’t you?*)\n- **There is/are** → use *there*: *There’s a problem, isn’t there?*\n- **Have got** (possession, BrE): *You’ve got a car, haven’t you?* • Simple *have* (habit): *You have breakfast, don’t you?*\n\n### Negative meaning = positive tag\n\n- After *no one, nobody, nothing, few, little, never, rarely, hardly, seldom* etc., use a **positive** tag.\n- *Nobody called, did they?* • *She hardly eats, does she?*\n\nUse **they** after *nobody/no one/everyone*: *Everyone’s ready, aren’t they?*\n\n### Pronouns & word choice\n\n- Repeat the **subject** with a pronoun: *Sam plays tennis, doesn’t he?*\n- Keep the **same tense** and polarity rule.\n- Contract negatives in tags (*isn’t, don’t, won’t*) in natural speech.\n\n### Mini reference\n\n- **Positive → Negative** You know him, **don’t** you?\n- **Negative → Positive** She hasn’t arrived, **has** she?\n- **Unsure (⤴)** You’re free tonight, aren’t you?\n- **Agreeing (⤵)** It’s cold today, isn’t it?\n\n","module":3,"audioNumber":8,"audioTitle":"How do you travel to work?","speaker":"Chris","audio":"https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/67aa2baa0c65412632c4b3d1/697252fa88db469ca8def031_SCRIPT%201%20(Male%20speaker%20%E2%80%94%20Chris)_%20How%20do%20you%20travel%20to%20work_%20(B1).mp3","transcript":["Hi, I’m Chris. I live in the suburbs, so I travel to work every day. On weekdays I usually take the train. I leave home at about 7:20, walk to the station, and catch the 7:40 train. The ride takes around 30 minutes. I like the train because it’s predictable, and I don’t have to sit in traffic. I often listen to a podcast or read the news on my phone.","Sometimes there are delays, especially in winter, and that can be stressful. On those days I try to stay calm and use the time to plan my tasks for the day. I don’t drive to work often because parking is expensive in the city center. But if I have an early meeting, I might take a taxi or ask a colleague for a lift. Overall, the train works best for me. It saves time, and it makes my mornings easier."],"mappingNote":"Commute facts are used in a follow-up dialogue; no question tags occur in the monologue.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"Chris takes the train, ___?","answer":"doesn’t he","options":["doesn’t he","isn’t he","does he"],"why":"Positive present simple takes a negative does tag.","id":"g22-1"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Nobody called, ___?","answer":"did they","options":["did they","didn’t they","did he not"],"why":"Nobody is negative in meaning, so the tag is positive; use they.","id":"g22-2"},{"type":"gap","prompt":"I’m early, ___ I?","answer":"aren’t","why":"The special positive I am tag is aren’t I.","id":"g22-3"},{"type":"gap","prompt":"Let’s leave, ___ we?","answer":"shall","why":"Let’s takes shall we.","id":"g22-4"},{"type":"order","prompt":"Build a natural sentence. Use every word.","answer":"You have finished the report, haven’t you?","why":"Use have in the tag to match present perfect; add a comma in normal writing.","id":"g22-5"},{"type":"edit","prompt":"Correct the sentence: She can’t drive, can’t she?","answer":"She can’t drive, can she?","why":"A negative statement takes a positive tag.","id":"g22-6"},{"type":"rewrite","prompt":"Add the correct tag: There is a problem.","answer":"There is a problem, isn’t there?|There is a problem, is there not?","why":"A there statement repeats there in the tag.","id":"g22-7"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"A rising tag normally suggests what?","answer":"The speaker is less certain and asks a real question.","options":["The speaker is less certain and asks a real question.","The speaker never wants an answer.","The sentence refers only to the past."],"why":"Intonation changes the communicative purpose of a tag.","id":"g22-8"}],"listening":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"Where does Chris live?","answer":"In the suburbs","options":["In the suburbs","In the city centre","Beside his office"],"evidence":"I live in the suburbs","id":"a8-1"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"When does his usual train leave?","answer":"7:40","options":["7:40","7:20","8:10"],"evidence":"catch the 7:40 train","id":"a8-2"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why does he rarely drive to work?","answer":"Parking is expensive.","options":["Parking is expensive.","He cannot drive.","His car is broken."],"evidence":"parking is expensive","id":"a8-3"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"The train journey takes around half an hour.","answer":"True","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"around 30 minutes","id":"a8-4"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"Chris never considers a taxi.","answer":"False","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"I might take a taxi","id":"a8-5"},{"type":"sequence","prompt":"Put these events or ideas in the order they appear in the recording.","events":[["He leaves home.","I leave home"],["He walks to the station.","walk to the station"],["He catches the train.","catch the 7:40 train"]],"answer":["He leaves home.","He walks to the station.","He catches the train."],"evidence":"I leave home / walk to the station / catch the 7:40 train","id":"a8-6"}],"vocab":[{"id":"v8-1","word":"suburbs","definition":"residential areas outside a city centre"},{"id":"v8-2","word":"predictable","definition":"likely to happen in an expected way"},{"id":"v8-3","word":"delay","definition":"extra waiting beyond the expected time"},{"id":"v8-4","word":"commute","definition":"a regular journey between home and work"},{"id":"v8-5","word":"a lift","definition":"a ride offered in someone’s vehicle"}],"writing":"Compare two commuting options and recommend one for a specific person. Focus: Confirm information with matching auxiliaries, pronouns and polarity.","speaking":"Describe your usual journey and a backup plan for delays. Focus: Confirm information with matching auxiliaries, pronouns and polarity."},{"n":23,"title":"A / An, the, and Zero Article","goal":"Choose articles by reference, countability, sound and geographical pattern.","theory":"\nWhen to use each article — concise B1 reference with clear examples.\n\n## Use **a / an**\n\n- **First mention** of a singular countable noun: I saw an old woman with a dog.\n- **Jobs & classifications**: Paula is a teacher. · That’s a chimpanzee.\n- **Meaning “one”**: Can I have an orange?\n- **Rates / frequency / speed** (*per*): €500 a month · 70 km an hour · three times a week.\n- **Exclamations** with *what* / *such* + singular noun: What a great idea! · Such a good friend.\n- **Form tip**: use a before a *consonant sound* and an before a *vowel sound*: a university /juː…/ · an hour /aʊ…/.\n- **Rule**: singular countable nouns cannot stand alone → need *a/an*, *the* or a determiner: I don’t have a driving licence.\n\n## Use **the**\n\n- **Known / mentioned before**: A man and a woman sat down. The man was British. · Where are the kids? In the garden.\n- **Made specific** by a phrase or clause: I sat on the chair in the corner. · The man who tried to rob me.\n- **Unique things**: the sun, the moon, the internet, the manager.\n- **Common public places** in a town (as places to go): the bank · the library · the station · the cinema · the doctor.\n- **Superlatives / ordinals**: the best, the first, the most exciting.\n- **Musical instruments**: play the guitar / the piano.\n- **Geographical names** (use *the* with):\n\n  **Category**\n\n  **Examples**\n\n  Oceans / seas / rivers / canals\n\n  the Mediterranean, the Amazon, the Suez Canal\n\n  Ranges / deserts / island groups\n\n  the Alps, the Andes, the Sahara, the Bahamas\n\n  Regions with *of*\n\n  the south of France, the Gulf of Mexico\n\n  Countries (plural / state / kingdom / republic)\n\n  the Netherlands, the Philippines, the UK, the Czech Republic\n\n## Use **no article** (zero article)\n\n- **General meaning** with plural or uncountable nouns: I love music. · Mushrooms are delicious.\n\n  Specific reference takes *the*: I loved the music at the party.\n- **Home / work / bed / school / university / hospital / prison** (as institutions in their main function): David isn’t at school today. · She is in hospital.\n\n  Use *the* for the building as a place: I went to the hospital to visit my gran.\n- **Meals**: Dinner is ready. · Let’s have breakfast.\n- **Days, months, years**: Friday is my favourite day. · He graduated in 2020.\n- **Noun + number**: room 15, page 86, platform 2.\n- **Media & transport**: on TV, by car / bus / plane.\n\n  But: Turn off the TV. · on the bus (specific bus as a place).\n- **next / last + time word** (relative to *now*): next Thursday, last week.\n\n  Different meaning with definite reference: The last week in London was fantastic.\n- **Most place names**: continents, most countries, cities, single mountains: Asia, Spain, Rome, Mount Everest.\n- **Languages & school subjects**: She speaks Spanish. · I study biology.\n\n## Quick contrasts\n\n- I’m in bed. (resting) vs I looked under the bed. (the piece of furniture)\n- go to school (to study) vs go to the school (to the building)\n- on TV vs on the radio (radio normally takes *the*)\n- a university (any one) vs the university (a specific one)\n\n","module":4,"audioNumber":13,"audioTitle":"Grand Canyon","speaker":"Mark","audio":"https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/67aa2baa0c65412632c4b3d1/6975f2ec5259031cc0e372e6_Grand%20Canyon.mp3","transcript":["Hi, I’m Mark. Today I want to talk about the Grand Canyon in the USA. It is one of the most famous natural places in the world, and it really feels unreal when you see it for the first time.","The Grand Canyon is in Arizona, and it was made by the Colorado River over a very long time. The canyon is huge. It is deep, wide, and full of different colors. The rocks can look red, orange, brown, and even purple, depending on the light. In the morning and at sunset, the view is especially beautiful because the colors change every minute.","Many visitors go to the South Rim because it is easier to reach and it has lots of viewpoints. You can walk along the rim, stop at different places, and take photos. There are also short hiking trails if you want to go a little lower and feel the size of the canyon more directly. But you need to be careful. The weather can change quickly, and the heat can be strong, especially in summer. It’s important to bring water, wear good shoes, and not take risks.","Some people also visit the Skywalk, a glass bridge where you can stand above the canyon. Others choose a helicopter tour to see the canyon from the air. If you like adventure, you can even hike down toward the river, but that needs planning and good fitness.","For me, the Grand Canyon is not just a tourist spot. It makes you feel small in a good way. It reminds you how powerful nature is, and how much time it takes to create something so amazing. If you ever travel in the USA, I think it’s a place worth seeing at least once."],"mappingNote":"Geographical names: the Grand Canyon / the Colorado River / Arizona; first versus subsequent reference.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"We crossed ___ Colorado River.","answer":"the","options":["the","a","zero article"],"why":"River names normally take the.","id":"g23-1"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"She works as ___ engineer.","answer":"an","options":["an","a","the only"],"why":"Engineer starts with a vowel sound; first mention of a job takes an.","id":"g23-2"},{"type":"gap","prompt":"We stayed in ___ Arizona. (a/the/zero)","answer":"zero","why":"Most state names take no article.","id":"g23-3"},{"type":"gap","prompt":"I bought a map. ___ map was useful.","answer":"The","why":"The second mention is known to the listener.","id":"g23-4"},{"type":"order","prompt":"Build a natural sentence. Use every word.","answer":"He studies history at university.","why":"General subject and institution used for study take no article.","id":"g23-5"},{"type":"edit","prompt":"Correct the sentence: We need an university guide.","answer":"We need a university guide.","why":"University begins with a consonant /j/ sound.","id":"g23-6"},{"type":"rewrite","prompt":"Add only the necessary articles; keep the same words: We crossed Alps and visited Rome.","answer":"We crossed the Alps and visited Rome.","why":"Mountain ranges take the; most city names do not.","id":"g23-7"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"“She is in hospital” in British English usually focuses on ___.","answer":"her role as a patient","options":["her role as a patient","the specific building she is visiting","a job at the reception desk"],"why":"Institutional zero article can describe the main function.","id":"g23-8"}],"listening":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"In which state is the Grand Canyon?","answer":"Arizona","options":["Arizona","Florida","California"],"evidence":"in Arizona","id":"a13-1"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What formed the canyon according to Mark?","answer":"The Colorado River","options":["The Colorado River","A modern building project","A glacier last year"],"evidence":"made by the Colorado River","id":"a13-2"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why do many visitors choose the South Rim?","answer":"It is easier to reach and has viewpoints.","options":["It is easier to reach and has viewpoints.","It has no hiking trails.","It is always cold."],"evidence":"easier to reach","id":"a13-3"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"The weather can change quickly.","answer":"True","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"weather can change quickly","id":"a13-4"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"Hiking down towards the river requires no preparation.","answer":"False","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"needs planning and good fitness","id":"a13-5"},{"type":"sequence","prompt":"Put these events or ideas in the order they appear in the recording.","events":[["Mark describes the river and colours.","Colorado River"],["He discusses the South Rim.","South Rim"],["He mentions the Skywalk.","the Skywalk"]],"answer":["Mark describes the river and colours.","He discusses the South Rim.","He mentions the Skywalk."],"evidence":"Colorado River / South Rim / the Skywalk","id":"a13-6"}],"vocab":[{"id":"v13-1","word":"rim","definition":"the upper edge of something"},{"id":"v13-2","word":"trail","definition":"a path used for walking outdoors"},{"id":"v13-3","word":"fitness","definition":"physical condition and ability"},{"id":"v13-4","word":"unreal","definition":"so surprising that it seems hard to believe"},{"id":"v13-5","word":"take risks","definition":"do things that may lead to harm"}],"writing":"Describe a natural attraction and sensible preparation for a visit. Focus: Choose articles by reference, countability, sound and geographical pattern.","speaking":"Explain why a place impressed you and what visitors should consider. Focus: Choose articles by reference, countability, sound and geographical pattern."},{"n":24,"title":"Another, other, others, the other, the others","goal":"Distinguish an additional item, unspecified alternatives and the rest of a set.","theory":"\nAll forms at a glance — what they mean, where they go, and typical patterns.\n\n## Quick map\n\n**Form**\n\n**Followed by**\n\n**Meaning / Use**\n\n**Example**\n\n**another** (determiner)\n\nsingular countable noun\n\n“one more / a different one”\n\nI need another cup.\n\n**another** (pronoun)\n\n—\n\n“one more thing/person”\n\nI’ll have another.\n\n**other** (determiner)\n\nplural or uncountable; also with a determiner (*my/this/any/no*) + singular\n\n“additional / different”\n\nother doctors · any other day · my other brother · other information\n\n**others** (pronoun)\n\n—\n\n“other people/things” (unspecified)\n\nSome prefer tea; others prefer coffee.\n\n**the other** (determiner/pronoun)\n\nsingular\n\n“the second of two / the opposite side”\n\nThis TV is new; the other one is old.\n\n**the other** (determiner)\n\nplural noun\n\n“the remaining” (specific group)\n\nWhere are the other kids?\n\n**the others** (pronoun)\n\n—\n\n“the rest (two or more) of a known group”\n\nJoel and Karen are here, but where are the others?\n\n## Determiners vs pronouns\n\n- **Determiners** come *before a noun*: another seat, other people, the other side.\n- **Pronouns** stand *alone*: another, others, the other, the others.\n- Don’t add a plural *-s* to determiners: other doctors ✅ not others doctors ❌.\n\n## **another** vs **other / others**\n\n- **another** = *one more / an additional one* (singular only): I’ll take another ticket.\n- **other** + plural/uncountable (or with a determiner + singular) = *different / additional*: other ideas, other advice, any other day, my other sister.\n- **others** (pronoun) = *other people/things* (not followed by a noun): We’ve hired five designers; we’ll interview others soon.\n\n## **the other** & **the others**\n\n- **the other** (singular) = “the second of two” or “the opposite one”: Hold it with the other hand.\n- **the other + plural noun** = “the remaining members of a known set”: Where are the other keys?\n- **the others** (pronoun) = “the rest of them”: Two students stayed; the others left.\n- Prefer the other + noun to clumsy the others of + noun: the other students ✅ not the others of the students ❌.\n\n## Useful patterns & notes\n\n- **another + number + plural noun**: another two days, another three questions.\n- **Comparatives**: faster than any other player (not including the subject in the group) · faster than the other players (the rest of a specific group).\n- **Pronoun choices**: another one ✅, the other one ✅, but others ones ❌.\n- **Uncountable nouns**: use other, not another: other advice / other furniture.\n- **Do not confuse** with reciprocal pronouns: each other / one another = “mutually”: They help each other.\n\n## Mini summary\n\n**another** = one more (singular). **other** = different/additional (plural/uncountable, or with a determiner + singular). **others** = other things/people (pronoun). **the other** = the second of two / the remaining one. **the others** = the rest of a known group.\n\n","module":4,"audioNumber":18,"audioTitle":"Community Gardens in the City","speaker":"Sophie","audio":"https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/67aa2baa0c65412632c4b3d1/69763a21cb59e12ed442e2e7_Community%20Gardens%20in%20the%20City_.mp3","transcript":["Hi, I’m Sophie. Today I want to talk about something I really like in big cities: community gardens.","A community garden is a small green space where people grow vegetables, herbs, and flowers together. It can be in a quiet corner of a park, behind apartment buildings, or even on a rooftop. At first, I thought it was only for people who already knew a lot about plants. But then I joined one near my home, and I learned that beginners are welcome too.","In our garden, everyone has a small job. Some people water the plants, others pull weeds, and someone checks if the soil is dry. We grow tomatoes, cucumbers, mint, basil, and sometimes strawberries. It’s not a perfect system—sometimes we forget to water, and sometimes insects eat the leaves. But we help each other, and the garden still grows.","For me, the best part is not only the food. It’s the feeling of community. I meet neighbors I never talked to before. We share seeds, we share advice, and sometimes we share simple recipes. It also changes the way I think about shopping. When you grow even a little food, you understand how much work is behind it.","Another thing I like is how it helps the city feel calmer. After a stressful day, I can spend thirty minutes in the garden, and my mind slows down. I don’t look at my phone so much, and I feel more present.","In the future, I think community gardens will become even more important. Cities are getting bigger, and people want more nature, cleaner air, and healthier habits. A small garden won’t solve every problem, but it can make everyday life better—one green corner at a time."],"mappingNote":"Explicit some … others / each other / another thing.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"I have two gloves. One is here; ___ is missing.","answer":"the other","options":["the other","another","others"],"why":"The other is the second of two known items.","id":"g24-1"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Some neighbours water plants; ___ remove weeds.","answer":"others","options":["others","other","another"],"why":"Others stands alone for other people.","id":"g24-2"},{"type":"gap","prompt":"Could I have ___ cup of tea?","answer":"another","why":"Another + singular noun means one more.","id":"g24-3"},{"type":"gap","prompt":"We need other ___, not these damaged ones. (seed; plural)","answer":"seeds","why":"Other is a determiner, so the plural ending goes on the noun.","id":"g24-4"},{"type":"order","prompt":"Build a natural sentence. Use every word.","answer":"We stayed for another two days.","why":"Another can precede number + plural noun.","id":"g24-5"},{"type":"edit","prompt":"Correct the sentence: Other advice are welcome.","answer":"Other advice is welcome.","why":"Advice is uncountable and takes a singular verb.","id":"g24-6"},{"type":"rewrite","prompt":"Replace the repeated noun with a pronoun: Some people prefer tea; other people prefer coffee.","answer":"Some people prefer tea; others prefer coffee.","why":"Others stands alone without a noun.","id":"g24-7"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which expression means the remaining members of a known group?","answer":"the others","options":["the others","another","other advice"],"why":"The others refers to the rest of a specific set.","id":"g24-8"}],"listening":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"Where did Sophie join a garden?","answer":"Near her home","options":["Near her home","Inside her office","In another country"],"evidence":"one near my home","id":"a18-1"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What surprised her about joining?","answer":"Beginners were welcome.","options":["Beginners were welcome.","Only experts could join.","No vegetables could be grown."],"evidence":"beginners are welcome too","id":"a18-2"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does she value beyond the food?","answer":"The feeling of community","options":["The feeling of community","The chance to avoid all neighbours","Winning prizes"],"evidence":"the feeling of community","id":"a18-3"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"The garden sometimes has problems with insects.","answer":"True","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"insects eat the leaves","id":"a18-4"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"Sophie believes one small garden will solve every urban problem.","answer":"False","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"won’t solve every problem","id":"a18-5"},{"type":"sequence","prompt":"Put these events or ideas in the order they appear in the recording.","events":[["She joins a garden.","I joined one"],["She describes shared jobs.","everyone has a small job"],["She explains community benefits.","feeling of community"]],"answer":["She joins a garden.","She describes shared jobs.","She explains community benefits."],"evidence":"I joined one / everyone has a small job / feeling of community","id":"a18-6"}],"vocab":[{"id":"v18-1","word":"herb","definition":"a plant used for flavour or other purposes"},{"id":"v18-2","word":"weed","definition":"an unwanted plant growing among cultivated plants"},{"id":"v18-3","word":"soil","definition":"the ground in which plants grow"},{"id":"v18-4","word":"seed","definition":"the small part of a plant from which a new plant grows"},{"id":"v18-5","word":"present","definition":"mentally attentive to what is happening now"}],"writing":"Propose a small community project with jobs, benefits and limitations. Focus: Distinguish an additional item, unspecified alternatives and the rest of a set.","speaking":"Invite a neighbour to a community project and explain how beginners can help. Focus: Distinguish an additional item, unspecified alternatives and the rest of a set."},{"n":25,"title":"Comparatives & Superlatives — Adjectives and Adverbs (B1)","goal":"Compare qualities and performance, including degree and equal comparison.","theory":"\nForm · Use · Spelling rules · Useful patterns\n\n## Core ideas\n\n- **Comparative** compares two: A is taller than B.\n- **Superlative** compares 3+: A is the tallest in the class.\n- Use **than** with comparatives; use **the** (or *my/your/Anna’s*) with superlatives.\n\n### Adjectives — form\n\n- **1 syllable**: add **-er / -est** → old → older → the oldest.\n- **Ends in -e**: add **-r / -st** → large → larger → the largest.\n- **C–V–C ending**: double final consonant → big → bigger → the biggest.\n- **Ends in -y** (consonant + y): **-ier / -iest** → happy → happier → the happiest.\n- **Two+ syllables**: use **more / most** → comfortable → more comfortable → the most comfortable.\n- *Some 2-syllable adjectives* (e.g., clever, quiet, narrow, simple) accept either pattern: cleverer / more clever.\n- **Irregular**:\n\n  **Positive**\n\n  **Comparative**\n\n  **Superlative**\n\n  good / well\n\n  better\n\n  the best\n\n  bad / badly\n\n  worse\n\n  the worst\n\n  far\n\n  farther / further\n\n  the farthest / the furthest\n\n  little (amount)\n\n  less\n\n  the least\n\n  much / many\n\n  more\n\n  the most\n\n  old\n\n  older / *elder\\**\n\n  the oldest / *eldest\\**\n\n  \\**elder/eldest* only before nouns for family members: my elder sister.\n- **Don’t double**: never use both forms together: ✗ *more better*, ✗ *the most easiest*.\n\n### Adverbs — form\n\n- Adverbs in **-ly** use **more / most**: quietly → more quietly → the most quietly.\n- Adverbs with the same form as adjectives take **-er / -est**: fast → faster → the fastest; hard → harder → the hardest; early → earlier → the earliest.\n- Irregular: well → better → the best, badly → worse → the worst.\n\n### How we use them\n\n- **Comparatives**\n  - White meat is healthier than red meat.\n  - **less + adjective + than**: This route is less direct than that one.\n  - **(not) as + adj. + as**: It isn’t as easy as it looks.\n  - **Pronouns** after *than / as*: She’s taller than me / than I am.\n  - **Modifiers**: a bit / slightly, much / far / a lot → far more interesting, a bit cheaper.\n  - **any / no + comparative**: Your plan is no better than mine. / It isn’t any cheaper.\n  - **Comparative + and + comparative**: change over time → It’s getting hotter and hotter.\n  - **The + comparative, the + comparative** (cause–result): The more you practise, the better you get.\n- **Superlatives**\n  - Use with **the** (or possessives): the best · my most expensive jacket.\n  - **Place/time phrases**: **in** + places/groups → the tallest in the class; **of** + periods/sets → the best day of my life.\n  - **one of the + superlative + plural noun**: one of the most exciting cities.\n  - **by far** to intensify: by far the easiest option.\n  - With present perfect + *ever*: the best book I’ve ever read.\n  - **the least** = opposite of *the most*: the least useful feature.\n\n### Extra notes & examples\n\n- **Actions with adverbs**: She drives fast, but I drive faster. · He plays better than me.\n- **Meaning contrasts**: the latest (= most recent) vs the last (= final one in a series).\n- **Natural style**: prefer than me in everyday speech; than I am is more formal/careful.\n\n","module":4,"audioNumber":14,"audioTitle":"The Most Beautiful Places in the World","speaker":"Jordan","audio":"https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/67aa2baa0c65412632c4b3d1/69772b70262916134925b2d9_The%20Most%20Beautiful%20Places%20in%20the%20World_.mp3","transcript":["Hi, I’m Jordan. People often ask, “What are the most beautiful places in the world?” Of course, beauty is different for everyone, but I think some places are special because they make you feel small, calm, and amazed at the same time.","For me, one of the most beautiful places is a big mountain area, like the Swiss Alps. The air feels fresh, the peaks look unreal, and the lakes are so clear that they reflect the sky like a mirror. Even if you are not a professional hiker, you can just take a cable car, walk a little, and enjoy the views.","Another type of beauty is found in tropical islands, like in the Maldives or Hawaii. The water is bright blue, the sand is soft, and the sunsets look like a movie. But what I like most is the peaceful feeling. When you hear only waves and wind, your mind finally slows down.","I also think deserts can be beautiful, which surprises some people. Places like the Sahara or the deserts in the USA have a quiet, powerful atmosphere. At night, the sky is full of stars, because there are no city lights. It feels like a different planet.","Finally, I can’t forget old cities, like Rome or Kyoto. They are beautiful in a human way. You can walk through narrow streets, see historical buildings, and feel the story of the place. It’s not only about nature, but also about culture and history.","So, in my opinion, the most beautiful places are not just “pretty.” They change your mood. They make you stop, look around, and remember the moment."],"mappingNote":"Most beautiful / one of the most beautiful; comparing place types.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"This path is ___ than the road.","answer":"narrower","options":["narrower","the narrowest","more narrower"],"why":"Use one comparative form with than.","id":"g25-1"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Kyoto is one of ___ cities I have visited.","answer":"the most interesting","options":["the most interesting","the most interestingest","most interesting a"],"why":"One of the + superlative + plural noun.","id":"g25-2"},{"type":"gap","prompt":"She explains the route ___ than I do. (clearly; comparative)","answer":"more clearly","why":"-ly adverbs use more for comparison.","id":"g25-3"},{"type":"gap","prompt":"This café isn’t as quiet ___ the library.","answer":"as","why":"Equality pattern: as + adjective + as.","id":"g25-4"},{"type":"order","prompt":"Build a natural sentence. Use every word.","answer":"The more we travel, the more we learn.","why":"The + comparative repeated links changing degrees; normally insert a comma.","id":"g25-5"},{"type":"edit","prompt":"Correct the sentence: This is the most easiest trail.","answer":"This is the easiest trail.","why":"Do not double the superlative marking.","id":"g25-6"},{"type":"rewrite","prompt":"Rewrite using not as … as: The tram is slower than the train. Start “The tram”.","answer":"The tram is not as fast as the train.|The tram isn’t as fast as the train.","why":"Use the opposite adjective fast to preserve the comparison.","id":"g25-7"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which is a correct intensified comparative?","answer":"much more carefully","options":["much more carefully","very more carefully","more better carefully"],"why":"Much can modify a comparative adverb.","id":"g25-8"}],"listening":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does Jordan value most about tropical islands?","answer":"A peaceful feeling","options":["A peaceful feeling","Large shopping malls","Fast internet"],"evidence":"the peaceful feeling","id":"a14-1"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why is the desert night sky striking?","answer":"There are no city lights.","options":["There are no city lights.","There is always snow.","Stars are closer to the ground."],"evidence":"there are no city lights","id":"a14-2"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What kind of beauty do old cities offer?","answer":"Culture and history","options":["Culture and history","Only untouched nature","Only modern technology"],"evidence":"culture and history","id":"a14-3"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"Jordan says beauty is the same for everyone.","answer":"False","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"beauty is different for everyone","id":"a14-4"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"You can enjoy the Alps without being a professional hiker.","answer":"True","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"not a professional hiker","id":"a14-5"},{"type":"sequence","prompt":"Put these events or ideas in the order they appear in the recording.","events":[["Jordan discusses mountains.","Swiss Alps"],["He discusses tropical islands.","tropical islands"],["He discusses deserts.","deserts can be beautiful"]],"answer":["Jordan discusses mountains.","He discusses tropical islands.","He discusses deserts."],"evidence":"Swiss Alps / tropical islands / deserts can be beautiful","id":"a14-6"}],"vocab":[{"id":"v14-1","word":"peak","definition":"the top of a mountain"},{"id":"v14-2","word":"reflect","definition":"show an image on a surface"},{"id":"v14-3","word":"tropical","definition":"related to warm areas near the equator"},{"id":"v14-4","word":"atmosphere","definition":"the feeling of a place"},{"id":"v14-5","word":"historical","definition":"connected with events from the past"}],"writing":"Compare two places using different kinds of beauty and personal criteria. Focus: Compare qualities and performance, including degree and equal comparison.","speaking":"Describe your favourite type of landscape and justify your choice. Focus: Compare qualities and performance, including degree and equal comparison."},{"n":26,"title":"So, such, such a, so much & so many — how to use them","goal":"Express strong degree, quantity and result without confusing purpose.","theory":"\n### Core patterns\n\n- **so + adjective/adverb** (+(*that*)+ result): *You’re so stubborn (that)…* • *He drives so slowly (that)…*\n- **such a/an + (adjective) + singular countable noun** (+(*that*)): *Such a brilliant actress (that)…*\n- **such + (adjective) + plural/uncountable noun** (+(*that*)): *such terrible weather (that)…*\n- **so much + uncountable noun** (+(*that*)): *so much food (that)…*\n- **so many + plural noun** (+(*that*)): *so many people (that)…*\n- **so little / so few + noun** for small amounts: *so little time*, *so few buses*.\n\nThe conjunction **that** is optional in result clauses and often dropped in speech.\n\n### Meaning & use\n\n- **so** / **such** intensify (= “very/really”). *It was such a bad idea.* • *That’s so kind.*\n- They can also mean “**like this**”: *I didn’t know it was so important.* • *such a mistake*.\n- **Exclamations**: *It’s so cold!* • *Such a pity!* • *Such nonsense!*\n\n### so … that (result) vs so that (purpose)\n\n- **so … that** gives a result: *She was so tired that she fell asleep.*\n- **so that + subject + can/could/will/would** gives a purpose: *Leave early so that you can catch the train.*\n\n### Common mistakes\n\n- **✗ so + adjective + noun** → **✓ such (a/an) + adjective + noun**: *He is **such a** nice person* (**not** *so nice person*).\n- **so much/many** (strong degree) vs **too much/many** (excess, negative): *so many people* / *too many people* (= more than desirable).\n- Don’t confuse **such** with **such as** (= “for example”).\n\n### Mini examples\n\n- **so + adj/adv**: *He’s so good that he gets bored.* • *John drives so slowly that people get impatient.*\n- **such a + sing.**: *It was such a party (that) we stayed till dawn.*\n- **such + plural/unc.**: *We had such terrible weather (that) we left early.*\n- **so much / so many**: *She spends so much time online.* • *There were so many fans we couldn’t move.*\n- **so little / so few**: *We had so little money.* • *There are so few buses at night.*\n\n","module":4,"audioNumber":2,"audioTitle":"Life in a Very Big Family","speaker":"Ryan","audio":"https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/67aa2baa0c65412632c4b3d1/69776353264693754b633e82_Life%20in%20a%20Very%20Big%20Family_.mp3","transcript":["Hi, I’m Ryan. Today I want to tell you about a family I know that is very big. The parents, Laura and Mike, already have nine children, and now they are expecting their tenth baby. For many people this sounds shocking, but for them it feels normal, because they have lived this way for years.","Their house is always busy. In the morning, they wake up early because the younger kids need help with breakfast, clothes, and school bags. The older children are more independent. They make simple food, pack lunches, and sometimes help their brothers and sisters get ready. In this family, everyone has small responsibilities. For example, one child feeds the dog, another sets the table, and someone else helps clean the kitchen after dinner.","Of course, raising so many kids is not easy. The parents say the hardest part is time. They don’t have much quiet time, and they need to plan everything. Shopping takes a long time, and the grocery bill is big. They also need a bigger car, and family trips are expensive. Sometimes the parents feel tired, and they admit that they can’t give the same attention to every child every day.","But there are also positive sides. The children learn to share, cooperate, and solve problems together. They are rarely bored, because they always have someone to play with. The house feels warm and full of energy. The parents say they laugh a lot, even on difficult days.","Personally, I think this lifestyle is not for everyone. But if a family is organized, supportive, and realistic about the challenges, it can work. For them, the new baby is not just “number ten.” It is another person they will love and care for—and they are already preparing the room, buying baby clothes, and planning a new routine."],"mappingNote":"So many kids / so many sounds and obligations support degree and quantity practice.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"It was ___ noisy house that I couldn’t hear the phone.","answer":"such a","options":["such a","so","such"],"why":"Such a + adjective + singular countable noun.","id":"g26-1"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"We spoke quietly so that the baby could sleep. This expresses ___.","answer":"purpose","options":["purpose","a large quantity","an unexpected past fact"],"why":"So that + could states an intended purpose.","id":"g26-2"},{"type":"gap","prompt":"There were ___ many bags that the car was full.","answer":"so","why":"So many + plural countable noun.","id":"g26-3"},{"type":"gap","prompt":"We had ___ little time that we skipped lunch.","answer":"so","why":"So little + uncountable noun.","id":"g26-4"},{"type":"order","prompt":"Build a natural sentence. Use every word.","answer":"The meal was so good that everyone asked for more.","why":"So + adjective + that introduces a result.","id":"g26-5"},{"type":"edit","prompt":"Correct the sentence: She is so patient teacher.","answer":"She is such a patient teacher.","why":"Use such a with adjective + singular noun.","id":"g26-6"},{"type":"rewrite","prompt":"Rewrite with such a: The guide was so helpful that we thanked her. Start “She was”.","answer":"She was such a helpful guide that we thanked her.","why":"Such a precedes adjective + singular countable noun.","id":"g26-7"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which sentence expresses a result rather than a purpose?","answer":"It was so noisy that we left.","options":["It was so noisy that we left.","We left so that we could sleep.","We spoke quietly to help the baby sleep."],"why":"Degree so … that expresses result.","id":"g26-8"}],"listening":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"How many children do Laura and Mike already have?","answer":"Nine","options":["Nine","Ten","Eight"],"evidence":"already have nine children","id":"a2-1"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What do they say is the hardest part?","answer":"Time","options":["Time","Finding a dog","Learning languages"],"evidence":"the hardest part is time","id":"a2-2"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is a benefit for the children?","answer":"They learn to cooperate.","options":["They learn to cooperate.","They never have responsibilities.","They never disagree."],"evidence":"share, cooperate, and solve problems together","id":"a2-3"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"The parents can give equal attention to every child every day.","answer":"False","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"they can’t give the same attention","id":"a2-4"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"The older children sometimes help younger ones get ready.","answer":"True","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"sometimes help their brothers and sisters get ready","id":"a2-5"},{"type":"sequence","prompt":"Put these events or ideas in the order they appear in the recording.","events":[["The parents wake up early.","they wake up early"],["The older children pack lunches.","pack lunches"],["The kitchen is cleaned after dinner.","clean the kitchen after dinner"]],"answer":["The parents wake up early.","The older children pack lunches.","The kitchen is cleaned after dinner."],"evidence":"they wake up early / pack lunches / clean the kitchen after dinner","id":"a2-6"}],"vocab":[{"id":"v2-1","word":"expecting","definition":"waiting for the birth of a baby"},{"id":"v2-2","word":"responsibility","definition":"a duty you are expected to carry out"},{"id":"v2-3","word":"independent","definition":"able to do things without help"},{"id":"v2-4","word":"cooperate","definition":"work together towards a shared aim"},{"id":"v2-5","word":"supportive","definition":"giving help and encouragement"}],"writing":"Describe the advantages and challenges of living in a large household. Focus: Express strong degree, quantity and result without confusing purpose.","speaking":"Compare a small and a large family, avoiding assumptions about every family. Focus: Express strong degree, quantity and result without confusing purpose."},{"n":27,"title":"-ed / -ing adjectives — meaning & use","goal":"Separate a feeling from the person or situation that causes it.","theory":"\n### Quick rule\n\n- **-ed** adjectives describe how people/animals *feel*: “I’m bored.”\n- **-ing** adjectives describe the thing or situation that *causes* the feeling: “The film is boring.”\n- Typical with linking verbs: *be, feel, seem, look, sound, get* (“He got excited.”).\n- Use *more/most* for comparison: “more interesting”, “the most tiring”.\n\n### Form & meaning (verb → -ing cause / -ed feeling)\n\n| **Verb** | **-ing (cause)** | **-ed (feeling)** | **Example** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| amaze | amazing | amazed | The view was amazing; we were amazed. |\n| annoy | annoying | annoyed | That noise is annoying. I’m annoyed. |\n| astonish | astonishing | astonished | Her progress is astonishing. The teacher was astonished. |\n| bore | boring | bored | The lecture was boring. Students felt bored. |\n| confuse | confusing | confused | The map is confusing. I’m confused. |\n| depress | depressing | depressed | Rainy winters can be depressing. He felt depressed. |\n| disappoint | disappointing | disappointed | The result was disappointing. We were disappointed. |\n| embarrass | embarrassing | embarrassed | It was an embarrassing moment; she was embarrassed. |\n| excite | exciting | excited | The match was exciting. Fans were excited. |\n| frighten / terrify | frightening / terrifying | frightened / terrified | The dog was frightening. The child was frightened. |\n| interest | interesting | interested | An interesting article; I’m interested in it. |\n| irritate | irritating | irritated | The delay was irritating. Passengers were irritated. |\n| shock | shocking | shocked | The news was shocking. We were shocked. |\n| surprise | surprising | surprised | Her visit was surprising. I was surprised. |\n| tire / exhaust | tiring / exhausting | tired / exhausted | The hike was exhausting. We were exhausted. |\n| worry | worrying | worried | The report is worrying. Parents are worried. |\n\n### Useful prepositions with *-ed* adjectives\n\n| **Adjective** | **Common preposition** | **Example** |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| interested | **in** | She’s interested in biology. |\n| bored | **with / by** | He’s bored with the game. |\n| excited | **about** | We’re excited about the trip. |\n| surprised / shocked | **at / by** | I was surprised by the price. |\n| annoyed / angry | **with / at / by** | She’s annoyed with her brother. |\n| frightened / afraid | **of / by** | He’s afraid of spiders. |\n| tired | **of / from** | Tired of waiting; tired from work. |\n| worried | **about** | They’re worried about exams. |\n\nCause can also be shown with *by*: “I was annoyed by the noise.”\n\n### Placement & intensifiers\n\n- **Before a noun**: “a boring lesson”, “a tired player”.\n- **After linking verbs**: “The lesson was boring.” “I feel tired.”\n- **Change / becoming** with *get*: “They got excited.”\n- **Intensifiers**: very/really/so (*very tired*, *really interesting*); extreme adjectives often take *absolutely / completely / utterly* (*absolutely terrified*).\n\n### Common mistakes to avoid\n\n- **People are usually -ed; things are usually -ing**: “I’m bored,” not “I’m boring” (unless you mean your personality causes boredom!).\n- Use *more/most*, not *-er/-est*: “more interesting” ✅ “interestinger” ❌\n- -ed adjectives here describe emotions (not completed actions). Compare: “a closed door” (past participle of the verb *close*) vs. “I’m interested.”\n\n","module":4,"audioNumber":12,"audioTitle":"“A Day Trip to Washington, D.C.”","speaker":"Chris","audio":"https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/67aa2baa0c65412632c4b3d1/69724d77ac5a1d06ea9e5c9c_%E2%80%9CA%20Day%20Trip%20to%20Washington%2C%20D.C.%E2%80%9D.mp3","transcript":["Hi, I’m Chris. Today I want to tell you about a day trip I took to Washington, D.C. I went there for the first time recently, and I was surprised by how much you can do in one day, even with a simple plan.","I arrived in the morning by train. The first thing I noticed was how clean and organized the city felt, especially around the main streets. I didn’t rent a car because traffic can be stressful, and the metro in D.C. is easy to use. I bought a ticket, checked the map, and within minutes I was on my way.","My main goal was to see the National Mall area. It’s a long open space with famous monuments and museums, so I started walking and just followed the crowd. I saw the Washington Monument from far away, and it looked huge in real life. After that, I walked toward the Lincoln Memorial. The steps were full of people taking photos, but the atmosphere was still respectful and quiet.","One of the best parts of the trip was visiting a museum. I chose a Smithsonian museum because many of them are free, which is amazing. I spent about an hour inside, but honestly, you could stay there all day. I liked how the exhibits were clear and interactive, not boring like some old museums.","Around lunchtime I got hungry, so I grabbed something simple—just a sandwich and coffee from a small café. Prices were a bit higher than in my city, but the service was quick, and I didn’t want to waste time sitting too long.","In the afternoon I walked more, and I also stopped near the White House. You can’t get very close, of course, but it was still interesting to see it with my own eyes. Before I left, I sat on a bench for a few minutes and watched people: tourists, office workers, families, and students. The city felt busy but not chaotic.","By the evening, I was tired from walking, but I felt happy. D.C. is a great place for a short visit because it’s full of history, culture, and landmarks. Next time, I want to explore more neighborhoods and try local food, not just the tourist spots."],"mappingNote":"Surprised by / interesting / not boring / tired from walking.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"The exhibition was ___. I wanted to learn more.","answer":"interesting","options":["interesting","interested","interest"],"why":"The exhibition causes the feeling, so use -ing.","id":"g27-1"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"After walking all day, Chris felt ___.","answer":"exhausted","options":["exhausted","exhausting","exhaust"],"why":"The person feels the emotion or condition: -ed.","id":"g27-2"},{"type":"gap","prompt":"The instructions were ___, so I asked a guide. (confuse; cause)","answer":"confusing","why":"Instructions cause confusion.","id":"g27-3"},{"type":"gap","prompt":"I’m excited ___ our day trip.","answer":"about","why":"Excited about is the common preposition pair.","id":"g27-4"},{"type":"order","prompt":"Build a natural sentence. Use every word.","answer":"The visitors were surprised by the price.","why":"People feel surprised; by introduces the cause.","id":"g27-5"},{"type":"edit","prompt":"Correct the sentence: I felt boring during the lecture.","answer":"I felt bored during the lecture.","why":"Bored describes your feeling; boring describes causing boredom.","id":"g27-6"},{"type":"rewrite","prompt":"Rewrite to describe your feeling: The film bored me. Start “I was”.","answer":"I was bored by the film.","why":"The experiencer feels bored; the film causes boredom.","id":"g27-7"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"“He is boring.” What does it normally describe?","answer":"The effect he has on other people","options":["The effect he has on other people","His own feeling of boredom","An unfinished future plan"],"why":"An -ing adjective can describe a person who causes a feeling.","id":"g27-8"}],"listening":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"How did Chris arrive in Washington, D.C.?","answer":"By train","options":["By train","By rented car","By plane"],"evidence":"by train","id":"a12-1"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What was his main sightseeing area?","answer":"The National Mall","options":["The National Mall","Only the airport","A suburban shopping centre"],"evidence":"National Mall area","id":"a12-2"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why did he choose a Smithsonian museum?","answer":"Many of them are free.","options":["Many of them are free.","It was the only open building.","It included a free hotel room."],"evidence":"many of them are free","id":"a12-3"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"He spent the whole day inside one museum.","answer":"False","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"about an hour inside","id":"a12-4"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"He saw the White House from outside.","answer":"True","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"stopped near the White House","id":"a12-5"},{"type":"sequence","prompt":"Put these events or ideas in the order they appear in the recording.","events":[["He visited the National Mall area.","National Mall area"],["He ate lunch at a café.","Around lunchtime"],["He stopped near the White House.","In the afternoon"]],"answer":["He visited the National Mall area.","He ate lunch at a café.","He stopped near the White House."],"evidence":"National Mall area / Around lunchtime / In the afternoon","id":"a12-6"}],"vocab":[{"id":"v12-1","word":"landmark","definition":"a well-known building or feature of a place"},{"id":"v12-2","word":"exhibit","definition":"an object or display shown in a museum"},{"id":"v12-3","word":"interactive","definition":"allowing visitors to take part"},{"id":"v12-4","word":"chaotic","definition":"confused and lacking order"},{"id":"v12-5","word":"viewpoint","definition":"a position or place for seeing a view"}],"writing":"Plan a one-day city visit with a route, priorities and time for a break. Focus: Separate a feeling from the person or situation that causes it.","speaking":"Retell the trip and say which stop you would choose. Focus: Separate a feeling from the person or situation that causes it."},{"n":28,"title":"Reflexive pronouns: myself, yourself, himself…","goal":"Distinguish reflexive, emphatic, solitary and reciprocal meanings.","theory":"\n### Forms\n\n| **Person** | **Singular** | **Plural** |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| 1st person | **myself** | **ourselves** |\n| 2nd person | **yourself** | **yourselves** |\n| 3rd person | **himself / herself / itself** | **themselves** |\n\nWith singular **they**, **themselves** is common; **themself** is also used. Match other generic subjects: “one … oneself”, “a person … themselves”.\n\n### When do we use reflexives?\n\n- **Same subject and object**: She cut herself chopping the onions. • We really enjoyed ourselves.\n- **After a preposition** referring back to the subject: He’s very proud of himself. • You should take care of yourself.\n- **Emphasis** (no one else did it): I’ll talk to Anna myself. • Paul himself designed the logo. (position: after the subject or at the end).\n\n### by myself / on my own\n\nMeans **alone** or **without anyone’s help**:\n\n- My son doesn’t like being by himself.\n- She raised four children on her own.\n\n### Reciprocal pronouns\n\n**each other / one another** = A does something to B and B does something to A.\n\n- The twins finally found each other.\n- You should try to understand one another.\n\nPossession: *each other’s* / *one another’s* — “We borrowed *each other’s* books.”\n\n### ⚠️ Verbs that are *not* reflexive in English\n\nLearners often add *myself/yourself…* because their language uses a reflexive form. In English we use a plain verb:\n\n- **wash / shave / dress / relax / hurry / concentrate / open**: “I need to concentrate.” (NOT *concentrate myself*) • “He shaved and dressed.”\n- After preposition: “She looked at herself in the mirror.” (Preposition *at* is necessary.)\n\n","module":4,"audioNumber":16,"audioTitle":"My Weekend Hike","speaker":"Alex","audio":"https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/67aa2baa0c65412632c4b3d1/6972475e6fffe2f71b511e75_B1%20Intermediate)%20%E2%80%94%20%E2%80%9CMy%20Weekend%20Hike%E2%80%9D%20Alex.mp3","transcript":["Hi, I’m Alex. Last weekend I went on a hike not far from my city, and it was exactly what I needed. I spend most of my week indoors, so on Saturday morning I decided to get some fresh air and move my body a little.","I didn’t plan anything complicated. The night before, I checked the weather, charged my phone, and packed a small backpack. I took a bottle of water, a sandwich, some nuts, and a light jacket. I also wore comfortable shoes because I knew the path could be muddy after rain.","I left home early, around eight, and took a bus to the edge of the city. From there, I walked for about twenty minutes until I reached the start of the trail. At first the hike was easy. The road was wide, and I could walk fast. But after a while it became narrower and steeper. I had to slow down and watch my steps. I met a few people on the way—some were jogging, some were walking dogs, and others were taking photos.","My favorite part was the view near the top. There was a quiet spot with rocks and tall trees, and I could see the city in the distance. I sat there for a while, ate my sandwich, and just listened to the wind. It felt peaceful, like my mind finally had space.","On the way down, I took a different route. It was a bit longer, but it was more interesting because it went next to a small river. I almost slipped once, but luckily I caught myself. When I got back home in the afternoon, I was tired, but in a good way. My legs hurt a little, but my mood was much better.","Now I want to do this more often. It doesn’t cost much, it reduces stress, and it helps me feel more active. Next time, I’m going to invite a friend, but I’m also fine going alone."],"mappingNote":"Explicit caught myself / going alone; pairs with relationship each other in earlier audio.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"Alex almost slipped but caught ___.","answer":"himself","options":["himself","herself","each other"],"why":"Subject and object refer to the same man.","id":"g28-1"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"We supported ___ during the climb. Each helped the other.","answer":"each other","options":["each other","ourselves alone","itself"],"why":"Reciprocal means mutual help.","id":"g28-2"},{"type":"gap","prompt":"I repaired the strap ___. Nobody helped me.","answer":"myself","why":"Reflexive for emphasis: I did it myself.","id":"g28-3"},{"type":"gap","prompt":"They are proud of ___ for finishing.","answer":"themselves","why":"After of refer back to plural they.","id":"g28-4"},{"type":"order","prompt":"Build a natural sentence. Use every word.","answer":"She enjoys hiking on her own.","why":"On her own means alone or without help.","id":"g28-5"},{"type":"edit","prompt":"Correct the sentence: I need to concentrate myself on the path.","answer":"I need to concentrate on the path.","why":"Concentrate normally does not take a reflexive pronoun.","id":"g28-6"},{"type":"rewrite","prompt":"Replace “without anyone’s help” with a reflexive expression: I carried the boxes without anyone’s help.","answer":"I carried the boxes by myself.","why":"By myself can mean without help.","id":"g28-7"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"“They looked at themselves” versus “They looked at each other”: which means mutual looking?","answer":"They looked at each other.","options":["They looked at each other.","They looked at themselves.","Both must mean looking in a mirror."],"why":"Reciprocal pronouns express action between group members.","id":"g28-8"}],"listening":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"What did Alex check the night before?","answer":"The weather","options":["The weather","A flight booking","His work emails"],"evidence":"I checked the weather","id":"a16-1"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What happened to the path as he climbed?","answer":"It became narrower and steeper.","options":["It became narrower and steeper.","It became a motorway.","It disappeared under snow."],"evidence":"narrower and steeper","id":"a16-2"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What was special about the return route?","answer":"It followed a small river.","options":["It followed a small river.","It was much shorter.","It passed through an airport."],"evidence":"next to a small river","id":"a16-3"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"Alex took a light jacket.","answer":"True","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"a light jacket","id":"a16-4"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"He definitely wants to avoid hiking alone next time.","answer":"False","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"I’m also fine going alone","id":"a16-5"},{"type":"sequence","prompt":"Put these events or ideas in the order they appear in the recording.","events":[["He packed a backpack.","packed a small backpack"],["He sat near the top.","view near the top"],["He took a different way down.","a different route"]],"answer":["He packed a backpack.","He sat near the top.","He took a different way down."],"evidence":"packed a small backpack / view near the top / a different route","id":"a16-6"}],"vocab":[{"id":"v16-1","word":"muddy","definition":"covered with wet soil"},{"id":"v16-2","word":"steep","definition":"rising or falling at a sharp angle"},{"id":"v16-3","word":"route","definition":"a way taken from one place to another"},{"id":"v16-4","word":"slip","definition":"lose your footing unintentionally"},{"id":"v16-5","word":"in the distance","definition":"far away but still visible"}],"writing":"Tell the story of an outdoor trip with preparation, an event and reflection. Focus: Distinguish reflexive, emphatic, solitary and reciprocal meanings.","speaking":"Retell Alex’s hike without inventing details, then suggest a next trip. Focus: Distinguish reflexive, emphatic, solitary and reciprocal meanings."},{"n":29,"title":"Quantifiers — much, many, a lot, (a) little/(a) few, some, any, no, none","goal":"Describe large, small and zero quantities with suitable noun agreement.","theory":"\n### Countable vs uncountable — quick guide\n\n| **Large quantity** | **Countable (plural)** | **Uncountable** |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Neutral/positive | **a lot of / lots of / plenty of** • **many** (rare in +) | **a lot of / lots of / plenty of** • **much** (rare in +) |\n| Negative/question | **many** • *How many…?* | **much** • *How much…?* |\n| **Small quantity** | **(a) few** (positive: “some, enough”) • **few** (negative: “not many”) | **(a) little** / **a bit of** (positive) • **little** (negative) |\n| **Zero** | **no** + noun • **none** (pronoun) • **not any** | |\n\n✅ **a lot / lots** can stand alone at sentence end: *We laughed a lot / had lots.* • Use **of** before a noun: *a lot of time*.\n\n### much / many\n\n- **many** + plural countable; **much** + uncountable. Common in negatives & questions: *There isn’t much coffee.* • *Were there many people?*\n- **too/so much**, **too/so many** are fine in affirmatives: *so many people*, *too much salt*.\n- **How much / How many** to ask about quantity.\n\n### a lot of / lots of / plenty of\n\n- Use with both countable and uncountable, usually in positive sentences: *We’ve got plenty of time.*\n- **a lot / lots** (no *of*) only without a following noun: *I like her a lot.*\n\n### (a) few / (a) little / a bit of\n\n- **(a) few** + plural countable; **(a) little** / **a bit of** + uncountable.\n- **a few / a little** = some/enough (positive): *We’ve got a few eggs.*\n- **few / little** = almost none/not enough (negative): *There’s little milk.*\n\nStyle tip: in formal English, use **fewer** with countables and **less** with uncountables: *fewer cars*, *less traffic*.\n\n### some / any / no\n\n- **some** in positives: *I have some questions.* | **any** in negatives & questions: *There isn’t any sugar.* • *Have you got any friends?*\n- **no** in affirmative sentences: *There are no biscuits left.*\n- Use **some** (not *any*) in offers/requests: *Would you like some tea?* • *Can I have some help?*\n- **any** can mean “it doesn’t matter which”: *Ask me any question.*\n\nAll three work with countable *(plural)* and uncountable nouns: *any students / any water*.\n\n### none / none of\n\n- **none** is a pronoun (no noun after it): *How many were left?* — *None.*\n- **none of + noun/pronoun**: *None of the questions were answered.*\n- Agreement: *none of + plural* usually takes a plural verb in modern English: *None of them are ready.*\n\n### Mini examples\n\n- *There were so many people* that we left.\n- *How much coffee* have you had today?\n- He always gets good results with *very little* effort.\n- We had *a few* problems, but we solved them.\n- “Beers?” — “No, thanks. I’ve had *plenty*.”\n\nSpoken tip: **lots of** is friendlier/more informal than **a lot of**.\n\n","module":5,"audioNumber":19,"audioTitle":"The Problem with Fast Fashion","speaker":"Jake","audio":"https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/67aa2baa0c65412632c4b3d1/69772745c25f5c43d958f016_The%20Problem%20with%20Fast%20Fashion_.mp3","transcript":["Hi, I’m Jake. Today I want to talk about fast fashion and why it has become a big problem.","Fast fashion means cheap clothes that are produced very quickly. New styles appear in shops every week, so people feel they need to buy something new all the time. At first, it sounds great: the prices are low, and you can change your look often. But there is a serious cost behind it.","One problem is waste. Many clothes are not made to last long. After a few washes, they can lose shape, get holes, or look old. People throw them away, and huge amounts of clothing end up in landfills. Some clothes are even burned, which also harms the environment.","Another problem is pollution. Making fabric uses a lot of water and energy. Some factories also use strong chemicals to dye clothes, and these chemicals can go into rivers. There is also a lot of pollution from transport, because clothes are often made in one country and sold in another.","Fast fashion can also affect workers. In some places, people work long hours for low pay. They may not have safe conditions, but the companies still want to produce faster and cheaper.","So what can we do? I think we can start with small changes. We can buy fewer items and choose better quality. We can repair clothes instead of throwing them away. We can also buy second-hand clothes, swap with friends, or donate what we don’t need. Fashion can be fun, but it is even better when it is more responsible."],"mappingNote":"Huge amounts / a lot of water / fewer items; countable versus uncountable resources.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"We must use ___ water in production.","answer":"less","options":["less","fewer","many"],"why":"Water is uncountable, so use less.","id":"g29-1"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"I have ___ good coats, so I don’t need a new one.","answer":"a few","options":["a few","a little","much"],"why":"Plural countable coats take a few.","id":"g29-2"},{"type":"gap","prompt":"There isn’t ___ fabric left. (much/many)","answer":"much","why":"Fabric is uncountable in this context.","id":"g29-3"},{"type":"gap","prompt":"How ___ shirts were donated?","answer":"many","why":"Shirts is plural countable.","id":"g29-4"},{"type":"order","prompt":"Build a natural sentence. Use every word.","answer":"None of the jackets were thrown away.","why":"None of + plural can take a plural verb.","id":"g29-5"},{"type":"edit","prompt":"Correct the sentence: We don’t have no spare buttons.","answer":"We don’t have any spare buttons.","why":"Avoid double negatives in standard English.","id":"g29-6"},{"type":"rewrite","prompt":"Rewrite with fewer: We should reduce the number of plastic bags. Start “We should use”.","answer":"We should use fewer plastic bags.","why":"Use fewer with plural countable bags.","id":"g29-7"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"“There is little time” compared with “There is a little time”: which sounds insufficient?","answer":"There is little time.","options":["There is little time.","There is a little time.","Both always mean no time."],"why":"Without a, little often carries a negative not-enough meaning.","id":"g29-8"}],"listening":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does fast fashion involve?","answer":"Cheap clothes produced quickly","options":["Cheap clothes produced quickly","Only handmade luxury items","Clothes designed to last forever"],"evidence":"cheap clothes that are produced very quickly","id":"a19-1"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Where does much discarded clothing end up?","answer":"Landfills","options":["Landfills","Museums","Schools"],"evidence":"end up in landfills","id":"a19-2"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which action does Jake recommend?","answer":"Repairing clothes","options":["Repairing clothes","Buying something new every week","Throwing away all old clothes"],"evidence":"repair clothes","id":"a19-3"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"Clothing production uses water and energy.","answer":"True","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"a lot of water and energy","id":"a19-4"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"Workers always have safe conditions.","answer":"False","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"They may not have safe conditions","id":"a19-5"},{"type":"sequence","prompt":"Put these events or ideas in the order they appear in the recording.","events":[["Jake explains waste.","One problem is waste"],["He explains pollution.","Another problem is pollution"],["He suggests changes.","start with small changes"]],"answer":["Jake explains waste.","He explains pollution.","He suggests changes."],"evidence":"One problem is waste / Another problem is pollution / start with small changes","id":"a19-6"}],"vocab":[{"id":"v19-1","word":"landfill","definition":"a place where waste is buried"},{"id":"v19-2","word":"fabric","definition":"material used to make clothing"},{"id":"v19-3","word":"dye","definition":"colour a material using a substance"},{"id":"v19-4","word":"second-hand","definition":"previously owned by another person"},{"id":"v19-5","word":"swap","definition":"exchange one thing for another"}],"writing":"Recommend three practical changes to clothing habits and explain why. Focus: Describe large, small and zero quantities with suitable noun agreement.","speaking":"Discuss the trade-offs between cheap prices, quality and waste. Focus: Describe large, small and zero quantities with suitable noun agreement."},{"n":30,"title":"All & Both — Quantifiers","goal":"Use all and both with nouns, pronouns and auxiliaries.","theory":"\n### Quick overview\n\n- **both** = exactly two people/things: *Both (the) students passed.*\n- **all** = the whole group (3+ or uncountable): *All (the) students hate homework.* • *All music moves me.*\n- **all** works with **plural countables** and **uncountables**; **both** works only with **plural countables** that refer to two.\n\nMeaning difference: **both** refers to two; **all** to a whole group or amount. **Both** requires plural countable nouns. **All** also works with some singular time/whole expressions: “all day”, “all the way”.\n\n### With nouns\n\n- **both (the/my/etc.) + plural noun**: *Both the matches* were exciting.\n- **all + plural noun / uncountable noun** (general): *All students* need feedback. • *All water* was tested.\n- **all + the/my/etc. + noun** (specific): *All the students in my class* need feedback.\n\nYou can add **of** before a determiner: *both (of the) students*, *all (of the) water*. It’s often omitted in speech.\n\n### With *of* & pronouns\n\n- With pronouns, _**of**_** is required**: *all of us*, *both of them*, *all of you*.\n- As subjects: *All of us were* at the party. • *Both of them are* wrong.\n- As objects (after the verb): *The judge sent them all to jail.* • *She loves them both.*\n\nCase with pronouns: use *us/them* (not *we/they*) after **of**: **both of we** → **both of us**.\n\n### Word order (mid position)\n\n- **Before a main verb**: *We both like* tennis. • *They all left* early.\n- **After *be*** when it’s the only verb: *The boys were all* happy. • *The players are both* tired.\n- **After an auxiliary** (or the first auxiliary): *The robbers have both been* arrested. • *They are all going* to leave.\n- **Questions**: place after the subject: *Have they both finished?*\n\n### Common mistakes\n\n- **All/Both of students** → **All/Both of the students**.\n- **The both students** → **Both (the) students**.\n- **All/Both of we/they** → **All/Both of us/them**.\n- **Both water** (uncountable) → use **all (the) water**.\n\nCorrelative pattern (extra): **both … and …** joins two items: *Both Alice and Ben came.*\n\n### Mini examples\n\n- *Both my parents* work from home. / *All my parents* ❌ (only two → use **both**).\n- *All the furniture* was delivered. (uncountable)\n- *You both need* to sign. • *I called you all* yesterday.\n- *All of the emails* were answered. • *Both of the answers* are correct.\n\n","module":5,"audioNumber":6,"audioTitle":"My work experience","speaker":"Ethan","audio":"https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/67aa2baa0c65412632c4b3d1/696f4a00567295b3de3268a5_SCRIPT%201%20(Male%20speaker%20%E2%80%94%20Ethan)_%20My%20work%20experience.mp3","transcript":["Hi, I’m Ethan. My first real job was in a small electronics store when I was twenty. I thought it would be easy: smile, sell phones, and go home. But the first week was stressful because I had to learn a lot of details—prices, plans, returns, and all the “small rules” customers love to test. Some people were friendly, but others came in angry before I even said hello. I learned quickly that customer service is not only about being polite. It’s about staying calm and solving problems fast.","After that, I moved into an office job as a junior project assistant. The work was very different. In the shop, everything was immediate. In the office, tasks lasted weeks, and I had to follow processes, write emails, and keep track of deadlines. At the beginning I made a common mistake: I said “yes” to everything. I wanted to look helpful, so I accepted tasks even when my schedule was full. Then I started missing small deadlines, and I felt embarrassed. My manager gave me simple advice: “If you can’t do it today, say when you can do it.” That changed everything. I began to plan my day, use a task list, and set realistic expectations.","One project I remember well was a company event for clients. I had to book a venue, coordinate catering, and prepare a short presentation. Two days before the event, the venue called and said they had a problem with the electricity. It was a nightmare. I spent the whole day calling other places, comparing prices, and checking availability. In the end we found a new location, and the event went well. I was exhausted, but I felt proud because I didn’t panic.","Now I work in operations, and I still use lessons from my earlier jobs. I’m better at communication, I don’t take complaints personally, and I know that problems are part of the job. The key is how you respond when something goes wrong."],"mappingNote":"Work-team context for all/both; constructions applied in follow-up rather than claimed from audio.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"There are two organisers. ___ organisers are here.","answer":"Both","options":["Both","All of two","Both of"],"why":"Both refers to exactly two.","id":"g30-1"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"All ___ us helped at the event.","answer":"of","options":["of","zero","for"],"why":"Of is required before the object pronoun us.","id":"g30-2"},{"type":"gap","prompt":"The suppliers have ___ confirmed. Every supplier replied.","answer":"all","why":"All follows the first auxiliary.","id":"g30-3"},{"type":"gap","prompt":"She thanked them ___. There were exactly two assistants.","answer":"both","why":"Object pronoun + both is natural.","id":"g30-4"},{"type":"order","prompt":"Build a natural sentence. Use every word.","answer":"The guests were all happy with the event.","why":"With be as the only verb, all follows be.","id":"g30-5"},{"type":"edit","prompt":"Correct the sentence: Both of we signed the form.","answer":"Both of us signed the form.","why":"After of use object pronoun us.","id":"g30-6"},{"type":"rewrite","prompt":"Rewrite using all in mid-position: Every one of us has finished. Start “We”.","answer":"We have all finished.","why":"All comes after the first auxiliary.","id":"g30-7"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which phrase can refer to uncountable material?","answer":"all the furniture","options":["all the furniture","both furniture","both of furniture"],"why":"All can refer to a whole amount; both requires two countable items.","id":"g30-8"}],"listening":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"Where was Ethan’s first real job?","answer":"An electronics store","options":["An electronics store","A restaurant","A bank"],"evidence":"in a small electronics store","id":"a6-1"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What mistake did he make in his office job?","answer":"Accepting too many tasks","options":["Accepting too many tasks","Never answering messages","Refusing every task"],"evidence":"I said “yes” to everything","id":"a6-2"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What happened shortly before the client event?","answer":"The venue reported an electricity problem.","options":["The venue reported an electricity problem.","All guests cancelled.","The catering was stolen."],"evidence":"a problem with the electricity","id":"a6-3"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"His manager advised him to give realistic timing.","answer":"True","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"say when you can do it","id":"a6-4"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"They held the event at the original venue.","answer":"False","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"we found a new location","id":"a6-5"},{"type":"sequence","prompt":"Put these events or ideas in the order they appear in the recording.","events":[["He worked in a shop.","a small electronics store"],["He became a project assistant.","junior project assistant"],["He works in operations now.","Now I work in operations"]],"answer":["He worked in a shop.","He became a project assistant.","He works in operations now."],"evidence":"a small electronics store / junior project assistant / Now I work in operations","id":"a6-6"}],"vocab":[{"id":"v6-1","word":"coordinate","definition":"organise people or activities to work together"},{"id":"v6-2","word":"venue","definition":"a place where an event is held"},{"id":"v6-3","word":"catering","definition":"providing food and drinks at an event"},{"id":"v6-4","word":"availability","definition":"whether something can be used or booked"},{"id":"v6-5","word":"expectations","definition":"what someone believes will happen"}],"writing":"Write a professional update explaining a problem, its impact and a solution. Focus: Use all and both with nouns, pronouns and auxiliaries.","speaking":"Role-play telling your manager that a deadline needs to change. Focus: Use all and both with nouns, pronouns and auxiliaries."},{"n":31,"title":"Both, either & neither — Quantifiers","goal":"Choose between two alternatives and handle agreement and negative meaning.","theory":"\n### Core meanings\n\n- **both** = *A and B* (the two): *Both Jane and Mark passed.*\n- **either** = *A or B* (one of the two): *Either date works.*\n- **neither** = *not A and not B* (zero of two): *Neither answer is correct.*\n\nUse **both** with plural nouns; use **either / neither** with singular nouns.\n\n### With nouns (determiners)\n\n- **both + plural noun**: *I like both cars.*\n- **either/neither + singular noun**: *Either side* is fine. • *Neither parent* came.\n- **both (of) / either of / neither of + the/my/these + plural noun**: *Both (of) your parents* are kind. • *Either of those dates* is perfect. • *Neither of the players* had a good game.\n\nWe often drop **of** with **both** before a noun: *Both the students* (not only *both of the students*).\n\n### With pronouns\n\n- Before a verb (subject): *Both of them* passed. • *Neither of us* knows.\n- After a verb (object): *She invited them both.* • *I like neither of them.*\n\nUse **of** with pronouns: **both them** → **both of them**. Or move **both**: **They both passed.**\n\n### Standalone & correlative patterns\n\n- **Pronouns alone**: *—Coffee or tea? —Either.* • *—French or Spanish? —Both.* • *—Which car? —Neither.*\n- **both … and …**: joins two items → verb usually plural: *Both Alice and Ben are coming.*\n- **either … or …** / **neither … nor …**: verb agrees with the nearer subject: *Either the students or the teacher is late.* • *Neither the teacher nor the students are ready.*\n\n### Agreement & polarity\n\n- **either / neither** take a **singular** verb in standard English: *Either option is* fine. *Neither answer is* correct. (Plural is informal with *neither of + plural*.)\n- **neither** is already negative — don’t add another *not*: **Neither John nor Paula can’t come** → **Neither John nor Paula can come**.\n- After negatives, use **either** (= *also not*): *I don’t like either of the pictures* (= *I like neither*).\n\n### Common mistakes\n\n- **Both of students** → **Both (of the) students**.\n- **Neither of the candidates are** (B1) → prefer **Neither of the candidates is**.\n- **I don’t like both** → **I don’t like either** / **I like neither**.\n\n### Mini examples\n\n- *Both my knees* hurt today. (two → **both**)\n- *Either road* leads to the beach. (one of two)\n- *Neither suggestion* solves the problem. (zero of two)\n- *They both* apologized. / *Both of them* apologized.\n- *Neither of us* was ready, and *I don’t want either of the shirts*.\n\n","module":5,"audioNumber":11,"audioTitle":"Where did you stay?","speaker":"Liam","audio":"https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/67aa2baa0c65412632c4b3d1/696f27572805207d9d7a5c5e_B1%20Where%20did%20you%20stay_%20(Liam).mp3","transcript":["Hi, I’m Liam. Last autumn I went to Lisbon for nine days, and I stayed in a small guesthouse in the old part of the city. It wasn’t a big hotel with a gym and a pool, but it had character. The building was old, with narrow stairs and high ceilings, and my room had wooden floors and a small balcony. I could see a bit of the river if I leaned over the railing.","I chose that place because of the location. I wanted to be able to walk everywhere, and I really could. In the mornings I walked to cafés for coffee and pastries, then I took the tram to different neighbourhoods. In the evening I didn’t need to worry about transport because I could always walk back, even if it was late.","The guesthouse was comfortable, but not perfect. The room was clean and the bed was fine, yet the sound insulation was weak. On my first night I could hear people talking in the corridor, and I woke up early because someone slammed a door. After that, I used earplugs and it was much better. Another small issue was the shower: the water pressure changed sometimes, especially when other guests were using water.","The good part was the staff. They were friendly and gave me great tips, like which viewpoint was less crowded and where to eat seafood without paying tourist prices. They also arranged a taxi to the airport on my last day, so it was stress-free. Overall, it was a great stay: simple, central, and full of atmosphere. Next time I would still choose a guesthouse, but I might pick a room that faces the inner courtyard for more quiet."],"mappingNote":"Guesthouse choices supply a two-option follow-up; either/neither not in the original monologue.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"___ room is quiet: one faces the street and the other faces a bar.","answer":"Neither","options":["Neither","Both","Either of"],"why":"Neither + singular noun means not one and not the other.","id":"g31-1"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"___ the hotel or the guesthouse is available. Choose one.","answer":"Either","options":["Either","Both","Neither nor"],"why":"Either … or offers alternatives.","id":"g31-2"},{"type":"gap","prompt":"Both ___ those rooms have balconies. (of/for)","answer":"of","why":"Both of + determiner + plural noun.","id":"g31-3"},{"type":"gap","prompt":"Neither of us ___ ready. (be; standard singular)","answer":"is","why":"Neither of us normally takes singular is.","id":"g31-4"},{"type":"order","prompt":"Build a natural sentence. Use every word.","answer":"Either the students or the teacher is late.","why":"In either … or, the nearer subject teacher governs agreement.","id":"g31-5"},{"type":"edit","prompt":"Correct the sentence: Neither Liam nor Maya can’t come.","answer":"Neither Liam nor Maya can come.","why":"Neither already makes the pair negative.","id":"g31-6"},{"type":"rewrite","prompt":"Rewrite with neither: I do not like the first room and I do not like the second room. Start “I like”.","answer":"I like neither room.","why":"Neither + singular noun covers zero of two.","id":"g31-7"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"“I don’t like both rooms” necessarily means ___.","answer":"Not both; it does not necessarily reject each room.","options":["Not both; it does not necessarily reject each room.","I like neither room.","I like both rooms equally."],"why":"Not both can mean liking only one; either/neither is clearer for rejecting both.","id":"g31-8"}],"listening":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"Where did Liam stay?","answer":"A guesthouse in Lisbon","options":["A guesthouse in Lisbon","A large hotel in Rome","A campsite in Madrid"],"evidence":"a small guesthouse","id":"a11-1"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why did he choose it?","answer":"Its location","options":["Its location","Its swimming pool","Its gym"],"evidence":"because of the location","id":"a11-2"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What helped with noise?","answer":"Earplugs","options":["Earplugs","A new balcony","A taxi"],"evidence":"I used earplugs","id":"a11-3"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"He stayed for nine days.","answer":"True","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"for nine days","id":"a11-4"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"The water pressure was always stable.","answer":"False","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"the water pressure changed sometimes","id":"a11-5"},{"type":"sequence","prompt":"Put these events or ideas in the order they appear in the recording.","events":[["He chose a central location.","because of the location"],["He experienced noise on the first night.","On my first night"],["Staff arranged an airport taxi.","arranged a taxi to the airport"]],"answer":["He chose a central location.","He experienced noise on the first night.","Staff arranged an airport taxi."],"evidence":"because of the location / On my first night / arranged a taxi to the airport","id":"a11-6"}],"vocab":[{"id":"v11-1","word":"guesthouse","definition":"a small place providing accommodation"},{"id":"v11-2","word":"railing","definition":"a barrier along an edge for safety"},{"id":"v11-3","word":"sound insulation","definition":"material or design reducing sound transmission"},{"id":"v11-4","word":"earplugs","definition":"small objects placed in ears to reduce noise"},{"id":"v11-5","word":"courtyard","definition":"an open area surrounded by buildings"}],"writing":"Write a balanced accommodation review with benefits, problems and advice. Focus: Choose between two alternatives and handle agreement and negative meaning.","speaking":"Choose between two rooms and explain your preference. Focus: Choose between two alternatives and handle agreement and negative meaning."},{"n":32,"title":"Any, no & none — how they work","goal":"Express zero quantity and free choice without double negatives.","theory":"\n### Core rules\n\n- **any + noun** in **negatives & questions**: *We don’t have any time.* • *Is there any coffee?*\n- **no + noun** with a **positive verb** (= not any): *We have no time.*\n- **any** can be a **pronoun**: *Is there milk? —There isn’t any.*\n- **none** is a **pronoun** (= zero). Not followed by a noun: *How many ideas? —None.*\n\nUse with both countable (plural) and uncountable nouns: *any/no chairs*, *any/no money*.\n\n### **any** in affirmative = “it doesn’t matter which”\n\n- *You can come any weekend.* (= whichever weekend)\n- *Any idiot* could use this app.\n- Also with compounds: *We can go anywhere / ask anybody / do anything.*\n\n### Equivalent forms\n\n| **Negative with any** | **Positive with no** | **Pronoun none** |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| I don’t have **any** money. | I have **no** money. | I have **none**. |\n| We didn’t get **any** replies. | We got **no** replies. | **None** came in. |\n\n### **none of** + group\n\n- **none of + the/my/these + plural noun**: *None of the students* is / are from France.\n- **none of + us/you/them**: *None of them* agreed.\n\nAfter **none of** + plural, the verb can be **singular** (more formal) or **plural** (informal spoken English).\n\n### Compounds: anything / anybody / anywhere\n\n- Use **anything / anybody / anywhere** in **negatives & questions**: *We didn’t see anybody.* • *Have you been anywhere interesting?*\n- Use **nothing / nobody / nowhere** with **positive** verbs: *We saw nobody.* • *There’s nothing we can do.*\n- In affirmatives, **any-** can mean “it doesn’t matter which/who/where”: *Ask anybody.* • *Go anywhere.*\n\n### Avoid double negatives\n\n- **I don’t have no money.** → **I don’t have any money.** / **I have no money.**\n- **We didn’t see nobody.** → **We didn’t see anybody.** / **We saw nobody.**\n\nStandard English does not combine a negative verb with a *no-/nobody/nothing/nowhere* word.\n\n### Mini examples\n\n- There isn’t **any** sugar left. / There is **no** sugar left. / There’s **none** left.\n- “Do we have **any** questions?” — “**None**.”\n- “Where can we eat?” — “**Anywhere** you like.”\n- We went **nowhere** yesterday because of the storm.\n\n","module":5,"audioNumber":27,"audioTitle":"Hidden Underground Tunnel Network","speaker":"Jake","audio":"https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/67aa2baa0c65412632c4b3d1/69776d90f0ad342d528014f8_Hidden%20Underground%20Tunnel%20Network.mp3","transcript":["Hi, I’m Jake. Today I want to tell you about something unusual under many big cities: hidden underground tunnels. I don’t mean modern metro lines. I’m talking about old service tunnels that people don’t notice, and sometimes they don’t even know they exist.","In one city I visited, there was a small museum tour about these tunnels. Many of them were built a long time ago for practical reasons. Some tunnels carried water, some were used for heating pipes, and others were made for delivering goods between buildings. During cold winters, workers could move underground and avoid bad weather. In the past, it was also safer and faster than using crowded streets.","Now most of these tunnels are closed to the public. They can be dangerous because the floors are wet, the air is heavy, and parts of the tunnels may be unstable. Some places also have old cables, broken stairs, or deep holes. That’s why legal tours usually show only a small, safe section. On the tour, we walked through a short corridor with low ceilings, and we saw signs on the walls, old metal doors, and pipes that were no longer used. The guide told us that some tunnels connect important buildings, like old stations, warehouses, and basements of historic hotels.","What I found most interesting was the feeling down there. Above ground, the city was loud and full of traffic. Underground, it was quiet, cool, and a bit creepy. You could hear water drops and your own footsteps. It made me think about how cities have “two lives”: the one we see every day, and the one hidden below.","I’m not planning to explore tunnels by myself, because it’s risky and often illegal. But as a story and a piece of history, underground tunnels are fascinating. They show how people solved problems in the past—and how much is still hidden under our feet."],"mappingNote":"Unknown hidden spaces and no public access support negative and free-choice contrasts.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"“How many public entrances are open?” “___.”","answer":"None","options":["None","No","Any of"],"why":"None stands alone as a pronoun.","id":"g32-1"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Choose ___ safe tour you like.","answer":"any","options":["any","no of","none"],"why":"Any in an affirmative means whichever.","id":"g32-2"},{"type":"gap","prompt":"There is ___ access without a ticket. (no/none)","answer":"no","why":"No is a determiner before a noun.","id":"g32-3"},{"type":"gap","prompt":"We didn’t see ___ in the corridor. (anybody/nobody)","answer":"anybody","why":"Use anybody after a negative verb.","id":"g32-4"},{"type":"order","prompt":"Build a natural sentence. Use every word.","answer":"None of the doors were open.","why":"None of + the + plural noun refers to a known group.","id":"g32-5"},{"type":"edit","prompt":"Correct the sentence: I didn’t hear nothing underground.","answer":"I didn’t hear anything underground.","why":"Negative verb + anything avoids a double negative.","id":"g32-6"},{"type":"rewrite","prompt":"Rewrite using none: We did not receive any replies. Start “We received”.","answer":"We received none.","why":"None replaces the noun phrase as a pronoun.","id":"g32-7"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"“You can choose any seat.” What does any mean?","answer":"Whichever seat you want","options":["Whichever seat you want","There are zero seats","Only the seat already mentioned"],"why":"Affirmative any often expresses unrestricted choice.","id":"g32-8"}],"listening":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"What kind of tunnels does Jake describe?","answer":"Old service tunnels","options":["Old service tunnels","Modern metro lines only","New tourist shopping tunnels"],"evidence":"old service tunnels","id":"a27-1"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why do legal tours show limited sections?","answer":"Other sections can be dangerous.","options":["Other sections can be dangerous.","The tunnels are all brightly lit.","There is no history elsewhere."],"evidence":"They can be dangerous","id":"a27-2"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How did underground life feel compared with above ground?","answer":"Quiet, cool and a bit creepy","options":["Quiet, cool and a bit creepy","Hot and full of car traffic","Exactly the same"],"evidence":"quiet, cool, and a bit creepy","id":"a27-3"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"Jake plans to explore tunnels on his own.","answer":"False","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"I’m not planning to explore tunnels by myself","id":"a27-4"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"Some tunnels were used to deliver goods.","answer":"True","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"delivering goods between buildings","id":"a27-5"},{"type":"sequence","prompt":"Put these events or ideas in the order they appear in the recording.","events":[["He joins a museum tour.","a small museum tour"],["He walks through a short corridor.","walked through a short corridor"],["He reflects on two city lives.","cities have “two lives”"]],"answer":["He joins a museum tour.","He walks through a short corridor.","He reflects on two city lives."],"evidence":"a small museum tour / walked through a short corridor / cities have “two lives”","id":"a27-6"}],"vocab":[{"id":"v27-1","word":"service tunnel","definition":"an underground passage for utilities or practical work"},{"id":"v27-2","word":"unstable","definition":"not secure and liable to change or collapse"},{"id":"v27-3","word":"warehouse","definition":"a building used to store goods"},{"id":"v27-4","word":"creepy","definition":"causing an uneasy or frightened feeling"},{"id":"v27-5","word":"beneath","definition":"under or below something"}],"writing":"Describe a legal historic tour and explain its atmosphere and limitations. Focus: Express zero quantity and free choice without double negatives.","speaking":"Compare what visitors see above ground with what may be hidden below. Focus: Express zero quantity and free choice without double negatives."},{"n":33,"title":"Gerund or Infinitive — do / to do / doing","goal":"Use gerunds, to-infinitives and bare infinitives, including meaning changes.","theory":"\n### Quick map\n\n- **to-infinitive** = purpose, plans/decisions, after many adjectives & some verbs. *We went out to eat.*\n- **-ing (gerund)** = activities in general, after prepositions, as a subject/object. *Swimming is relaxing.*\n- **bare infinitive** (no *to*) = after modals; after *make/let*; sometimes after *help*. *You must call.*\n\nNegative forms: **not to + verb** / **not + verb-ing** → *He promised not to be late.* • *I enjoy not cooking on Fridays.*\n\n### Use the to-infinitive\n\n- **Purpose**: *I called to ask a question.* (thing’s purpose: *A fork is for eating.*)\n- **After adjectives**: *It’s hard to understand.* • *Nice to meet you.*\n- **too / enough**: *too young to drive* • *old enough to vote*\n- **would like/love/hate/prefer**: *I’d like to go.*\n- **Verb + object + to-inf**: *He told me to wait.* • *They want us to help.*\n- **after wh-words** (*ask/know/decide* etc.): *I don’t know what to do.*\n- **the first/next/only/last**: *the first person to arrive*.\n\n**Common verbs + to-inf**: agree, decide, hope, plan, want, need, promise, refuse, learn, offer, expect, seem, appear, manage, happen, fail, afford, choose, pretend.\n\n### Use the -ing form\n\n- **As subject/object**: *Reading helps me relax.* • *I enjoy cooking.*\n- **After prepositions**: *I’m good at drawing.* • *Thanks for coming.* • *Before eating, wash your hands.*\n- **After spend/waste time/money**: *We spent hours waiting.*\n- **After like/love/hate/prefer/don’t mind** (general preference): *She loves cycling.*\n- **Fixed expressions**: *be busy (doing)*, *can’t stand (doing)*, *look forward to (doing)*, *there’s no point (in doing)*.\n\n**Common verbs + -ing**: admit, avoid, consider, delay, deny, enjoy, finish, imagine, involve, keep, mind, miss, practise, recommend, regret, suggest, risk.\n\n### Use the bare infinitive (without *to*)\n\n- **After modals**: *can/could, will/would, shall/should, may/might, must* → *You must call.*\n- **After *make/let* + object**: *They made me wait.* • *Let her go.*\n- ***help*** can be *with or without* *to*: *She helped me (to) carry the bags.*\n\nVerbs of perception (*see/hear/feel*) + object + **bare infinitive** (complete action) or **-ing** (action in progress): *I saw him cross / crossing the road.*\n\n### Same verb, different meaning\n\n| **Pattern** | **Meaning** | **Example** |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **forget + to do** | fail to do a duty/plan | *I forgot to lock* the door. |\n| **forget + doing** | a (usually unforgettable) memory | I’ll never *forget visiting* Kyoto. |\n| **remember + to do** | not forget a duty | *Remember to call* me. |\n| **remember + doing** | recall a past event | I *remember meeting* her. |\n| **try + to do** | make an effort to achieve | I'll *try to finish* today. |\n| **try + doing** | do as an experiment/solution | *Try restarting* the laptop. |\n| **stop + to do** | stop one action *in order to* start another | We *stopped to eat*. |\n| **stop + doing** | finish/quit an activity | He *stopped smoking*. |\n\n### Useful contrasts & tips\n\n- **like / love / hate** + **-ing** (general) vs. **would like / would love / would hate** + **to-inf** (specific/now).\n- **begin/start/continue** can take **to-inf** or **-ing** with little or no change: *It started to rain / raining.*\n- After **prepositions** always use **-ing**, even when the word is *to* as a preposition (*look forward to meeting*).\n- Passive **make** takes a to-infinitive: *I was made to wait.* **Let** is not normally used in that passive pattern; say *I was allowed to leave*.\n\n","module":5,"audioNumber":22,"audioTitle":"Why Small Habits Matter","speaker":"Olivia","audio":"https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/67aa2baa0c65412632c4b3d1/697650def12e55efd0d8e96f_Why%20Small%20Habits%20Matter.mp3","transcript":["Hi, I’m Olivia. Today I want to talk about something simple but powerful: small habits and how they can change your life over time.","People often think they need a big plan to improve. They say, “On Monday, I will change everything.” But in real life, big changes can feel scary, and we get tired quickly. That’s why small habits work better for many people. They are easy to start, and they don’t feel heavy.","For example, last year I wanted to be healthier, but I didn’t want to go to the gym every day. So I started with a short walk after dinner. At first, it was only ten minutes. Some days I didn’t feel motivated, but ten minutes felt possible. After a few weeks, my walk became longer, and I noticed I was sleeping better. I didn’t “force” a new lifestyle. I built it slowly.","Small habits can also help with learning. If you study for five minutes a day, it sounds too little, right? But five minutes every day is better than one long study session once a month. You remember more, and you feel more confident because you keep going. The same idea works with cleaning, saving money, or even improving your mood.","Of course, small habits are not magic. You still need patience. Sometimes you will miss a day, and that’s normal. The important thing is to return to the habit without feeling guilty. Progress is not perfect. It is consistent.","In the future, I want to keep focusing on small steps. I don’t need to change my whole life in one week. I just need to choose one simple habit, repeat it, and let time do the work."],"mappingNote":"Keep focusing / without feeling / choose … repeat …; habit change gives varied verb patterns.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"She enjoys ___ after dinner.","answer":"walking","options":["walking","to walk only","walk"],"why":"Enjoy takes a gerund.","id":"g33-1"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Try ___ the app if it freezes; that may solve it.","answer":"restarting","options":["restarting","to restarted","restart to"],"why":"Try doing suggests an experiment or solution.","id":"g33-2"},{"type":"gap","prompt":"I promised not ___ miss another session.","answer":"to","why":"Negative infinitive: not to + base verb.","id":"g33-3"},{"type":"gap","prompt":"Remember ___ call the tutor tomorrow.","answer":"to","why":"Remember to do means remember a future duty.","id":"g33-4"},{"type":"order","prompt":"Build a natural sentence. Use every word.","answer":"My friend helped me build a routine.","why":"Help + object can take a bare infinitive.","id":"g33-5"},{"type":"edit","prompt":"Correct the sentence: I look forward to meet the group.","answer":"I look forward to meeting the group.","why":"To is a preposition in look forward to.","id":"g33-6"},{"type":"rewrite","prompt":"Rewrite with stopped + to-infinitive: She paused her walk in order to check the map. Start “She”.","answer":"She stopped to check the map.","why":"Stop to do means pause one activity for a different purpose.","id":"g33-7"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"“I remember locking the door.” What is remembered?","answer":"A past action","options":["A past action","A duty still to do","A general future intention"],"why":"Remember doing recalls a past event.","id":"g33-8"}],"listening":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"What habit did Olivia start?","answer":"A short walk after dinner","options":["A short walk after dinner","A daily two-hour gym session","Buying new exercise clothes"],"evidence":"a short walk after dinner","id":"a22-1"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How long were the first walks?","answer":"Ten minutes","options":["Ten minutes","An hour","Thirty seconds"],"evidence":"only ten minutes","id":"a22-2"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does she advise after missing a day?","answer":"Return without guilt","options":["Return without guilt","Give up permanently","Double every task immediately"],"evidence":"return to the habit without feeling guilty","id":"a22-3"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"Olivia says small habits are magic.","answer":"False","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"small habits are not magic","id":"a22-4"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"Daily five-minute study can beat one monthly long session.","answer":"True","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"better than one long study session once a month","id":"a22-5"},{"type":"sequence","prompt":"Put these events or ideas in the order they appear in the recording.","events":[["She starts a short walk.","started with a short walk"],["The walks grow longer.","my walk became longer"],["She notices better sleep.","I was sleeping better"]],"answer":["She starts a short walk.","The walks grow longer.","She notices better sleep."],"evidence":"started with a short walk / my walk became longer / I was sleeping better","id":"a22-6"}],"vocab":[{"id":"v22-1","word":"motivated","definition":"feeling willing and ready to act"},{"id":"v22-2","word":"patience","definition":"ability to wait or continue calmly"},{"id":"v22-3","word":"guilty","definition":"feeling bad about something you have done"},{"id":"v22-4","word":"consistent","definition":"maintained regularly over time"},{"id":"v22-5","word":"over time","definition":"gradually as time passes"}],"writing":"Choose one small habit, explain a realistic plan and anticipate a setback. Focus: Use gerunds, to-infinitives and bare infinitives, including meaning changes.","speaking":"Persuade a friend to start small rather than change everything at once. Focus: Use gerunds, to-infinitives and bare infinitives, including meaning changes."},{"n":34,"title":"Reported / Indirect Speech — quick guide","goal":"Report statements, questions and requests with perspective and word order changes.","theory":"\n### What it is\n\nWe use **reported (indirect) speech** to tell someone what another person said or thought. Pronouns, time words and—often—verb tenses change because the speaker and time are different.\n\n- *“I don’t have time.”* → She said (that) **she didn’t** have time.\n- *“I’m tired.”* → He said (that) **he was** tired.\n\nWe often omit **that** after reporting verbs: *She said she was late.*\n\n### Say vs. tell (and friends)\n\n- **say** + clause: *She said (that) it was urgent.*\n- **tell** + **object** + clause: *She told me (that) it was urgent.*\n- Other useful verbs: *ask*, *advise*, *warn*, *promise*, *invite*, *remind*, *suggest*.\n\n### Tense changes (backshift)\n\n| **Direct speech** | **→ Reported speech** | **Example** |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Present simple | Past simple | “I *like* it.” → He said he *liked* it. |\n| Present continuous | Past continuous | “I’m *studying*.” → She said she *was studying*. |\n| Past simple | Past perfect | “I *lost* it.” → He said he *had lost* it. |\n| Present perfect / Past perfect | Past perfect (unchanged) | “I’ve *seen* it.” → She said she *had seen* it. |\n| Will | **would** | “I’ll help.” → He said he *would* help. |\n| Can | **could** | “I can come.” → She said she *could* come. |\n| May | **might** | “I may be late.” → He said he *might* be late. |\n| Must (obligation) | **had to** | “I must leave.” → She said she *had to* leave. |\n| *could / should / would / might / ought to* | usually unchanged | “We should go.” → They said we *should* go. |\n\nNo backshift is also possible when the reporting verb is in the present (*He says he is tired*) or when the statement is still true/general fact (*She said water boils at 100℃*).\n\n### Time & place words\n\n| **Direct** | **→ Reported** |\n| --- | --- |\n| now | then / at that moment |\n| today / tonight | that day / that night |\n| yesterday / last night | the day/night before |\n| tomorrow / next week | the next/following day / week |\n| last week | the previous week / the week before |\n| this week | that week |\n| here | there |\n| … ago | … before |\n\n### Reported questions\n\n- **wh- questions** keep the wh-word and use **statement order** (no *do/does/did*):  \n  “*When did they arrive?*” → He asked *when they had arrived*.\n- **Yes/No questions** use **if / whether**:  \n  “*Did they call?*” → He asked *if/whether they had called*.\n\n### Reported orders, requests & advice\n\n- **tell/ask + object + to-infinitive**:  \n  “*Give me the money.*” → He *told me to give* him the money.\n- **Negative**: **told/asked + object + not to**  \n  “*Don’t answer the door.*” → He *asked me not to answer* the door.\n- Common patterns: *advise/warn/encourage + object + to-infinitive*; *promise + to-infinitive* or *promise (someone) that + clause*; *suggest + -ing / that + clause*.\n\n### Pronoun & reference changes\n\n- First person becomes third person from the original speaker’s view: *“I will help you.”* → He said **he** would help **me**.\n- Possessives change accordingly: *“That’s my pen.”* → She said it was **her** pen.\n\nKeep pronouns if the speaker/listener haven’t changed (*You said, “I love you.” → You told me you loved me*).\n\n","module":6,"audioNumber":28,"audioTitle":"The Voice Behind the Metro Announcements","speaker":"Ryan","audio":"https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/67aa2baa0c65412632c4b3d1/69777117d897784581732ee9_The%20Voice%20Behind%20the%20Metro%20Announcements.mp3","transcript":["Hi, I’m Ryan. Today I want to tell you about a person most people never meet, but they hear every day: the woman behind the metro announcements.","In many cities, the voice that says things like “Mind the gap” or “Next station” belongs to a real voice actor. Let’s call her Claire. She is not famous like a movie star, but her voice is everywhere. Millions of commuters hear her on their way to work, school, or home, and most of them never ask, “Who is she?”","Claire started as a radio student. She practiced clear pronunciation and calm intonation. One day, she saw an advertisement for a recording job. The city transport company wanted a voice that sounded friendly, confident, and easy to understand. They tested many people, and Claire got the job.","Recording announcements is more difficult than it seems. Claire had to read hundreds of station names, warnings, and short messages. She repeated the same words again and again, but she needed to keep the tone consistent. Sometimes the producer stopped her and said, “A bit slower,” or “More neutral,” or “Smile with your voice.” She also learned how to pronounce names from different languages correctly, which took time and patience.","What I find interesting is how anonymous the job is. Claire can sit in a café and hear her own voice from a speaker, but nobody recognizes her. At first, that felt strange. Later, she said it felt powerful in a quiet way. Her voice helps people: tourists, older passengers, parents with children, and anyone who is tired after a long day.","So next time you hear a calm voice in public transport, remember: it might belong to someone who works hard behind the scenes—just to make your trip a little easier."],"mappingNote":"Direct producer remarks / she said it felt powerful provide authentic reporting contexts.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"“I am tired,” Claire said. Claire said she ___ tired.","answer":"was","options":["was","is being yesterday","had tiring"],"why":"Backshift present be to past was in a past report.","id":"g34-1"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"“Where do you work?” He asked me ___.","answer":"where I worked","options":["where I worked","where did I work","where do I worked"],"why":"Reported questions use statement order.","id":"g34-2"},{"type":"gap","prompt":"“I’ll help tomorrow.” He said he ___ help the following day.","answer":"would","why":"Will becomes would in backshift.","id":"g34-3"},{"type":"gap","prompt":"“Don’t rush.” The producer told her ___ rush.","answer":"not to","why":"Negative reported command: object + not to.","id":"g34-4"},{"type":"order","prompt":"Build a natural sentence. Use every word.","answer":"She told me that the recording was ready.","why":"Tell requires the person addressed.","id":"g34-5"},{"type":"edit","prompt":"Correct the sentence: He said me that he was busy.","answer":"He told me that he was busy.","why":"Tell takes an object; say does not take me directly.","id":"g34-6"},{"type":"rewrite","prompt":"Report the request: “Please wait here,” she said to me. Start “She asked”.","answer":"She asked me to wait there.|She asked me to wait here.","why":"Ask + object + to-infinitive; here changes if the location changes.","id":"g34-7"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"When can a reported statement keep its original present tense?","answer":"When it remains true or the report is immediate","options":["When it remains true or the report is immediate","Never","Only when it contains a passive"],"why":"Backshift depends on the reporting context, not a mechanical rule.","id":"g34-8"}],"listening":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"What kind of work did Claire train for initially?","answer":"Radio","options":["Radio","Medicine","Architecture"],"evidence":"a radio student","id":"a28-1"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What did the transport company want from a voice?","answer":"Friendly, confident and easy to understand","options":["Friendly, confident and easy to understand","Famous and very loud","Fast and dramatic"],"evidence":"friendly, confident, and easy to understand","id":"a28-2"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What was a recording challenge?","answer":"Keeping the tone consistent","options":["Keeping the tone consistent","Writing every train timetable","Building speakers"],"evidence":"keep the tone consistent","id":"a28-3"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"Claire is recognised by everyone who hears her voice.","answer":"False","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"nobody recognizes her","id":"a28-4"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"She had to learn names from different languages.","answer":"True","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"names from different languages correctly","id":"a28-5"},{"type":"sequence","prompt":"Put these events or ideas in the order they appear in the recording.","events":[["She sees a recording-job advertisement.","advertisement for a recording job"],["She gets the job.","Claire got the job"],["She records many station names.","hundreds of station names"]],"answer":["She sees a recording-job advertisement.","She gets the job.","She records many station names."],"evidence":"advertisement for a recording job / Claire got the job / hundreds of station names","id":"a28-6"}],"vocab":[{"id":"v28-1","word":"commuter","definition":"someone who travels regularly to work"},{"id":"v28-2","word":"intonation","definition":"the rise and fall of the voice in speech"},{"id":"v28-3","word":"neutral","definition":"not showing strong emotion or taking sides"},{"id":"v28-4","word":"anonymous","definition":"not publicly identified or recognised"},{"id":"v28-5","word":"behind the scenes","definition":"working without being seen by the public"}],"writing":"Describe an overlooked job and explain its value to other people. Focus: Report statements, questions and requests with perspective and word order changes.","speaking":"Role-play a producer giving polite recording instructions. Focus: Report statements, questions and requests with perspective and word order changes."},{"n":35,"title":"Verb + preposition (dependent prepositions)","goal":"Use dependent prepositions in statements, questions and verb-ing patterns.","theory":"\n### Quick rules\n\n- Some verbs are followed by a fixed preposition *before* the object: *listen **to** music*, *depend **on** trains*. These are called **dependent prepositions**.\n- **Preposition + verb-ing**: after any preposition, use the *-ing* form: *look forward to seeing you*, *insist on paying*.\n- **Questions & relatives**: the preposition can “move” to the end (*stranded*): *Who are you talking to?* / *The person (who) I spoke to*.\n- **Passive** keeps the preposition: *He was laughed at*.\n\n### Common patterns (verb + preposition)\n\n| **Preposition** | **Typical verbs** | **Example** |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **to** | belong *to*, listen *to*, talk/speak *to*, write *to*, say *to* sb, refer *to*, reply *to*, get married *to*, compare A *to* B (show similarity) | She *belongs to* a hiking club. • He *got married to* Mia. • Don’t forget to *write to* me. |\n| **for** | ask *for*, apply *for*, pay *for*, wait *for*, look *for*, care *for* (=like/ take care of) | I’m *waiting for* the bus. • She *applied for* a visa. |\n| **at** | arrive *at* (building/event), look *at*, laugh *at*, smile *at*, shout *at* (angrily), aim *at* | We *arrived at* the station. • He *laughed at* the joke. |\n| **in** | arrive *in* (city/country), believe *in*, invest *in*, succeed *in*, specialize *in* | They *arrived in* Rome. • She *succeeded in* passing. |\n| **on** | depend *on*, insist *on*, spend (money/time) *on*, agree *on* (a plan), focus *on* | It *depends on* the weather. • We *agreed on* the schedule. |\n| **of** | think *of*, hear *of*, approve *of*, consist *of*, remind sb *of* sth | She *reminds me of* her mother. • Have you *heard of* it? |\n| **with** | agree *with* (a person/idea), argue *with*, deal *with*, provide/supply sb *with* sth, compare A *with* B (show difference) | I *agree with* you. • They *provided us with* maps. |\n| **about** | talk/speak *about*, think *about*, dream *about*, complain *about*, worry *about*, argue *about* | Let’s *talk about* your plans. • He *complained about* the noise. |\n| **to + -ing** | *look forward to*, *be used to*, *object to*, *confess to* | I *look forward to meeting* you. (preposition → -ing) |\n\nMany verbs take two prepositions to show people + things: *apologize to someone for something*, *complain to the manager about the bill*, *thank sb for sth*, *provide sb with sth*, *charge sb for sth*.\n\n### Common contrasts (easy to mix up)\n\n- **arrive in** a city/country vs **arrive at** a building/event; **arrive Ø** home.\n- **agree with** (a person/opinion) vs **agree on** (a plan/date) vs **agree about** (a topic in general).\n- **think of** (=have an idea/opinion) vs **think about** (=consider/reflect).\n- **shout at** (angry) vs **shout to** (in order to be heard).\n- **talk/speak to** (one-way) vs **talk/speak with** (two-way, esp. AmE). Both are fine at B1.\n- **laugh at** (as a reaction) vs **laugh about** (discuss humorously).\n\n### Verbs with *no* preposition (avoid “\\*about”)\n\n- **discuss** a problem (NOT *discuss about*)\n- **enter** a building (NOT *enter into*)\n- **answer** a question (NOT *answer to*)\n- **reach** a goal / a decision (NOT *reach to*)\n- **marry** someone (but: *get married to someone*)\n- **phone/call** someone (NOT *phone to*)\n- **ask** someone **for** something (NOT *ask to someone*)\n- **tell** someone something (not *tell to someone*); **say** something **to** someone is also correct.\n\n### Useful two-object patterns\n\n| **Pattern** | **Examples** |\n| --- | --- |\n| **verb + sb + about** | *inform*/*warn*/*remind* sb *about* sth • *ask* sb *about* sth |\n| **verb + sb + for** | *ask*/*blame*/*forgive*/*thank* sb *for* sth |\n| **verb + sb + with** | *provide*/*supply*/*present* sb *with* sth |\n| **verb + sb + to** | *explain*/*describe* sth *to* sb • *introduce* sb *to* sb |\n\nWhen two prepositions are needed, keep the order: *complain to the manager about the bill*.\n\n### Mini example bank\n\n- **to** He **got married to** his high-school sweetheart. • I **look forward to meeting** you.\n- **for** She **applied for** the job and **asked for** feedback.\n- **at** We **arrived at** the airport and everyone **smiled at** the kids.\n- **in** They **arrived in** New York and quickly **invested in** a small studio.\n- **on** The plan **depends on** funding, so the team **insisted on** a meeting.\n- **of** That story **reminds me of** my childhood; I’m **thinking of** writing it down.\n- **with** He rarely **agrees with** his brother, and he **argues with** him about politics.\n- **about** We **talked about** the future and he **complained about** the traffic.\n\n### Natural usage tips\n\n- In speaking, the preposition is usually *unstressed*: *Who are you talking to?*\n- Avoid repeating prepositions: say *Where are you from?* (not *From where…*) in everyday speech.\n\n","module":6,"audioNumber":9,"audioTitle":"How People Can Build a Career Change","speaker":"Ethan","audio":"https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/67aa2baa0c65412632c4b3d1/69765c2549b820f3ac47dc51_How%20People%20Can%20Build%20a%20Career%20Change.mp3","transcript":["Hi, I’m Ethan. Today I want to talk about something many adults think about: changing your career and starting a new direction.","When people hear “career change,” they imagine quitting their job and beginning again from zero. That sounds risky, especially if you have bills to pay. But in real life, most career changes happen slowly. It’s more like building a bridge from your current life to a new one.","A friend of mine worked in a hotel for years. He liked the people, but he felt tired of the same routine. He didn’t quit immediately. First, he asked himself simple questions: What do I enjoy? What am I good at? What kind of day do I want to have? He noticed he liked solving problems and helping customers, so he started learning basic IT support online. Just one hour in the evenings, a few times a week.","At the beginning, he felt confused. New words, new tools, new rules. But after a few months, he could explain simple technical problems. He also talked to people who already worked in IT. This helped him understand what the job is really like, not just what it looks like on social media.","Later, he updated his CV and applied for junior roles. He got rejected several times, but he learned from each interview. Finally, he found a company that wanted someone with strong communication skills. His hotel experience became an advantage, not a weakness.","For me, the main lesson is this: a career change is not only about learning new skills. It’s also about being patient, staying consistent, and using what you already have. If you take small steps, the future can look very different in one year."],"mappingNote":"Applied for / talked to / learning about work provide dependent-preposition contexts.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"Ethan applied ___ a junior role.","answer":"for","options":["for","to on","at"],"why":"Apply for a job or role.","id":"g35-1"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"The decision depends ___ your experience.","answer":"on","options":["on","of","from"],"why":"Depend takes on.","id":"g35-2"},{"type":"gap","prompt":"We arrived ___ the office at nine.","answer":"at","why":"Arrive at a building; arrive in a city.","id":"g35-3"},{"type":"gap","prompt":"She apologised to me ___ missing the call.","answer":"for","why":"Apologise to a person for an action.","id":"g35-4"},{"type":"order","prompt":"Build a natural sentence. Use every word.","answer":"Who did you speak to about the job?","why":"In everyday questions the preposition can be stranded at the end.","id":"g35-5"},{"type":"edit","prompt":"Correct the sentence: We discussed about the interview.","answer":"We discussed the interview.","why":"Discuss takes a direct object without about.","id":"g35-6"},{"type":"rewrite","prompt":"Rewrite as a natural question using who: You were talking to someone.","answer":"Who were you talking to?","why":"The preposition can go at the end of the question.","id":"g35-7"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which sentence has the correct dependent preposition?","answer":"We agreed on a date.","options":["We agreed on a date.","We depended of the result.","We discussed about the plan."],"why":"Agree on is used for selecting a plan or date.","id":"g35-8"}],"listening":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"How does Ethan describe most career changes?","answer":"Gradual","options":["Gradual","Instant","Impossible"],"evidence":"most career changes happen slowly","id":"a9-1"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What did his friend learn?","answer":"Basic IT support","options":["Basic IT support","Advanced medicine","Restaurant cooking"],"evidence":"basic IT support online","id":"a9-2"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which previous skill became an advantage?","answer":"Communication","options":["Communication","Aircraft repair","Professional painting"],"evidence":"strong communication skills","id":"a9-3"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"The friend quit his hotel job immediately.","answer":"False","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"He didn’t quit immediately","id":"a9-4"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"He was rejected several times before finding a role.","answer":"True","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"He got rejected several times","id":"a9-5"},{"type":"sequence","prompt":"Put these events or ideas in the order they appear in the recording.","events":[["He asked what he enjoyed.","What do I enjoy?"],["He studied IT support.","learning basic IT support online"],["He applied for junior roles.","applied for junior roles"]],"answer":["He asked what he enjoyed.","He studied IT support.","He applied for junior roles."],"evidence":"What do I enjoy? / learning basic IT support online / applied for junior roles","id":"a9-6"}],"vocab":[{"id":"v9-1","word":"career change","definition":"moving into a different type of work"},{"id":"v9-2","word":"consistent","definition":"continuing regularly without frequent changes"},{"id":"v9-3","word":"junior role","definition":"a job intended for someone with limited experience"},{"id":"v9-4","word":"reject","definition":"decide not to accept a person or proposal"},{"id":"v9-5","word":"advantage","definition":"something that makes success more likely"}],"writing":"Design a realistic three-step plan for a possible career change. Focus: Use dependent prepositions in statements, questions and verb-ing patterns.","speaking":"Discuss how previous experience could help in a new career. Focus: Use dependent prepositions in statements, questions and verb-ing patterns."},{"n":36,"title":"Adjective + preposition (dependent prepositions)","goal":"Choose adjective-preposition pairs and follow them with nouns or gerunds.","theory":"\n### Quick rules\n\n- Some adjectives are followed by a **fixed preposition**: *interested in music*, *proud of my team*. These pairs are called **dependent prepositions**.\n- After a preposition, use a **noun** or **verb-ing**: *keen on cycling*, *afraid of flying*.\n- Meaning can change with the preposition (see “Key contrasts”).\n\n### Key contrasts you should know\n\n- **good at** (skill), **good for** (beneficial), **good to** (kind): *She’s good at Maths; Fruit is good for you; He was very good to me.*\n- **sorry for** (you did something / sympathy for someone) vs **sorry about** (a situation/thing): *Sorry for being late.* • *Sorry about the noise.*\n- **angry with** (a person) / **angry at/about** (a thing/situation).\n- **different from** (usual), **different to** (BrE), **different than** before a clause in AmE: *different from mine* • *different than I expected*.\n- **surprised/shocked/amazed at/by** (both OK); **afraid/scared/frightened of** (most common).\n\nTip: many feelings take **of** (*afraid of, proud of, ashamed of, jealous of*), abilities take **at**, interests take **in**, dependence takes **on**, and benefit/harm takes **for**.\n\n### Common adjective + preposition pairs\n\n| **Preposition** | **Adjectives** | **Example** |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| **to** | addicted to, close to, kind to, married to, rude to, similar to, opposed to | It’s important to be *kind to* animals. • She’s *married to* a musician. |\n| **for** | bad for, good for, famous for, ready for, responsible for, sorry for | Smoking is *bad for* your health. • I’m *responsible for* the budget. |\n| **at** | good at, brilliant at, bad/terrible at, angry at (a thing), surprised at | He’s *good at* chess. • She was *angry at* the decision. |\n| **in** | interested in, involved in, successful in, rich in (vitamins), experienced in | Are you *interested in* learning Spanish? |\n| **on** | keen on (BrE), hooked on, dependent on, reliant on/upon | My brother is *keen on* playing the guitar. |\n| **of** | afraid of, capable of, fond of, proud of, ashamed of, tired of, jealous of, aware of, full of, short of | They’re *proud of* their heritage. • I’m *tired of* waiting. |\n| **with** | angry/annoyed/fed up with (a person/thing), bored with, obsessed with, pleased/delighted with, patient with, good with (people/things) | She’s *good with* children. • He’s *fed up with* the delays. |\n| **about** | angry/annoyed about, excited about, happy/unhappy about, worried/nervous/anxious about, certain/unsure about | We’re *excited about* our trip but a bit *worried about* the weather. |\n| **by** | surprised/shocked/amazed by, impressed by, bored by | The manager was *impressed by* your portfolio. |\n\n### Mini example bank\n\n- **to** She’s **addicted to** coffee; he’s **rude to** strangers; your drawing is **similar to** mine.\n- **for** Italy is **famous for** food; I’m **sorry for** the inconvenience.\n- **at** He’s **bad at** lying; she was **angry at** herself.\n- **in** They’re **interested in** robotics.\n- **on** She got **hooked on** a new series.\n- **of** Many kids are **afraid of** the dark; I’m **tired of** the routine.\n- **with** She was **angry with** him; they were **pleased with** the results.\n- **about** He’s **sorry about** the broken window; we’re **worried about** the forecast.\n\nRemember: after the preposition, use a noun or *-ing* form: *interested in learning*, *afraid of flying*, *keen on playing.*\n\n","module":6,"audioNumber":29,"audioTitle":"Burgers Made From Insects","speaker":"Jordan","audio":"https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/67aa2baa0c65412632c4b3d1/697759a686e83767e9645767_Burgers%20Made%20From%20Insects_.mp3","transcript":["Hi, I’m Jordan. Today I want to talk about a food idea that sounds strange to many people: burgers made from insects. In some countries, people have eaten insects for a long time, but in Europe and the US it is still unusual. Now, some companies are making insect burgers and trying to sell them as a modern, eco-friendly choice.","So, what are these burgers made of? Usually, the insects are dried and ground into a fine powder. Then the powder is mixed with other ingredients, like spices, vegetables, or grains, to create a patty. The goal is to make something that looks and tastes similar to a normal burger, but with a different source of protein.","One reason people talk about insect burgers is the environment. Some experts say insects need less land, less water, and less food than cows. They also produce fewer greenhouse gases. Because of this, insect-based food is sometimes presented as a possible solution for the future, especially as the world population grows.","Another reason is nutrition. Insects can be high in protein, and they may contain useful vitamins and minerals. Supporters say it’s a smart way to get protein without using so many natural resources.","But there are also problems. The biggest one is psychology. Many people feel disgusted by the idea, even if the burger doesn’t look like insects. There are also questions about allergies. People who are allergic to shellfish might react to insects too, so clear labeling is important. And, of course, people want to know if it is safe, how it is produced, and what rules companies follow.","Personally, I think insect burgers are an interesting experiment. Some people will never try them, and that’s normal. But others may be curious, especially if the taste is good and the price is reasonable. Maybe in the future it will feel normal—just like sushi or tofu became normal in many places over time."],"mappingNote":"Allergic to / similar to / curious and uncertain contexts for dependent adjective prepositions.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"This burger is similar ___ the one I tried before.","answer":"to","options":["to","with","at"],"why":"Similar takes to.","id":"g36-1"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"I’m interested ___ learning about sustainable food.","answer":"in","options":["in","on","for"],"why":"Interested in + noun or gerund.","id":"g36-2"},{"type":"gap","prompt":"The chef is responsible ___ the labels.","answer":"for","why":"Responsible for a task.","id":"g36-3"},{"type":"gap","prompt":"We are worried ___ possible allergies.","answer":"about","why":"Worried about a situation.","id":"g36-4"},{"type":"order","prompt":"Build a natural sentence. Use every word.","answer":"She is good at explaining new ideas.","why":"Good at describes a skill and takes a gerund.","id":"g36-5"},{"type":"edit","prompt":"Correct the sentence: They are proud for their project.","answer":"They are proud of their project.","why":"Proud of is the standard pair.","id":"g36-6"},{"type":"rewrite","prompt":"Rewrite with afraid of: Flying scares her. Start “She is”.","answer":"She is afraid of flying.","why":"Afraid of takes a noun or gerund.","id":"g36-7"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"“Good at” and “good for” differ because ___.","answer":"at describes skill and for describes benefit","options":["at describes skill and for describes benefit","both always describe skill","for always describes kindness to a person"],"why":"Dependent prepositions help determine the adjective meaning.","id":"g36-8"}],"listening":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"How are the insects prepared in the described process?","answer":"Dried and ground into powder","options":["Dried and ground into powder","Served alive in a bun","Only frozen whole"],"evidence":"dried and ground into a fine powder","id":"a29-1"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is one environmental argument mentioned?","answer":"Insects may need fewer resources than cows.","options":["Insects may need fewer resources than cows.","Insects need no resources at all.","All transport pollution disappears."],"evidence":"less land, less water, and less food","id":"a29-2"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why is clear labelling important?","answer":"Some people may have allergic reactions.","options":["Some people may have allergic reactions.","It makes all food cheaper.","It hides the ingredients."],"evidence":"People who are allergic to shellfish","id":"a29-3"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"Everyone is expected to try insect burgers.","answer":"False","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"Some people will never try them","id":"a29-4"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"Supporters mention protein as a benefit.","answer":"True","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"high in protein","id":"a29-5"},{"type":"sequence","prompt":"Put these events or ideas in the order they appear in the recording.","events":[["Jordan explains making the patties.","powder is mixed"],["He explains environmental arguments.","the environment"],["He describes concerns and allergies.","there are also problems"]],"answer":["Jordan explains making the patties.","He explains environmental arguments.","He describes concerns and allergies."],"evidence":"powder is mixed / the environment / there are also problems","id":"a29-6"}],"vocab":[{"id":"v29-1","word":"patty","definition":"a flat round portion used in a burger"},{"id":"v29-2","word":"protein","definition":"a nutrient important for body tissues"},{"id":"v29-3","word":"resource","definition":"something used to produce or support things"},{"id":"v29-4","word":"label","definition":"information attached to a product"},{"id":"v29-5","word":"reasonable","definition":"fair, sensible or not excessive"}],"writing":"Present a balanced view of a new food idea with benefits, concerns and uncertainty. Focus: Choose adjective-preposition pairs and follow them with nouns or gerunds.","speaking":"Explain the production process and discuss a personal preference without making health claims. Focus: Choose adjective-preposition pairs and follow them with nouns or gerunds."},{"n":37,"title":"Compound adjectives with numbers: a two-day trip","goal":"Use singular units and hyphens in number compounds.","theory":"\n### What is a compound adjective?\n\n- An adjective made of **two or more words** used *before* a noun: *a well-dressed man*, *a part-time job*, *a first-class ticket*.\n- We usually join the parts with a **hyphen** to show they form *one idea*.\n\nAdjectives can appear before a noun (*a fantastic idea*) or after *be*/sense verbs (*John looks tired*), but number-compounds are mainly used **before** a noun.\n\n### Core pattern: *number + singular measure noun + main noun*\n\n| **Pattern** | **Examples** |\n| --- | --- |\n| **[number]-[singular unit] + noun** | *a two-day trip* • *a five-minute walk* • *a 10-mile hike* • *a 50-metre pool* • *a 30-minute show* |\n| **Always use the unit in singular** | ✓ *a two-day journey* • ✗ *a two-days journey* |\n| **Hyphenate the parts** | ✓ *a three-hour flight* (one idea) • ✗ *a three hour flight* (can be ambiguous) |\n| **Decimals & fractions** | *a 2.5-hour meeting* • *a two-and-a-half-hour class* |\n\nUK/US spelling: *metre/litre* (BrE) vs *meter/liter* (AmE). Irregular plurals also stay singular: *a 20-foot wall* (not *feet*).\n\n### Age, money, & complex chains\n\n- **Age**: *a seven-year-old child* • *a two-year-old laptop*. (But after the noun: *He is two years old.*)\n- **Cost/quantity**: *a 10-million-dollar house* • *a three-kilogram bag* • *a 200-page book*.\n- **Ranges** (style choice): *a 10–12-minute talk* (en dash for 10–12).\n\n### Number + body part + *-ed*\n\n- *a three-legged table* • *a one-eyed alien* • *a four-armed robot* • *a seven-headed dragon*.\n- Double the final consonant when the last syllable is stressed and has C-V-C: *leg → legged*.\n\n### After the noun (predicate position)\n\n- We normally rephrase with ***be + number + plural unit + long*** or ***last(ed)***:  \n  ✓ *The flight was three hours long.* / *The meeting lasted two hours.*  \n  ✗ *The flight was three-hour.*\n- Possessive alternative (esp. BrE, no article): *We had three weeks’ holiday.* (not recommended at B1 for ages/cost).\n\n### Hyphen notes (good style)\n\n- No hyphen with adverbs in **-ly**: *a highly paid job* (but *a well-paid job* uses a hyphen).\n- Keep small numbers in words (*a two-day pass*) or use digits with units (*a 21-day visa*) — both are acceptable; be consistent on your page.\n- When a symbol/unit is attached, keep the hyphen: *a 10-km hike*, *a 5-GB file*.\n\n### Common mistakes\n\n- ✗ *a two-days trip* → ✓ **a two-day trip**.\n- ✗ *a three hour meeting* (no hyphen) → ✓ **a three-hour meeting**.\n- ✗ *The class is two-hour* → ✓ **The class is two hours long** / **It’s a two-hour class**.\n- ✗ Using plural units inside the compound: *a 20-feet container* → ✓ **a 20-foot container**.\n- ✗ Missing hyphens in longer chains: *a two and a half hour lesson* → ✓ **a two-and-a-half-hour lesson**.\n\n### Mini example bank\n\n- **travel** We took **a three-hour flight** to Rome. • They’re on **a two-day trip**.\n- **study** I have to write **a ten-page report** tonight.\n- **home** They bought **a two-bedroom apartment** with **a 50-metre pool**.\n- **tech** This phone has **an eight-core processor**.\n- **age** She’s **a two-year-old** toddler. • She is **two years old**.\n- **body** The statue shows **a one-eyed hero** and **a three-legged stool**.\n\n","module":6,"audioNumber":17,"audioTitle":"How do you keep fit?","speaker":"Chris","audio":"https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/67aa2baa0c65412632c4b3d1/6975cb76b0ff80ea782e87a7_SCRIPT%201%20(Male%20speaker%20%E2%80%94%20Chris)_%20How%20do%20you%20keep%20fit_%20(B1).mp3","transcript":["Hi, I’m Chris. I try to keep fit in simple ways because I don’t love strict gym routines. On weekdays, I walk a lot—usually 8,000 to 10,000 steps—because I get off the bus two stops early and walk the rest. Two evenings a week, I do a short workout at home: push-ups, squats, and planks. It’s only about 20 minutes, but I do it regularly, and that’s what matters. On Saturdays, I go cycling with a friend in a park near my apartment. The hardest part for me is motivation after a long day, so I keep everything easy and realistic. When I feel stressed, even a quick walk helps me clear my head and sleep better."],"mappingNote":"A short workout of twenty minutes becomes a twenty-minute workout in a follow-up.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"Choose the correct noun phrase.","answer":"a twenty-minute workout","options":["a twenty-minute workout","a twenty-minutes workout","a twenty minute-workout"],"why":"Singular unit minute; hyphen joins the compound.","id":"g37-1"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"After the noun: The hike was ___.","answer":"three hours long","options":["three hours long","three-hour","three-hours long"],"why":"Predicate wording normally uses plural units + long.","id":"g37-2"},{"type":"gap","prompt":"We booked a two-___ trip. (day/days)","answer":"day","why":"The unit stays singular inside the adjective.","id":"g37-3"},{"type":"gap","prompt":"They built a twenty-___ wall. (foot/feet)","answer":"foot","why":"Even irregular unit nouns stay singular.","id":"g37-4"},{"type":"order","prompt":"Build a natural sentence. Use every word.","answer":"She bought a two-year-old laptop.","why":"Age compound before a noun uses singular year and hyphens.","id":"g37-5"},{"type":"edit","prompt":"Correct the sentence: It was a two and a half hour lesson.","answer":"It was a two-and-a-half-hour lesson.","why":"Hyphenate all parts of the number compound.","id":"g37-6"},{"type":"rewrite","prompt":"Rewrite using a number compound: The walk lasts five minutes. Start “It is”.","answer":"It is a five-minute walk.|It’s a five-minute walk.","why":"Use singular minute and a hyphen before the main noun.","id":"g37-7"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which phrase describes a child’s age correctly?","answer":"a seven-year-old child","options":["a seven-year-old child","a seven-years-old child","a seven year-olds child"],"why":"The unit year stays singular inside the compound adjective.","id":"g37-8"}],"listening":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"How does Chris add walking to his commute?","answer":"He gets off the bus two stops early.","options":["He gets off the bus two stops early.","He runs beside the train.","He walks only on Sundays."],"evidence":"two stops early","id":"a17-1"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How long is his home workout?","answer":"About twenty minutes","options":["About twenty minutes","Two hours","Five minutes"],"evidence":"about 20 minutes","id":"a17-2"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is his biggest difficulty?","answer":"Motivation after a long day","options":["Motivation after a long day","Buying exercise equipment","Finding a park"],"evidence":"motivation after a long day","id":"a17-3"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"He cycles with a friend on Saturdays.","answer":"True","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"On Saturdays, I go cycling with a friend","id":"a17-4"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"He loves strict gym routines.","answer":"False","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"I don’t love strict gym routines","id":"a17-5"},{"type":"sequence","prompt":"Put these events or ideas in the order they appear in the recording.","events":[["He describes weekday walking.","On weekdays"],["He describes evening workouts.","Two evenings a week"],["He describes Saturday cycling.","On Saturdays"]],"answer":["He describes weekday walking.","He describes evening workouts.","He describes Saturday cycling."],"evidence":"On weekdays / Two evenings a week / On Saturdays","id":"a17-6"}],"vocab":[{"id":"v17-1","word":"keep fit","definition":"maintain physical health and condition"},{"id":"v17-2","word":"workout","definition":"a session of physical exercise"},{"id":"v17-3","word":"regularly","definition":"at repeated or consistent intervals"},{"id":"v17-4","word":"realistic","definition":"practical and achievable"},{"id":"v17-5","word":"clear my head","definition":"reduce mental stress or confusion"}],"writing":"Design a realistic weekly activity routine and explain how to maintain it. Focus: Use singular units and hyphens in number compounds.","speaking":"Discuss ways to make a healthy habit easier to continue. Focus: Use singular units and hyphens in number compounds."},{"n":38,"title":"B1 Phrasal verbs — form, types, and core meanings","goal":"Recognise phrasal verb meanings and place pronoun objects correctly.","theory":"\n### What is a phrasal verb?\n\n- A verb + one or more **particles** (adverb/preposition) that work as a single unit: *carry out*, *get on with*, *give in*.\n- Meaning is often **idiomatic** (not the same as the base verb).\n\n### Types & word order (very important at B1)\n\n| **Type** | **Object?** | **Word order** | **Example** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Intransitive** | No object | — | *The meeting is over.* • *Our car broke down.* |\n| **Transitive, inseparable** | Needs object | Verb + particle + object (even with pronouns) | *deal with* a problem / *deal with it* • *get rid of* it |\n| **Transitive, separable** | Needs object | Verb + object + particle or verb + particle + object.<br>**Pronouns** go in the middle. | *carry out* the plan = *carry* the plan *out* • *carry it out* (✓) not ✗ *carry out it* |\n| **Three-word verbs** | Usually inseparable | Verb + particle + preposition + object | *get on with* your work • *get rid of* old papers |\n\nTransport pattern: we **get on/off** buses, trains, planes; we **get in/out of** cars and taxis.\n\n### Reference list (A–G)\n\n- **be over** **(intr.)** — finished. *The film is over at ten.*\n- **break down** **(intr.)** — stop working / lose control emotionally. *The lift broke down.*\n- **break in / break into** **(intr./tr.)** — enter by force (usually to steal). *They broke into a shop.*\n- **break up (with)** **(intr.)** — end a relationship. *They broke up last year.*\n- **bring up** **(tr., separable)** — raise a child; often passive. *She was brought up in Spain.*\n- **call for (sb)** **(tr., inseparable)** — go to collect someone (BrE). *I’ll call for you at 7.* Also “demand/require”: *This calls for action.*\n- **carry on (doing)** **(intr.)** — continue. *Carry on reading.*\n- **carry out** **(tr., separable)** — perform/execute. *The lab carried out tests.* Passive common: *The plan was carried out.*\n- **check out (of)** **(intr.)** — leave a hotel after paying. *We checked out at noon.* Informal: “check out *sth*” = look at/examine.\n- **come on!** **(intr.)** — hurry/encourage; sometimes disbelief. *Come on! You can do it!*\n- **cross out** **(tr., separable)** — draw a line through. *Cross it out.*\n- **cut up** **(tr., separable)** — cut into pieces. *Cut up the vegetables.*\n- **deal with** **(tr., inseparable)** — give attention to / handle. *I’ll deal with the emails.*\n- **depend on** **(tr., inseparable)** — be decided/affected by; need support. *It depends on the price.* • *She depends on her parents.*\n- **end up** **(intr.)** — finally arrive/finish (often unexpectedly). Patterns: *end up + -ing / in/at + place / as + noun*. *We ended up taking a taxi.*\n\n### Reference list (G–Z)\n\n- **fill in** **(tr., separable)** — complete a form. *Fill in the blanks.* AmE: often “fill out”.\n- **fill up** **(tr./intr.)** — make completely full. *Fill up the tank.*\n- **find out** **(tr., separable)** — discover/learn. *I’ll find it out / I’ll find out the truth.*\n- **get along (with)** **(intr.)** — have a friendly relationship. *Do you get along with your neighbours?*\n- **get back** **(intr.)** — return. *What time did you get back?*\n- **get down** **(intr./tr.)** — lower your body; also “write sth”: *Get it down before you forget.*\n- **get in** **(intr.)** — enter/arrive (often with difficulty or for transport). *The train gets in at 6.*\n- **get off** **(intr.)** — leave a bus/train/plane. *We got off at the museum.*\n- **get on (with)** **(intr.)** — start/continue doing; show progress; also board a bus/train. *Let’s get on with the project.* • *How are you getting on?*\n- **get rid of** **(3-word, inseparable)** — remove/eliminate. *We must get rid of this old sofa.*\n- **give back** **(tr., separable)** — return to the owner. *Give it back to her.*\n- **give in** **(intr.)** — surrender/stop resisting. *They finally gave in.* BrE also “give/hand in” = submit work: *Give your forms in by Friday.*\n- **give out** **(tr., separable)** — distribute. *They gave out flyers.* Also “stop working”: *My torch gave out.*\n\n### Common mistakes & good style\n\n- Pronoun position with separable verbs: ✓ *carry it out* / ✗ *carry out it*.\n- Using the wrong preposition: ✓ *depend on* / ✗ *depend of*.\n- Transport: ✓ *get on/off* a bus; ✓ *get in/out of* a car.\n- Register: *come on!* is informal; avoid in formal writing.\n- Spelling as nouns: many have hyphenated noun forms: *a break-in*, *a break-up*, *the check-out time*.\n\nMany phrasal verbs have **more than one meaning**. The senses above are the core B1 meanings you’ll meet most often.\n\n","module":7,"audioNumber":1,"audioTitle":"Stories of relationships","speaker":"Chris","audio":"https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/67aa2baa0c65412632c4b3d1/6972587ca943dc2732102e58_SCRIPT%201%20(Male%20speaker%20%E2%80%94%20Chris)_%20Stories%20of%20relationships%20(B1).mp3","transcript":["Hi, I’m Chris. I want to tell you about a relationship that changed me a lot—my first serious relationship. We met at work, and at the beginning everything felt easy. We had similar interests, we laughed a lot, and we spent most weekends together. After a few months, we started arguing about small things: time, plans, and how often we should see each other. I wanted more space, and she wanted more attention.","At first, we didn’t talk about it clearly. We just got annoyed and stayed quiet. Later we tried to communicate better. We made simple rules: one evening a week for our own hobbies, and one day for friends and family. It helped for a while, but in the end we wanted different lifestyles. We broke up, and it was painful, but it taught me something important: love is not only feelings. It’s also communication, respect, and making time for each other."],"mappingNote":"Explicit broke up; provides separability, relationship and problem-solving review.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"The relationship ended. They ___.","answer":"broke up","options":["broke up","brought up it","filled up"],"why":"Break up means end a relationship.","id":"g38-1"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"The instruction is wrong. Please ___.","answer":"cross it out","options":["cross it out","cross out it","cross it of"],"why":"Pronoun objects go between a separable verb and particle.","id":"g38-2"},{"type":"gap","prompt":"The car broke ___ on the way to work.","answer":"down","why":"Break down means stop working.","id":"g38-3"},{"type":"gap","prompt":"We need to deal ___ the problem.","answer":"with","why":"Deal with is inseparable.","id":"g38-4"},{"type":"order","prompt":"Build a natural sentence. Use every word.","answer":"They carried out the plan successfully.","why":"Carry out means execute or perform.","id":"g38-5"},{"type":"edit","prompt":"Correct the sentence: Please fill in it before Friday.","answer":"Please fill it in before Friday.","why":"Fill in is separable; pronoun goes in the middle.","id":"g38-6"},{"type":"rewrite","prompt":"Use a pronoun instead of “the form”: Please fill the form in.","answer":"Please fill it in.","why":"With separable fill in, pronoun it goes in the middle.","id":"g38-7"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"“We ended up taking a taxi.” What does ended up add?","answer":"This was the eventual outcome.","options":["This was the eventual outcome.","This was the only original plan.","The taxi stopped working."],"why":"End up often introduces an unexpected final result.","id":"g38-8"}],"listening":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"Where did Chris meet his first serious partner?","answer":"At work","options":["At work","At university","On a hiking trip"],"evidence":"We met at work","id":"a1-1"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What did Chris want more of?","answer":"Personal space","options":["Personal space","Expensive holidays","Attention"],"evidence":"I wanted more space","id":"a1-2"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why did they eventually break up?","answer":"They wanted different lifestyles.","options":["They wanted different lifestyles.","One of them moved abroad.","They stopped seeing their friends."],"evidence":"we wanted different lifestyles","id":"a1-3"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"Their new rules helped permanently.","answer":"False","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"It helped for a while","id":"a1-4"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"Chris believes communication is part of love.","answer":"True","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"It’s also communication","id":"a1-5"},{"type":"sequence","prompt":"Put these events or ideas in the order they appear in the recording.","events":[["They enjoyed similar interests.","We had similar interests"],["They created rules about time.","We made simple rules"],["They separated.","We broke up"]],"answer":["They enjoyed similar interests.","They created rules about time.","They separated."],"evidence":"We had similar interests / We made simple rules / We broke up","id":"a1-6"}],"vocab":[{"id":"v1-1","word":"argue","definition":"disagree with someone, often emotionally"},{"id":"v1-2","word":"attention","definition":"time and interest given to someone"},{"id":"v1-3","word":"annoyed","definition":"slightly angry or irritated"},{"id":"v1-4","word":"break up","definition":"end a romantic relationship"},{"id":"v1-5","word":"respect","definition":"consideration for another person’s needs"}],"writing":"Write to a friend about a disagreement and a constructive way to handle it. Focus: Recognise phrasal verb meanings and place pronoun objects correctly.","speaking":"Explain what helps people balance time together and personal space. Focus: Recognise phrasal verb meanings and place pronoun objects correctly."},{"n":39,"title":"B1 Phrasal verbs","goal":"Use common phrasal verbs for home, work and plans.","theory":"\n### Quick rules you’ll need\n\n- **Types**: intransitive (no object), transitive *inseparable* (verb + particle + object), transitive *separable* (verb + object + particle / verb + particle + object). Three-word verbs are usually inseparable.\n- **Pronouns** with separable verbs go in the middle: ✓ *hand it in*, ✓ *pick her up*, ✗ *hand in it*.\n- **Patterns** you’ll see below: *go on = continue*; *look forward to + -ing*; *keep on + -ing*.\n- **Register**: some are informal (*hang out*, *come on!*).\n\n### Phrasal verbs\n\n| **#** | **Phrasal verb** | **Type** | **Core meaning / pattern** | **Example** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 31 | **give up** | tr., separable | stop doing/taking | I’m going to *give up* sugar. |\n| 32 | **give way** | intr. | let another vehicle go first (BrE road sign) | The car didn’t *give way* to the ambulance. |\n| 33 | **go for** | tr., inseparable | try to get/achieve; fixed phrase *go for it!* | You should *go for* the job. |\n| 34 | **go off** | intr. | stop working / alarm starts / bomb explodes; BrE also *food goes off* = spoils | The alarm *went off* at six. |\n| 35 | **go on** | intr. | happen / continue | Be quiet—an exam is *going on*. |\n| 36 | **go out** | intr. | leave home for fun; also “*go out with*” = date (tr., inseparable) | We’re *going out* tonight. |\n| 37 | **go with** | tr., inseparable | look/taste good together | This wine *goes with* the fish. |\n| 38 | **go together** | intr. | look good together | Blue and gold *go together*. |\n| 39 | **grow up** | intr. | become an adult (not \\*grow someone up\\*) | She *grew up* in the countryside. |\n| 40 | **hand in** | tr., separable | submit to a person in authority | Please *hand in* your essays. |\n\n### Phrasal verbs\n\n| **#** | **Phrasal verb** | **Type** | **Core meaning / pattern** | **Example** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 41 | **hand out** | tr., separable | give one to each person | The teacher *handed out* the exams. |\n| 42 | **hang out** | intr. | spend time (informal) *hang out with sb* | We *hang out* after work. |\n| 43 | **hang up** | intr./tr., separable | end a phone call | Sorry, I must *hang up*. |\n| 44 | **hold up** | tr. | delay; (extra) rob with a weapon (not B1) | Traffic *held us up*. |\n| 45 | **keep in** | tr., separable | make someone stay inside | The doctor *kept* him *in* overnight. |\n| 46 | **keep on** | intr./tr. | *keep on + -ing* = continue | She *kept on talking*. |\n| 47 | **keep up** | intr./tr. | continue doing well; (contrast) *keep up with* = maintain same speed | *Keep up* the good work! |\n| 48 | **knock down** | tr., separable | hit so they fall; (extra) demolish a building | A bus *knocked* him *down*. |\n| 49 | **lie down** | intr. | move into a horizontal position to rest | I’ll *lie down* for a bit. |\n| 50 | **look after** | tr., inseparable | take care of | She *looks after* her grandma. |\n\n### Phrasal verbs\n\n| **#** | **Phrasal verb** | **Type** | **Core meaning / pattern** | **Example** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 51 | **look for** | tr., inseparable | try to find / try to get | She’s *looking for* a job. |\n| 52 | **look forward to** | 3-word, inseparable | **+ noun / + -ing** (not bare infinitive) | I *look forward to* meeting you. |\n| 53 | **look out!** | intr. (imperative) | warn of danger | *Look out!* There’s a bike. |\n| 54 | **look up** | tr., separable | check information (book/site/list) | *Look it up* online. |\n| 55 | **pass on** | tr., separable | give something to the next person | Please *pass* this message *on*. |\n| 56 | **pick up** | tr., separable | collect someone/something (often by car) | I’ll *pick you up* at 8. |\n| 57 | **put away** | tr., separable | return to the usual place | *Put* the toys *away*. |\n| 58 | **put down** | tr., separable | write your name/details on a list | *Put* my name *down* for tennis. |\n| 59 | **put off** | tr., separable | postpone to a later time; passive *be put off (by)* = be discouraged | We *put off* the meeting until Tuesday. |\n| 60 | **put on** | tr., separable | wear / switch on / gain weight | She *puts on* make-up every morning. |\n\n**Do not confuse:** *look up* (search) vs *look up to* (admire, 3-word, not in this list); *keep up* vs *keep up with* (same speed); *go off* (alarm/explode) vs *go out* (leave / light stops burning).\n\n","module":7,"audioNumber":3,"audioTitle":"Moving to a new apartment and starting over","speaker":"Elena","audio":"https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/67aa2baa0c65412632c4b3d1/6975d3adafd5a9c2c43b0346_SCRIPT%20(Female%20speaker%20%E2%80%94%20Elena)_%20Moving%20to%20a%20new%20apartment%20and%20starting%20over%20(B1).mp3","transcript":["Hi, I’m Elena. Recently, I moved to a new apartment, and it felt like a fresh start for me. I didn’t move to a different city, but even changing neighborhoods made a big difference. My old place was small and noisy. The street was busy, and at night I could hear cars and people outside. I got used to it, but I was always tired in the morning.","When I found the new apartment, it wasn’t perfect, but it had two things I really wanted: more light and more quiet. The first week was stressful. I had boxes everywhere, and I couldn’t find simple things like my phone charger or a clean towel. I also realized I had too many unnecessary items. So I started sorting my stuff. I donated some clothes, threw away broken things, and kept only what I actually use.","Now I’m slowly making the space feel like home. I bought a small desk because I sometimes work online, and I put a plant near the window. I also made a simple routine: I wake up, open the curtains, and drink coffee while I plan my day. It sounds small, but it helps me feel calm and organized.","The best part is that this move pushed me to change other habits too. I started walking more, cooking at home more often, and spending less time on my phone in the evening. I still have a lot to do—like hanging pictures and choosing a lamp—but I enjoy the process. For me, moving wasn’t only about a new apartment. It was about creating a better daily life, step by step."],"mappingNote":"Choosing and organising a home supplies look for / put away / put off applications.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"Elena wants a lamp and is ___ one online.","answer":"looking for","options":["looking for","looking after","looking forward"],"why":"Look for means try to find.","id":"g39-1"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"The meeting was postponed. They ___ until Tuesday.","answer":"put it off","options":["put it off","put off it","put it on"],"why":"Put off is separable and means postpone.","id":"g39-2"},{"type":"gap","prompt":"Please hand ___ your report by six.","answer":"in","why":"Hand in means submit.","id":"g39-3"},{"type":"gap","prompt":"We look forward to ___ you. (see)","answer":"seeing","why":"Look forward to takes a noun or gerund.","id":"g39-4"},{"type":"order","prompt":"Build a natural sentence. Use every word.","answer":"I will pick you up after work.","why":"Pick up + pronoun: pick you up.","id":"g39-5"},{"type":"edit","prompt":"Correct the sentence: She grew her children up in London.","answer":"She brought her children up in London.","why":"Bring up is transitive raise; grow up is intransitive become an adult.","id":"g39-6"},{"type":"rewrite","prompt":"Use a pronoun instead of “my sister”: I will pick my sister up.","answer":"I will pick her up.|I’ll pick her up.","why":"Pick up is separable; her goes between the parts.","id":"g39-7"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which sentence uses look forward to correctly?","answer":"I look forward to seeing you.","options":["I look forward to seeing you.","I look forward to see you.","I look forward seeing to you."],"why":"To is a preposition, so it takes a gerund.","id":"g39-8"}],"listening":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"Did Elena move to another city?","answer":"No, she changed neighbourhoods.","options":["No, she changed neighbourhoods.","Yes, she moved overseas.","The transcript does not say."],"evidence":"I didn’t move to a different city","id":"a3-1"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which two things did she want in her new home?","answer":"More light and quiet","options":["More light and quiet","A bigger television and pool","A cheaper car and garden"],"evidence":"more light and more quiet","id":"a3-2"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What did she do with some clothes?","answer":"Donated them","options":["Donated them","Sold all of them","Stored them at work"],"evidence":"I donated some clothes","id":"a3-3"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"Elena has finished every home-improvement task.","answer":"False","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"I still have a lot to do","id":"a3-4"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"She started spending less time on her phone in the evening.","answer":"True","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"spending less time on my phone in the evening","id":"a3-5"},{"type":"sequence","prompt":"Put these events or ideas in the order they appear in the recording.","events":[["She found a new apartment.","When I found the new apartment"],["She sorted unnecessary items.","I started sorting my stuff"],["She created a morning routine.","I also made a simple routine"]],"answer":["She found a new apartment.","She sorted unnecessary items.","She created a morning routine."],"evidence":"When I found the new apartment / I started sorting my stuff / I also made a simple routine","id":"a3-6"}],"vocab":[{"id":"v3-1","word":"fresh start","definition":"a chance to begin again in a better way"},{"id":"v3-2","word":"neighbourhood","definition":"an area within a town or city"},{"id":"v3-3","word":"sort","definition":"organise things into groups"},{"id":"v3-4","word":"donate","definition":"give something to help others"},{"id":"v3-5","word":"routine","definition":"a regular way of doing things"}],"writing":"Describe a change to your home and how it affected your daily life. Focus: Use common phrasal verbs for home, work and plans.","speaking":"Recommend three small changes that could make a home calmer. Focus: Use common phrasal verbs for home, work and plans."},{"n":40,"title":"B1 Phrasal Verbs 61–90 — form, meaning & usage","goal":"Use phrasal verbs for starting, changing, running out and recording.","theory":"\n### How phrasal verbs work\n\n- **Form** **verb + particle** (adverb/preposition): *turn on, run out (of), set up*.\n- **Separable vs. inseparable** Some can split with the object: *turn the light off* / *turn it off*. With pronouns, the particle must go *after* the pronoun: **turn it off** (not \\*turn off it).\n- **Three-word verbs** such as *run out of* are normally inseparable. *Turn into* has two words and is also inseparable in the meaning “become”.\n- **Transitivity** Some take an object (*write down* the address), some don’t (*set off* at 7).\n- **Tense** Conjugate the main verb as usual: *took off*, *has set up*, *is working out*.\n\nLegend in tables: **Sep.**=separable, **Insep.**=inseparable, **—**=no object / intransitive.\n\n### 61–70\n\n| **#** | **Verb** | **Meaning** | **Example** | **Sep.?** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 61 | **put out** | extinguish | Put out that cigarette. | Sep. |\n| 62 | **put (sb) through** | connect by phone | Can you put me through to the manager? | Sep. |\n| 63 | **put up** | increase (price/cost) | They put up the price last week. | Sep. |\n| 64 | **ring back** | return a call | I’ll ring you back later. | Sep. |\n| 65 | **ring up** | phone | She rang me up to say hello. | Sep. |\n| 66 | **run out (of)** | finish a supply | We’ve run out of milk. | Insep. (of + noun) |\n| 67 | **set off** | start a journey | We set off at dawn. | — |\n| 68 | **set out (to)** | begin with an aim | He set out to write a novel. | — |\n| 69 | **set up** | start/establish | They set up a tech startup. | Sep. |\n| 70 | **show up** | arrive/appear | He didn’t show up to the party. | — |\n\n### 71–80\n\n| **#** | **Verb** | **Meaning** | **Example** | **Sep.?** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 71 | **sit down** | take a seat | Please sit down. | — |\n| 72 | **split up (with)** | end a relationship | They split up last year. | — |\n| 73 | **stay behind** | remain after others leave | He stayed behind to help. | — |\n| 74 | **take off** (clothes) | remove | Take your shoes off / take off your shoes. | Sep. |\n| 75 | **take off** (plane) | start flying | The flight took off on time. | — |\n| 76 | **take up** | start a hobby/activity | She took up yoga. | Sep. |\n| 77 | **throw away** | discard | Don’t throw it away. | Sep. |\n| 78 | **tidy up** | put things in order | Please tidy up your room. | Often sep. |\n| 79 | **try on** | test clothing | Try these jeans on. | Sep. |\n| 80 | **turn down** | decrease (level/volume) | Could you turn the music down? | Sep. |\n\n### 81–90\n\n| **#** | **Verb** | **Meaning** | **Example** | **Sep.?** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| 81 | **turn into** | become / change to | The rain turned into snow. | Insep. (+ into) |\n| 82 | **turn off** | make sth stop working | Turn the TV off / turn off the TV. | Sep. |\n| 83 | **turn on** | make sth start working | She turned on the radio. | Sep. |\n| 84 | **turn up** | increase level/volume | Turn it up a little. | Sep. |\n| 85 | **wake (up)** | stop sleeping / make sb stop | They woke me up at six. | Sep. (trans.) / — (intrans.) |\n| 86 | **wash up** | wash hands/face; (BrE) wash the dishes | Go wash up before dinner. | — / often intrans. |\n| 87 | **wear out** | use until unusable | Running wore my shoes out. | Sep. |\n| 88 | **work out** | do physical exercise | I work out three times a week. | — |\n| 89 | **wrap up** | cover / package | Please wrap it up — it’s a gift. | Sep. |\n| 90 | **write down** | record on paper | Write down the address. | Sep. |\n\n**Pronoun rule** With separable verbs, pronouns sit in the middle: *turn **it** off*, *pick **them** up*.\n\n","module":7,"audioNumber":23,"audioTitle":"Social Media Billionaires","speaker":"Alex","audio":"https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/67aa2baa0c65412632c4b3d1/69760de39b53bdf7dbf45b9b_Social%20Media%20Billionaires_.mp3","transcript":["Hi, I’m Alex. When people talk about new, modern billionaires, many of them made their fortune from social networks and online communities.","A good example is Mark Zuckerberg. He started Facebook as a small project, and it later grew into a huge company with different apps and services. Another example is Evan Spiegel, one of the creators of Snapchat. His idea was simple: people wanted quick photos and messages that feel private and fun.","There are other stories too. Jack Dorsey helped build Twitter, and later he worked on payment technology as well. And in recent years, TikTok became a global trend. Its parent company is ByteDance, founded by Zhang Yiming. A product like that can grow very fast because people share videos every day.","What do these stories have in common? First, they built something people use daily. Second, they grew through sharing and strong communities. And third, they earned money through ads, partnerships, and sometimes subscriptions.","But it’s not only success. Social networks also bring big problems: privacy, fake news, addiction, and pressure from governments. In the future, new billionaires may come from creator tools, short video, and AI features inside social apps. The main lesson is simple: build something useful, and protect trust—because trust is the real long-term value."],"mappingNote":"Company growth offers set up / turn into applications; not every target phrase occurs in source.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"They started a company. They ___.","answer":"set it up","options":["set it up","set up it","show it up"],"why":"Set up means establish; pronoun goes in the middle.","id":"g40-1"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"We have no paper left. We have ___.","answer":"run out of paper","options":["run out of paper","run paper out of","run of paper out"],"why":"Run out of is inseparable.","id":"g40-2"},{"type":"gap","prompt":"Could you turn the music ___? It is too loud.","answer":"down","why":"Turn down means decrease volume.","id":"g40-3"},{"type":"gap","prompt":"Please write ___ the meeting address.","answer":"down","why":"Write down means record in writing.","id":"g40-4"},{"type":"order","prompt":"Build a natural sentence. Use every word.","answer":"Do not throw those notes away.","why":"Throw away can be split by a noun object.","id":"g40-5"},{"type":"edit","prompt":"Correct the sentence: She turned off it after the call.","answer":"She turned it off after the call.","why":"Turn off is separable; it goes between verb and particle.","id":"g40-6"},{"type":"rewrite","prompt":"Use a pronoun instead of “the radio”: Please turn the radio down.","answer":"Please turn it down.","why":"Turn down is separable.","id":"g40-7"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does “We have run out of paper” mean?","answer":"No paper remains in our supply.","options":["No paper remains in our supply.","We have left a paper factory.","We have written on one piece."],"why":"Run out of expresses depletion of a supply.","id":"g40-8"}],"listening":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"Who does Alex identify as a creator of Snapchat?","answer":"Evan Spiegel","options":["Evan Spiegel","Jack Dorsey","Zhang Yiming"],"evidence":"Evan Spiegel","id":"a23-1"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which company is TikTok’s parent in this recording?","answer":"ByteDance","options":["ByteDance","Snapchat","Facebook"],"evidence":"ByteDance","id":"a23-2"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does Alex describe as long-term value?","answer":"Trust","options":["Trust","Only follower numbers","Only fast growth"],"evidence":"trust is the real long-term value","id":"a23-3"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"The recording says every social network earns only through subscriptions.","answer":"False","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"ads, partnerships, and sometimes subscriptions","id":"a23-4"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"Privacy is mentioned as a problem.","answer":"True","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"privacy, fake news, addiction","id":"a23-5"},{"type":"sequence","prompt":"Put these events or ideas in the order they appear in the recording.","events":[["Alex gives Facebook as an example.","Mark Zuckerberg"],["He describes Snapchat.","Evan Spiegel"],["He discusses risks and trust.","But it’s not only success"]],"answer":["Alex gives Facebook as an example.","He describes Snapchat.","He discusses risks and trust."],"evidence":"Mark Zuckerberg / Evan Spiegel / But it’s not only success","id":"a23-6"}],"vocab":[{"id":"v23-1","word":"fortune","definition":"a large amount of wealth"},{"id":"v23-2","word":"community","definition":"a group connected by shared interests or location"},{"id":"v23-3","word":"partnership","definition":"an arrangement to work together"},{"id":"v23-4","word":"privacy","definition":"control over personal information"},{"id":"v23-5","word":"trust","definition":"belief that someone or something is reliable"}],"writing":"Discuss one useful social-media feature, one risk and a way to protect trust. Focus: Use phrasal verbs for starting, changing, running out and recording.","speaking":"Explain why a digital product may grow and why popularity is not enough. Focus: Use phrasal verbs for starting, changing, running out and recording."},{"n":41,"title":"Would rather & Would sooner — expressing preferences","goal":"Express personal preferences and tactful preferences about another person.","theory":"\n### Quick overview\n\n- **would rather** (= **would sooner**) expresses what we prefer *now or in the future*. *I’d rather sit inside.*\n- **Two core patterns**:  \n  1) **would rather/sooner + bare infinitive** (*I’d rather stay*) — *my* preference for *my* action.  \n  2) **would rather/sooner + subject + past simple** (*I’d rather you went*) — preference about *someone else’s* action.\n- **Contractions** are normal in speech/writing: *I’d rather… / I’d sooner…*\n- **would sooner** is less common and a little more formal/literary, but the meaning is the same.\n\n### Form 1 — *would rather / would sooner + bare infinitive*\n\n| **Pattern** | **Examples** |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Affirmative**<br>**S + ’d rather/sooner + V** | *I’d rather go* by taxi. • *She’d rather stay* home. |\n| **Negative**<br>**S + ’d rather/sooner not + V** | *I’d rather not have* chicken again. |\n| **Questions**<br>**Would + S + rather/sooner + V?** | *Would you rather* sit inside? |\n| **Short reply** | “Come to the cinema?” — “*I’d rather not.*” |\n\n**No “to”** and no *-ing* after *rather/sooner*: ✓ *I’d rather stay* • ✗ *I’d rather to stay* • ✗ *I’d rather staying*.\n\n### Form 2 — *would rather / would sooner + subject + past simple*\n\n| **Meaning** | **Examples** |\n| --- | --- |\n| Preference about another person’s action (present/future meaning) | *I’d rather you went* home now. • *They’d rather their daughter studied* medicine. |\n| **Negative** with *didn’t* | *I’d rather you didn’t* smoke here. |\n| **Questions** | *Would you rather he came* with us? • *Would you rather I waited* here? |\n| **Short reply** | “Shall I open the window?” — “*I’d rather you didn’t.*” |\n\nUse this pattern only when the subjects are **different**: ✓ *I’d rather we left* / ✓ *I’d rather you left* • ✗ *I’d rather I waited* → say *I’d rather wait*.\n\n### *rather than* for contrasts\n\n- After *would rather* we often compare choices with **than** or **rather than**:  \n  ✓ *I’d rather take a taxi than drive.*  \n  ✓ *I’d rather stay in rather than go out.*\n- **Parallel forms**: keep the same structure on both sides (verb with verb, noun with noun): *I’d rather read than watch* / *rather tea than coffee*.\n- At the start of a clause, *rather than* is often followed by **-ing** in careful style: *Rather than driving, we took a taxi.*\n\n### Compare: *would rather/sooner* vs *would prefer / prefer*\n\n| **Expression** | **Form** | **Example** | **Notes** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **would rather/sooner** | **’d rather + V** | I’d rather sit inside. | Modal-like; no *to*. |\n| **would prefer** | **would prefer + to-infinitive** | I’d prefer to sit inside. | Same meaning, different grammar. |\n| **prefer** (general taste) | **prefer + -ing / noun (+ to …)** | I prefer reading to watching TV. | Habit/general preference (present simple). |\n| **would rather + sb + past** | **’d rather + S + past** | I’d rather you came early. | Present/future meaning about another person. |\n\n### Common mistakes\n\n- ✗ *I’d rather to stay* / ✗ *I’d rather staying* → ✓ **I’d rather stay**.\n- ✗ *I’d rather I waited here* → ✓ **I’d rather wait here** / ✓ **I’d rather we waited**.\n- ✗ Negative order with Form 2: *I’d rather not you came* → ✓ **I’d rather you didn’t come**.\n- Mixing structures: keep forms parallel after *than / rather than*.\n\nAll examples work with **would sooner** as well: *I’d sooner stay*, *I’d sooner you went*. It is less frequent but perfectly correct.\n\n### Mini example bank\n\n- **self** *I’d rather* cook at home *than* eat out.\n- **negative** *We’d rather not* travel in August.\n- **other people** *I’d rather you* called me tomorrow.\n- **short reply** “Can I open the window?” — “*I’d rather you didn’t.*”\n","module":7,"audioNumber":24,"audioTitle":"Why Coffee Shops Became So Popular Worldwide","speaker":"Natalie","audio":"https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/67aa2baa0c65412632c4b3d1/6975ed1d4e7be7358d249eae_Why%20Coffee%20Shops%20Became%20So%20Popular%20Worldwide.mp3","transcript":["Hi, I’m Natalie. Today I want to talk about something we see almost everywhere: coffee shops. In many cities, it feels like there is a café on every corner. In my opinion, coffee shops became so popular worldwide not only because of coffee, but because of the experience and the lifestyle they offer.","First, coffee shops are comfortable “third places.” Home is the first place, work or school is the second, and a café becomes the third. People go there to relax, meet friends, or simply sit alone with a book. The atmosphere is usually warm: soft music, nice smells, and a calm feeling. Even if you are busy, you can take a short break and feel better in ten minutes.","Second, coffee shops are useful for modern life. Many people work remotely now, so they need a place with Wi-Fi, a table, and a bit of background noise. A café gives you that without the pressure of an office. You can answer emails, study, or prepare for an exam. Some cafés even design their space for this: they have sockets, bigger tables, and quiet corners.","Another reason is social media. Coffee drinks look beautiful. People love taking photos of cappuccino foam, colorful lattes, and desserts. Cafés often understand this and create “Instagram-friendly” spaces. This makes the café popular online, and then more people want to visit it in real life.","Finally, coffee culture has become international. Years ago, many people drank simple instant coffee at home. Now we have espresso, latte, flat white, cold brew, and many other options. People like to try new tastes and feel a little “modern” and global.","So, I think coffee shops went global because they are more than a place to drink coffee. They are places to rest, work, connect, and feel part of city life."],"mappingNote":"Café preferences are a follow-up speaking context; would rather does not occur in the supplied recording.","tasks":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"I’d rather ___ in a quiet café.","answer":"sit","options":["sit","to sit","sitting"],"why":"Would rather takes a bare infinitive.","id":"g41-1"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"I’d prefer ___ near a socket.","answer":"to sit","options":["to sit","sit to","sitting to"],"why":"Would prefer takes a to-infinitive.","id":"g41-2"},{"type":"gap","prompt":"I’d rather you ___ music here. (not play; past simple)","answer":"didn’t play|did not play","why":"Another person’s action uses subject + past simple.","id":"g41-3"},{"type":"gap","prompt":"We’d rather ___ travel at rush hour.","answer":"not","why":"Same subject: would rather not + base verb.","id":"g41-4"},{"type":"order","prompt":"Build a natural sentence. Use every word.","answer":"I would rather read than watch television.","why":"Keep parallel bare verbs after rather … than.","id":"g41-5"},{"type":"edit","prompt":"Correct the sentence: I’d rather to work at home.","answer":"I’d rather work at home.","why":"No to after would rather.","id":"g41-6"},{"type":"rewrite","prompt":"Rewrite using would prefer: I’d rather sit near the window.","answer":"I would prefer to sit near the window.|I’d prefer to sit near the window.","why":"Would prefer takes to + verb; would rather takes a bare verb.","id":"g41-7"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"“I’d rather you didn’t smoke here.” What time does this normally refer to?","answer":"Present or future","options":["Present or future","A definite completed past event","A past historical habit"],"why":"Past form expresses a preference about someone else’s action now or later.","id":"g41-8"}],"listening":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does Natalie mean by a third place?","answer":"A place beyond home and work or school","options":["A place beyond home and work or school","The third café on a street","A private office"],"evidence":"Home is the first place","id":"a24-1"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What makes cafés useful for remote work?","answer":"Wi-Fi, a table and background noise","options":["Wi-Fi, a table and background noise","A compulsory uniform","Free accommodation"],"evidence":"Wi-Fi, a table","id":"a24-2"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How can social media help a café?","answer":"Photos can attract visitors.","options":["Photos can attract visitors.","It removes all running costs.","It replaces staff entirely."],"evidence":"popular online","id":"a24-3"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"Natalie says cafés are popular only because of coffee.","answer":"False","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"not only because of coffee","id":"a24-4"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"Some cafés provide sockets and quiet corners.","answer":"True","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"sockets, bigger tables, and quiet corners","id":"a24-5"},{"type":"sequence","prompt":"Put these events or ideas in the order they appear in the recording.","events":[["Natalie explains the third-place idea.","third places"],["She explains remote-work needs.","work remotely"],["She explains social-media influence.","Another reason is social media"]],"answer":["Natalie explains the third-place idea.","She explains remote-work needs.","She explains social-media influence."],"evidence":"third places / work remotely / Another reason is social media","id":"a24-6"}],"vocab":[{"id":"v24-1","word":"remote work","definition":"work done away from a traditional office"},{"id":"v24-2","word":"background noise","definition":"sounds present behind the main activity"},{"id":"v24-3","word":"socket","definition":"a point for connecting electrical equipment"},{"id":"v24-4","word":"instant coffee","definition":"coffee made quickly by adding water to granules"},{"id":"v24-5","word":"connect","definition":"form relationships or communicate with others"}],"writing":"Review a café as a place to study, rest or meet others, using clear criteria. Focus: Express personal preferences and tactful preferences about another person.","speaking":"Discuss whether a café or home is a better place to study. Focus: Express personal preferences and tactful preferences about another person."}],"audioLibrary":[{"n":1,"title":"Stories of relationships","speaker":"Chris","audio":"https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/67aa2baa0c65412632c4b3d1/6972587ca943dc2732102e58_SCRIPT%201%20(Male%20speaker%20%E2%80%94%20Chris)_%20Stories%20of%20relationships%20(B1).mp3","transcript":["Hi, I’m Chris. I want to tell you about a relationship that changed me a lot—my first serious relationship. We met at work, and at the beginning everything felt easy. We had similar interests, we laughed a lot, and we spent most weekends together. After a few months, we started arguing about small things: time, plans, and how often we should see each other. I wanted more space, and she wanted more attention.","At first, we didn’t talk about it clearly. We just got annoyed and stayed quiet. Later we tried to communicate better. We made simple rules: one evening a week for our own hobbies, and one day for friends and family. It helped for a while, but in the end we wanted different lifestyles. We broke up, and it was painful, but it taught me something important: love is not only feelings. It’s also communication, respect, and making time for each other."],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"Where did Chris meet his first serious partner?","answer":"At work","options":["At work","At university","On a hiking trip"],"evidence":"We met at work","id":"a1-1"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What did Chris want more of?","answer":"Personal space","options":["Personal space","Expensive holidays","Attention"],"evidence":"I wanted more space","id":"a1-2"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why did they eventually break up?","answer":"They wanted different lifestyles.","options":["They wanted different lifestyles.","One of them moved abroad.","They stopped seeing their friends."],"evidence":"we wanted different lifestyles","id":"a1-3"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"Their new rules helped permanently.","answer":"False","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"It helped for a while","id":"a1-4"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"Chris believes communication is part of love.","answer":"True","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"It’s also communication","id":"a1-5"},{"type":"sequence","prompt":"Put these events or ideas in the order they appear in the recording.","events":[["They enjoyed similar interests.","We had similar interests"],["They created rules about time.","We made simple rules"],["They separated.","We broke up"]],"answer":["They enjoyed similar interests.","They created rules about time.","They separated."],"evidence":"We had similar interests / We made simple rules / We broke up","id":"a1-6"}],"vocab":[["argue","disagree with someone, often emotionally"],["attention","time and interest given to someone"],["annoyed","slightly angry or irritated"],["break up","end a romantic relationship"],["respect","consideration for another person’s needs"]]},{"n":2,"title":"Life in a Very Big Family","speaker":"Ryan","audio":"https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/67aa2baa0c65412632c4b3d1/69776353264693754b633e82_Life%20in%20a%20Very%20Big%20Family_.mp3","transcript":["Hi, I’m Ryan. Today I want to tell you about a family I know that is very big. The parents, Laura and Mike, already have nine children, and now they are expecting their tenth baby. For many people this sounds shocking, but for them it feels normal, because they have lived this way for years.","Their house is always busy. In the morning, they wake up early because the younger kids need help with breakfast, clothes, and school bags. The older children are more independent. They make simple food, pack lunches, and sometimes help their brothers and sisters get ready. In this family, everyone has small responsibilities. For example, one child feeds the dog, another sets the table, and someone else helps clean the kitchen after dinner.","Of course, raising so many kids is not easy. The parents say the hardest part is time. They don’t have much quiet time, and they need to plan everything. Shopping takes a long time, and the grocery bill is big. They also need a bigger car, and family trips are expensive. Sometimes the parents feel tired, and they admit that they can’t give the same attention to every child every day.","But there are also positive sides. The children learn to share, cooperate, and solve problems together. They are rarely bored, because they always have someone to play with. The house feels warm and full of energy. The parents say they laugh a lot, even on difficult days.","Personally, I think this lifestyle is not for everyone. But if a family is organized, supportive, and realistic about the challenges, it can work. For them, the new baby is not just “number ten.” It is another person they will love and care for—and they are already preparing the room, buying baby clothes, and planning a new routine."],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"How many children do Laura and Mike already have?","answer":"Nine","options":["Nine","Ten","Eight"],"evidence":"already have nine children","id":"a2-1"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What do they say is the hardest part?","answer":"Time","options":["Time","Finding a dog","Learning languages"],"evidence":"the hardest part is time","id":"a2-2"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is a benefit for the children?","answer":"They learn to cooperate.","options":["They learn to cooperate.","They never have responsibilities.","They never disagree."],"evidence":"share, cooperate, and solve problems together","id":"a2-3"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"The parents can give equal attention to every child every day.","answer":"False","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"they can’t give the same attention","id":"a2-4"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"The older children sometimes help younger ones get ready.","answer":"True","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"sometimes help their brothers and sisters get ready","id":"a2-5"},{"type":"sequence","prompt":"Put these events or ideas in the order they appear in the recording.","events":[["The parents wake up early.","they wake up early"],["The older children pack lunches.","pack lunches"],["The kitchen is cleaned after dinner.","clean the kitchen after dinner"]],"answer":["The parents wake up early.","The older children pack lunches.","The kitchen is cleaned after dinner."],"evidence":"they wake up early / pack lunches / clean the kitchen after dinner","id":"a2-6"}],"vocab":[["expecting","waiting for the birth of a baby"],["responsibility","a duty you are expected to carry out"],["independent","able to do things without help"],["cooperate","work together towards a shared aim"],["supportive","giving help and encouragement"]]},{"n":3,"title":"Moving to a new apartment and starting over","speaker":"Elena","audio":"https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/67aa2baa0c65412632c4b3d1/6975d3adafd5a9c2c43b0346_SCRIPT%20(Female%20speaker%20%E2%80%94%20Elena)_%20Moving%20to%20a%20new%20apartment%20and%20starting%20over%20(B1).mp3","transcript":["Hi, I’m Elena. Recently, I moved to a new apartment, and it felt like a fresh start for me. I didn’t move to a different city, but even changing neighborhoods made a big difference. My old place was small and noisy. The street was busy, and at night I could hear cars and people outside. I got used to it, but I was always tired in the morning.","When I found the new apartment, it wasn’t perfect, but it had two things I really wanted: more light and more quiet. The first week was stressful. I had boxes everywhere, and I couldn’t find simple things like my phone charger or a clean towel. I also realized I had too many unnecessary items. So I started sorting my stuff. I donated some clothes, threw away broken things, and kept only what I actually use.","Now I’m slowly making the space feel like home. I bought a small desk because I sometimes work online, and I put a plant near the window. I also made a simple routine: I wake up, open the curtains, and drink coffee while I plan my day. It sounds small, but it helps me feel calm and organized.","The best part is that this move pushed me to change other habits too. I started walking more, cooking at home more often, and spending less time on my phone in the evening. I still have a lot to do—like hanging pictures and choosing a lamp—but I enjoy the process. For me, moving wasn’t only about a new apartment. It was about creating a better daily life, step by step."],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"Did Elena move to another city?","answer":"No, she changed neighbourhoods.","options":["No, she changed neighbourhoods.","Yes, she moved overseas.","The transcript does not say."],"evidence":"I didn’t move to a different city","id":"a3-1"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which two things did she want in her new home?","answer":"More light and quiet","options":["More light and quiet","A bigger television and pool","A cheaper car and garden"],"evidence":"more light and more quiet","id":"a3-2"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What did she do with some clothes?","answer":"Donated them","options":["Donated them","Sold all of them","Stored them at work"],"evidence":"I donated some clothes","id":"a3-3"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"Elena has finished every home-improvement task.","answer":"False","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"I still have a lot to do","id":"a3-4"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"She started spending less time on her phone in the evening.","answer":"True","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"spending less time on my phone in the evening","id":"a3-5"},{"type":"sequence","prompt":"Put these events or ideas in the order they appear in the recording.","events":[["She found a new apartment.","When I found the new apartment"],["She sorted unnecessary items.","I started sorting my stuff"],["She created a morning routine.","I also made a simple routine"]],"answer":["She found a new apartment.","She sorted unnecessary items.","She created a morning routine."],"evidence":"When I found the new apartment / I started sorting my stuff / I also made a simple routine","id":"a3-6"}],"vocab":[["fresh start","a chance to begin again in a better way"],["neighbourhood","an area within a town or city"],["sort","organise things into groups"],["donate","give something to help others"],["routine","a regular way of doing things"]]},{"n":4,"title":"My college experience","speaker":"Ryan","audio":"https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/67aa2baa0c65412632c4b3d1/696f3792729243672263f240_SCRIPT%201%20(Male%20speaker%20%E2%80%94%20Ryan)_%20My%20college%20experience.mp3","transcript":["Hi, I’m Ryan. I started college when I was nineteen, and honestly, the first month was a shock. In high school, teachers reminded us about everything. In college, no one chased me. I had to plan my time, remember deadlines, and figure out my own routine. At the beginning I missed two small assignments because I didn’t check the online system properly. It wasn’t a disaster, but it was a good lesson.","I studied Business Administration at a big public university. The campus was like a small city: libraries, cafés, sports halls, and buildings for every department. My first-year classes were huge. In one lecture there were more than two hundred students. The professor spoke fast, the slides changed quickly, and if you didn’t take notes, you were lost. After a few weeks I learned a simple strategy: I always read a little before class, and I wrote down questions. That way, the lecture made more sense.","The best part of college for me was meeting people from different places. My roommate was from a small town, and my best friend was an international student. We had very different backgrounds, but we helped each other. We studied together before exams, shared food, and talked late at night about our plans. I also joined a student club connected to marketing. We organised small events, invited guest speakers, and worked on real projects for local businesses. That experience helped me a lot later when I applied for internships.","Of course, it wasn’t always fun. There were stressful weeks with exams and presentations. I remember one semester when I worked part-time and studied full-time. I slept too little and drank too much coffee. Now, when I look back, I understand that college taught me more than business theory. It taught me independence, communication, and how to keep going when things are hard."],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"How old was Ryan when he started college?","answer":"Nineteen","options":["Nineteen","Twenty-one","Seventeen"],"evidence":"when I was nineteen","id":"a4-1"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What did he study?","answer":"Business Administration","options":["Business Administration","Medicine","Engineering"],"evidence":"I studied Business Administration","id":"a4-2"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What helped lectures make more sense?","answer":"Reading beforehand and writing questions","options":["Reading beforehand and writing questions","Avoiding note-taking","Studying only on exam day"],"evidence":"read a little before class","id":"a4-3"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"Ryan missed two small assignments at first.","answer":"True","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"I missed two small assignments","id":"a4-4"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"He never combined part-time work with full-time study.","answer":"False","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"I worked part-time and studied full-time","id":"a4-5"},{"type":"sequence","prompt":"Put these events or ideas in the order they appear in the recording.","events":[["He missed assignments.","I missed two small assignments"],["He learned a lecture strategy.","I learned a simple strategy"],["He joined a marketing club.","I also joined a student club connected to marketing"]],"answer":["He missed assignments.","He learned a lecture strategy.","He joined a marketing club."],"evidence":"I missed two small assignments / I learned a simple strategy / I also joined a student club connected to marketing","id":"a4-6"}],"vocab":[["deadline","the latest time for completing a task"],["assignment","a piece of work set for a student"],["lecture","a talk given to a class of students"],["internship","a temporary job providing work experience"],["background","a person’s earlier life and experience"]]},{"n":5,"title":"Are exams necessary?","speaker":"Ryan","audio":"https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/67aa2baa0c65412632c4b3d1/6975e60d5bf60318198f54c3_SCRIPT%201%20(Male%20speaker%20%E2%80%94%20Ryan).mp3","transcript":["Hi, I’m Ryan. I think exams are necessary, but only in a smart way. In school and university, we need some method to check progress. Without exams, it can be hard to know if students really understand the material or if they are just present in class. Exams create a clear deadline, and deadlines can help people focus. When I was a student, I didn’t always feel motivated, but before an exam I studied seriously. That pressure helped me build discipline.","At the same time, I don’t believe exams should be the only measure. Some people are good at tests, but they don’t use the knowledge well in real life. Others understand a subject deeply, but they feel anxious, forget everything, and perform badly for two hours. That doesn’t mean they are “bad students.” It means the method is limited.","So for me, exams are useful if they are balanced with other things: projects, presentations, and regular homework. Also, exams should test understanding, not memorization. If an exam only asks you to repeat facts, you will forget them quickly. But if it asks you to explain ideas, solve problems, or apply knowledge, then it becomes meaningful. In short, yes—exams are necessary, but the system needs variety and fairness."],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is Ryan’s main view?","answer":"Exams are useful as part of a varied system.","options":["Exams are useful as part of a varied system.","All exams should be removed.","Exams should replace all projects."],"evidence":"balanced with other things","id":"a5-1"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why can exams help motivation?","answer":"They create a clear deadline.","options":["They create a clear deadline.","They guarantee a job.","They remove all pressure."],"evidence":"Exams create a clear deadline","id":"a5-2"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What should a meaningful exam test?","answer":"Understanding and application","options":["Understanding and application","Only memorised facts","Only speed"],"evidence":"test understanding, not memorization","id":"a5-3"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"A poor test performance always means a student lacks understanding.","answer":"False","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"Others understand a subject deeply","id":"a5-4"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"Ryan supports projects and presentations alongside exams.","answer":"True","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"projects, presentations, and regular homework","id":"a5-5"},{"type":"sequence","prompt":"Put these events or ideas in the order they appear in the recording.","events":[["Exams can focus students.","deadlines can help people focus"],["Test anxiety limits the method.","they feel anxious"],["A balanced system is recommended.","balanced with other things"]],"answer":["Exams can focus students.","Test anxiety limits the method.","A balanced system is recommended."],"evidence":"deadlines can help people focus / they feel anxious / balanced with other things","id":"a5-6"}],"vocab":[["progress","improvement towards a goal"],["discipline","the ability to keep working consistently"],["anxious","worried and uneasy"],["balanced","giving suitable weight to different parts"],["apply knowledge","use what you know in a situation"]]},{"n":6,"title":"My work experience","speaker":"Ethan","audio":"https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/67aa2baa0c65412632c4b3d1/696f4a00567295b3de3268a5_SCRIPT%201%20(Male%20speaker%20%E2%80%94%20Ethan)_%20My%20work%20experience.mp3","transcript":["Hi, I’m Ethan. My first real job was in a small electronics store when I was twenty. I thought it would be easy: smile, sell phones, and go home. But the first week was stressful because I had to learn a lot of details—prices, plans, returns, and all the “small rules” customers love to test. Some people were friendly, but others came in angry before I even said hello. I learned quickly that customer service is not only about being polite. It’s about staying calm and solving problems fast.","After that, I moved into an office job as a junior project assistant. The work was very different. In the shop, everything was immediate. In the office, tasks lasted weeks, and I had to follow processes, write emails, and keep track of deadlines. At the beginning I made a common mistake: I said “yes” to everything. I wanted to look helpful, so I accepted tasks even when my schedule was full. Then I started missing small deadlines, and I felt embarrassed. My manager gave me simple advice: “If you can’t do it today, say when you can do it.” That changed everything. I began to plan my day, use a task list, and set realistic expectations.","One project I remember well was a company event for clients. I had to book a venue, coordinate catering, and prepare a short presentation. Two days before the event, the venue called and said they had a problem with the electricity. It was a nightmare. I spent the whole day calling other places, comparing prices, and checking availability. In the end we found a new location, and the event went well. I was exhausted, but I felt proud because I didn’t panic.","Now I work in operations, and I still use lessons from my earlier jobs. I’m better at communication, I don’t take complaints personally, and I know that problems are part of the job. The key is how you respond when something goes wrong."],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"Where was Ethan’s first real job?","answer":"An electronics store","options":["An electronics store","A restaurant","A bank"],"evidence":"in a small electronics store","id":"a6-1"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What mistake did he make in his office job?","answer":"Accepting too many tasks","options":["Accepting too many tasks","Never answering messages","Refusing every task"],"evidence":"I said “yes” to everything","id":"a6-2"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What happened shortly before the client event?","answer":"The venue reported an electricity problem.","options":["The venue reported an electricity problem.","All guests cancelled.","The catering was stolen."],"evidence":"a problem with the electricity","id":"a6-3"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"His manager advised him to give realistic timing.","answer":"True","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"say when you can do it","id":"a6-4"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"They held the event at the original venue.","answer":"False","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"we found a new location","id":"a6-5"},{"type":"sequence","prompt":"Put these events or ideas in the order they appear in the recording.","events":[["He worked in a shop.","a small electronics store"],["He became a project assistant.","junior project assistant"],["He works in operations now.","Now I work in operations"]],"answer":["He worked in a shop.","He became a project assistant.","He works in operations now."],"evidence":"a small electronics store / junior project assistant / Now I work in operations","id":"a6-6"}],"vocab":[["coordinate","organise people or activities to work together"],["venue","a place where an event is held"],["catering","providing food and drinks at an event"],["availability","whether something can be used or booked"],["expectations","what someone believes will happen"]]},{"n":7,"title":"Workplace etiquette and small mistakes","speaker":"Ethan","audio":"https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/67aa2baa0c65412632c4b3d1/696f77d424888b9201e07369_SCRIPT%201%20(Male%20speaker%20%E2%80%94%20Ethan)_%20First%20week%20in%20the%20office.mp3","transcript":["Hi, I’m Ethan. When I started my current job, I learned something quickly: in an office, you don’t only work—you also follow a lot of small rules. Some rules are written, and some are not, but they still matter.","On my first day, my manager said, “You must wear your badge at all times.” That was clear. But there were other things I had to discover myself. For example, we have shared meeting rooms. You must book them before you use them, and you must leave the room clean. You can’t just walk in and take it if another team has reserved it. I didn’t know this, and once I almost interrupted a meeting. It was embarrassing, but it taught me a lesson.","Another thing is communication. In my team, you should reply to messages within a reasonable time, even if it’s just “I’ll check and get back to you.” People don’t expect you to answer instantly, so you don’t have to be online every second. But you shouldn’t disappear for hours without warning, especially during work time.","There are also unwritten rules about noise. You can talk, of course, but you shouldn’t speak loudly near people who are concentrating. If you need a long call, you should use a meeting room or a quiet area. And you definitely can’t play videos without headphones. That one is a big “no.”","I also learned about timing. You must join meetings on time. If you are late, you should send a quick message. It sounds simple, but it shows respect. And if you don’t understand something, you should ask. You don’t have to pretend you know everything. In fact, it’s better to ask early than to make a bigger mistake later.","Now I feel much more comfortable at work. I still make small mistakes sometimes, but I know the basics. Office etiquette is not about being perfect. It’s about helping everyone work together in a smooth way."],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"What must Ethan wear at all times?","answer":"His badge","options":["His badge","Headphones","A uniform jacket"],"evidence":"wear your badge at all times","id":"a7-1"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How should a shared meeting room be left?","answer":"Clean","options":["Clean","Locked by the last visitor","Full of personal belongings"],"evidence":"leave the room clean","id":"a7-2"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What should someone do if late for a meeting?","answer":"Send a quick message","options":["Send a quick message","Disappear for the day","Pretend the meeting was cancelled"],"evidence":"send a quick message","id":"a7-3"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"Employees must stay online every second.","answer":"False","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"you don’t have to be online every second","id":"a7-4"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"Videos without headphones are not acceptable.","answer":"True","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"can’t play videos without headphones","id":"a7-5"},{"type":"sequence","prompt":"Put these events or ideas in the order they appear in the recording.","events":[["Ethan learns the badge rule.","wear your badge"],["He learns room-booking rules.","must book them"],["He discusses timely communication.","Another thing is communication"]],"answer":["Ethan learns the badge rule.","He learns room-booking rules.","He discusses timely communication."],"evidence":"wear your badge / must book them / Another thing is communication","id":"a7-6"}],"vocab":[["etiquette","accepted rules of polite behaviour"],["badge","a small identification sign worn on clothing"],["reasonable","fair and sensible"],["concentrate","give close attention to a task"],["pretend","act as if something is true when it is not"]]},{"n":8,"title":"How do you travel to work?","speaker":"Chris","audio":"https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/67aa2baa0c65412632c4b3d1/697252fa88db469ca8def031_SCRIPT%201%20(Male%20speaker%20%E2%80%94%20Chris)_%20How%20do%20you%20travel%20to%20work_%20(B1).mp3","transcript":["Hi, I’m Chris. I live in the suburbs, so I travel to work every day. On weekdays I usually take the train. I leave home at about 7:20, walk to the station, and catch the 7:40 train. The ride takes around 30 minutes. I like the train because it’s predictable, and I don’t have to sit in traffic. I often listen to a podcast or read the news on my phone.","Sometimes there are delays, especially in winter, and that can be stressful. On those days I try to stay calm and use the time to plan my tasks for the day. I don’t drive to work often because parking is expensive in the city center. But if I have an early meeting, I might take a taxi or ask a colleague for a lift. Overall, the train works best for me. It saves time, and it makes my mornings easier."],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"Where does Chris live?","answer":"In the suburbs","options":["In the suburbs","In the city centre","Beside his office"],"evidence":"I live in the suburbs","id":"a8-1"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"When does his usual train leave?","answer":"7:40","options":["7:40","7:20","8:10"],"evidence":"catch the 7:40 train","id":"a8-2"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why does he rarely drive to work?","answer":"Parking is expensive.","options":["Parking is expensive.","He cannot drive.","His car is broken."],"evidence":"parking is expensive","id":"a8-3"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"The train journey takes around half an hour.","answer":"True","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"around 30 minutes","id":"a8-4"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"Chris never considers a taxi.","answer":"False","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"I might take a taxi","id":"a8-5"},{"type":"sequence","prompt":"Put these events or ideas in the order they appear in the recording.","events":[["He leaves home.","I leave home"],["He walks to the station.","walk to the station"],["He catches the train.","catch the 7:40 train"]],"answer":["He leaves home.","He walks to the station.","He catches the train."],"evidence":"I leave home / walk to the station / catch the 7:40 train","id":"a8-6"}],"vocab":[["suburbs","residential areas outside a city centre"],["predictable","likely to happen in an expected way"],["delay","extra waiting beyond the expected time"],["commute","a regular journey between home and work"],["a lift","a ride offered in someone’s vehicle"]]},{"n":9,"title":"How People Can Build a Career Change","speaker":"Ethan","audio":"https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/67aa2baa0c65412632c4b3d1/69765c2549b820f3ac47dc51_How%20People%20Can%20Build%20a%20Career%20Change.mp3","transcript":["Hi, I’m Ethan. Today I want to talk about something many adults think about: changing your career and starting a new direction.","When people hear “career change,” they imagine quitting their job and beginning again from zero. That sounds risky, especially if you have bills to pay. But in real life, most career changes happen slowly. It’s more like building a bridge from your current life to a new one.","A friend of mine worked in a hotel for years. He liked the people, but he felt tired of the same routine. He didn’t quit immediately. First, he asked himself simple questions: What do I enjoy? What am I good at? What kind of day do I want to have? He noticed he liked solving problems and helping customers, so he started learning basic IT support online. Just one hour in the evenings, a few times a week.","At the beginning, he felt confused. New words, new tools, new rules. But after a few months, he could explain simple technical problems. He also talked to people who already worked in IT. This helped him understand what the job is really like, not just what it looks like on social media.","Later, he updated his CV and applied for junior roles. He got rejected several times, but he learned from each interview. Finally, he found a company that wanted someone with strong communication skills. His hotel experience became an advantage, not a weakness.","For me, the main lesson is this: a career change is not only about learning new skills. It’s also about being patient, staying consistent, and using what you already have. If you take small steps, the future can look very different in one year."],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"How does Ethan describe most career changes?","answer":"Gradual","options":["Gradual","Instant","Impossible"],"evidence":"most career changes happen slowly","id":"a9-1"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What did his friend learn?","answer":"Basic IT support","options":["Basic IT support","Advanced medicine","Restaurant cooking"],"evidence":"basic IT support online","id":"a9-2"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which previous skill became an advantage?","answer":"Communication","options":["Communication","Aircraft repair","Professional painting"],"evidence":"strong communication skills","id":"a9-3"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"The friend quit his hotel job immediately.","answer":"False","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"He didn’t quit immediately","id":"a9-4"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"He was rejected several times before finding a role.","answer":"True","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"He got rejected several times","id":"a9-5"},{"type":"sequence","prompt":"Put these events or ideas in the order they appear in the recording.","events":[["He asked what he enjoyed.","What do I enjoy?"],["He studied IT support.","learning basic IT support online"],["He applied for junior roles.","applied for junior roles"]],"answer":["He asked what he enjoyed.","He studied IT support.","He applied for junior roles."],"evidence":"What do I enjoy? / learning basic IT support online / applied for junior roles","id":"a9-6"}],"vocab":[["career change","moving into a different type of work"],["consistent","continuing regularly without frequent changes"],["junior role","a job intended for someone with limited experience"],["reject","decide not to accept a person or proposal"],["advantage","something that makes success more likely"]]},{"n":10,"title":"Learning a New Skill for the Future","speaker":"Sofia","audio":"https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/67aa2baa0c65412632c4b3d1/6975f90657c0ac11f0f2fe11_SCRIPT%20(Female%20speaker%20%E2%80%94%20Sofia)_%20%E2%80%9CLearning%20a%20New%20Skill%20for%20the%20Future%E2%80%9D_.mp3","transcript":["Hi, I’m Sofia. Today I want to talk about learning a new skill, because I’m trying to change my life step by step. This year I decided to learn digital skills for the future, not just for fun. I want to feel more confident at work and have more options later.","A few months ago, I noticed something simple: many jobs are changing. People use online tools, automation, and AI more and more. At first, it sounded scary, because I thought, “This is not for me.” But then I realized that I don’t need to be a genius. I just need a clear plan and a little practice every day.","So I started small. I chose one topic: how to organize tasks and save time. I learned how to use calendars, reminders, and simple templates. Then I tried basic automation—like sending messages automatically or moving information from one place to another. It felt difficult in the beginning, but after a week I could already see results. I wasted less time, and I felt calmer.","Now I’m learning how to write better prompts for AI tools. I use them to check my English, create short texts, and explain words in a simple way. I don’t copy everything. I read the result, change it, and learn from it. It’s like having a patient tutor.","My next goal is to build a small project. I want to create a simple learning page with short listening texts and exercises. I will improve it every month. I know it won’t be perfect at first, but that’s okay. The important thing is progress.","For me, learning a new skill is not only about technology. It’s about preparing for the future, being flexible, and believing that I can grow."],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why does Sofia want digital skills?","answer":"More confidence and future options","options":["More confidence and future options","To avoid all work","To become famous immediately"],"evidence":"feel more confident at work","id":"a10-1"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What was her first topic?","answer":"Organising tasks and saving time","options":["Organising tasks and saving time","Building complex robots","Making cinema films"],"evidence":"how to organize tasks and save time","id":"a10-2"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How does she handle AI results?","answer":"She reads, changes and learns from them.","options":["She reads, changes and learns from them.","She copies everything.","She ignores all feedback."],"evidence":"read the result, change it","id":"a10-3"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"She expects her first project to be perfect.","answer":"False","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"it won’t be perfect at first","id":"a10-4"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"She wants to improve the project every month.","answer":"True","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"improve it every month","id":"a10-5"},{"type":"sequence","prompt":"Put these events or ideas in the order they appear in the recording.","events":[["She chose a small practical topic.","I chose one topic"],["She tried basic automation.","I tried basic automation"],["She started learning to write prompts.","Now I’m learning how to write better prompts"]],"answer":["She chose a small practical topic.","She tried basic automation.","She started learning to write prompts."],"evidence":"I chose one topic / I tried basic automation / Now I’m learning how to write better prompts","id":"a10-6"}],"vocab":[["automation","making a process run with less manual work"],["template","a reusable pattern for creating something"],["prompt","an instruction or question given to an AI tool"],["flexible","able to adapt to changing situations"],["option","a possible choice"]]},{"n":11,"title":"Where did you stay?","speaker":"Liam","audio":"https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/67aa2baa0c65412632c4b3d1/696f27572805207d9d7a5c5e_B1%20Where%20did%20you%20stay_%20(Liam).mp3","transcript":["Hi, I’m Liam. Last autumn I went to Lisbon for nine days, and I stayed in a small guesthouse in the old part of the city. It wasn’t a big hotel with a gym and a pool, but it had character. The building was old, with narrow stairs and high ceilings, and my room had wooden floors and a small balcony. I could see a bit of the river if I leaned over the railing.","I chose that place because of the location. I wanted to be able to walk everywhere, and I really could. In the mornings I walked to cafés for coffee and pastries, then I took the tram to different neighbourhoods. In the evening I didn’t need to worry about transport because I could always walk back, even if it was late.","The guesthouse was comfortable, but not perfect. The room was clean and the bed was fine, yet the sound insulation was weak. On my first night I could hear people talking in the corridor, and I woke up early because someone slammed a door. After that, I used earplugs and it was much better. Another small issue was the shower: the water pressure changed sometimes, especially when other guests were using water.","The good part was the staff. They were friendly and gave me great tips, like which viewpoint was less crowded and where to eat seafood without paying tourist prices. They also arranged a taxi to the airport on my last day, so it was stress-free. Overall, it was a great stay: simple, central, and full of atmosphere. Next time I would still choose a guesthouse, but I might pick a room that faces the inner courtyard for more quiet."],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"Where did Liam stay?","answer":"A guesthouse in Lisbon","options":["A guesthouse in Lisbon","A large hotel in Rome","A campsite in Madrid"],"evidence":"a small guesthouse","id":"a11-1"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why did he choose it?","answer":"Its location","options":["Its location","Its swimming pool","Its gym"],"evidence":"because of the location","id":"a11-2"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What helped with noise?","answer":"Earplugs","options":["Earplugs","A new balcony","A taxi"],"evidence":"I used earplugs","id":"a11-3"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"He stayed for nine days.","answer":"True","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"for nine days","id":"a11-4"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"The water pressure was always stable.","answer":"False","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"the water pressure changed sometimes","id":"a11-5"},{"type":"sequence","prompt":"Put these events or ideas in the order they appear in the recording.","events":[["He chose a central location.","because of the location"],["He experienced noise on the first night.","On my first night"],["Staff arranged an airport taxi.","arranged a taxi to the airport"]],"answer":["He chose a central location.","He experienced noise on the first night.","Staff arranged an airport taxi."],"evidence":"because of the location / On my first night / arranged a taxi to the airport","id":"a11-6"}],"vocab":[["guesthouse","a small place providing accommodation"],["railing","a barrier along an edge for safety"],["sound insulation","material or design reducing sound transmission"],["earplugs","small objects placed in ears to reduce noise"],["courtyard","an open area surrounded by buildings"]]},{"n":12,"title":"“A Day Trip to Washington, D.C.”","speaker":"Chris","audio":"https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/67aa2baa0c65412632c4b3d1/69724d77ac5a1d06ea9e5c9c_%E2%80%9CA%20Day%20Trip%20to%20Washington%2C%20D.C.%E2%80%9D.mp3","transcript":["Hi, I’m Chris. Today I want to tell you about a day trip I took to Washington, D.C. I went there for the first time recently, and I was surprised by how much you can do in one day, even with a simple plan.","I arrived in the morning by train. The first thing I noticed was how clean and organized the city felt, especially around the main streets. I didn’t rent a car because traffic can be stressful, and the metro in D.C. is easy to use. I bought a ticket, checked the map, and within minutes I was on my way.","My main goal was to see the National Mall area. It’s a long open space with famous monuments and museums, so I started walking and just followed the crowd. I saw the Washington Monument from far away, and it looked huge in real life. After that, I walked toward the Lincoln Memorial. The steps were full of people taking photos, but the atmosphere was still respectful and quiet.","One of the best parts of the trip was visiting a museum. I chose a Smithsonian museum because many of them are free, which is amazing. I spent about an hour inside, but honestly, you could stay there all day. I liked how the exhibits were clear and interactive, not boring like some old museums.","Around lunchtime I got hungry, so I grabbed something simple—just a sandwich and coffee from a small café. Prices were a bit higher than in my city, but the service was quick, and I didn’t want to waste time sitting too long.","In the afternoon I walked more, and I also stopped near the White House. You can’t get very close, of course, but it was still interesting to see it with my own eyes. Before I left, I sat on a bench for a few minutes and watched people: tourists, office workers, families, and students. The city felt busy but not chaotic.","By the evening, I was tired from walking, but I felt happy. D.C. is a great place for a short visit because it’s full of history, culture, and landmarks. Next time, I want to explore more neighborhoods and try local food, not just the tourist spots."],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"How did Chris arrive in Washington, D.C.?","answer":"By train","options":["By train","By rented car","By plane"],"evidence":"by train","id":"a12-1"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What was his main sightseeing area?","answer":"The National Mall","options":["The National Mall","Only the airport","A suburban shopping centre"],"evidence":"National Mall area","id":"a12-2"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why did he choose a Smithsonian museum?","answer":"Many of them are free.","options":["Many of them are free.","It was the only open building.","It included a free hotel room."],"evidence":"many of them are free","id":"a12-3"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"He spent the whole day inside one museum.","answer":"False","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"about an hour inside","id":"a12-4"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"He saw the White House from outside.","answer":"True","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"stopped near the White House","id":"a12-5"},{"type":"sequence","prompt":"Put these events or ideas in the order they appear in the recording.","events":[["He visited the National Mall area.","National Mall area"],["He ate lunch at a café.","Around lunchtime"],["He stopped near the White House.","In the afternoon"]],"answer":["He visited the National Mall area.","He ate lunch at a café.","He stopped near the White House."],"evidence":"National Mall area / Around lunchtime / In the afternoon","id":"a12-6"}],"vocab":[["landmark","a well-known building or feature of a place"],["exhibit","an object or display shown in a museum"],["interactive","allowing visitors to take part"],["chaotic","confused and lacking order"],["viewpoint","a position or place for seeing a view"]]},{"n":13,"title":"Grand Canyon","speaker":"Mark","audio":"https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/67aa2baa0c65412632c4b3d1/6975f2ec5259031cc0e372e6_Grand%20Canyon.mp3","transcript":["Hi, I’m Mark. Today I want to talk about the Grand Canyon in the USA. It is one of the most famous natural places in the world, and it really feels unreal when you see it for the first time.","The Grand Canyon is in Arizona, and it was made by the Colorado River over a very long time. The canyon is huge. It is deep, wide, and full of different colors. The rocks can look red, orange, brown, and even purple, depending on the light. In the morning and at sunset, the view is especially beautiful because the colors change every minute.","Many visitors go to the South Rim because it is easier to reach and it has lots of viewpoints. You can walk along the rim, stop at different places, and take photos. There are also short hiking trails if you want to go a little lower and feel the size of the canyon more directly. But you need to be careful. The weather can change quickly, and the heat can be strong, especially in summer. It’s important to bring water, wear good shoes, and not take risks.","Some people also visit the Skywalk, a glass bridge where you can stand above the canyon. Others choose a helicopter tour to see the canyon from the air. If you like adventure, you can even hike down toward the river, but that needs planning and good fitness.","For me, the Grand Canyon is not just a tourist spot. It makes you feel small in a good way. It reminds you how powerful nature is, and how much time it takes to create something so amazing. If you ever travel in the USA, I think it’s a place worth seeing at least once."],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"In which state is the Grand Canyon?","answer":"Arizona","options":["Arizona","Florida","California"],"evidence":"in Arizona","id":"a13-1"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What formed the canyon according to Mark?","answer":"The Colorado River","options":["The Colorado River","A modern building project","A glacier last year"],"evidence":"made by the Colorado River","id":"a13-2"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why do many visitors choose the South Rim?","answer":"It is easier to reach and has viewpoints.","options":["It is easier to reach and has viewpoints.","It has no hiking trails.","It is always cold."],"evidence":"easier to reach","id":"a13-3"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"The weather can change quickly.","answer":"True","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"weather can change quickly","id":"a13-4"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"Hiking down towards the river requires no preparation.","answer":"False","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"needs planning and good fitness","id":"a13-5"},{"type":"sequence","prompt":"Put these events or ideas in the order they appear in the recording.","events":[["Mark describes the river and colours.","Colorado River"],["He discusses the South Rim.","South Rim"],["He mentions the Skywalk.","the Skywalk"]],"answer":["Mark describes the river and colours.","He discusses the South Rim.","He mentions the Skywalk."],"evidence":"Colorado River / South Rim / the Skywalk","id":"a13-6"}],"vocab":[["rim","the upper edge of something"],["trail","a path used for walking outdoors"],["fitness","physical condition and ability"],["unreal","so surprising that it seems hard to believe"],["take risks","do things that may lead to harm"]]},{"n":14,"title":"The Most Beautiful Places in the World","speaker":"Jordan","audio":"https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/67aa2baa0c65412632c4b3d1/69772b70262916134925b2d9_The%20Most%20Beautiful%20Places%20in%20the%20World_.mp3","transcript":["Hi, I’m Jordan. People often ask, “What are the most beautiful places in the world?” Of course, beauty is different for everyone, but I think some places are special because they make you feel small, calm, and amazed at the same time.","For me, one of the most beautiful places is a big mountain area, like the Swiss Alps. The air feels fresh, the peaks look unreal, and the lakes are so clear that they reflect the sky like a mirror. Even if you are not a professional hiker, you can just take a cable car, walk a little, and enjoy the views.","Another type of beauty is found in tropical islands, like in the Maldives or Hawaii. The water is bright blue, the sand is soft, and the sunsets look like a movie. But what I like most is the peaceful feeling. When you hear only waves and wind, your mind finally slows down.","I also think deserts can be beautiful, which surprises some people. Places like the Sahara or the deserts in the USA have a quiet, powerful atmosphere. At night, the sky is full of stars, because there are no city lights. It feels like a different planet.","Finally, I can’t forget old cities, like Rome or Kyoto. They are beautiful in a human way. You can walk through narrow streets, see historical buildings, and feel the story of the place. It’s not only about nature, but also about culture and history.","So, in my opinion, the most beautiful places are not just “pretty.” They change your mood. They make you stop, look around, and remember the moment."],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does Jordan value most about tropical islands?","answer":"A peaceful feeling","options":["A peaceful feeling","Large shopping malls","Fast internet"],"evidence":"the peaceful feeling","id":"a14-1"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why is the desert night sky striking?","answer":"There are no city lights.","options":["There are no city lights.","There is always snow.","Stars are closer to the ground."],"evidence":"there are no city lights","id":"a14-2"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What kind of beauty do old cities offer?","answer":"Culture and history","options":["Culture and history","Only untouched nature","Only modern technology"],"evidence":"culture and history","id":"a14-3"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"Jordan says beauty is the same for everyone.","answer":"False","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"beauty is different for everyone","id":"a14-4"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"You can enjoy the Alps without being a professional hiker.","answer":"True","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"not a professional hiker","id":"a14-5"},{"type":"sequence","prompt":"Put these events or ideas in the order they appear in the recording.","events":[["Jordan discusses mountains.","Swiss Alps"],["He discusses tropical islands.","tropical islands"],["He discusses deserts.","deserts can be beautiful"]],"answer":["Jordan discusses mountains.","He discusses tropical islands.","He discusses deserts."],"evidence":"Swiss Alps / tropical islands / deserts can be beautiful","id":"a14-6"}],"vocab":[["peak","the top of a mountain"],["reflect","show an image on a surface"],["tropical","related to warm areas near the equator"],["atmosphere","the feeling of a place"],["historical","connected with events from the past"]]},{"n":15,"title":"the most ancient cities in the world.","speaker":"Jordan","audio":"https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/67aa2baa0c65412632c4b3d1/697731615226f422e628292d_The%20Most%20Ancient%20Cities%20in%20the%20World_.mp3","transcript":["Hi, I’m Jordan. Today I want to talk about some of the most ancient cities in the world. When we say “ancient,” we usually mean places where people have lived for thousands of years, and the city has continued, in some form, up to modern times. It’s hard to be 100% sure, because history is complicated, but these cities are often mentioned as the oldest.","One famous example is Damascus, in Syria. Many historians believe it is one of the oldest continuously inhabited cities. It has been important for trade, religion, and politics for a very long time. When you walk through the old parts of Damascus, you can still see narrow streets and traditional markets.","Another ancient city is Jericho, in the West Bank. Some archaeological evidence suggests people lived there many thousands of years ago. Jericho is known for its ancient walls and its location near a water source, which helped people survive in a dry region.","In Europe, Athens in Greece is also very old. It became a powerful city in ancient times and is famous for democracy, philosophy, and art. Today you can visit places like the Acropolis and imagine what life was like in the past.","In the Middle East, there is Byblos in Lebanon, another city linked to very early history. It was an important port and trading center. Some people even connect the name “Bible” to Byblos because of trade in writing materials long ago.","What I find interesting is that ancient cities are not just museums. People live there now. They go to work, buy food, and meet friends, but they are surrounded by history every day. For travelers, visiting an ancient city is like stepping into a living timeline. You can see modern life and ancient stones in the same place, and it makes you respect how long human life and culture have been developing."],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why is naming the oldest city difficult?","answer":"History is complicated.","options":["History is complicated.","No ancient cities survive.","All cities were founded together."],"evidence":"history is complicated","id":"a15-1"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which city is linked to ancient walls and a water source?","answer":"Jericho","options":["Jericho","Athens","Byblos"],"evidence":"Jericho is known for its ancient walls","id":"a15-2"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which site does Jordan mention in Athens?","answer":"The Acropolis","options":["The Acropolis","The Eiffel Tower","The Colosseum"],"evidence":"the Acropolis","id":"a15-3"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"Byblos was an important port.","answer":"True","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"an important port","id":"a15-4"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"Ancient cities are only museums without residents.","answer":"False","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"People live there now","id":"a15-5"},{"type":"sequence","prompt":"Put these events or ideas in the order they appear in the recording.","events":[["Jordan describes Damascus.","Damascus"],["He describes Jericho.","Jericho"],["He describes Athens.","Athens in Greece"]],"answer":["Jordan describes Damascus.","He describes Jericho.","He describes Athens."],"evidence":"Damascus / Jericho / Athens in Greece","id":"a15-6"}],"vocab":[["inhabited","having people living in a place"],["archaeological","related to studying remains of past societies"],["trade","buying and selling goods"],["port","a place where ships load and unload"],["timeline","an ordered sequence of events in time"]]},{"n":16,"title":"My Weekend Hike","speaker":"Alex","audio":"https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/67aa2baa0c65412632c4b3d1/6972475e6fffe2f71b511e75_B1%20Intermediate)%20%E2%80%94%20%E2%80%9CMy%20Weekend%20Hike%E2%80%9D%20Alex.mp3","transcript":["Hi, I’m Alex. Last weekend I went on a hike not far from my city, and it was exactly what I needed. I spend most of my week indoors, so on Saturday morning I decided to get some fresh air and move my body a little.","I didn’t plan anything complicated. The night before, I checked the weather, charged my phone, and packed a small backpack. I took a bottle of water, a sandwich, some nuts, and a light jacket. I also wore comfortable shoes because I knew the path could be muddy after rain.","I left home early, around eight, and took a bus to the edge of the city. From there, I walked for about twenty minutes until I reached the start of the trail. At first the hike was easy. The road was wide, and I could walk fast. But after a while it became narrower and steeper. I had to slow down and watch my steps. I met a few people on the way—some were jogging, some were walking dogs, and others were taking photos.","My favorite part was the view near the top. There was a quiet spot with rocks and tall trees, and I could see the city in the distance. I sat there for a while, ate my sandwich, and just listened to the wind. It felt peaceful, like my mind finally had space.","On the way down, I took a different route. It was a bit longer, but it was more interesting because it went next to a small river. I almost slipped once, but luckily I caught myself. When I got back home in the afternoon, I was tired, but in a good way. My legs hurt a little, but my mood was much better.","Now I want to do this more often. It doesn’t cost much, it reduces stress, and it helps me feel more active. Next time, I’m going to invite a friend, but I’m also fine going alone."],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"What did Alex check the night before?","answer":"The weather","options":["The weather","A flight booking","His work emails"],"evidence":"I checked the weather","id":"a16-1"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What happened to the path as he climbed?","answer":"It became narrower and steeper.","options":["It became narrower and steeper.","It became a motorway.","It disappeared under snow."],"evidence":"narrower and steeper","id":"a16-2"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What was special about the return route?","answer":"It followed a small river.","options":["It followed a small river.","It was much shorter.","It passed through an airport."],"evidence":"next to a small river","id":"a16-3"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"Alex took a light jacket.","answer":"True","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"a light jacket","id":"a16-4"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"He definitely wants to avoid hiking alone next time.","answer":"False","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"I’m also fine going alone","id":"a16-5"},{"type":"sequence","prompt":"Put these events or ideas in the order they appear in the recording.","events":[["He packed a backpack.","packed a small backpack"],["He sat near the top.","view near the top"],["He took a different way down.","a different route"]],"answer":["He packed a backpack.","He sat near the top.","He took a different way down."],"evidence":"packed a small backpack / view near the top / a different route","id":"a16-6"}],"vocab":[["muddy","covered with wet soil"],["steep","rising or falling at a sharp angle"],["route","a way taken from one place to another"],["slip","lose your footing unintentionally"],["in the distance","far away but still visible"]]},{"n":17,"title":"How do you keep fit?","speaker":"Chris","audio":"https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/67aa2baa0c65412632c4b3d1/6975cb76b0ff80ea782e87a7_SCRIPT%201%20(Male%20speaker%20%E2%80%94%20Chris)_%20How%20do%20you%20keep%20fit_%20(B1).mp3","transcript":["Hi, I’m Chris. I try to keep fit in simple ways because I don’t love strict gym routines. On weekdays, I walk a lot—usually 8,000 to 10,000 steps—because I get off the bus two stops early and walk the rest. Two evenings a week, I do a short workout at home: push-ups, squats, and planks. It’s only about 20 minutes, but I do it regularly, and that’s what matters. On Saturdays, I go cycling with a friend in a park near my apartment. The hardest part for me is motivation after a long day, so I keep everything easy and realistic. When I feel stressed, even a quick walk helps me clear my head and sleep better."],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"How does Chris add walking to his commute?","answer":"He gets off the bus two stops early.","options":["He gets off the bus two stops early.","He runs beside the train.","He walks only on Sundays."],"evidence":"two stops early","id":"a17-1"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How long is his home workout?","answer":"About twenty minutes","options":["About twenty minutes","Two hours","Five minutes"],"evidence":"about 20 minutes","id":"a17-2"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is his biggest difficulty?","answer":"Motivation after a long day","options":["Motivation after a long day","Buying exercise equipment","Finding a park"],"evidence":"motivation after a long day","id":"a17-3"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"He cycles with a friend on Saturdays.","answer":"True","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"On Saturdays, I go cycling with a friend","id":"a17-4"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"He loves strict gym routines.","answer":"False","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"I don’t love strict gym routines","id":"a17-5"},{"type":"sequence","prompt":"Put these events or ideas in the order they appear in the recording.","events":[["He describes weekday walking.","On weekdays"],["He describes evening workouts.","Two evenings a week"],["He describes Saturday cycling.","On Saturdays"]],"answer":["He describes weekday walking.","He describes evening workouts.","He describes Saturday cycling."],"evidence":"On weekdays / Two evenings a week / On Saturdays","id":"a17-6"}],"vocab":[["keep fit","maintain physical health and condition"],["workout","a session of physical exercise"],["regularly","at repeated or consistent intervals"],["realistic","practical and achievable"],["clear my head","reduce mental stress or confusion"]]},{"n":18,"title":"Community Gardens in the City","speaker":"Sophie","audio":"https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/67aa2baa0c65412632c4b3d1/69763a21cb59e12ed442e2e7_Community%20Gardens%20in%20the%20City_.mp3","transcript":["Hi, I’m Sophie. Today I want to talk about something I really like in big cities: community gardens.","A community garden is a small green space where people grow vegetables, herbs, and flowers together. It can be in a quiet corner of a park, behind apartment buildings, or even on a rooftop. At first, I thought it was only for people who already knew a lot about plants. But then I joined one near my home, and I learned that beginners are welcome too.","In our garden, everyone has a small job. Some people water the plants, others pull weeds, and someone checks if the soil is dry. We grow tomatoes, cucumbers, mint, basil, and sometimes strawberries. It’s not a perfect system—sometimes we forget to water, and sometimes insects eat the leaves. But we help each other, and the garden still grows.","For me, the best part is not only the food. It’s the feeling of community. I meet neighbors I never talked to before. We share seeds, we share advice, and sometimes we share simple recipes. It also changes the way I think about shopping. When you grow even a little food, you understand how much work is behind it.","Another thing I like is how it helps the city feel calmer. After a stressful day, I can spend thirty minutes in the garden, and my mind slows down. I don’t look at my phone so much, and I feel more present.","In the future, I think community gardens will become even more important. Cities are getting bigger, and people want more nature, cleaner air, and healthier habits. A small garden won’t solve every problem, but it can make everyday life better—one green corner at a time."],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"Where did Sophie join a garden?","answer":"Near her home","options":["Near her home","Inside her office","In another country"],"evidence":"one near my home","id":"a18-1"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What surprised her about joining?","answer":"Beginners were welcome.","options":["Beginners were welcome.","Only experts could join.","No vegetables could be grown."],"evidence":"beginners are welcome too","id":"a18-2"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does she value beyond the food?","answer":"The feeling of community","options":["The feeling of community","The chance to avoid all neighbours","Winning prizes"],"evidence":"the feeling of community","id":"a18-3"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"The garden sometimes has problems with insects.","answer":"True","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"insects eat the leaves","id":"a18-4"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"Sophie believes one small garden will solve every urban problem.","answer":"False","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"won’t solve every problem","id":"a18-5"},{"type":"sequence","prompt":"Put these events or ideas in the order they appear in the recording.","events":[["She joins a garden.","I joined one"],["She describes shared jobs.","everyone has a small job"],["She explains community benefits.","feeling of community"]],"answer":["She joins a garden.","She describes shared jobs.","She explains community benefits."],"evidence":"I joined one / everyone has a small job / feeling of community","id":"a18-6"}],"vocab":[["herb","a plant used for flavour or other purposes"],["weed","an unwanted plant growing among cultivated plants"],["soil","the ground in which plants grow"],["seed","the small part of a plant from which a new plant grows"],["present","mentally attentive to what is happening now"]]},{"n":19,"title":"The Problem with Fast Fashion","speaker":"Jake","audio":"https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/67aa2baa0c65412632c4b3d1/69772745c25f5c43d958f016_The%20Problem%20with%20Fast%20Fashion_.mp3","transcript":["Hi, I’m Jake. Today I want to talk about fast fashion and why it has become a big problem.","Fast fashion means cheap clothes that are produced very quickly. New styles appear in shops every week, so people feel they need to buy something new all the time. At first, it sounds great: the prices are low, and you can change your look often. But there is a serious cost behind it.","One problem is waste. Many clothes are not made to last long. After a few washes, they can lose shape, get holes, or look old. People throw them away, and huge amounts of clothing end up in landfills. Some clothes are even burned, which also harms the environment.","Another problem is pollution. Making fabric uses a lot of water and energy. Some factories also use strong chemicals to dye clothes, and these chemicals can go into rivers. There is also a lot of pollution from transport, because clothes are often made in one country and sold in another.","Fast fashion can also affect workers. In some places, people work long hours for low pay. They may not have safe conditions, but the companies still want to produce faster and cheaper.","So what can we do? I think we can start with small changes. We can buy fewer items and choose better quality. We can repair clothes instead of throwing them away. We can also buy second-hand clothes, swap with friends, or donate what we don’t need. Fashion can be fun, but it is even better when it is more responsible."],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does fast fashion involve?","answer":"Cheap clothes produced quickly","options":["Cheap clothes produced quickly","Only handmade luxury items","Clothes designed to last forever"],"evidence":"cheap clothes that are produced very quickly","id":"a19-1"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Where does much discarded clothing end up?","answer":"Landfills","options":["Landfills","Museums","Schools"],"evidence":"end up in landfills","id":"a19-2"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which action does Jake recommend?","answer":"Repairing clothes","options":["Repairing clothes","Buying something new every week","Throwing away all old clothes"],"evidence":"repair clothes","id":"a19-3"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"Clothing production uses water and energy.","answer":"True","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"a lot of water and energy","id":"a19-4"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"Workers always have safe conditions.","answer":"False","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"They may not have safe conditions","id":"a19-5"},{"type":"sequence","prompt":"Put these events or ideas in the order they appear in the recording.","events":[["Jake explains waste.","One problem is waste"],["He explains pollution.","Another problem is pollution"],["He suggests changes.","start with small changes"]],"answer":["Jake explains waste.","He explains pollution.","He suggests changes."],"evidence":"One problem is waste / Another problem is pollution / start with small changes","id":"a19-6"}],"vocab":[["landfill","a place where waste is buried"],["fabric","material used to make clothing"],["dye","colour a material using a substance"],["second-hand","previously owned by another person"],["swap","exchange one thing for another"]]},{"n":20,"title":"Talking About Money","speaker":"Ethan","audio":"https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/67aa2baa0c65412632c4b3d1/696f5d6cefd9e406c6222b80_SCRIPT%203%20(Male%20speaker%20%E2%80%94%20Ethan)_%20A%20mistake%20I%20made%20with%20money.mp3","transcript":["Hi, I’m Ethan, and my relationship with money has changed a lot over the years. When I was a teenager, I didn’t really think about it. If I had some cash, I spent it. I bought snacks, games, and things I didn’t need. I didn’t save because I felt like saving was only for “older people.”","My first real job changed that. I worked part-time and got paid every two weeks. At first, it felt amazing. I finally had my own income, so I treated myself all the time. But then I had a month where everything happened at once: my phone broke, my friend had a birthday, and I had to pay for a course. I realised I had no safety net. I wasn’t in debt, but I felt stressed because I couldn’t cover everything comfortably.","After that, I started doing something simple. I divided my money into three parts: essentials, savings, and “fun.” Essentials were transport, food, and phone bills. Savings were small at first—maybe 10%. Fun was whatever was left. The system was not perfect, but it gave me control. I stopped feeling guilty about spending because I knew I had already saved something.","One of the biggest lessons I learned was about subscriptions and small daily spending. I used to think, “It’s only a coffee,” or “It’s only a small fee,” but those small costs add up. When I checked my bank app, I was shocked. I had multiple subscriptions I didn’t even use, and they were quietly taking money every month. I cancelled most of them, and the difference was real.","Now, I still enjoy life, but I try to be intentional. I don’t buy things just because they are on sale. I ask myself, “Would I buy this at full price?” If the answer is no, I usually skip it. I also try to save for experiences, not only for objects. For me, money is not only about having more. It’s about having less stress and more freedom."],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"What problem exposed Ethan’s lack of savings?","answer":"Several expenses arrived in one month.","options":["Several expenses arrived in one month.","He lost all his income forever.","He bought a house."],"evidence":"everything happened at once","id":"a20-1"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How did he divide his budget?","answer":"Essentials, savings and fun","options":["Essentials, savings and fun","Travel, rent and gifts only","Cash and debt only"],"evidence":"I divided my money into three parts","id":"a20-2"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What did he cancel after checking his bank app?","answer":"Unused subscriptions","options":["Unused subscriptions","His job","All transport"],"evidence":"I cancelled most of them","id":"a20-3"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"He was in debt during the difficult month.","answer":"False","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"I wasn’t in debt","id":"a20-4"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"He now asks whether he would pay full price.","answer":"True","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"Would I buy this at full price?","id":"a20-5"},{"type":"sequence","prompt":"Put these events or ideas in the order they appear in the recording.","events":[["He spent freely as a teenager.","When I was a teenager"],["He created a three-part budget.","three parts"],["He cancelled unused subscriptions.","I cancelled most of them"]],"answer":["He spent freely as a teenager.","He created a three-part budget.","He cancelled unused subscriptions."],"evidence":"When I was a teenager / three parts / I cancelled most of them","id":"a20-6"}],"vocab":[["income","money earned or received regularly"],["safety net","something that protects you during difficulty"],["essentials","things that are necessary"],["subscription","a regular payment for continuing access"],["intentional","done with a clear purpose"]]},{"n":21,"title":"Moments that changed my opinion","speaker":"Ethan","audio":"https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/67aa2baa0c65412632c4b3d1/696f6fb24f6ac5577ca7cb94_SCRIPT%201%20(Male%20speaker%20%E2%80%94%20Ethan)_%20I%20changed%20my%20opinion%20about%20public%20transport.mp3","transcript":["Hi, I’m Ethan. For a long time, I thought public transport was always uncomfortable. In my mind, buses were slow, trains were crowded, and the subway was stressful. I preferred driving because I liked feeling independent. If I needed to go somewhere, I would take my car even for short trips.","But last year, something happened that changed my opinion. My car broke down on a Monday morning, right before a busy week. I had meetings, errands, and a dentist appointment. At first, I panicked because I thought I wouldn’t manage without a car. Still, I didn’t have a choice, so I bought a weekly pass and started using the subway and buses.","The first surprise was time. I expected my commute to be longer, but it wasn’t. In fact, it was sometimes faster because I didn’t sit in traffic. The second surprise was how much I could do while travelling. I listened to podcasts, answered messages, and even read a few chapters of a book. When I drove, I couldn’t do any of that.","Of course, it wasn’t perfect. Some days the train was full, and once a bus was late. But I realised something important: I didn’t need public transport to be perfect. I just needed it to be reliable most of the time. And it was.","Another thing that changed my mind was money. When I calculated the cost of petrol, parking, and small repairs, I saw how expensive driving was. I hadn’t noticed it before because I paid little by little. But when I looked at the numbers, the difference was clear.","Now I still drive sometimes, especially on weekends or when I go out of town. But during the week, I often choose public transport. I used to think it was only for people who don’t have a car. Now I think it’s simply a smart option, and it makes city life easier."],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"What forced Ethan to use public transport?","answer":"His car broke down.","options":["His car broke down.","He moved to another country.","He lost his driving licence."],"evidence":"My car broke down","id":"a21-1"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What surprised him about commuting time?","answer":"It was sometimes faster.","options":["It was sometimes faster.","It always doubled.","It took all day."],"evidence":"sometimes faster","id":"a21-2"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What costs did he compare?","answer":"Petrol, parking and repairs","options":["Petrol, parking and repairs","Only train tickets","Only insurance"],"evidence":"petrol, parking, and small repairs","id":"a21-3"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"He never drives now.","answer":"False","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"I still drive sometimes","id":"a21-4"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"He could read while travelling by public transport.","answer":"True","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"read a few chapters of a book","id":"a21-5"},{"type":"sequence","prompt":"Put these events or ideas in the order they appear in the recording.","events":[["His car stopped working.","My car broke down"],["He bought a weekly pass.","bought a weekly pass"],["He compared driving costs.","calculated the cost"]],"answer":["His car stopped working.","He bought a weekly pass.","He compared driving costs."],"evidence":"My car broke down / bought a weekly pass / calculated the cost","id":"a21-6"}],"vocab":[["errand","a short journey to do a practical task"],["weekly pass","a ticket valid for a week"],["reliable","able to be trusted to work well"],["repair","an action that fixes something damaged"],["little by little","gradually in small amounts"]]},{"n":22,"title":"Why Small Habits Matter","speaker":"Olivia","audio":"https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/67aa2baa0c65412632c4b3d1/697650def12e55efd0d8e96f_Why%20Small%20Habits%20Matter.mp3","transcript":["Hi, I’m Olivia. Today I want to talk about something simple but powerful: small habits and how they can change your life over time.","People often think they need a big plan to improve. They say, “On Monday, I will change everything.” But in real life, big changes can feel scary, and we get tired quickly. That’s why small habits work better for many people. They are easy to start, and they don’t feel heavy.","For example, last year I wanted to be healthier, but I didn’t want to go to the gym every day. So I started with a short walk after dinner. At first, it was only ten minutes. Some days I didn’t feel motivated, but ten minutes felt possible. After a few weeks, my walk became longer, and I noticed I was sleeping better. I didn’t “force” a new lifestyle. I built it slowly.","Small habits can also help with learning. If you study for five minutes a day, it sounds too little, right? But five minutes every day is better than one long study session once a month. You remember more, and you feel more confident because you keep going. The same idea works with cleaning, saving money, or even improving your mood.","Of course, small habits are not magic. You still need patience. Sometimes you will miss a day, and that’s normal. The important thing is to return to the habit without feeling guilty. Progress is not perfect. It is consistent.","In the future, I want to keep focusing on small steps. I don’t need to change my whole life in one week. I just need to choose one simple habit, repeat it, and let time do the work."],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"What habit did Olivia start?","answer":"A short walk after dinner","options":["A short walk after dinner","A daily two-hour gym session","Buying new exercise clothes"],"evidence":"a short walk after dinner","id":"a22-1"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How long were the first walks?","answer":"Ten minutes","options":["Ten minutes","An hour","Thirty seconds"],"evidence":"only ten minutes","id":"a22-2"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does she advise after missing a day?","answer":"Return without guilt","options":["Return without guilt","Give up permanently","Double every task immediately"],"evidence":"return to the habit without feeling guilty","id":"a22-3"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"Olivia says small habits are magic.","answer":"False","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"small habits are not magic","id":"a22-4"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"Daily five-minute study can beat one monthly long session.","answer":"True","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"better than one long study session once a month","id":"a22-5"},{"type":"sequence","prompt":"Put these events or ideas in the order they appear in the recording.","events":[["She starts a short walk.","started with a short walk"],["The walks grow longer.","my walk became longer"],["She notices better sleep.","I was sleeping better"]],"answer":["She starts a short walk.","The walks grow longer.","She notices better sleep."],"evidence":"started with a short walk / my walk became longer / I was sleeping better","id":"a22-6"}],"vocab":[["motivated","feeling willing and ready to act"],["patience","ability to wait or continue calmly"],["guilty","feeling bad about something you have done"],["consistent","maintained regularly over time"],["over time","gradually as time passes"]]},{"n":23,"title":"Social Media Billionaires","speaker":"Alex","audio":"https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/67aa2baa0c65412632c4b3d1/69760de39b53bdf7dbf45b9b_Social%20Media%20Billionaires_.mp3","transcript":["Hi, I’m Alex. When people talk about new, modern billionaires, many of them made their fortune from social networks and online communities.","A good example is Mark Zuckerberg. He started Facebook as a small project, and it later grew into a huge company with different apps and services. Another example is Evan Spiegel, one of the creators of Snapchat. His idea was simple: people wanted quick photos and messages that feel private and fun.","There are other stories too. Jack Dorsey helped build Twitter, and later he worked on payment technology as well. And in recent years, TikTok became a global trend. Its parent company is ByteDance, founded by Zhang Yiming. A product like that can grow very fast because people share videos every day.","What do these stories have in common? First, they built something people use daily. Second, they grew through sharing and strong communities. And third, they earned money through ads, partnerships, and sometimes subscriptions.","But it’s not only success. Social networks also bring big problems: privacy, fake news, addiction, and pressure from governments. In the future, new billionaires may come from creator tools, short video, and AI features inside social apps. The main lesson is simple: build something useful, and protect trust—because trust is the real long-term value."],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"Who does Alex identify as a creator of Snapchat?","answer":"Evan Spiegel","options":["Evan Spiegel","Jack Dorsey","Zhang Yiming"],"evidence":"Evan Spiegel","id":"a23-1"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which company is TikTok’s parent in this recording?","answer":"ByteDance","options":["ByteDance","Snapchat","Facebook"],"evidence":"ByteDance","id":"a23-2"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does Alex describe as long-term value?","answer":"Trust","options":["Trust","Only follower numbers","Only fast growth"],"evidence":"trust is the real long-term value","id":"a23-3"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"The recording says every social network earns only through subscriptions.","answer":"False","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"ads, partnerships, and sometimes subscriptions","id":"a23-4"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"Privacy is mentioned as a problem.","answer":"True","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"privacy, fake news, addiction","id":"a23-5"},{"type":"sequence","prompt":"Put these events or ideas in the order they appear in the recording.","events":[["Alex gives Facebook as an example.","Mark Zuckerberg"],["He describes Snapchat.","Evan Spiegel"],["He discusses risks and trust.","But it’s not only success"]],"answer":["Alex gives Facebook as an example.","He describes Snapchat.","He discusses risks and trust."],"evidence":"Mark Zuckerberg / Evan Spiegel / But it’s not only success","id":"a23-6"}],"vocab":[["fortune","a large amount of wealth"],["community","a group connected by shared interests or location"],["partnership","an arrangement to work together"],["privacy","control over personal information"],["trust","belief that someone or something is reliable"]]},{"n":24,"title":"Why Coffee Shops Became So Popular Worldwide","speaker":"Natalie","audio":"https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/67aa2baa0c65412632c4b3d1/6975ed1d4e7be7358d249eae_Why%20Coffee%20Shops%20Became%20So%20Popular%20Worldwide.mp3","transcript":["Hi, I’m Natalie. Today I want to talk about something we see almost everywhere: coffee shops. In many cities, it feels like there is a café on every corner. In my opinion, coffee shops became so popular worldwide not only because of coffee, but because of the experience and the lifestyle they offer.","First, coffee shops are comfortable “third places.” Home is the first place, work or school is the second, and a café becomes the third. People go there to relax, meet friends, or simply sit alone with a book. The atmosphere is usually warm: soft music, nice smells, and a calm feeling. Even if you are busy, you can take a short break and feel better in ten minutes.","Second, coffee shops are useful for modern life. Many people work remotely now, so they need a place with Wi-Fi, a table, and a bit of background noise. A café gives you that without the pressure of an office. You can answer emails, study, or prepare for an exam. Some cafés even design their space for this: they have sockets, bigger tables, and quiet corners.","Another reason is social media. Coffee drinks look beautiful. People love taking photos of cappuccino foam, colorful lattes, and desserts. Cafés often understand this and create “Instagram-friendly” spaces. This makes the café popular online, and then more people want to visit it in real life.","Finally, coffee culture has become international. Years ago, many people drank simple instant coffee at home. Now we have espresso, latte, flat white, cold brew, and many other options. People like to try new tastes and feel a little “modern” and global.","So, I think coffee shops went global because they are more than a place to drink coffee. They are places to rest, work, connect, and feel part of city life."],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"What does Natalie mean by a third place?","answer":"A place beyond home and work or school","options":["A place beyond home and work or school","The third café on a street","A private office"],"evidence":"Home is the first place","id":"a24-1"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What makes cafés useful for remote work?","answer":"Wi-Fi, a table and background noise","options":["Wi-Fi, a table and background noise","A compulsory uniform","Free accommodation"],"evidence":"Wi-Fi, a table","id":"a24-2"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How can social media help a café?","answer":"Photos can attract visitors.","options":["Photos can attract visitors.","It removes all running costs.","It replaces staff entirely."],"evidence":"popular online","id":"a24-3"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"Natalie says cafés are popular only because of coffee.","answer":"False","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"not only because of coffee","id":"a24-4"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"Some cafés provide sockets and quiet corners.","answer":"True","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"sockets, bigger tables, and quiet corners","id":"a24-5"},{"type":"sequence","prompt":"Put these events or ideas in the order they appear in the recording.","events":[["Natalie explains the third-place idea.","third places"],["She explains remote-work needs.","work remotely"],["She explains social-media influence.","Another reason is social media"]],"answer":["Natalie explains the third-place idea.","She explains remote-work needs.","She explains social-media influence."],"evidence":"third places / work remotely / Another reason is social media","id":"a24-6"}],"vocab":[["remote work","work done away from a traditional office"],["background noise","sounds present behind the main activity"],["socket","a point for connecting electrical equipment"],["instant coffee","coffee made quickly by adding water to granules"],["connect","form relationships or communicate with others"]]},{"n":25,"title":"why languages die.","speaker":"Ethan","audio":"https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/67aa2baa0c65412632c4b3d1/697661dfff50ab3842cebebe_why%20languages%20die..mp3","transcript":["Hi, I’m Ethan. Today I want to talk about a sad but important topic: why languages die.","A language usually dies when people stop using it in daily life. This often happens slowly, over many years. One common reason is that a bigger, more powerful language becomes more useful. For example, if a country has one official language for school, work, and government, parents may decide to teach their children that language first. They want their kids to have better jobs and more opportunities. As a result, the small local language stays only with older people.","Another reason is migration. When families move to a new city or another country, they often change the language they speak at home. Children quickly learn the new language from school and friends, and they may feel shy about using the old language. Over time, they understand it, but they stop speaking it.","History also plays a big role. Some languages disappear because of war, colonization, or political pressure. In some places, people were punished for speaking their own language, so they stopped using it to protect themselves.","The problem is that when a language dies, we lose more than words. We lose stories, songs, jokes, and a special way of thinking. The good news is that some communities are trying to save their languages by teaching them in schools, making apps, and creating videos online. It’s not easy, but it can work if young people feel proud to speak their language."],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"What often happens when a local language declines?","answer":"It remains mainly with older people.","options":["It remains mainly with older people.","Everyone starts writing novels in it.","It becomes every country’s official language."],"evidence":"stays only with older people","id":"a25-1"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What can migration change?","answer":"The language spoken at home","options":["The language spoken at home","The age of parents","All school subjects overnight"],"evidence":"change the language they speak at home","id":"a25-2"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How do some communities try to preserve languages?","answer":"Teaching, apps and online videos","options":["Teaching, apps and online videos","Banning young speakers","Stopping all school lessons"],"evidence":"teaching them in schools, making apps","id":"a25-3"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"Language loss affects stories and ways of thinking.","answer":"True","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"We lose stories, songs, jokes","id":"a25-4"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"Political pressure has never caused language decline.","answer":"False","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"political pressure","id":"a25-5"},{"type":"sequence","prompt":"Put these events or ideas in the order they appear in the recording.","events":[["Ethan explains powerful languages.","a bigger, more powerful language"],["He explains migration.","Another reason is migration"],["He explains preservation efforts.","trying to save their languages"]],"answer":["Ethan explains powerful languages.","He explains migration.","He explains preservation efforts."],"evidence":"a bigger, more powerful language / Another reason is migration / trying to save their languages","id":"a25-6"}],"vocab":[["migration","moving to live in another place"],["official language","a language used formally by a state"],["opportunity","a chance to do something useful"],["preserve","keep something from being lost or damaged"],["heritage","traditions and culture received from the past"]]},{"n":26,"title":"Biography of Mozart","speaker":"Jake","audio":"https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/67aa2baa0c65412632c4b3d1/6977220963e248121d7c7f12_Biography%20of%20Mozart.mp3","transcript":["Hi, I’m Jake. Today I want to tell you about the life of Wolfgang Amadeus Mozart, one of the most famous composers in history.","Mozart was born in 1756 in Salzburg, which is now in Austria. His family was very musical. His father, Leopold Mozart, was a musician and a music teacher. He noticed very early that Wolfgang had a special talent. When Mozart was only a small child, he could play the keyboard and understand music in a surprising way.","Mozart did not have a normal childhood. Instead of spending his time only with toys and games, he practiced music many hours a day. His father took him and his sister, Nannerl, on long trips around Europe. They played music for kings, queens, and rich families. People were amazed because Mozart was so young, but he played like an adult professional.","As he got older, Mozart wanted more freedom. He left Salzburg and moved to Vienna, a city full of music and culture. In Vienna, he worked as a composer and pianist. He wrote music for concerts, for the church, and for the theatre. He became especially famous for his operas, such as The Marriage of Figaro, Don Giovanni, and The Magic Flute. His music was full of emotion, energy, and beautiful melodies.","However, Mozart’s life was not always easy. He often had money problems, because being a freelance musician was risky. He worked very hard, but he did not always earn enough.","Sadly, Mozart died very young, in 1791, when he was only 35 years old. Even though his life was short, Mozart wrote more than 600 pieces of music. Today, people still listen to his work all over the world, and many musicians say his music is some of the best ever created."],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"Where was Mozart born?","answer":"Salzburg","options":["Salzburg","Vienna","Paris"],"evidence":"in Salzburg","id":"a26-1"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Who recognised his talent early?","answer":"His father Leopold","options":["His father Leopold","His employer in Vienna","A television producer"],"evidence":"His father, Leopold Mozart","id":"a26-2"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Which city did he move to for more freedom?","answer":"Vienna","options":["Vienna","Rome","Berlin"],"evidence":"moved to Vienna","id":"a26-3"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"Mozart’s life was always financially easy.","answer":"False","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"money problems","id":"a26-4"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"He wrote more than six hundred pieces according to Jake.","answer":"True","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"more than 600 pieces","id":"a26-5"},{"type":"sequence","prompt":"Put these events or ideas in the order they appear in the recording.","events":[["He travelled with his father and sister.","long trips around Europe"],["He moved to Vienna.","moved to Vienna"],["He died in 1791.","in 1791"]],"answer":["He travelled with his father and sister.","He moved to Vienna.","He died in 1791."],"evidence":"long trips around Europe / moved to Vienna / in 1791","id":"a26-6"}],"vocab":[["composer","a person who writes music"],["talent","a natural ability to do something well"],["freelance","working independently for different clients"],["melody","a sequence of musical notes forming a tune"],["opera","a dramatic performance told through singing and music"]]},{"n":27,"title":"Hidden Underground Tunnel Network","speaker":"Jake","audio":"https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/67aa2baa0c65412632c4b3d1/69776d90f0ad342d528014f8_Hidden%20Underground%20Tunnel%20Network.mp3","transcript":["Hi, I’m Jake. Today I want to tell you about something unusual under many big cities: hidden underground tunnels. I don’t mean modern metro lines. I’m talking about old service tunnels that people don’t notice, and sometimes they don’t even know they exist.","In one city I visited, there was a small museum tour about these tunnels. Many of them were built a long time ago for practical reasons. Some tunnels carried water, some were used for heating pipes, and others were made for delivering goods between buildings. During cold winters, workers could move underground and avoid bad weather. In the past, it was also safer and faster than using crowded streets.","Now most of these tunnels are closed to the public. They can be dangerous because the floors are wet, the air is heavy, and parts of the tunnels may be unstable. Some places also have old cables, broken stairs, or deep holes. That’s why legal tours usually show only a small, safe section. On the tour, we walked through a short corridor with low ceilings, and we saw signs on the walls, old metal doors, and pipes that were no longer used. The guide told us that some tunnels connect important buildings, like old stations, warehouses, and basements of historic hotels.","What I found most interesting was the feeling down there. Above ground, the city was loud and full of traffic. Underground, it was quiet, cool, and a bit creepy. You could hear water drops and your own footsteps. It made me think about how cities have “two lives”: the one we see every day, and the one hidden below.","I’m not planning to explore tunnels by myself, because it’s risky and often illegal. But as a story and a piece of history, underground tunnels are fascinating. They show how people solved problems in the past—and how much is still hidden under our feet."],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"What kind of tunnels does Jake describe?","answer":"Old service tunnels","options":["Old service tunnels","Modern metro lines only","New tourist shopping tunnels"],"evidence":"old service tunnels","id":"a27-1"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why do legal tours show limited sections?","answer":"Other sections can be dangerous.","options":["Other sections can be dangerous.","The tunnels are all brightly lit.","There is no history elsewhere."],"evidence":"They can be dangerous","id":"a27-2"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"How did underground life feel compared with above ground?","answer":"Quiet, cool and a bit creepy","options":["Quiet, cool and a bit creepy","Hot and full of car traffic","Exactly the same"],"evidence":"quiet, cool, and a bit creepy","id":"a27-3"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"Jake plans to explore tunnels on his own.","answer":"False","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"I’m not planning to explore tunnels by myself","id":"a27-4"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"Some tunnels were used to deliver goods.","answer":"True","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"delivering goods between buildings","id":"a27-5"},{"type":"sequence","prompt":"Put these events or ideas in the order they appear in the recording.","events":[["He joins a museum tour.","a small museum tour"],["He walks through a short corridor.","walked through a short corridor"],["He reflects on two city lives.","cities have “two lives”"]],"answer":["He joins a museum tour.","He walks through a short corridor.","He reflects on two city lives."],"evidence":"a small museum tour / walked through a short corridor / cities have “two lives”","id":"a27-6"}],"vocab":[["service tunnel","an underground passage for utilities or practical work"],["unstable","not secure and liable to change or collapse"],["warehouse","a building used to store goods"],["creepy","causing an uneasy or frightened feeling"],["beneath","under or below something"]]},{"n":28,"title":"The Voice Behind the Metro Announcements","speaker":"Ryan","audio":"https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/67aa2baa0c65412632c4b3d1/69777117d897784581732ee9_The%20Voice%20Behind%20the%20Metro%20Announcements.mp3","transcript":["Hi, I’m Ryan. Today I want to tell you about a person most people never meet, but they hear every day: the woman behind the metro announcements.","In many cities, the voice that says things like “Mind the gap” or “Next station” belongs to a real voice actor. Let’s call her Claire. She is not famous like a movie star, but her voice is everywhere. Millions of commuters hear her on their way to work, school, or home, and most of them never ask, “Who is she?”","Claire started as a radio student. She practiced clear pronunciation and calm intonation. One day, she saw an advertisement for a recording job. The city transport company wanted a voice that sounded friendly, confident, and easy to understand. They tested many people, and Claire got the job.","Recording announcements is more difficult than it seems. Claire had to read hundreds of station names, warnings, and short messages. She repeated the same words again and again, but she needed to keep the tone consistent. Sometimes the producer stopped her and said, “A bit slower,” or “More neutral,” or “Smile with your voice.” She also learned how to pronounce names from different languages correctly, which took time and patience.","What I find interesting is how anonymous the job is. Claire can sit in a café and hear her own voice from a speaker, but nobody recognizes her. At first, that felt strange. Later, she said it felt powerful in a quiet way. Her voice helps people: tourists, older passengers, parents with children, and anyone who is tired after a long day.","So next time you hear a calm voice in public transport, remember: it might belong to someone who works hard behind the scenes—just to make your trip a little easier."],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"What kind of work did Claire train for initially?","answer":"Radio","options":["Radio","Medicine","Architecture"],"evidence":"a radio student","id":"a28-1"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What did the transport company want from a voice?","answer":"Friendly, confident and easy to understand","options":["Friendly, confident and easy to understand","Famous and very loud","Fast and dramatic"],"evidence":"friendly, confident, and easy to understand","id":"a28-2"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What was a recording challenge?","answer":"Keeping the tone consistent","options":["Keeping the tone consistent","Writing every train timetable","Building speakers"],"evidence":"keep the tone consistent","id":"a28-3"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"Claire is recognised by everyone who hears her voice.","answer":"False","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"nobody recognizes her","id":"a28-4"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"She had to learn names from different languages.","answer":"True","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"names from different languages correctly","id":"a28-5"},{"type":"sequence","prompt":"Put these events or ideas in the order they appear in the recording.","events":[["She sees a recording-job advertisement.","advertisement for a recording job"],["She gets the job.","Claire got the job"],["She records many station names.","hundreds of station names"]],"answer":["She sees a recording-job advertisement.","She gets the job.","She records many station names."],"evidence":"advertisement for a recording job / Claire got the job / hundreds of station names","id":"a28-6"}],"vocab":[["commuter","someone who travels regularly to work"],["intonation","the rise and fall of the voice in speech"],["neutral","not showing strong emotion or taking sides"],["anonymous","not publicly identified or recognised"],["behind the scenes","working without being seen by the public"]]},{"n":29,"title":"Burgers Made From Insects","speaker":"Jordan","audio":"https://cdn.prod.website-files.com/67aa2baa0c65412632c4b3d1/697759a686e83767e9645767_Burgers%20Made%20From%20Insects_.mp3","transcript":["Hi, I’m Jordan. Today I want to talk about a food idea that sounds strange to many people: burgers made from insects. In some countries, people have eaten insects for a long time, but in Europe and the US it is still unusual. Now, some companies are making insect burgers and trying to sell them as a modern, eco-friendly choice.","So, what are these burgers made of? Usually, the insects are dried and ground into a fine powder. Then the powder is mixed with other ingredients, like spices, vegetables, or grains, to create a patty. The goal is to make something that looks and tastes similar to a normal burger, but with a different source of protein.","One reason people talk about insect burgers is the environment. Some experts say insects need less land, less water, and less food than cows. They also produce fewer greenhouse gases. Because of this, insect-based food is sometimes presented as a possible solution for the future, especially as the world population grows.","Another reason is nutrition. Insects can be high in protein, and they may contain useful vitamins and minerals. Supporters say it’s a smart way to get protein without using so many natural resources.","But there are also problems. The biggest one is psychology. Many people feel disgusted by the idea, even if the burger doesn’t look like insects. There are also questions about allergies. People who are allergic to shellfish might react to insects too, so clear labeling is important. And, of course, people want to know if it is safe, how it is produced, and what rules companies follow.","Personally, I think insect burgers are an interesting experiment. Some people will never try them, and that’s normal. But others may be curious, especially if the taste is good and the price is reasonable. Maybe in the future it will feel normal—just like sushi or tofu became normal in many places over time."],"tasks":[{"type":"choice","prompt":"How are the insects prepared in the described process?","answer":"Dried and ground into powder","options":["Dried and ground into powder","Served alive in a bun","Only frozen whole"],"evidence":"dried and ground into a fine powder","id":"a29-1"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"What is one environmental argument mentioned?","answer":"Insects may need fewer resources than cows.","options":["Insects may need fewer resources than cows.","Insects need no resources at all.","All transport pollution disappears."],"evidence":"less land, less water, and less food","id":"a29-2"},{"type":"choice","prompt":"Why is clear labelling important?","answer":"Some people may have allergic reactions.","options":["Some people may have allergic reactions.","It makes all food cheaper.","It hides the ingredients."],"evidence":"People who are allergic to shellfish","id":"a29-3"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"Everyone is expected to try insect burgers.","answer":"False","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"Some people will never try them","id":"a29-4"},{"type":"truth","prompt":"Supporters mention protein as a benefit.","answer":"True","options":["True","False"],"evidence":"high in protein","id":"a29-5"},{"type":"sequence","prompt":"Put these events or ideas in the order they appear in the recording.","events":[["Jordan explains making the patties.","powder is mixed"],["He explains environmental arguments.","the environment"],["He describes concerns and allergies.","there are also problems"]],"answer":["Jordan explains making the patties.","He explains environmental arguments.","He describes concerns and allergies."],"evidence":"powder is mixed / the environment / there are also problems","id":"a29-6"}],"vocab":[["patty","a flat round portion used in a burger"],["protein","a nutrient important for body tissues"],["resource","something used to produce or support things"],["label","information attached to a product"],["reasonable","fair, sensible or not excessive"]]}],"audioTranscriptMatchVerified":false,"hints":{"g1-1":"“Usually” describes Laura’s regular routine. Laura is one person: check the verb ending for a singular subject.","g1-2":"“Please wait” and “right now” place Mike’s action in progress. Look for be + an -ing form.","g1-3":"“These days” describes a changing shopping pattern. “Families” is plural: choose its form of be before adding -ing.","g1-4":"Here “think” introduces an opinion, not an activity of considering something. Ask whether continuous grammar fits that meaning.","g1-5":"Find the plural subject “The children”. Keep the auxiliary next to it; “getting” introduces the change in their independence.","g1-6":"“Doesn’t” already carries the third-person ending. Inspect “knows”: should the main verb carry that ending too?","g1-7":"“This month” limits the stay to a temporary period. Keep the subject I, then use be + the -ing form of stay.","g1-8":"Compare a neutral habit, “He always interrupts”, with “He is always interrupting me”. What feeling does the added emphasis suggest?","g2-1":"The printer problem has just been mentioned, and the speaker immediately offers help. This is a decision made during the conversation.","g2-2":"“Booked for 10 tomorrow” means the meeting is already arranged. Build a future arrangement with be + -ing.","g2-3":"“Already decided” tells you the intention existed before speaking. Agree be with Sofia, then use the requested future pattern.","g2-4":"The blank is inside the clause beginning with “when”. The main clause already gives the future; the time clause uses a present form.","g2-5":"“The next train” is a timetable item. Find its present-simple verb, then place “at seven” after the departure information.","g2-6":"“Will” is a modal. Its negative attaches not to the modal itself; do not add an auxiliary from present-simple grammar.","g2-7":"A booked meeting with Anna is an arrangement, not a routine. Use I + be + meet in its -ing form; keep the time.","g2-8":"“Look!” draws attention to something visible now. Is the prediction based on present evidence or only a general belief?","g3-1":"“For centuries” describes a state extending to now. Athens is singular; combine its perfect auxiliary with the participle of be.","g3-2":"Compare “has gone” with “has been”. Which one tells you that Maya’s visit is not yet a completed round trip?","g3-3":"“Twice” counts visits up to now, with no finished date given. We takes have; check the irregular participle of see.","g3-4":"The question asks whether completion has happened by now. Which of already and yet normally goes at the end of this question?","g3-5":"Keep “have” with the participle “visited”. The frequency word “never” belongs inside that perfect verb group.","g3-6":"“Yesterday” gives a finished past time. You need one past-simple verb rather than a perfect auxiliary plus participle.","g3-7":"Meeting in 2020 is a starting event; being friends is the continuing state. Use the state in the perfect and retain 2020 as the start.","g3-8":"The speaker connects losing the keys with the current situation. Ask what consequence matters now, rather than when it happened.","g4-1":"“2018” names when the friendship started, not its length. Select the connector for a starting point.","g4-2":"The words “nine to six” give the two ends of an opening-hours range. Which connector normally partners with to?","g4-3":"“Half an hour” answers “How long?”, not “When did it begin?”. Choose the duration connector.","g4-4":"“They got married” identifies the event when living here began. The following words form a starting-point clause.","g4-5":"Keep “From now on” together: it introduces a rule beginning now. Put the subject before the future verb group.","g4-6":"The knowing began last year and continues now. Change the tense of know; retain the starting-point phrase.","g4-7":"Three years ago is the start, but she still works here. Express the ongoing employment with a perfect form and a three-year duration.","g4-8":"Compare continuing perfect situations with a past-simple sentence that gives both a start and an end. An endpoint closes the period.","g5-1":"“The budget is ready” highlights a completed result. Choose the perfect form that focuses on checked figures, not the checking process.","g5-2":"“All morning” describes the activity’s duration, and tiredness is its current effect. Look for the perfect continuous structure.","g5-3":"“Three subscriptions” counts completed cancellations. She needs a singular perfect auxiliary; add the participle, not an -ing form.","g5-4":"“How long” asks about waiting in progress. Build have + been + -ing; write the missing parts in sentence order.","g5-5":"Owning a flat is a state. Keep the perfect auxiliary and participle together; place the duration after the possession.","g5-6":"Knowing someone describes a state. Preserve the ten-year duration but replace the continuous verb group with a simple perfect one.","g5-7":"From nine to eleven is a two-hour activity that is still happening. Use the perfect continuous and give that duration with for.","g5-8":"A completed-quantity question asks for a number of finished items. A duration question asks how long the activity has continued.","g6-1":"The manager’s call interrupts the checking. Describe the longer background action with was/were + -ing.","g6-2":"“Already” says the departure preceded our arrival. Use the perfect that looks back from another past event.","g6-3":"Finding a venue and sending invitations are finished story events. Use the irregular past-simple form of find.","g6-4":"People is plural. Their chatting was in progress during the microphone test: agree the past form of be with the subject.","g6-5":"The light repair happened before the event started. Keep the earlier action in its had + participle group after after.","g6-6":"“Did” already marks a past question. Check whether the following main verb should still have an -ed ending.","g6-7":"Arrival is the later event. Keep “When I arrived” in past simple and move the lecture’s earlier start into past perfect.","g6-8":"“Was packing” describes an action in progress at her arrival. Does this say the packing was already finished?","g7-1":"“But now I take the train” contrasts with a former driving habit. The blank precedes a base verb, not an -ing form.","g7-2":"“Feels normal now” describes being accustomed. In this pattern to is a preposition, so inspect the form after it.","g7-3":"“Did” already marks past time. Check the spelling of use in a question before the existing word to.","g7-4":"The route still feels strange: adaptation is happening, not finished. Complete the expression for becoming accustomed.","g7-5":"“Usually” modifies the main action listen. Place it after I but before that action; keep the travel phrase at the end.","g7-6":"In “is used to”, to is a preposition. Change the form of commute rather than treating it as an infinitive.","g7-7":"Daily driving was repeated in the past and no longer happens. Use the former-habit pattern followed by base drive.","g7-8":"“Getting” adds a process to “used to”. Is the speaker already fully comfortable, or still becoming comfortable?","g8-1":"After the blank comes “the evening”, a noun phrase. There is no subject + verb clause after the connector.","g8-2":"“About twenty minutes” measures the length of the walk. Choose the connector that answers “How long?”.","g8-3":"“My friend was resting” has its own subject and verb. Use the connector for two simultaneous actions.","g8-4":"“The storm” names an event. Locate the stay within that event rather than treating storm as a measured duration.","g8-5":"Keep “While I was waiting” as the background clause. The news-reading action forms the main clause that follows.","g8-6":"“Two hours” measures duration. Replace the connector for an event with the connector for a length of time.","g8-7":"Turn the noun phrase “my wait” into I + past continuous. Keep reading the magazine as the main event.","g8-8":"Falling asleep happens at a point inside the film. Does during itself tell you that the event lasted from start to finish?","g9-1":"“Three months ago” places the move in finished past time. Eliminate tenses that connect the action to an unfinished period.","g9-2":"“Cannot talk now” implies another action is occupying her. Use the form for choosing something in progress.","g9-3":"“Five boxes so far” counts completed results up to now. Use the perfect auxiliary for I with the participle of unpack.","g9-4":"Cooking was already happening when the furniture arrived. Use the past continuous for that background activity.","g9-5":"Keep “am going to” together as the future intention. Follow it with the base action and put tomorrow at the end.","g9-6":"The first clause gives a future promise. The clause beginning when uses a present form even though finishing is future.","g9-7":"Own describes possession, not an ongoing activity. Keep the requested perfect opening and express the five-year duration.","g9-8":"A background action was in progress around the main story event. Look for was/were plus an -ing verb.","g10-1":"The if-clause gives a realistic future condition. Its result needs a future modal followed by base feel.","g10-2":"Joining depends on returning the form. Choose a connector expressing a requirement, rather than prevention or contrast.","g10-3":"“Unless” already means “if not”. Inside that condition, use a present form of save without a future modal.","g10-4":"The blank follows “as soon as”, a future time connector. Lecture is singular: remember present-simple agreement.","g10-5":"Taking the notebook prepares for a possible phone problem. Keep the in case clause after the precaution.","g10-6":"The future meaning does not require will inside this if-clause. Replace the future group with the corresponding present form.","g10-7":"Begin with the negative result “I won’t join”. The saving condition must become an exception introduced by unless.","g10-8":"The charger is taken before the possible battery problem. Ask whether it is a precaution or evidence that the problem already happened.","g11-1":"“I would retrain” gives an imagined result. Match it with a past-form condition about possessing a free year.","g11-2":"“If I were you” introduces hypothetical advice. The blank needs the modal used for its suggested result.","g11-3":"Speaking French is imagined here. Choose the modal for ability in that hypothetical situation, not certainty or obligation.","g11-4":"“Wouldn’t apply” belongs to an unreal situation. The condition introduced by unless needs a past form of be.","g11-5":"This is a question about an imagined career change. Put the result modal before you; keep the if-condition afterward.","g11-6":"Would belongs in the result clause. Replace “would know” in the condition with the past form of know.","g11-7":"Start with the fixed advice clause “If I were you”. Then change should ask into a hypothetical result with a base verb.","g11-8":"The result “would travel” shows imagination. In this conditional, a past-looking form need not describe a finished past month.","g12-1":"“A realistic plan” tells you the speaker sees the course completion as possible. Match present finish with a real future result.","g12-2":"“An imaginary situation” means the speaker is not presenting millionaire status as a fact. Use unreal-condition grammar.","g12-3":"The requested result is an instruction. After the real if-condition, use the base verb directly as an imperative.","g12-4":"“If I had more free time” is an imagined condition. Complete the result with the requested modal before base learn.","g12-5":"“Won a scholarship” is the imagined condition; “might” expresses a possible result. Keep the two clauses in that relationship.","g12-6":"“Would study” is an imaginary result. Match its condition with a past form, not a future form.","g12-7":"Change both clauses: possession of time becomes unreal, and studying abroad becomes an imagined rather than predicted result.","g12-8":"Compare present see + a future result with past saw + an imagined result. Which presents meeting him as realistic?","g13-1":"The deadline was missed. Imagine an alternative past result of checking the system: modal + have + participle.","g13-2":"“Yesterday” keeps the imagined result in the past. Preserve the negative meaning and use a perfect result form.","g13-3":"The existing result is “might have passed”. The earlier study condition therefore needs had plus a participle.","g13-4":"“Now” makes the result present, despite the past condition. Here have means possessing a job, not a perfect auxiliary.","g13-5":"Keep “could have” with its participle to express a past possibility. Put the earlier had-clause after the result.","g13-6":"The third conditional does not put would in the condition. The if-clause needs had + the participle of know.","g13-7":"Reverse the missed action into an imagined map check. Then reverse getting lost into a negative imagined past result.","g13-8":"Look at “now” and the non-perfect result “would live”. These locate the consequence, independently of the past job decision.","g14-1":"“When Liam was a child” describes a general ability in the past, not success on one specific occasion.","g14-2":"Despite a delay, they succeeded in catching a particular tram. Use a form for a specific past achievement.","g14-3":"The perfect auxiliary haven’t needs the participle of be before the existing words “able to”.","g14-4":"“Be able” is followed by a to-infinitive. The future auxiliary is already present earlier in the sentence.","g14-5":"This is a polite request, not a statement of past ability. Start with the polite modal, then you and the base verb.","g14-6":"After “would like to” you need an infinitive. Can cannot occupy that position: express ability with a be-based phrase.","g14-7":"The room was actually found on one difficult occasion. Replace “successfully found” with managed to + base find.","g14-8":"“Haven’t” requires a participle. Ask whether can supplies that form, or whether ability needs a be-based alternative.","g15-1":"“Optional” means printing is allowed but unnecessary. Choose the negative that removes obligation rather than forbids printing.","g15-2":"“Bans” signals prohibition. The negative must say sharing is not allowed, rather than merely unnecessary.","g15-3":"“Yesterday” requires a past obligation form. The word to is already supplied after the blank.","g15-4":"Does already carries singular present agreement. Inspect whether the main obligation verb needs its base or -s form.","g15-5":"Visitors are subject to a rule. Put the subject before the necessity phrase; keep to with the required action.","g15-6":"Must is a modal, even with he. Inspect the added -s rather than changing the base verb wear.","g15-7":"“Not necessary” removes an obligation; it does not prohibit staying. Keep you as the subject and stay as a base verb.","g15-8":"Compare prohibition with optional entry. Which expression says that entering is against the rule?","g16-1":"The waiting client and lost booking create an immediate consequence. Choose strong near-future advice, not a past action.","g16-2":"“It’s high time” suggests the printer repair is overdue now. A past verb form follows the subject in this pattern.","g16-3":"Place the negative marker after better, before base share. Do not use a do-based negative here.","g16-4":"“For us” identifies who should leave. It is followed by a to-infinitive, unlike “It’s time we left”.","g16-5":"Keep “It’s time” before the subject clause. The past-looking change verb expresses an overdue action now.","g16-6":"“Had better” takes a bare infinitive. Inspect the extra to before ask.","g16-7":"After “It’s high time” put we and a past-form verb. Keep the computer as the object of the overdue action.","g16-8":"Look for advice linked to a possible missed flight. That consequence makes it more urgent than a casual café recommendation.","g17-1":"The wet floor is evidence for a strong positive guess. The modal should mean near-certainty, not permission.","g17-2":"Only the guard has the key. Judge whether a visitor’s opening the locked gate is a plausible past explanation.","g17-3":"“Leading” is an -ing activity happening now. Insert the auxiliary required between might and that activity.","g17-4":"“Closed” is a participle describing an earlier action. Build the past-deduction pattern after must.","g17-5":"The explanation is possible, not certain, and concerns a past action. Keep the modal, perfect auxiliary and participle together.","g17-6":"“I saw him elsewhere” is evidence against his being there. Replace the prohibition modal with a negative-deduction modal.","g17-7":"“Almost sure” requires a strong positive deduction. Since leaving is past, follow the given must opening with perfect grammar.","g17-8":"Separate possibility from certainty. The negative applies to seeing the email; the speaker does not claim to know for sure.","g18-1":"The powder receives the mixing action. For a regular production process, use present be plus a participle.","g18-2":"An unavailable kitchen suggests work happening now. The receiver needs a continuous passive, not an active verb.","g18-3":"Have is already given and checked is a participle. Supply the missing passive form of be between them.","g18-4":"Must is already given; labelled is a participle. A modal passive needs a base form of be between those words.","g18-5":"The subject receives a planned action. Keep the going-to group together, then add the passive be + participle group.","g18-6":"“Have been” already sets up the perfect passive. Inspect the final form of check rather than changing the auxiliaries.","g18-7":"Make “the labels” the subject receiving the check. Keep the original perfect tense and add passive be in its participle form.","g18-8":"Compare “is painted” with “is being painted”. The added form of be marks the painting as an activity in progress.","g19-1":"Find the sentence whose subject is the clothes receiving an action. Check for be plus a participle.","g19-2":"A normal passive needs a verb that can act on an object. Which option uses a verb without a direct object?","g19-3":"The designer performs the action, rather than being its instrument. Choose the preposition introducing the doer.","g19-4":"The receipt was sent to me, so I can become the passive subject. Agree past be with I before sent.","g19-5":"Distinguish the person doing the jacket repair from the needle used. The instrument phrase stays attached to its tool.","g19-6":"In the original active idea, shirts are sold and the company sells them. Make the receiver the passive subject.","g19-7":"Move “the coat” into subject position, retain past time, and add Maya as the agent rather than dropping her.","g19-8":"The passive focuses on the documents and their loss. Does its grammar necessarily tell you who caused that loss?","g20-1":"The missing word refers to Claire, a person, and performs recorded in the relative clause. Choose a subject relative.","g20-2":"The commas mark extra information about an already identified sister. Check which relative pronoun is excluded with commas.","g20-3":"The train change happened at the station. Choose the relative word that incorporates that place relationship.","g20-4":"The bag belongs to the passenger. The blank must express possession before the noun bag.","g20-5":"Within the relative clause, you is the subject of hear. The announcement is its object: keep these roles distinct.","g20-6":"The relative who already supplies the subject of recorded. Find the second, unnecessary subject referring to the same woman.","g20-7":"Insert Claire’s residence as extra information after her name. Enclose that relative clause with two commas.","g20-8":"An object relative can disappear when another subject already follows it. Compare “I bought” with clauses where the pronoun itself acts.","g21-1":"“The storm” is a noun phrase, not a clause with a subject and verb. Choose a reason connector that can precede a noun.","g21-2":"“Few people spoke it” is a complete clause. It contrasts with the language surviving, so use a clause-based contrast connector.","g21-3":"The following clause contains young people + could learn. Complete the connector for the videos’ intended purpose.","g21-4":"Despite functions as a preposition here. Convert feel into the form a preposition can introduce.","g21-5":"Preserving the language is the purpose of recording the stories. Attach the to-infinitive to that recording action.","g21-6":"Although already establishes the contrast. Find the second connector that unnecessarily repeats that same relationship.","g21-7":"Replace the full clause “it was raining” with a noun phrase naming the weather. Despite cannot directly introduce that full clause.","g21-8":"Finding a seat is the intended goal of leaving, rather than the cause of an earlier event. Inspect the infinitive’s role.","g22-1":"Takes is positive present simple with a singular subject. The tag needs the matching auxiliary, opposite polarity and a pronoun for Chris.","g22-2":"Nobody gives the statement negative meaning. Called is past simple; use a positive tag and a pronoun for an unspecified person.","g22-3":"A positive statement beginning I am has a special contracted negative tag. Keep I as the tag subject.","g22-4":"Let’s invites joint action. Its conventional tag asks whether we should do that action together.","g22-5":"The statement is present perfect. Reuse its auxiliary in the negative tag, then the matching subject pronoun.","g22-6":"Can’t already makes the statement negative. Keep the same modal in the tag but reverse its polarity.","g22-7":"There is uses the existential subject there. Reuse that subject in the tag rather than substituting it.","g22-8":"A rising voice sounds like asking for information; a falling voice more often expects agreement. Consider the speaker’s uncertainty.","g23-1":"“Colorado River” is a geographical river name. Apply the article convention for rivers rather than the one for cities.","g23-2":"A job is a singular countable classification. Listen to the first sound of engineer when choosing its indefinite article.","g23-3":"Arizona is a state name, not an ordinary singular countable object. Consider whether this type of proper name needs an article.","g23-4":"The map has already been introduced with a. Its second mention refers to that same known item.","g23-5":"The subject describes study in general, and the institution is used for its normal educational function. Avoid unnecessary articles.","g23-6":"University begins with the /j/ sound, like you. Choose the indefinite article by that sound, not by the written vowel.","g23-7":"Treat Alps as a mountain range and Rome as a city name. Apply their different geographical article conventions.","g23-8":"The zero article in “in hospital” can describe the institution’s normal function. Compare that with visiting a particular building.","g24-1":"There are exactly two gloves, and one is already identified. Refer to the remaining member of that specific pair.","g24-2":"The blank stands for people, with no following noun. Choose a plural pronoun rather than a determiner needing a noun.","g24-3":"Cup is singular and countable. The speaker requests one additional cup, not the remainder of an identified pair.","g24-4":"The sentence contrasts several seeds with damaged ones. Keep other as the determiner and mark plurality on seed.","g24-5":"Keep “another” before the number-and-duration group. That group expresses an additional amount, even though its unit is plural.","g24-6":"Advice is uncountable. The problem is the verb agreement, not an invitation to add a plural -s to advice.","g24-7":"Delete the repeated noun people after other. The remaining expression must work as a plural pronoun on its own.","g24-8":"The question specifies a known group and its remainder. Distinguish that from one extra item or unspecified other people.","g25-1":"“Than the road” signals a comparison between two routes. Use one comparative marker rather than doubling it.","g25-2":"“One of” introduces membership in a group of cities. Follow it with the definite superlative and a plural noun.","g25-3":"Clearly is an -ly adverb modifying explains. Compare the action using more rather than adding -er to the adverb.","g25-4":"The phrase begins “as quiet”. Complete the equality pattern with the connector that introduces the comparison standard.","g25-5":"Each half links a changing degree to another degree. Keep the two “the + comparative” groups together.","g25-6":"Easiest already has a superlative ending. Inspect the additional word that marks the same degree a second time.","g25-7":"If the tram is slower, it falls short of the train’s speed. Choose the opposite adjective inside “not as … as”.","g25-8":"An intensified comparison needs a suitable degree word before the comparative adverb. Avoid both double comparison and very + comparative.","g26-1":"“Noisy house” includes an adjective and a singular countable noun. Choose the intensifier pattern that includes an indefinite article.","g26-2":"Speaking quietly is deliberate preparation for the baby’s sleep. Distinguish that intention from an unintended degree-based result.","g26-3":"Many already introduces a large number of countable bags. Supply the degree word that combines with many.","g26-4":"Little measures the uncountable noun time. The following that-clause gives the consequence of this small amount.","g26-5":"The degree of the adjective causes a result introduced by that. Keep the adjective-based intensifier before that adjective.","g26-6":"Patient is followed by the singular noun teacher. The intensifier needs the noun-based pattern and an indefinite article.","g26-7":"Your new sentence describes the noun guide. Place the intensifier and article before helpful, and preserve the result clause.","g26-8":"A result follows a degree such as “so noisy”. A purpose clause instead states an intended goal, often with could.","g27-1":"The exhibition caused your interest. Choose an adjective describing the cause rather than the person experiencing the feeling.","g27-2":"Chris experiences the condition after the walk. Choose the feeling adjective, not the description of something causing tiredness.","g27-3":"The instructions cause confusion. Form the adjective for that cause from confuse.","g27-4":"The blank links an emotion to the planned day trip. Recall the adjective-preposition pairing used for being excited about an event.","g27-5":"We are the people experiencing surprise. Keep the feeling adjective with be and attach the price as its cause.","g27-6":"The sentence describes your feeling during the lecture. Change the cause adjective into the experiencer adjective.","g27-7":"Make I the person experiencing the feeling caused by the film. Follow was with that feeling’s -ed adjective.","g27-8":"An -ing adjective can describe a person who causes a feeling. It does not necessarily describe what that person feels.","g28-1":"Alex performs the catching and is also the person caught. Match a reflexive object to that singular male subject.","g28-2":"“Each helped the other” describes help between different people. Choose a reciprocal expression, not a reflexive one.","g28-3":"“Nobody helped me” emphasises that I performed the repair. Add the reflexive matching the first-person subject.","g28-4":"They are the people feeling pride about their own achievement. Match the pronoun after of to that plural subject.","g28-5":"Keep “on her own” as a unit expressing being alone or doing something without help. Place it after the action.","g28-6":"Concentrate normally takes the on-phrase directly. Inspect the unnecessary self-pronoun between concentrate and on.","g28-7":"The subject is I, and the phrase means without help. Use by followed by the reflexive matching I.","g28-8":"Looking at oneself returns the action to its subject. Looking mutually directs the action between different members of the group.","g29-1":"Water is uncountable, and the sentence compares an amount used. Choose the reduction word for an amount rather than a number of items.","g29-2":"Coats are plural countable items. “So I don’t need a new one” suggests a positive, sufficient small number.","g29-3":"Fabric names material rather than individual items here. Select the quantifier for an uncountable noun.","g29-4":"Shirts are separate countable items. Complete the quantity question for a number, not an amount of material.","g29-5":"Keep “None of” with the specific plural group. The remaining words form the statement about that group.","g29-6":"Don’t already makes the verb negative. Replace the second negative quantifier with the form used after a negative verb.","g29-7":"Plastic bags can be counted individually. Express a smaller number after “We should use”, keeping the noun plural.","g29-8":"Compare a small but usable amount with an amount presented as almost insufficient. Notice the presence or absence of a.","g30-1":"The group contains exactly two organisers. Choose the quantifier for a pair rather than a general whole group.","g30-2":"Us is an object pronoun. The quantifier requires a linking preposition before that pronoun.","g30-3":"“Every supplier” means the whole group. In the perfect verb group, place the quantifier after the first auxiliary.","g30-4":"The object pronoun them refers to two assistants. Add the pair quantifier after that object pronoun.","g30-5":"With be as the only verb, the whole-group quantifier follows be. Keep the feeling adjective after that quantifier.","g30-6":"Of is a preposition. Inspect the case of we after it: that position requires an object-form pronoun.","g30-7":"Begin with We and build the perfect verb group. Put the whole-group quantifier after its first auxiliary.","g30-8":"Uncountable material is treated as an amount, not a pair of separate items. Which quantifier can cover the entire amount?","g31-1":"The street and the bar make each of the two rooms noisy. Choose the quantifier rejecting both alternatives.","g31-2":"The existing or joins two alternative bookings. Complete the matching first half of that paired expression.","g31-3":"Those rooms is a specific plural group. Choose the preposition linking both with that determiner-and-noun phrase.","g31-4":"The task asks for standard singular agreement after neither of us. Select a singular present form of be.","g31-5":"In this either-or structure, inspect the subject closest to the verb. Keep the verb consistent with that nearer subject.","g31-6":"Neither … nor already rejects both people. Remove the extra negative while preserving the modal ability verb.","g31-7":"Both room options are rejected. After the positive verb like, use the negative quantifier for zero members of a pair.","g31-8":"“Not both” rules out liking the entire pair. Does it also logically rule out liking just one of the rooms?","g32-1":"The answer stands alone without repeating entrances. Use the pronoun for a zero quantity, not a determiner needing a noun.","g32-2":"The positive instruction permits an unrestricted choice of safe tour. Pick the quantifier meaning whichever.","g32-3":"The blank immediately precedes the noun access. You need a negative determiner, not a standalone pronoun.","g32-4":"Didn’t already makes the clause negative. Choose the person-word compatible with a negative verb.","g32-5":"The sentence concerns a specific group of entrances. Keep the zero-quantity pronoun with of and its determiner phrase.","g32-6":"Didn’t already negates hearing. Replace the second negative word with the matching any-compound.","g32-7":"Keep “We received” positive. Replace the full object phrase referring to zero replies with a standalone zero-quantity pronoun.","g32-8":"A positive permission followed by any seat allows a choice. Compare that meaning with any used under a negative verb.","g33-1":"Enjoy determines the form of the following activity. Look for an -ing form rather than a to-infinitive.","g33-2":"Restarting the app is proposed as an experiment that may fix it. Distinguish try doing from trying to achieve a difficult goal.","g33-3":"The verb promise takes an infinitive, and not negates that infinitive. Insert its marker before base miss.","g33-4":"The call is a duty for tomorrow, not a memory of a completed call. Use the remember pattern for a future duty.","g33-5":"Help is followed by the person helped and then an action. Keep that object between help and the action verb.","g33-6":"To belongs to the expression “look forward to” as a preposition. The following action cannot stay in its base form.","g33-7":"Walking was paused for another purpose. Follow stopped with the infinitive for the new map-checking action.","g33-8":"Locking is an -ing complement of remember. Does it name an earlier event being recalled or an instruction for later?","g34-1":"The reporting verb said is past. Change present am to the past form agreeing with the reported subject she.","g34-2":"Retain where, but arrange the reported question as a statement. Remove the question auxiliary rather than inverting the subject.","g34-3":"“The following day” shows a later report of the promise. Backshift the future modal, keeping help in its base form.","g34-4":"The command was negative. After told her, place the negative marker before the infinitive marker.","g34-5":"Tell needs the person addressed before its reported clause. Find that object and keep it next to told.","g34-6":"Me cannot attach directly to said in this structure. Use the reporting verb that takes a person as its object.","g34-7":"This is a polite request to me. Build asked + person + to-infinitive; consider the location word when reporting from elsewhere.","g34-8":"Ask whether the reporting context requires a change of tense. A still-true fact or present reporting verb can preserve the present.","g35-1":"The junior role is the position Ethan wants. Recall the fixed preposition used after apply for a job.","g35-2":"Experience influences the decision. Recall the fixed preposition in the expression depend followed by a factor.","g35-3":"An office is a building, rather than a city or country. Choose the arrive preposition for that type of destination.","g35-4":"Me is the person receiving the apology; missing the call is its reason. Choose the preposition for the reason.","g35-5":"The question asks about the person addressed. In everyday English, the preposition can remain after the talking verb.","g35-6":"Discuss is directly followed by its object, the interview. Inspect the extra preposition inserted between them.","g35-7":"Who replaces the person someone. Form a past-continuous question and keep the original talking preposition at the end.","g35-8":"A selected date or plan is something people agree on together. Distinguish that relationship from agreement with a person.","g36-1":"Similar links two things being compared for resemblance. Recall its dependent preposition before the comparison target.","g36-2":"Learning is an -ing action complement of interested. Use the adjective’s fixed preposition before that activity.","g36-3":"The labels are the chef’s responsibility or task. Select the preposition introducing the thing under that responsibility.","g36-4":"Possible allergies are the situation causing worry. Recall the preposition connecting worried with a concern.","g36-5":"Good describes a skill here, rather than a benefit to someone. Attach the skill activity after its dependent preposition.","g36-6":"Their project is the object of their pride. Replace the preposition with the standard pairing for proud.","g36-7":"She experiences fear of the activity flying. After “She is”, build afraid + its preposition + the activity form.","g36-8":"Compare skill in an activity with a beneficial effect on someone. The different prepositions select those different meanings.","g37-1":"The measurement is an adjective before a noun. Its unit stays singular, and a hyphen joins number with unit.","g37-2":"The measurement comes after was, not before a noun. Express the length using plural units and long.","g37-3":"In “two-___ trip”, the measure is inside a compound adjective. The number does not make that unit plural.","g37-4":"The unit inside the number adjective stays singular even when its ordinary plural is irregular. Inspect foot versus feet.","g37-5":"Keep the laptop’s age expression as one hyphenated adjective before laptop. Its measure noun cannot take a plural ending there.","g37-6":"The whole number-and-measure group modifies lesson. Join all its parts with hyphens, leaving the measure singular.","g37-7":"Convert a five-minute duration into an adjective before walk. Include an article and join the number to the singular unit.","g37-8":"An age before child is a compound adjective. Check its hyphens and the singular form of its year unit.","g38-1":"The clue is that a romantic relationship ended. Choose the phrasal verb for separation, not a mechanical failure.","g38-2":"The object is the pronoun it. In a separable phrasal verb, place that pronoun between the verb and its particle.","g38-3":"The sentence describes a car stopping working. Complete the breakdown expression, not an expression about entering by force.","g38-4":"The problem is something to handle. Recall the fixed preposition in the inseparable expression deal followed by a problem.","g38-5":"Find the two parts of the verb meaning execute a plan. Keep the noun object in a position allowed by that separable verb.","g38-6":"Fill in is separable and it is a pronoun object. Move the pronoun between the two verb parts.","g38-7":"Replace the form with it while keeping the request unchanged. A pronoun cannot follow both parts of fill in.","g38-8":"“Ended up” introduces the final outcome of events. Ask whether the taxi was necessarily the original plan.","g39-1":"Elena wants to find a lamp. The sentence says she is doing this now, so choose the searching expression in its -ing form.","g39-2":"Postponing means moving the meeting later. Choose that phrasal meaning and inspect where a pronoun object can go.","g39-3":"The report must be submitted by six. Complete the phrasal verb used for giving work to someone in authority.","g39-4":"To is part of “look forward to” and functions as a preposition. Change see into the form that can follow it.","g39-5":"The person being collected is you. Place that pronoun between the two parts of the collection verb.","g39-6":"Children become adults themselves, but someone raises them. Replace the intransitive growth expression with a transitive raising expression.","g39-7":"My sister becomes her. With a separable collection verb, put the pronoun inside the verb-and-particle pair.","g39-8":"Treat the to in “look forward to” as a preposition, not an infinitive marker. Inspect the action form that follows it.","g40-1":"Starting a company means establishing it. Choose the establishment phrasal verb and place a pronoun object between its separable parts.","g40-2":"The clue says the paper supply is completely used. Keep the fixed supply-depletion expression together before its object.","g40-3":"“Too loud” calls for decreasing volume. Select the particle for a reduction rather than an increase.","g40-4":"The address needs to be recorded in writing. Choose the particle that forms the note-taking expression with write.","g40-5":"The object is a noun rather than a pronoun. It can go between the two parts of the discard expression.","g40-6":"It is a pronoun object of a separable switching verb. Move it between turned and its particle.","g40-7":"Replace “the radio” with it. Keep that pronoun between the verb and the volume-reduction particle.","g40-8":"The phrase describes the state of a supply after use. Does it say paper remains available, or that the supply is exhausted?","g41-1":"Would rather is followed directly by a base verb when I performs the preferred action. Inspect any added to or -ing.","g41-2":"Would prefer uses different grammar from would rather. Choose the infinitive pattern that includes its marker.","g41-3":"The preferred action belongs to you, not I. After that second subject, use a negative past-simple clause.","g41-4":"We performs the preferred action. Put the negative after rather, before the base travel verb.","g41-5":"Keep the two compared actions grammatically parallel. Neither base action after would rather needs an infinitive marker.","g41-6":"The subject of the preferred action is still I. Remove the unnecessary infinitive marker after rather.","g41-7":"Retain the preference and the window location. Changing rather to prefer also changes the following infinitive pattern.","g41-8":"The past-form clause expresses a preference about another person. Ask whether it describes a past event or a requested behaviour now.","a1-1":"The question asks for a meeting place, not the stage of their relationship. Focus on Chris’s opening background.","a1-2":"Compare what Chris wanted with what his partner wanted. Keep the two people’s needs separate.","a1-3":"Look for the final reason after the rules were tried. Early arguments are not necessarily the eventual explanation.","a1-4":"“Permanently” means the improvement lasted. Check the limiting phrase Chris uses about how long the rules helped.","a1-5":"Focus on Chris’s final lesson about love. Does he describe only feelings, or also ways people treat each other?","a1-6":"Use the change from early happiness to difficulties as an anchor. Place the final lesson after the relationship’s outcome.","a2-1":"Separate the children already born from the baby being expected. The question asks about the existing children.","a2-2":"Several difficulties are listed, but the question asks which the parents identify as the hardest. Listen for that emphasis.","a2-3":"Choose a skill or experience the children gain, rather than a cost or challenge faced by the parents.","a2-4":"Inspect the words “equal”, “every child” and “every day”. Compare that absolute claim with the parents’ admission.","a2-5":"Listen to the description of the older children’s morning responsibilities. Check whether it includes helping siblings.","a2-6":"Separate the family introduction, the household jobs and the later discussion of challenges. End with preparing for the baby.","a3-1":"Distinguish moving neighbourhoods from moving cities. Elena explicitly compares these two kinds of move.","a3-2":"Find the pair of qualities Elena wanted when choosing the apartment. Do not substitute things she bought later.","a3-3":"Focus on sorting belongings. Broken objects and unwanted clothes have different destinations.","a3-4":"“Every task” is an absolute claim. Inspect the unfinished decorating jobs Elena mentions near the end.","a3-5":"Listen to the changed evening habits, not just the new morning routine. Compare her phone use before and after moving.","a3-6":"Start with the old home and move; then distinguish sorting possessions from creating routines and changing habits.","a4-1":"The question asks his age at entry, not the duration of college. Listen to the first sentence of the story.","a4-2":"Find the named degree subject. The marketing club is an extracurricular activity, not necessarily his degree.","a4-3":"Look for the strategy he learned after struggling in large lectures. Distinguish preparation from simply attending.","a4-4":"Focus on Ryan’s early failure to check the online system. Verify the number and the type of work missed.","a4-5":"Never rules out every such experience. Listen for the semester when Ryan describes both his work and study commitments.","a4-6":"Anchor the sequence in the first-month shock, the lecture strategy and the later friendships and practical projects.","a5-1":"Ryan qualifies his view rather than giving an unconditional yes or no. Pay attention to what follows “but”.","a5-2":"Find how deadlines affected his own studying. The question concerns focus and behaviour, not test content.","a5-3":"Compare repeating facts with explaining, solving and using ideas. Ryan distinguishes these approaches near the end.","a5-4":"Always is a strong claim. Compare it with the example of knowledgeable students who become anxious in tests.","a5-5":"Listen for the assessment methods Ryan wants in addition to exams. Check whether he presents them as alternatives only.","a5-6":"Track his argument: a reason for exams, limitations of tests, then the balanced assessment approach.","a6-1":"The first job is described before the office position. Do not confuse the electronics job with later operations work.","a6-2":"Find the behaviour that made his schedule too full. Distinguish the cause from the missed deadlines it produced.","a6-3":"“Shortly before” points to the last-minute problem with the event. Listen for the venue’s phone call.","a6-4":"The manager’s advice concerns what to say when today is impossible. Compare that with a promise to do everything immediately.","a6-5":"Original refers to the location booked first. Follow the search for another location before deciding where the event happened.","a6-6":"Put the shop experience before the office lessons. The venue problem belongs to the later client-event episode.","a7-1":"Listen to the manager’s explicit first-day requirement. It concerns something Ethan wears, not something he books.","a7-2":"The question asks the room’s condition after use. Booking it beforehand is a separate rule.","a7-3":"Find the action recommended when arriving late. Distinguish communicating about lateness from pretending it did not happen.","a7-4":"“Every second” exaggerates the online requirement. Compare it with the team’s expectation of a reasonable response time.","a7-5":"Focus on the specific rule about videos and headphones. The recording distinguishes permitted conversation from disruptive noise.","a7-6":"Follow the topic changes: badge and rooms, message etiquette, noise, then meeting timing and asking questions.","a8-1":"The question concerns his home location, not the city centre where he works. Listen to the opening sentence.","a8-2":"There are two morning times: leaving home and catching the train. Select the time attached to the train departure.","a8-3":"Look for the explanation immediately after he says he does not drive often. Do not confuse it with winter train delays.","a8-4":"The train duration is separate from the walk to the station. Compare “half an hour” with the stated ride length.","a8-5":"Never would exclude all taxi trips. Listen for what Chris might do when he has an early meeting.","a8-6":"Separate the normal train routine from delays and occasional alternatives. Keep the final evaluation at the end.","a9-1":"Ethan compares change with a bridge. Ask whether that metaphor suggests an immediate jump or gradual preparation.","a9-2":"Find the specific subject his hotel-working friend studied online. Communication was an existing skill, not the new subject.","a9-3":"The eventual employer valued a skill from the hotel job. Separate that transferable strength from newly learned technical skills.","a9-4":"Immediately is the key claim. Compare it with the actions the friend took while still in his old job.","a9-5":"Listen to the application stage after the CV update. Does getting a role happen on the first attempt?","a9-6":"Arrange reflection and learning before applications. Rejections belong to the interview stage, not the initial decision.","a10-1":"Sofia distinguishes preparation for work and future choices from learning only for fun. Focus on her opening motivation.","a10-2":"Find the single topic she chose when starting small. AI prompts come later in the learning process.","a10-3":"Compare copying everything with reading and changing the result. Focus on how she uses the output to learn.","a10-4":"Perfect is the key word. Listen to what Sofia accepts about the first version of her project.","a10-5":"Distinguish a one-time finished project from her plan for ongoing changes. Listen for the stated interval.","a10-6":"Use the progression from organising tasks to automation, then prompts and a planned project.","a11-1":"Listen for the type of accommodation in the opening. Liam contrasts it with a large hotel and its facilities.","a11-2":"Find why the location suited his daily plans. Staff kindness is a benefit he mentions later, not the initial selection reason.","a11-3":"Identify the change Liam made after the first noisy night. The shower issue has a different cause.","a11-4":"Verify the duration in the opening holiday description. Do not confuse it with a particular night at the property.","a11-5":"Always means there were no changes. Compare that with the shower problem when other guests used water.","a11-6":"Start with the accommodation and location, then problems, staff help and the final assessment of the stay.","a12-1":"Arrival transport appears before his decision about renting a car. Do not confuse the journey to D.C. with transport within it.","a12-2":"Find the sightseeing goal named before the monument visits. The White House is a later stop, not the initial main area.","a12-3":"Listen for the benefit of Smithsonian museums that he calls amazing. The length of his visit is a separate detail.","a12-4":"Distinguish how long he stayed from how long he says someone could stay. Possibility is not his actual visit duration.","a12-5":"Focus on how close Chris could get to the White House. Seeing it is different from entering it.","a12-6":"Arrange arrival and the main monuments before the museum and lunch. The White House visit belongs later in the day.","a13-1":"The state is named in the geographical introduction. Do not confuse it with the river or a viewing area.","a13-2":"The question asks the natural agent that made the canyon. Listen for the formation explanation, not a tourist activity.","a13-3":"Find the practical reasons for choosing the South Rim. Helicopters and the Skywalk are separate options.","a13-4":"Focus on the safety warning, which describes changing weather as well as strong heat. Check the possibility wording.","a13-5":"“No preparation” conflicts or agrees with which planning and physical-condition requirements? Listen to the river-hike warning.","a13-6":"Follow the introduction and formation, then viewpoints and walking, before alternative activities and the final reflection.","a14-1":"Jordan describes visual qualities and then says what he likes most. Find that final emphasis in the island section.","a14-2":"The desert section links visible stars to a condition at night. Focus on the explanation introduced by because.","a14-3":"Old cities are contrasted with natural settings. Look for the human elements of their beauty.","a14-4":"“The same for everyone” is absolute. Compare it with Jordan’s opening qualification about personal views of beauty.","a14-5":"Focus on the alternative offered to a professional hike. Does enjoying the views require specialised hiking ability?","a14-6":"Keep the examples in the spoken order of categories, ending with the explanation of how beauty affects people.","a15-1":"Jordan qualifies the claim about the oldest cities. Find the historical uncertainty before the individual examples.","a15-2":"Match the walls and water-source clues to one city description. Do not rely only on which city name sounds familiar.","a15-3":"Find the named place in the Athens section. Separate the monument from the city’s contributions to culture.","a15-4":"Listen to the economic role described for Byblos. A modern attraction is different from an ancient trade function.","a15-5":"“Only museums” excludes current residents. Compare it with the everyday activities described in the final paragraph.","a15-6":"Follow the cities in the order Jordan presents them, then end with his point about modern life among ancient remains.","a16-1":"The night-before section lists preparation actions. Identify what was checked rather than charged or packed.","a16-2":"Compare the easy early path with the later climb. Listen for the change in its width and slope.","a16-3":"The return route differs from the uphill one. Find the natural feature that made the longer route interesting.","a16-4":"Check the contents of the backpack, rather than the clothes worn on his feet. The jacket is a packing detail.","a16-5":"“Definitely avoid” is a strong claim. Compare it with Alex’s separate views on inviting a friend and going alone.","a16-6":"Keep night-before preparation before the journey and climb. The view comes before the different route down.","a17-1":"The question concerns a change to his bus journey. Find how he makes room for walking before reaching his destination.","a17-2":"Distinguish workout length from frequency. The question asks minutes per home session, not evenings per week.","a17-3":"Listen for what he calls the hardest part. The exercises themselves are listed separately from that obstacle.","a17-4":"Check both the companion and the day in his cycling routine. Do not mix this with his weekday walks.","a17-5":"Listen to Chris’s opening attitude towards strict routines. Compare that attitude with the simple alternatives he uses.","a17-6":"Track weekday walking, home workouts and Saturday cycling, then his difficulty and the benefit of a quick walk.","a18-1":"Many possible garden locations are described. The question asks where Sophie joined, not every possible location.","a18-2":"Compare Sophie’s initial belief about plant expertise with what she learned on joining. Notice the contrast after but.","a18-3":"Look for the paragraph beginning with what matters beyond the food. Which social experience does she emphasise?","a18-4":"Focus on what can happen to the leaves. Check whether the garden is described as a perfect system.","a18-5":"“Every urban problem” makes the claim much stronger. Compare it with the final qualification about a small garden.","a18-6":"Begin with the definition and joining experience, then garden jobs, community benefits and future possibilities.","a19-1":"The definition combines clothing price and production speed. Find both ideas before the discussion of environmental costs.","a19-2":"Focus on the destination of clothes people throw away. Burning is another mentioned outcome, not the same destination.","a19-3":"The closing section offers actions consumers can take. Choose a recommendation, not a description of factory behaviour.","a19-4":"Check the paragraph about making fabric. It identifies inputs to production before discussing dye and transport.","a19-5":"Always would mean no unsafe workplaces. Compare that with the qualification about conditions in some places.","a19-6":"Follow the definition, waste, pollution and workers, then the consumer changes proposed at the end.","a20-1":"Find the month when several expenses happened together. The important feature is the combined pressure, not one regular bill.","a20-2":"Ethan names three budget categories. Distinguish necessary costs, money kept aside and discretionary spending.","a20-3":"Checking the bank app revealed repeated payments for unused services. Find the items he then stopped paying for.","a20-4":"Financial stress is not automatically debt. Listen to the explicit statement distinguishing these two situations.","a20-5":"Focus on the question Ethan now asks before buying something on sale. Check whether discounts alone determine his choice.","a20-6":"Use teenage spending, first-job income and the difficult month as anchors before the budgeting system and later lessons.","a21-1":"Find the Monday event that removed his usual transport option. The busy week explains the pressure, not the mechanical cause.","a21-2":"Compare his expected commute length with the actual travel time. The first surprise is introduced as a contrast.","a21-3":"The money paragraph lists driving expenses. Do not replace that comparison with the earlier examples of activities on the train.","a21-4":"Never would exclude weekend and out-of-town trips. Listen to how Ethan qualifies his present driving habits.","a21-5":"Compare what was possible as a passenger with what was impossible while driving. Reading is one of the listed activities.","a21-6":"Begin with his old opinion and the breakdown. Put the practical surprises before the cost comparison and changed choices.","a22-1":"Olivia gives a health example before the learning example. Find the small action she added after dinner.","a22-2":"Separate the short initial walks from the longer habit that developed later. The question asks about the beginning.","a22-3":"Focus on the advice following “Sometimes you will miss a day”. Does she recommend abandoning the habit or resuming it?","a22-4":"Listen for Olivia’s explicit qualification about magic. Patience and consistency are part of that qualification.","a22-5":"Compare frequency as well as length: one study pattern is daily, while the other happens only monthly.","a22-6":"Arrange the big-plan problem before Olivia’s small-walk example, then learning, missed days and future small steps.","a23-1":"Match each named person with the platform associated with them. Focus specifically on the Snapchat example.","a23-2":"The question asks the parent company, not the product’s founder. Listen to the sentence following the TikTok introduction.","a23-3":"Alex’s closing value is not simply money or rapid growth. Listen to the reason for protecting users’ confidence.","a23-4":"Only subscriptions excludes every other revenue stream. Compare that with the recording’s list of income sources.","a23-5":"Focus on the problems paragraph rather than the success stories. Check which concerns about users’ information appear.","a23-6":"Start with the founder examples, then shared growth and income patterns. Put the problems before the final lesson.","a24-1":"Natalie numbers home and work or school before naming the third place. Interpret third socially, not as a café’s address.","a24-2":"The remote-work paragraph names practical facilities and atmosphere. Find what supports working away from an office.","a24-3":"Follow the connection between attractive photos, online attention and visits. Separate that from the coffee’s ingredients.","a24-4":"Only is the decisive word. Compare it with the opening claim and the different reasons developed afterwards.","a24-5":"Check the examples of spaces designed for work. Identify whether both electrical access and quiet areas are mentioned.","a24-6":"Follow the social-place reason before remote work, then online visibility and international coffee culture.","a25-1":"Compare the language young people learn with the language remaining among older people. Focus on the shift between generations.","a25-2":"Migration is described through families moving and children learning. Find which language-use setting changes inside the family.","a25-3":"The final paragraph gives preservation activities. Choose actions supporting use, rather than causes of language decline.","a25-4":"Look beyond vocabulary in the paragraph explaining the loss. Check the cultural and thinking-related examples.","a25-5":"Never excludes all historical pressure. Compare it with the paragraph on punishment, colonization and political factors.","a25-6":"Keep the official-language pressure before migration and historical factors. End with what is lost and preservation efforts.","a26-1":"The opening biography names a birthplace and explains its modern country. The later move belongs to another stage of life.","a26-2":"Find the person who noticed Mozart’s ability while he was very young. Distinguish that person from later audiences.","a26-3":"The move is explained by his wish for freedom as he grew older. Do not confuse this destination with his birthplace.","a26-4":"Always financially easy excludes the later money problems. Listen to the paragraph about freelance work and earnings.","a26-5":"Verify the quantity in the closing account of his work. Do not confuse it with his age or either date.","a26-6":"Follow childhood ability and European trips before the move, adult compositions, financial difficulties and death.","a27-1":"Jake explicitly distinguishes these tunnels from modern metro lines. Focus on their original practical functions.","a27-2":"The limitation is explained through conditions inside the tunnels. Select a safety reason rather than an entertainment preference.","a27-3":"Compare the two environments: traffic and noise above, then sensory details below. The question asks about that contrast.","a27-4":"Listen to Jake’s final statement about exploring alone. Interest in history does not necessarily mean a plan to explore.","a27-5":"Check the list of original tunnel purposes. Goods delivery is separate from moving water or heating pipes.","a27-6":"Arrange the introduction and old practical uses before present hazards and the tour experience, ending with Jake’s conclusion.","a28-1":"Claire’s initial training appears before the advertisement. Find the earlier area of study, not the later transport job.","a28-2":"The employer wanted particular voice qualities. Listen for that description rather than the number of applicants.","a28-3":"Focus on the recording process: repetition, consistency and pronunciation. Distinguish a work challenge from lack of celebrity.","a28-4":"Everyone recognised her would remove the anonymity described later. Compare a familiar voice with a recognisable person.","a28-5":"Listen to the pronunciation training beyond her usual language. Check whether station names created an extra learning task.","a28-6":"Begin with the familiar voice and her training, then the job selection, recording work and anonymous impact.","a29-1":"Follow the preparation process before the powder is mixed with other ingredients. The question concerns the insects themselves.","a29-2":"The environment paragraph compares insect production with cattle. Choose a claim about resource use, not taste or marketing.","a29-3":"Find the concern linked specifically to allergies. Separate that from general questions about price or flavour.","a29-4":"Everyone implies a universal expectation. Compare it with Jordan’s separate comments about people who will and will not try them.","a29-5":"Focus on the nutrition argument rather than the environmental one. Check the nutrient supporters identify.","a29-6":"Follow preparation before environmental and nutritional arguments, then barriers and the personal assessment.","v1-1":"Chris and his partner do this over plans when their wishes conflict. Is the interaction friendly agreement or disagreement?","v1-2":"His partner wants more of this from him. Think of noticing someone and making time for them.","v1-3":"They feel this before staying quiet. It describes a reaction to irritation, not happiness or extreme fear.","v1-4":"This happens to their relationship after the attempted rules. Interpret the two words together, not as physical breaking.","v1-5":"Chris lists this alongside communication. Think about considering a partner’s wishes rather than controlling them.","v2-1":"The parents already have nine children and are expecting another baby. This use concerns a birth, not an ordinary delivery.","v2-2":"Feeding the dog is a child’s responsibility. Is it an assigned duty or an optional entertainment?","v2-3":"The older children need less help getting ready. Relate independent to how much assistance a person needs.","v2-4":"Children do this when they share work and solve problems together. Think of joint action, not competition.","v2-5":"A supportive family helps members through challenges. Consider encouragement rather than criticism.","v3-1":"Elena describes the move as a fresh start. The phrase concerns a new stage of life, not the condition of food.","v3-2":"She changes neighbourhoods while staying in the same city. Think of the scale of this place.","v3-3":"Elena does this before deciding which belongings to keep. It concerns grouping and organising, not buying.","v3-4":"Some clothes go to help other people. Compare this action with selling for payment or throwing things away.","v3-5":"Her sequence of waking, opening curtains and drinking coffee illustrates routine. Think of repetition rather than an unusual event.","v4-1":"Missing a deadline means work arrives too late. Does the word concern a finishing limit or the beginning of a course?","v4-2":"Ryan misses two small assignments. In college, these are set by teachers and completed by students.","v4-3":"The professor speaks to more than two hundred students in a lecture. Identify the teaching format.","v4-4":"Club projects help Ryan apply for internships. Think of a temporary opportunity to gain workplace experience.","v4-5":"His roommate and friend have different backgrounds. Here the word concerns earlier lives, not a scene behind them.","v5-1":"Exams help check progress. The direction is towards understanding a subject better over time.","v5-2":"Studying seriously before deadlines helped Ryan build discipline. Focus on maintaining effort, not a university subject.","v5-3":"An anxious student may forget material during a test. Infer the feeling from that stressful situation.","v5-4":"Exams should be balanced with projects and homework. Think of the distribution of attention among several methods.","v5-5":"An exam may ask students to apply knowledge to a problem. Distinguish using understanding from merely repeating facts.","v6-1":"Ethan coordinates catering alongside a venue and presentation. The action connects several arrangements so they work together.","v6-2":"A company needs a venue before inviting clients to an event. This noun names a location, not an invitation.","v6-3":"Catering is needed for the client event. Which practical service provides what guests will eat and drink?","v6-4":"Ethan checks availability when calling alternative places. He needs to know whether they can accept his booking.","v6-5":"A realistic completion time changes others’ expectations. The noun concerns what people anticipate, not what has already happened.","v7-1":"Booking rooms and avoiding disruptive noise are examples of etiquette. Think of socially accepted behaviour at work.","v7-2":"Employees must wear a badge. The object helps identify the wearer rather than store belongings.","v7-3":"A reasonable response time does not mean replying every second. Look for a sensible standard rather than an extreme one.","v7-4":"People need quiet to concentrate. Infer the mental action from working without distraction.","v7-5":"Ethan says you need not pretend to know everything. Compare acting knowledgeable with genuinely understanding.","v8-1":"Chris lives here and travels into the city for work. Suburbs describes an area beyond the centre, not another country.","v8-2":"The train’s predictable timing makes mornings easier. Think of knowing what to expect before the journey.","v8-3":"Winter delays can make Chris wait longer. The word concerns a schedule problem, not a faster trip.","v8-4":"His daily train trip is a commute. The purpose and repeated home-to-work route matter.","v8-5":"A colleague can give Chris a lift. In this travel context, it is not a machine moving between floors.","v9-1":"The friend moves from hotel work towards IT. Career change concerns a type of work, not merely a new office.","v9-2":"Small study sessions continue over months. Consistent describes that steady pattern rather than occasional bursts.","v9-3":"He applies after learning basic IT support. A junior role is aimed at someone near the beginning of that work.","v9-4":"Several companies reject his applications. Consider whether this leads to an offer or its absence.","v9-5":"Communication skills become an advantage. Ask whether they make success easier or harder.","v10-1":"Automatically moving information illustrates automation. Compare a process running on its own with repeating every step manually.","v10-2":"Sofia uses templates to organise tasks. Think of a pattern that can be reused rather than an entirely new design each time.","v10-3":"A prompt goes into an AI tool before its response. Distinguish the input instruction from the generated output.","v10-4":"Preparing for changing jobs requires being flexible. Here it concerns adaptation, not bending an object.","v10-5":"New skills give Sofia more options. Think of different available possibilities, not a single compulsory action.","v11-1":"Liam’s guesthouse is smaller than a hotel with many facilities. The word names accommodation rather than a private visit.","v11-2":"He leans over a railing on the balcony. Consider the protective barrier along an exposed edge.","v11-3":"Weak sound insulation lets corridor noise reach the room. Focus on limiting sound passing through a structure.","v11-4":"Liam uses earplugs after a noisy night. The objects work at his ears, rather than changing the corridor itself.","v11-5":"An inner courtyard may offer more quiet. Picture an open area inside surrounding buildings.","v12-1":"The Washington Monument is an example of a landmark. Think of a recognisable feature visitors use or remember.","v12-2":"Chris views exhibits inside the museum. The word concerns displayed material, not visitors’ entry tickets.","v12-3":"Interactive exhibits let visitors engage rather than only look. Consider the visitor’s participation.","v12-4":"The city is busy but not chaotic. Chaotic adds a lack of order, not simply a large number of people.","v12-5":"A viewpoint offers a place to see a scene. In sightseeing, it is a viewing location rather than a personal opinion.","v13-1":"Visitors walk along the canyon rim. It is an edge at the top, not the river at the bottom.","v13-2":"A hiking trail takes walkers through the landscape. The word concerns a route rather than a vehicle.","v13-3":"The demanding descent needs fitness. Think of physical capacity, not the fit of someone’s clothes.","v13-4":"The canyon feels unreal when first seen. Here this describes amazement, not proof that the place is imaginary.","v13-5":"The warning says not to take risks. Think of exposing yourself to possible harm during an activity.","v14-1":"The Alps’ peaks rise above the lakes. A peak is a specific part of a mountain, not the whole range.","v14-2":"Clear lakes reflect the sky like a mirror. Think of what appears on the water’s surface.","v14-3":"The islands are tropical. Relate the adjective to warm climate regions rather than mountain height.","v14-4":"Deserts have a quiet, powerful atmosphere. Here it is the mood of a place, not a scientific description of gases.","v14-5":"Old-city buildings are historical. The adjective connects them with earlier times rather than new construction.","v15-1":"An inhabited city still has residents. Ancient ruins alone do not guarantee that this adjective applies.","v15-2":"Archaeological evidence comes from examining physical remains of earlier societies. Distinguish it from a modern opinion poll.","v15-3":"Damascus and Byblos were important for trade. Think of economic exchange rather than tourism alone.","v15-4":"Byblos was an important port. Its role connects ships, goods and a coastal location.","v15-5":"A living timeline combines ancient and modern stages. The image organises history by time rather than distance.","v16-1":"Rain can make the path muddy. Infer the ground condition from water mixing with soil.","v16-2":"Alex slows down as the path becomes steeper. The adjective concerns the angle of the climb.","v16-3":"His different route down runs beside a river. Route names the way taken, not the final destination only.","v16-4":"Alex almost slips and catches himself. The movement is accidental rather than a planned jump.","v16-5":"He can see the city in the distance. The phrase describes something visible but not nearby.","v17-1":"Chris uses walking, workouts and cycling to keep fit. The phrase concerns maintaining physical condition.","v17-2":"Push-ups, squats and planks form his workout. Think of an exercise session rather than an employment task.","v17-3":"He does short sessions regularly. Frequency and repetition matter more here than how long one session lasts.","v17-4":"An easy, realistic routine can continue after work. Think of something achievable rather than an ideal impossible plan.","v17-5":"A quick walk helps clear his head. This is a mental benefit, not physically washing his head.","v18-1":"Mint and basil are herbs in the garden. Their leaves can be useful for flavouring food.","v18-2":"Gardeners pull weeds out. These plants are unwanted among the plants they choose to grow.","v18-3":"Dry soil tells gardeners to water. The word concerns the material roots grow in, not the air.","v18-4":"Neighbours share seeds before growing plants. A seed can begin a new plant, unlike a harvested meal.","v18-5":"Sophie feels more present when away from her phone. Here the word describes attention, not a gift.","v19-1":"Thrown-away clothes end up in landfills. These places handle waste, rather than sell usable clothing.","v19-2":"Making fabric uses water and energy. Fabric is the material clothes are made from, not a finished outfit.","v19-3":"Factories dye clothing materials using chemicals. Think of changing colour rather than cutting or washing.","v19-4":"Second-hand clothes have a history with another owner. They are not necessarily broken or unwanted forever.","v19-5":"Friends can swap clothes. Both sides give and receive, rather than one person only donating.","v20-1":"Ethan’s job brings him income. Distinguish money received from money spent on bills.","v20-2":"With no safety net, unexpected expenses feel stressful. The expression means protection in difficulty, not a literal net.","v20-3":"Food and transport belong to essentials. These are necessary costs rather than optional treats.","v20-4":"Unused subscriptions still take money every month. Consider recurring access payments rather than a single purchase.","v20-5":"Intentional spending involves checking whether he really wants an item. It is deliberate rather than automatic.","v21-1":"Meetings, errands and a dentist visit fill Ethan’s week. An errand is a practical task involving a short trip.","v21-2":"The weekly pass covers his transport for a stated period. Distinguish it from a ticket for one ride.","v21-3":"Public transport need not be perfect to be reliable. Think of working dependably most of the time.","v21-4":"Small car repairs appear among driving costs. The action addresses damage or failure, not fuel consumption.","v21-5":"He paid car costs little by little. The phrase contrasts small gradual amounts with paying everything at once.","v22-1":"Some days Olivia does not feel motivated to walk. The adjective concerns willingness to begin or continue.","v22-2":"Small habits still require patience. Consider continuing calmly while results take time.","v22-3":"She advises returning without feeling guilty. The feeling involves blaming yourself for missing a day.","v22-4":"Progress should be consistent rather than perfect. Think of regular continuation despite occasional mistakes.","v22-5":"Changes happen over time. The phrase contrasts gradual development with transforming everything immediately.","v23-1":"The founders made their fortune from online products. Here fortune concerns wealth, not simply good luck.","v23-2":"An online community connects people who share something. They need not all live in the same neighbourhood.","v23-3":"A partnership is one possible way companies earn money. Think of cooperation between parties rather than a single isolated actor.","v23-4":"Privacy appears as a social-network concern. Relate it to control over personal information.","v23-5":"Alex says trust has lasting value. Consider users’ confidence in reliability rather than the number of advertisements.","v24-1":"Remote work is done using cafés and online tools. The expression describes working away from a traditional office.","v24-2":"A bit of background noise may suit café workers. It is surrounding sound, not the main conversation or music being studied.","v24-3":"Sockets help people use electrical devices in cafés. The word names a connection point, not a piece of furniture.","v24-4":"Instant coffee is compared with espresso and other drinks. Think of a quick preparation method using water.","v24-5":"Cafés are places to connect with people. Here connect has a social meaning rather than an electrical one.","v25-1":"Migration involves families moving to another city or country. The term concerns relocation, not a short holiday only.","v25-2":"An official language is used by schools, work and government. Consider its recognised public role.","v25-3":"Parents want better jobs and opportunities for children. These are chances available to them, not guaranteed results.","v25-4":"Communities teach languages to preserve them. The goal is to prevent loss rather than replace them.","v25-5":"Heritage includes stories and traditions passed down. The word connects current communities with their cultural past.","v26-1":"Mozart worked as a composer and pianist. Distinguish creating music from performing it on an instrument.","v26-2":"His father noticed talent early. Think of a notable natural ability rather than a paid position.","v26-3":"Freelance work made his earnings uncertain. He worked independently rather than holding one guaranteed permanent position.","v26-4":"His music has beautiful melodies. Focus on recognisable sequences of notes rather than a whole theatre building.","v26-5":"The Magic Flute is an opera. Think of a dramatic work performed through music and singing.","v27-1":"A service tunnel carries water or pipes or enables practical delivery. Distinguish it from a public metro route.","v27-2":"Unstable tunnel sections make visits dangerous. The adjective concerns security and possible collapse.","v27-3":"Some tunnels link warehouses with other buildings. A warehouse holds goods rather than being mainly someone’s home.","v27-4":"The quiet underground atmosphere feels creepy. Infer an uneasy feeling from darkness, sounds and isolation.","v27-5":"The hidden city lies beneath people’s feet. This preposition describes a lower position.","v28-1":"Commuters hear announcements on their way to work. The noun describes regular travellers rather than occasional tourists only.","v28-2":"Claire studies intonation alongside pronunciation. Focus on how the voice rises and falls, not individual consonants only.","v28-3":"The producer asks for a more neutral tone. Think of reducing strong emotion rather than making the sound louder.","v28-4":"Claire’s work is anonymous even though her voice is familiar. Distinguish hearing someone from knowing their identity.","v28-5":"Behind the scenes describes work passengers do not see. It refers to the hidden effort supporting the public experience.","v29-1":"Powder and other ingredients form a patty. It is a shaped part of the burger, not the whole meal.","v29-2":"Protein appears in the nutrition argument. This word describes a nutrient rather than a flavour or packaging material.","v29-3":"Land, water and food are resources used in production. Think of inputs supporting a process.","v29-4":"A clear label helps people check ingredients and allergies. It supplies information on the product.","v29-5":"A reasonable price may persuade curious customers. Here reasonable describes cost that is acceptable rather than extreme."}};
